Harpy: These monsters have the ability to fly and resemble ugly women- their skin is extremely oily and susceptible to fire. They're bones are quite brittle and will crack under pressure. Their feathers are fine for use in arrows and their pelts make-\n\n-for good middle class clothing. They've a cruel sense of humor. They are known to scoop up targets and drop them from lethal heights. If a Harpy nest is discovered it is suggested that it be burned immediately.\n\nKrummavisur: Resembling a Raven, though staggering in size. These beasts have awe-inspiring wing spans and considerable strength.\nThey scoop up their prey and toss them immeasurable distances. The beast is not to be fought alone. They can kick up dust-\n\n-with the density to block sight for yards. When effort is applied they can shove a man in full plate to his knees with the power of the air from their wings alone. It is suggested that the feet are struck, as they are quite thin and easy to amputate.\n\nCockatrice: An odd looking creature. Standing at the height of a small building with the width to match.\nThey are quite intimidating to fight up close and are able to fly, rendering close combat to be the inevitable result of an encounter.\n\nThe Cockatrice has the ability to exhale a thick gray smog that turns whoever comes in contact with it to stone. Being submerged in water will negate this effect.\nTheir skin is unholy in strength, steel will hardly damage it, an aurum blade is required.\n\nThe Beast's bones are strong as stone and they've the strength to topple a building with ease.\n\nLeaf Apes: Fundamentally illusive creatures that have flesh as hard as rock and bones like fresh timber. They possess decent strength for their size but struggle to penetrate leather. They tend to rush a target and make an effort to climb atop its face.\n\nOnce atop the victim's face, the Leaf Ape will tear at the flesh. Bladed steel weapons are practically useless against these creatures and will often break upon contact with their skin- blunt weapons are suggested as well as aurum swords.\n\nCave Leeches: A beast that drops down on it's targets and spews acid onto them, it has the strength to render armor useless. They only attack in complete darkness. \n\nNo one has survived an encounter thus far.\n\nDrakes: Creatures with bat-like wings, the ability to breathe flame, scaly hides, complete with muscular legs and tail-- these truly majestic beasts have captured the awe of men for centuries. Despite this, magical perversion has lead to the Drake species\n\nundergoing various mutations and become near extinct. It is expected that combat with a Drake be avoided and alternative methods to killing be used to neutralise the threat such a beast poses. The Drake's main weaknesses are its wings, mouth and eyes,\n\n-however this beast will continue fighting until the last drop of blood runs from it's wound. Decapitation and siege weaponry is suggested.\n\nEnlarged Arachnid:\n\nThe average spider, though colossal in size and extremely venomous- they are susceptible to fire and relatively easy to defeat compared to other beasts in this compendium.\n\n-Sentient Naturals-\n\nThese beings are to be reasoned with if possible and relocated away from areas where they may have negative effects on the local mundane population.\n\nTroll: Usually found lurking under bridges, in caves or forests. These beasts can be at times helpful and friendly-- at other times they are forces to be reckoned with. Trolls are generally quite intelligent and are capable of speech, complex emotion and-\n\n-co-operation. The best method of slaying a Troll is to use aurum and to disable it's method of movement, trolls are known to carry enormous amounts of weight in the form of fat around their waist and are unable to get up if any of their legs are damaged.\n\n-Sentient Supernaturals-\n\nThese beings are to be reasoned with and if possible relocated away from areas where they may have negative effects on the local mundane.\n\nSpecters: Spirits without bodies, these beings can be benevolent, neutral or malevolent, just like a living person. They can only be harmed by aurum as normal metals will pass straight through them.\nSpecters have the ability to wield enchanted weaponry\n-\n\n-and don enchanted armor, some have the ability to fly and cast spells, those that have this ability use it to deadly effect. It is suggested that aurum weaponry be used combined with a high-guard stance.\nSpecters are vulnerable to salt and it is-\n\n-suggested that the perimeter in which the spirit is manifesting be surrounded by lines of pure sea salt.\n\n-Constructs-\n\nThese are the result of spells or golemmancy, they are able to fit into the supernatural sentient category as well as the monstrous supernatural category depending on what they are- constructs usually have a master and a purpose to follow.\n\nHunters: \n\nSharing many traits with a specter, the Hunter has the ability to fly- it wears large, heavy armor but is not impeded in any way by this fact. The Hunters are apparently a sort of Golem gone rogue, they once served a holy order and sought to-\n\n-slay dangerous Magi. Now they slay any who wield magic, including Marked Men. Hunters explode when defeated and it is advised that those who have defeated it clear the area before it does so, as shrapnel is often projected.\nAurum seems to weaken the-\n\n-creature considerably, though it is probably wise to use steel in order to damage the armor before an aurum weapon is presented.\nHalf swording is suggested due to the armored nature of this foe.\n\nGolems: The result of powerful runes and magic, Golems can take many forms- from colossal lumbering humanoid to slithering metal snake, all Golems have an 'Impera' who dictates their every action-- Golems will attempt to protect these individuals at all-\n\n-costs. These constructs are often the result of a soul being bound to the magegold core, destroying this would most likely neutralise the threat. \nWays to destroy the Golem depend on a number of things including environment and the material the Golem is-\n\n-made of. Golems made of stronger materials may need to be cast from great heights, thrown into watery depths or toppled into a substance hot enough to melt metal.\n\n-Supernatural Monstrosities-\n\nThese creatures defy all reason. They serve no purpose in this world and are to be slain.\n\nNecrophages: These creatures come in two variations; Fleshed and Skeletal.\n\nFleshed typically maintain a slumped posture, they've considerable strength and an immunity to pain.\n\nAurum weaponry is recommended for these types of creatures.\n\nAlone, their presence is a joke, but when amassed it's often best to simply route.\nThe method of reanimation is unknown, however it is to be noted that they appear more frequently around areas of frequent fighting or wickedness.\nFire is a weakness.\n\nLich: The ultimate product of an individual's greed for power or knowledge. Liches are beings that have made a transformation into entities powered purely on arcane energies and a deep seated hatred for those that get in the way of more power.\n\nTypes of Liches vary broadly and improvisation is to be expected. One recurring trait seems to be the presence of a crystal known as the phylactery- it is where the Lich's soul is rumoured to be kept, destroying this crystal will kill the creature.\n\nBloodsuckers/Sangbriseur:\nThese creatures take the form of an obscene lump of slimey acid. Depending on their environment, the creature can absorb it's surroundings and become deadlier-- there are such things as Bloodsuckers who have absorbed fire,\n\nbecoming thrice as dangerous as their acidic counterparts. To dispose of the creature, it is suggested that it be cut into smaller pieces and set alight-- be sure that every chunk is burnt or else it will regenerate over time.", ""),
        StaticBook("Frost Witches", "§bGallidron Elindrow", "An\n       Encounter\n           With\n      Frost Witches\n/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\\n\n            By\n   Gallidron Elindrow\n\nI first learned of the dreaded creatures known as Frost Witches from one of the tomes of the grand libraries of old Haelun'or. I remember the fear that gripped me as a child when I learned of their macabre ways. Though as an adult of more mature\n\nconstitution and greater understanding, I revisted the subject of \"monsters\" and relearned the available details of the Frost Witch.\nThay are exclusivly women, so far as any adventurer who's survived an encounter with them can tell. They are grotesque and\n\nunnatural to look upon. Grey skinned and with eyes like ice they appear in their true form. But it is not so easy to spot them, for Ice Witches are masters of disguise and employ illusion to hide their nature. In fact, as the story goes, they will adopt\n\nthe guise of a young woman and sit and cry in the midst of a snowy glade to lure in unsuspecting men. The clever frost witch will attract the unwitting male and take him to the rest of her coven. There to be devoured by these fiends. They are, without\n\nexception, powerful magic users, though the older ones are the more dangerous and adept. In fact, every frost witch draws her power from a \"Witch Mother\" who is the head of her particular coven. Like unto a queen bee she will build her nest and gather\n\n\\    \"   `  I  '   \"     /\n  \\   \"   ` I '  \"     /\n    \\  O  {X}  O   /\n    /\\ , O II O ,  /\\ \n^ / % \\  ! I !   / % \\ ^\nl/ ``~'' \\  %  / ``~'' \\l\n\\ * X *  \\  /  * X * /\n  \\ i i,.,{   x   },.,i i /\n    \\     /  \\     /\n      \\ / `  ' \\  /\n            %\nG1\n\nworkers who rely upon her, and she them to some extent. When another frost witch is of sufficient power and experience she may set out to create her own coven. They begin by searching for women who are depressed or sadened. The frost witch will then mark\n\nwoman with a curse that will appear as little more than a bruise upon the back of the woman's neck. If the marked female succombs to her sadness and grief, she will become a frost witch herself. Thus do their numbers swell. It is reportedly a favorite\n\ntactic of the frost witches to eat a married man and then mark his grieving widow. Thus they gain both a meal and a potential coven member. The woman who is marked may not become a frost witch \"if her faith is strong and she resist her sadness to seek\n\nafter joy\", as the old tomes say. Though it should be noted that there is no known way to remove the curse once it is applied. The woman in question will most likely live with the mark for the rest of her days.\n\nIn order to destroy a frost witch coven one must destroy the witch mother. In some cases this alone is enough to cause the coven to whither and die over time. In other cases, however, another witch may be of sufficient strength to rise up and assume the\n\nrole of witch mother and the coven can limp on. If one is a \"monster hunter\" of sorts, then you will likely want to exterminate as many of the witches as possible to reduce the chances of this occuring.\n\nIt is important to note that frost witches are not rabid animals. They are clever and capable of reasoning. It is possible to treat with them, but the humor of the witch can change quickly and every moment you stand in their pressence you are in grave\n\ndanger. In truth they are fickle and there is no telling what a frost witch might do, should you seek to speak with them. It is said, however, that if one finds a frost witch in disguise and calls them out, the frost witch is bound by her curse to offer\n\nthe clever mortal one favor. But this is only rumor and would prove dangerous to study.\n\nA great deal of the knowledge I possess has come from the reading of books. But I have done some field research as well. The following pages will detail my, albeit brief, encounter with the frost witches.\n\nI was told of a coven of frost witches to be found far in the north, by a man of the nation of Salvas. His name was Archrim Elmoran and he had encoutered these witches himself. How the man survived I do not know, for it seems that the witches were intent\n\non eating him. I told me that there was an ancient ruin that the witches called home, upon a lonely and frozen peak. The coordinates were as follows.\n{X 153, Y -3762}\n\nI, being curious, set out to see this for myself. I traveled far into the north from\n\nmy home in the warm waters of New Haelun'or and traversed the mountains of the north.\n\nThere, I found a most curious set of ruins which seemed to at one point have been a church or temple of some sort. They were in a valley to the south of the\n\nreported witches lair. It is there that I found the symbols marked G1 (glyph 1) which you saw earlier in this book.  What ever ancient civilization left these ruins, I would be most interested to learn more about them. Whilst searching about I had the\n\ngood fortune to happen upon another Mali'aheral, a High Elf woman named Allona. After ensuring that she was not one of the dreaded frost witches I asked what she was doing in those parts. Apparently she was a mage and had sought solitude to meditate and\n\nincrease her skills. The ruins that we were in had provided such solitude until I had interrupted it. She warned me that the frost witches where still about, though further up the mountain range, and she advised me against going to investigate the area.\n\nWe spoke for a while longer before she showed me a cavern that she had discovered just north of the ruins. It held an ancient library of sorts and had evidently been of some importance to whatever people had left it behind. The books were too ruined to\n\nread, but the very exsistance of such a place is highly facinating. However, I digress.\n\nAfter parting ways I decided to go on and look in on the frost witches. It was not my intention to actually interact with them or get close enough to do so.\n\nWhen I approached their lonely peak an icey wind blew past me and seemed to steal the very warmth of my blood away. An ancient and imposing structure sat atop the peak and looked for all the world to be abandoned. Using some wit and no small amount of\n\ndexterity I climbed the peak, agianst my better judgment and explored the old fortress.\n\nIt was empty as I suspected, though the air was somehow even colder within. After looking around a bit I thought I saw foot prints in the snow, or at the very least\n\nimpressions of where something had been. They were by an alcove under the main floor of the ruin. As I approached I heard something moving on the other side of the alcove and quickly hid. A wall moved out and one of the menacing creatures emerged\n\ncautiosly from the hole. The frost witch looked about as though she knew I was there and crept forward. For all the world I thought I was doomed. Doomed for my curiosity and for my foolish assumption that the keep was empty. Only then did I realize. They\n\ndid not live in the delapidated structure atop the mountain, they lived beneith it. A man from Salvas had told me that those mountains were full of caverns and tunnels.\n\nThe witch moved forward and I could have sworn she could smell me. Then, just before\n\nshe reached my hiding place, she turned and went up the stairs to the main floor. I took no more chances and I ran for my life. I was out of the keep and down the mountain as quickly as gravity and semi-safe desent would permit.\n\nIn closing. Frost Witches are a dangerous facination and one should not seek them lightly. They are still there, so far as I know, lurking in the tunnels beneath that frozen mountian. \nI hope this book has been interesting at least and helpful at best.\n\nI hope that you remember what you have read should you ever have....\n\nAn encounter with Frost Witches.\n\nPublishing date\n18th of Deep cold, 1516\n\nby Gallidron Elindrow\n\nDedicated to Archrim Elmoran, the human who risked life and limb to discover the hold of the Frost Witches and told me about it.", ""),
        StaticBook("Killin' a troll", "§bJentos", "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n\n     Killin' a troll\n\n\n  By Jentos Adam, last representant of the house of Blackwood.\n\n\n\n\n\nxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n\n\"Dear reader, in this book you shall learn to: recognize, flee and kill a troll.\"\n\nIntroduction\nxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\"Troll. Nasty, cruel and stupid creatures capable of destroying whole towns, And fierce war-beasts.\"\nxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n\nWere to find a troll?\nxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n\nTroll's can be found about everywhere in these wild lands. From the frozen dwarven plains. To the heat of the Caliphate!\"\n\n\"Troll's like to live in unforested mountainous regions, dark caves in the\n\nmountains. Or in a big clearing in the forest.\"\n\nRecognizing a troll\nxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxWho has never pictured a troll? Big ugly figure walking in the forest? But the're are alot of different types of orcs and sub-trolls like half trolls half\n\nmen!\"\n\n\"Trolls can vary from 8 feet when young to a giant 20 feet tall!\nThey have a relative big orc like head with a few men-like features.\"\n\n\nFleeing a troll\nxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxRunning away from a\n\ntroll in a rather forested area is easier than in a mountainous or flat region.\"\n\nTactic's: \n\"If you find yourself in a forested or near a forested region, your best bet is to simply go we're the tree's are smaller so you can slow the troll\n\nand get out of its reach.\"\n\n\"Hiding in a cave isn't a good idea, a hungry troll is apt to wait a long time to be able to get his supper (especially if it's the trolls own cave!).\"\n\nKillin' a troll!\nxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n\n\"In this final section, you will learn how to \n\n       Slay\n      -----\nThese mighty creatures!.\n\n\"The troll's weak spot, (like many other beats) are the eye!\nUnfortunatly it is easy to see that being able to get a good shot at them isn't easy.\"\n\n\"The trick is to, hack the legs until it falls, wait t'ill it reaches down or bend or simply attack it\n\nfrom a good height!\nxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n\n\"On this. I say to you! Have thy luck I never had!\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy Recipes", "§bRosso", "Sleeping Draught\n\n==================\nWild Lettuce\nWater\n\n>Break leaves from plant\n>Steep leaves 1 hr. in hot water.\n\nBurn/Rash Oiuntment*\n\n==================\nWild Lettuce\n\n>Break leaves from plant\n>Grind leaves into poultice\n>Add water and a whole Wild Lettuce\n*Helps with dry skin\n\nRespiration Potion\n\n==================\nWater\nCaterpillar Fungus\n\n>Grind up fungus\n>Add fungus to heated water, and mix well\n\nHealing Water I\n\n==================\nWater\nLily pad\n\n>Heat up water\n>Cut up Lily Pad and mix with water\n>Pour some in wound\nWARNING:\n    Stings slightly\n\nHealing Water II\n\n==================\nRose\nSpider Eye\nBone\nWater\n\n>Gring up Rose, Spider Eye, and Bone\n>Mix with water that was once snow\n\nHealing water II\n                (Cont’d)\n==================\nLily Pad\nHealing Water II\n\n>Place a few drops in wound\n>Place Lily Pad on wound and bandage\n-If Lily Pad turns brown, it’s healing\n\nPoison\n\n==================\n3 Spider Eyes\n2 Bones\nWater\n\n>Grind up all 3 Spider Eyes\n>Grind up both bones into bonemeal\n>Add both to heated water\n\nPoison II\n\n==================\nPoison I\n2 Spider Eyes\nBone\nRose\nDead Lily Pad\n\n>Grind up Bone, Rose, Spider Eyes, and Lily Pad\n>Mix with Poison I\n\nBone Pain Reliever\n\n==================\nShroom of Immortality\nWater\nLily Pad\n\n>Grind up Shroom of Immortality\n>Gring up Lily Pad\n>Mix together, then add heated water\n\nStomach Pain Reliever\n\n==================\nJelly Ear\nWater\nSugarcane\n\n>Grin up Jelly Ear and mix with heated water\n>Extract sugar from sugarcane, and add to mixture\n\nDraught of Thought\n\n==================\n2 Magic Shrooms\nWater\nWild Lettuce\n\n>Grind Wild Lettuce into a poultice\n>Grind up Magic Shrooms\n>Mix together, then add water, and heat\n\nLimb Pain Reliever\n\n==================\n……. Shroom\nWater\nLily Pad\n\n>Grind up Lily Pad\n>Grind up ……. Shroom\n>Mix together, then add to heated water\n\nEnergy Potion\n\n==================\n2 Cocoa Beans\nSugarcane\nWater\n\n>Grind up Cocoa beans\n>Extract Sugar from Sugarcane\n>Mix together, then add to heated water\n\nEnergy Potion II\n\n==================\n3 Cocoa Beans\n2 Sugarcane\nWater\n\n>Steep Sugarcane in water half an hour\n>Grind up Cocoa beans\n>Get rid of sugarcane, and add Cocoa Beans, ground, to water\n\nPain Reliever\n\n===================\nTippens Root\n\nCopiers note: The book ends here, it is unfinished.\n\nAlchemy Recipes\n\nUnknown Author\nCopied by Rosso\nVaerHaven", ""),
        StaticBook("AlchemyGuide 1-1", "§bDaniel", "\\_+*+_/\n   Alchemy Guide\n\n\n\nVolume 1 – The Basics\nPart 1/2\n\n\n\n\n-By Daniel Nodal\n\nPrelude\nFor many years I have been an alchemist, not one of great name, not one of greater skill, I am one who focused on the everyday works of what alchemy may do. Even before reaching the rank of Mentor alchemist in the former Alchemy Guild in the\n\nlands of the Fringe, I already was teaching a student. Before my teacher disappeared for a few years, he promoted me to that rank, allowing me to fully teach anyone I wished, and make them alchemists with the time came to it. I have had many students\n\nthroughout the years, even being as young as I am, and believe I am capable of doing so. This book series is a combination of all I have learnt in alchemy, containing information from old alchemists, such as Tippen and the Traveling Alchemist, to the\n\nnewest masters. Alchemy is performed by all races, in the most diverse areas of expertise and the most diverse of people. What one can do with alchemy is only stopped by the reach of his imagination.\n\nAlchemy\nThe first question I ask all my students is for me to gather the reasons why they wish to study under me. What is alchemy? To my disbelief, most of those that come to learn from me, do not know that answer, or have clear misconceptions about\n\nthe topic. The answer I most receive is a simple “It’s the making of potions.” Many men and women want to simply to stand in front of a brewing stand or a cauldron and throw reagents inside it to then receive a potion that would grant them immortality!\n\nAlchemy is, of course, not potion making, which results in many students surprised, and many give up because I do not even speak of potion making until just before they become novice alchemists. Alchemy is much more than that, far more than potion making,\n\nas potions are only a way in which to receive the results of alchemy. The best way to describe alchemy is as such, the study and practice of the natural principle of Equivalent Exchange.\n\nEquivalent Exchange\nI have been asked a few times, as have many alchemists before me, if Alchemy is magical in it’s essence. While many of the desires and goals of alchemy might appear supernatural, the process itself, and the way of achieving it, ar\n\nfar from it. The principle of Equivalent exchange is at the core, the law that states that to get anything, something of equal value must be offered in return. The value I refer to here is not a monetary one, as some might be compelled to think. If so,\n\nall alchemists would be throwing diamonds into a pot over and over again to receive what they would want. That does not happen of course, because the value used in equivalent exchange is associated with the properties, characteristics and representations\n\nof an ingredient or reagent. The alchemist’s job is not to mix up the ingredients like a soup, but knowing which ingredients to use, how to prepare them, how much to use, when to add them, and keeping everything safe. This is what sorts the Alchemists\n\nfrom the chefs. The recipes that alchemists have are not ones simply like cooking, which state that you need 3 eggs, 2 cups of sugar, a bucket of milk, and then mix everything up, place in a hot oven for a known amount of time. Alchemy recipes do not\n\nspeak of exact quantities or times, for there are many ways to have the same value. The recipe does instead tell the reader what representations he needs, normally in terms of what are called Symbols.\n\nSymbols\nDuring my times as a student, I was very skeptical of most of the theory of alchemy. One of the things I could not understand was the need for Symbols. If the representation and properties of a reagent are what is used, the elements\n\nthey fit in are not necessary to be stated. After a few years of becoming an alchemist I finally realized the purpose, it is because not only do ingredients have perfect representations, but the sibling representations are as well necessary to\n\ncreate proper results. Perfect representations would never be able to be properly used and leveled, and no reagent has only one representation. For such, attaching a symbol to an ingredient fits far better than attaching only the representations.\n\nBy choosing the representation or symbol you can decide the properties of what you create. One could use the representation of life for healing potions, but depending if it’s an earth symbol or a water symbol, it will have other smaller scale\n\nrepresentations that will distinguish them. A water symbol will provide a more smooth and fluent healing rate, making the wound heal nicely, and the earth symbol will give more strength and resilience to the healing process, perhaps making it hard to get\n\nthe wound reopen. Everything fits into at least one of the four symbols or elements of alchemy, Fire, Water, Earth and Air.\n\nThe way you look at what a symbol represents is looking at the element in question and assessing what properties it has\n\nand what it is associated with. You do not look exactly at what the element is, assessing a symbol to a reagent is more about symbolism than pure correct connection. It’s to aid the alchemist design and investigate the ways of alchemy in a more practical\n\nway, without having to reach so fully into the details. Each ingredient or reagent may also be of different strengths depending on the properties and rarity it holds. Those which are rarer have a greater value than those which are common, and as such\n\nthey are have as well more strength of use in alchemy. An ingredient may be a Weak, Moderate or Strong symbol, those of greater strength required quite more skilled hands, but the payoff might be quite better.\n\nFire\nStarting with the more easily explainable one, fire is the symbol very used by alchemists that focus themselves on improving skills of fighting and giving new arsenal to use in wars. While I don’t find myself using many of fire symbols,\n\nthey are an absolute in the making of many of the truly fantastic deeds of alchemy. Most of the representations of fire are quite easy to grasp, because they fit perfectly onto what fire truly is. It may represent heat, burning, consumption, destruction,\n\nwarmth, power and chaos. Most of these have as such a great use for damaging others or to give power to oneself or others. Alchemist fire for example, is a great demonstration of what could be done with the use of fire symbols, fire in a bottle.\n\nDevastating and powerful, it is most likely the most dangerous conception that an alchemist can create. The representation of chaos is one more confusing, one that may bring unknown results as controlling chaos is quite hard job to have. Fire\n\ndoes have another way you may look at it, as many poets might say, “the flames of passion ran deep in his heart, but he acted as in a burning rage.” Fire may also represent emotions, both those of anger, regret, rage, confusion, and those of love,\n\npassion, confidence. These representation are especially useful in potions to improve oneself, and in poisons to bring damage emotionally to others. The mythical love potion though, is one that is by experience impossible to have in full effect, as love\n\nis in it’s own way subjective, and varies from person to person. The closest thing that may be created is one that gives well being and peace of mind, or ecstasy. Most of those are considered drugs.\n\nAs for the properties one may find generally in fire symbols, the idea of heat is normally present in them. A flower or plant that is warm to the touch could be a good indicator of a fire symbol. The effects it might bring to a person might be associated\n\nthat way as well, such as becoming feverish, or feeling a burning itchy sensation on touch. And of course the flavour of many of them, which I don’t really advise to be a way to figure out what kind of symbol it is, is most of the times spicy and strong,\n\nsometimes sweet as it goes well with raising emotions.\n\nWater\nSometimes even said to be the base ingredients, a symbol of water is essential to all potions and oils that alchemy can create. This is of course due to it’s liquid and coherent state, or it would not be a potion or oil. The base is the main\n\ncomponent of a potion, where all other reagents are added and mixed in. It is important to refer that it is considered a base only to the creation of potions and oils, but in the other more exotic fields of alchemy, they are not used as such. Normal\n\nwater is the most simple of bases to use, not very useful for any powerful potion, as it’s not all that pure, it’s used mainly in minor potions. Distilling it would purify it and make it far more useful in lesser potions. The purest form for a base you\n\ncan find is aqua vitae. Aqua vitae can also be called Alcohol, or distilled alcohol, it is simply the result of distilling brews such as ale, wine or any other intoxicating beverage. With it, the most powerful of potions can be created. The other base\n\none could use is lard, which is used only in the creation of oils.\n\nNow onto the representations that define a symbol of water. Like when you imagine a stream of water, you imagine calmness, fluidity, peace, order and balance of nature.\n\nContinuing with the balance of nature, you have to understand that water is one of the fundamentals of life, and as such, water represents many parts of it. Life, regrowth, rebirth are the most important of representations, making water symbols sometimes\n\nthe most important in healing potions, and antidotes. Tasting a water symbol normally brings the other representations that water might have, freshness and a minty flavour are normally what you get from safe to eat water symbols, even going so far as\n\ndescending into cold and ice, another two representations of water. Do not think that just because water symbols may represents life and are used in a lot of healing potions that they’re safe to eat, even water symbols might be essential to the creation\n\nof poisons, as a stream may be a thought of life and balance, but a strong river might be the location of one’s death.\n\nContinues in part 2\n\nWritten by Daniel Nodal\nThe First Seed, 1473", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy: Vol III", "§bSarrion", "Volume III\nAlchemy Potions\n\nBy: Unknown alchemist from the alchemist's guild\n\nRe-Written By:\nSarrion di' Pascali\n\nThe truer part of the thoughts of what most people think of alchemists is that they make potions. I have already stated in previous volumes that alchemy is not potion making, but potions are the best way for an alchemist to use\n\nhis knowledge in an applicable way. There are other methods of course, which shall be talked more about in the advanced alchemy volume, but the main result of an alchemist’s study is infact potions. They are easy to\n\ncollect, easy to move around, and easy to administer. Normally drank, they can in some rare or specific occasions poured on something. The recipes that alchemists create are not like those of cooks. Equivalent Exchange is their purpose,\n\nwhich means the order of the ingredients does not matter, and the ingredients used only have to obey the representation needed. More or less ingredients might be used considering the alchemist’s needs, and it may be more and less powerful.\n\nIn regular terms, a minor potion has small effects and more side effects but of smaller danger. A greater potion has far greater effects, and supposedly less side effects, but those that it has are quite strong as well.\n\nAn example in the use of potions is also in order, a potion of mending to heal a minor cut on a child’s knee should never be a greater potion of mending.\n\nNot only is it a waste, but also because the effects are too strong to heal a small wound, like you should never cook a pie in the great blacksmith furnaces of Urguan.\n-------------------\n\nA potion being in liquid form means that an achemist has to use water symbols as it’s base. Water, Distilled water, Aqua Vitae are the bases used for potions, with Aqua Vitae considered the finest one can use. Lard is used as well, not in potions\n\nbut in oils, serving to external uses. In the example before, I mention a minor and a greater potion. There are three types of the ‘same’ potion, and those are minor, lesser and greater. They are the same potion in name,\n\n as their effects are suppose to be the same, only in different powers. Minor potions are the weakest, normally left for the new alchemists and student alchemists to conduct and make. Their effects often times are minimal,\n\nyet they are far easier to do, but do not think they are useless, as many times an effect should be small to have a good impact. These are made with water as their base. Lesser potions come right after, with distilled water\n\n as their base, and greater potions are the greatest strength one can have of a potion, using aqua vitae in their base. Of course the recipes for the stronger potions require\n\nnot only purer reagents, but as more complexed and defined recipes.\n-------------------\n\nI can not demonstrate all recipes that exist, as each alchemist may do his’ or her’s potions, each has it’s mastery and orientation of alchemy. They have their taste in reagents, but do remember, that a good alchemist\n\nis one that uses logic and common sense in their potions. When doing a mending potion, an alchemists would never use and earth symbol which would represent death, but it’s necessary representations,\n\nbe it life or regrowth. The recipes do not come with reagents, but with the required symbols and representations. Different ways of preparing the reagents, and different reagents will leave mindly different results, which exist due\n\n to impurities and different representations in ingredients. Be careful of how Equivalent Exchange works, or the effects you get might be quite different than those that you are expecting.\n===================End.\n\nPotion Glossery:\n\nNote from Sarrion di' Pascali: \"Please this will list the name of known potions, for full details please refer to Alchemy: Potions.\n-Sarrion\"\n-------------------\n\nPotion of Mending\n\"Often times the most used potions of an alchemist’s arsenal.\"\n- Minor:\nBase: Water\n1 Symbol of Water (Life)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Life)\n\nPotion of Regeneration\n\"A quite potent potion, it is part of the healing potions\"\n\nBase: Blood\nMandragora\nLiver of a Mother\n2 Earth Symbols (Life)\n1 Air Symbol (Grace)\n\nPotion of Stoneskin\n\n\"A potion that almost seems like a legend come true.\"\nBase: Distilled water\n4 Symbols of Earth (Fortitude)\n2 Symbols of Water (Life)\n3 Symbols of Fire (Power)\n1 Symbol of Air (Speed)\n\nPotion of Strength\n\n\"capable of enhancing the strength of someone\"\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n1 Symbol of Earth (Fortitude, Power, Resilience)\n\nPotion of Celerity\n\n\" know as potion of agility and potion of speed\"\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n1 Symbol of Air (Speed)\n\nPotion of Accuity\n\n\" provides the drinker with heightened senses\"\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n2 Symbol of Air (Perception)\n\nPotion of Wizard’s Wisdom\n\n- Minor:\nBase: Water\n2 Symbols of Water (Balance, Clarity)\n1 Symbol of Fire (Power)\n2 Symbols of Earth (Endurance)\n1 Symbol of Air (Perception)\n\nPotion of Smoke Whispers\n\n\"a cloud of smoke is formed\"\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n4 Air Symbols (Flight, Transport)\n1 Fire Symbol (Combustion)\n\nPotion of Sleeping\n\n\"objective is placing someone to sleep.\"\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n2 Symbols of Water (Calmness and Relaxation)\n1 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n\nPotion of Freezing\n\n\"only intent is freezing or cooling something\"\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n1 Symbols of Water (Cold)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Resilience)\n\nWaterbreathing Potion\n\n\" allows someone to breath underwater\"\n Aqua Vitae mixed with a bit of seawater\nMermaid’s Gills and Liver\n1 Symbols of Water (Fluidity)\n1 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Resilience)\n\nFrost Oil\n\" An oil which has similarities with the potion of freezing\"\n\nBase: Lard\n3 Symbols of Water(Cold, Ice, Frost)\n2 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Fortitude)\n\nFlame Oil\n\n\"The Oil of Flame\"\n\nBase: Lard\n3 Symbols of Fire (Power, Destruction, Burning)\n2 Symbols of Earth (Endurance)\n1 Symbol of Water (Calmness, Balance)\n\nFortitude Oil:\n\n“Armor for your Armor”\n\nBase: Lard\n3 Symbols of Earth (Endurance, Fortitude, etc.)\n2 Symbols of Fire (Potency, Power)\n\nHair Rejuvenating Oil\n\n\" it rejuvenates hair\"\n\n- Minor\nBase: Lard\n1 Symbol of Air (Speed/Grace)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Hardiness)\n1 Symbol of Water (Rejuvenation)\n\nGlue Oil\n\n\"For your elf ears when they fall off\" -Sarrion\n\nBase: Lard\n1 Symbol of Air (Travel/Speed)\n2 Symbols of Earth (Hardiness, Connection)\n\nAlchemist’s Fire\n\n\"‘fire in a bottle,’\"\n\nPlease refer to:\nAlchemy: Poitons\n\nfor the recipe and the warning that comes with it.\n===================End.", ""),
        StaticBook("Elem. Alchemy", "Jistuma", "The Tales of a Travelling Alchemist\n------------\nElements of Alchemy\n\nVolume I-2 of V\n\nThe Elements:\n--------\nFire\n--\nEarth\n--\nWater\n--\nAir\n\nSkeptics often approach me with a wholly ignorant conundrum, and that is “how can you trace every spell and element back to these four basic elements.” But what they do not realize is that an understanding of what is meant by these four elements is\n\nrequired before that question can be answered and in fact, if that understanding is acquired then this question will never be asked.\n\nThese four basic, rudimentary elements, Fire, Earth, Water, and Air, are what every potion, oil, elixir, and\n\nconcept, in Alchemy of course, is based upon. An ignorant outlook on this statement is that what is meant is the physical, literal meaning behind these elements but one must look into the symbolism, they must look deeper than the surface. I shall go into\n\neach of these elements in detail, so that you, and budding alchemist or simply a well-read individual, may understand how Air may both aid someone’s speed, as well as their perception of their surroundings.\n\nFire\n===\nThe Destroyer, the Unmaker, the Cleanser.\n\nWe will begin with one of the most rudimentary and used element, Fire. Fire can represent many a feeling, action, and state and because of this, it is often one of the most useful elements of the Alchemist’s arsenal. Fire can represent rage, anger,\n\nhatred, as well as bravery, courage, and gusto. Through this a brew can be crafted to make someone unquestionably and savagely furious, but it could also craft a brew to strengthen one’s will, and allow them to do what other men would shrink away in fear\n\nat. These feelings, these emotions can be invoked by the element of Fire within a potion, but feelings are not the only thing that fire can produce, actions are another.\nActions are often the most recognized aspect of fire, and by this I mean the action\n\nof burning, consuming, and destroying. Alchemist’s Fire, a handy inferno in a bottle, is an example of this aspect of Fire, for within it is the action of destruction. This action can be further expanded upon with burning oil placed upon a weapon, but if\n\none were to use this example they would also have to include the element of pain, another feeling that fire an invoke. State is aspect that Fire can take one within an alchemist’s potion and this is often represented as Chaos.\nIt is true that Chaos is\n\noften something alchemist’s fear, for it is something athat often ruins a potion, but it is yet another aspect of Fire that must be recognized. Chaos, being the state that it is, may seem like it has little place in a potion but brilliant minds and\n\npowerful reason can bring it to use just the same as the first farmer realized a stone and a stick, two completely different objects, could help them till their fields by putting them together. As this is not a book on the philosophy of alchemy, or\n\nsolely on the element of fire, I will not go into depth on how Chaos can be used. The teacher must leave something for the student to ponder.\n\nEarth\n===\nThe Enduring, the Lifegiver, the Fortifying\n\nWith the conclusion of Fire, the other elements will not have as long as description for most of the fundamentals of the elements are covered in how they can represent different things, I will simply go over what they may represent, starting with Earth.\n\nEarth is a truly powerful element for it may represent metals, strength, the notion of being stalwart, solidity, firmness, flora, animals, and countless other, quite obvious, subjects. Earth is often used for potions that increase one’s endurance, or\n\nstrength, and it is a must in potions that toughen one’s skin. In fact, a strong series of representations of Earth are required to perfect a barksin, or stoneskin potion. Protecting oneself is not the only use for the element of Earth, for it is also\n\nused in antidote potions, disease-curing potions, and, of course, fertilizers. Antidotes use this element for it can represent animals, and among those elements are snakes and spiders, often associated with poison. Of course a symbol of Water is also\n\nrequired for a proper antidote, but this will be addressed later on. Many of the uses of the element of Earth are quite obvious, but a always, if one wishes to master the field of Alchemy they must learn creativity is their greatest friend, and using\n\nelements in odd and interesting ways can be useful, and quite detrimental, in numerous ways. A final note on the element of Earth is how it is associated with the fable of Transmutation. Now, as a scholarly and skeptic individual, I have doubts upon\n\ntransmutation, its existence, and possibility. This will be described more in depth, but know that the element of Earth is the greatly represented by metals, for metals are merely ores in their purest forms.\n\nWater\n===\nThe Base, the Purifier, the Healer.\n\nWater is the element that seems to get the most amount of use due to how it is a base for many a potion and it is true, for if someone wants a liquid spell they want to use the Element of Water. Often known as the “base” element, it truly lives up to its\n\nname for observations, and personal experience, lead all to believe it is necessary for life. This base element, is often associated with flowing, liquids, balance, peace, calm, order, life, and rebirth. One requires water for any potion that one plans on\n\ndrinking, and any oil that one plans on applying for to have a liquid, one must use the Element of Water. Now that and understanding on how Water is a base, we must go into how it is used beyond simply supporting the other elements.\n\nWater’s uses are varied, for it can be used both in antidote as well as poisons, showing that it is quite the versatile element. One may also use it to create a potion of calmness, or perhaps a potion of healing due to its association with life. But one\n\ncan also use this beautiful element in the creation of an oil to coat a weapon, or to coat and strengthen armor. This is not the same as enchanting armor or weaponry, mind you, for doing that is often permanent and requires magic. Through oils, one may\n\nreap similar benefits of enchantment but instead it is only temporary and anyone with basic sense can apply them. The purest base, and often representation of water, is Aqua Vitae or “Water of Life,” which is best made by distilling wine or other liquors.\n\nAir\n===\nThe Nimble, the Traveller, the All-Seeing.\n\nOur final element, the element of Air, is not the least by any stretch of imagination but is often the hardest one to put into representation and use in a potion. Now this element is often associated with agility, quickness, travel, feet, perception, and\n\neven flight, it can be quite difficult to embody the element of Air into a physical item due to our basic fault of lacking understanding of what we cannot see. But with reason and the opening of one’s eyes, the ways in which to represent Air are quite\n\nobvious in our everyday world.\nItems that can be used to represent air, and subsequently put through your tools to produce a potion, are rather easy to find during your average day but of course, as with all the elements, purer ingredients are often quite\n\nrarer. One representation that you may have already thought of is a mere feather, which is often the most common representation of Air. This, of course, is not the purest representation of the element but it will often due for your more basic potions\n\ncentered around this element. Another, more outlandish representation, are rabbits’ feet. Rabbits are often seen as quick and agile critters, and as Air takes on the aspect of agility and quickness, it becomes quite obvious how the feet of a rabbit and\n\nAir intermingle. With that said, it becomes rather easy to find out representations of Air through simply looking and observing the world around you. But do not fret if you are still hard-pressed to find these symbols, the next chapter will explain\n\nherbs that will best aid you in the process of finding symbols for all the elements.\n\nThe Tales of the Traveling Alchemist, Vol I-2\n\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven\n*Strange Numbers*\n((13/6/13))", ""),
        StaticBook("AlchemyGuide 1-1", "§bDaniel", "\\_+*+_/\n   Alchemy Guide\n\n\n\nVolume 1 – The Basics\nPart 1/2\n\n\n\n\n-By Daniel Nodal\n\nPrelude\nFor many years I have been an alchemist, not one of great name, not one of greater skill, I am one who focused on the everyday works of what alchemy may do. Even before reaching the rank of Mentor alchemist in the former Alchemy Guild in the\n\nlands of the Fringe, I already was teaching a student. Before my teacher disappeared for a few years, he promoted me to that rank, allowing me to fully teach anyone I wished, and make them alchemists with the time came to it. I have had many students\n\nthroughout the years, even being as young as I am, and believe I am capable of doing so. This book series is a combination of all I have learnt in alchemy, containing information from old alchemists, such as Tippen and the Traveling Alchemist, to the\n\nnewest masters. Alchemy is performed by all races, in the most diverse areas of expertise and the most diverse of people. What one can do with alchemy is only stopped by the reach of his imagination.\n\nAlchemy\nThe first question I ask all my students is for me to gather the reasons why they wish to study under me. What is alchemy? To my disbelief, most of those that come to learn from me, do not know that answer, or have clear misconceptions about\n\nthe topic. The answer I most receive is a simple “It’s the making of potions.” Many men and women want to simply to stand in front of a brewing stand or a cauldron and throw reagents inside it to then receive a potion that would grant them immortality!\n\nAlchemy is, of course, not potion making, which results in many students surprised, and many give up because I do not even speak of potion making until just before they become novice alchemists. Alchemy is much more than that, far more than potion making,\n\nas potions are only a way in which to receive the results of alchemy. The best way to describe alchemy is as such, the study and practice of the natural principle of Equivalent Exchange.\n\nEquivalent Exchange\nI have been asked a few times, as have many alchemists before me, if Alchemy is magical in it’s essence. While many of the desires and goals of alchemy might appear supernatural, the process itself, and the way of achieving it, are\n\nfar from it. The principle of Equivalent exchange is at the core, the law that states that to get anything, something of equal value must be offered in return. The value I refer to here is not a monetary one, as some might be compelled to think. If so,\n\nall alchemists would be throwing diamonds into a pot over and over again to receive what they would want. That does not happen of course, because the value used in equivalent exchange is associated with the properties, characteristics and representations\n\nof an ingredient or reagent. The alchemist’s job is not to mix up the ingredients like a soup, but knowing which ingredients to use, how to prepare them, how much to use, when to add them, and keeping everything safe. This is what sorts the Alchemists\n\nfrom the chefs. The recipes that alchemists have are not ones simply like cooking, which state that you need 3 eggs, 2 cups of sugar, a bucket of milk, and then mix everything up, place in a hot oven for a known amount of time. Alchemy recipes do not\n\nspeak of exact quantities or times, for there are many ways to have the same value. The recipe does instead tell the reader what representations he needs, normally in terms of what are called Symbols.\n\nSymbols\nDuring my times as a student, I was very skeptical of most of the theory of alchemy. One of the things I could not understand was the need for Symbols. If the representation and properties of a reagent are what is used, the elements\n\nthey fit in are not necessary to be stated. After a few years of becoming an alchemist I finally realized the purpose, it is because not only do ingredients have perfect representations, but the sibling representations are as well necessary to\n\ncreate proper results. Perfect representations would never be able to be properly used and leveled, and no reagent has only one representation. For such, attaching a symbol to an ingredient fits far better than attaching only the representations.\n\nBy choosing the representation or symbol you can decide the properties of what you create. One could use the representation of life for healing potions, but depending if it’s an earth symbol or a water symbol, it will have other smaller scale\n\nrepresentations that will distinguish them. A water symbol will provide a more smooth and fluent healing rate, making the wound heal nicely, and the earth symbol will give more strength and resilience to the healing process, perhaps making it hard to get\n\nthe wound reopen. Everything fits into at least one of the four symbols or elements of alchemy, Fire, Water, Earth and Air.\n\nThe way you look at what a symbol represents is looking at the element in question and assessing what properties it has\n\nand what it is associated with. You do not look exactly at what the element is, assessing a symbol to a reagent is more about symbolism than pure correct connection. It’s to aid the alchemist design and investigate the ways of alchemy in a more practical\n\nway, without having to reach so fully into the details. Each ingredient or reagent may also be of different strengths depending on the properties and rarity it holds. Those which are rarer have a greater value than those which are common, and as such\n\nthey are have as well more strength of use in alchemy. An ingredient may be a Weak, Moderate or Strong symbol, those of greater strength required quite more skilled hands, but the payoff might be quite better.\n\nFire\nStarting with the more easily explainable one, fire is the symbol very used by alchemists that focus themselves on improving skills of fighting and giving new arsenal to use in wars. While I don’t find myself using many of fire symbols,\n\nthey are an absolute in the making of many of the truly fantastic deeds of alchemy. Most of the representations of fire are quite easy to grasp, because they fit perfectly onto what fire truly is. It may represent heat, burning, consumption, destruction,\n\nwarmth, power and chaos. Most of these have as such a great use for damaging others or to give power to oneself or others. Alchemist fire for example, is a great demonstration of what could be done with the use of fire symbols, fire in a bottle.\n\nDevastating and powerful, it is most likely the most dangerous conception that an alchemist can create. The representation of chaos is one more confusing, one that may bring unknown results as controlling chaos is quite hard job to have. Fire\n\ndoes have another way you may look at it, as many poets might say, “the flames of passion ran deep in his heart, but he acted as in a burning rage.” Fire may also represent emotions, both those of anger, regret, rage, confusion, and those of love,\n\npassion, confidence. These representation are especially useful in potions to improve oneself, and in poisons to bring damage emotionally to others. The mythical love potion though, is one that is by experience impossible to have in full effect, as love\n\nis in it’s own way subjective, and varies from person to person. The closest thing that may be created is one that gives well being and peace of mind, or ecstasy. Most of those are considered drugs.\n\nAs for the properties one may find generally in fire symbols, the idea of heat is normally present in them. A flower or plant that is warm to the touch could be a good indicator of a fire symbol. The effects it might bring to a person might be associated\n\nthat way as well, such as becoming feverish, or feeling a burning itchy sensation on touch. And of course the flavour of many of them, which I don’t really advise to be a way to figure out what kind of symbol it is, is most of the times spicy and strong,\n\nsometimes sweet as it goes well with raising emotions.\n\nWater\nSometimes even said to be the base ingredients, a symbol of water is essential to all potions and oils that alchemy can create. This is of course due to it’s liquid and coherent state, or it would not be a potion or oil. The base is the main\n\ncomponent of a potion, where all other reagents are added and mixed in. It is important to refer that it is considered a base only to the creation of potions and oils, but in the other more exotic fields of alchemy, they are not used as such. Normal\n\nwater is the most simple of bases to use, not very useful for any powerful potion, as it’s not all that pure, it’s used mainly in minor potions. Distilling it would purify it and make it far more useful in lesser potions. The purest form for a base you\n\ncan find is aqua vitae. Aqua vitae can also be called Alcohol, or distilled alcohol, it is simply the result of distilling brews such as ale, wine or any other intoxicating beverage. With it, the most powerful of potions can be created. The other base\n\none could use is lard, which is used only in the creation of oils.\n\nNow onto the representations that define a symbol of water. Like when you imagine a stream of water, you imagine calmness, fluidity, peace, order and balance of nature.\n\nContinuing with the balance of nature, you have to understand that water is one of the fundamentals of life, and as such, water represents many parts of it. Life, regrowth, rebirth are the most important of representations, making water symbols sometimes\n\nthe most important in healing potions, and antidotes. Tasting a water symbol normally brings the other representations that water might have, freshness and a minty flavour are normally what you get from safe to eat water symbols, even going so far as\n\ndescending into cold and ice, another two representations of water. Do not think that just because water symbols may represents life and are used in a lot of healing potions that they’re safe to eat, even water symbols might be essential to the creation\n\nof poisons, as a stream may be a thought of life and balance, but a strong river might be the location of one’s death.\n\nContinues in part 2\n\nWritten by Daniel Nodal\nThe First Seed, 1473", ""),
        StaticBook("AlchemyGuide 2-3", "§bDaniel", "\\_+*+_/\n   Alchemy Guide\n\n\n\nVolume 2 – Reagents\nPart 3/4\n\n\n\n\n-By Daniel Nodal\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    Water Symbols\n\n           Weak\n\nName: Blissfoil\nRepresentation: Calmness\nDescription: Blissfoil is a plant which has more applications in healing medicine than in alchemical healing potions. While it has been found to have weak potency in\n\nalchemy, it’s effects are quite used in healing. I do not believe many good doctors exist which have not heard of the use of this plant, for it is one of the best numbing agents out there. The greatest problem with it, is the fact that overuse of it\n\ncauses permanent numbing to an area, and with the power it holds, it happening by someone who does not know of it’s effects is quite possible. The plant itself looks like a combination of many small blue flowers with a thick and flimsy stalk.\n\nIt’s harvesting is as easy as collecting a flower, and it is born and grows under birch trees near grassy areas.\nLocation: Under birch trees in grassy areas.\n\n         Moderate\n\nName: Serpent's Stalk\nRepresentations: Life, Rebirth, Calmness, Fluity\nDescription: This plant is one of the easiest to find in the forest of the realm. If one knows where there are small ponds and perhaps streams of\n\nwater, they would find the Serpent's Stalks. Long Emerald hued stalks, with stripes and ridges of a lighter green and brown along its length, almost like a serpent. Sometimes simple mistaken by normal sugar canes by those unknown of this plant, and to\n\ncollect it is the same as well, cutting it at the bottom. Another attribute it holds similar to a serpent’s it’s the poisonous nature it has. Specific insects that come in contact with it are normally found dead all around them, yet from all it’s use, no\n\nother creatures seem to die from it. It is actually used many times as a healing agent, specifically to heal and treat burn wounds. A normal oinkment from simply crushing the plant and mixing it with some water may smooth the pain and keep the wound\n\nsafe. It is as such a good water symbol that can be used for antidotes, oils, balms, healing potions and potions of calmness, as well as fire extinguishing potions. Those are good to have when preparing alchemist fire.\n\nLocation: Grows near small bodies of water in forests.\n\nName: Mandragora\nRepresentation: Rejuvenation, Life, Rebirth, Regrowth\nDescription: This is one of the reagents most alchemists use when creating more powerful healing potions. It’s meat rejuvenating traits makes it so that it’s very well\n\nsought by many alchemists. No good ingredient comes without its dangers thought, and Mandragora is one that can bring a collector to a very humiliating death. It is observed growing into the shallow waters of swamp lands, with thick green leaves flowing\n\nout of the water. Such leaves are quite delicious as garnish for soups and to make a quite rejuvenating tea. It is highly nutritious as well, filled with nutrients. It is not the leaves that one uses as a reagent thought, but the “meat” of the plant.\n\nThe part of the plant normally underwater is shaped like a parsnip. It is this “meat” that needs to be collected and prepared to use by the alchemists. Collecting it it’s not as easy as it sounds. This plant creates highly narcotic and paralyzing spores\n\nwhich are released quite easily when the plant is moved. Inhaling them brings the humiliating death of drowning in a foot high water level, as you simply fall on it and are unable to move any further. As such, one has to known when the spores are fully\n\nreleased, and only then retrieve them. Normally simply a group of at least two set out to find them, and one pulls the other from death if the spores are still with the plant.\nLocation: Low waters in wetlands.\n\nName: Zawabate\nRepresentations: Life, Calmness, Rebirth\nDescription: Zawabate is the only reagent I know of water representation in the desert lands. Of course it does not grow in the middle of the sand, it grows in the rare oasis one might\n\nfind. There is it protected from the strong heat and low humidity, in the fertile grounds around the waters. It is identified by it’s prominent colors of green and yellow, with short curvy petals in a long stalk. For those that live near them, especially\n\nthe orcish shamans, it is a plant which brings a higher connection with the spirits and out of body experiences. To say more simply, Zawabate is a hallucinogen, creating great comfort and sensations, which are further enhanced if made into a stong tea.\n\nThese flowers are certainly an amazing addition to potions designed to numb pain or heal because of their home in such a lush landscape among a wasteland of aridity. The amount to use however, should be greatly controlled, due to its hallucinogenic\n\nproperties. Too much, and the healing potion might cause delusions and mental problems, which means it can also be used for to create those same poison effects if wanted.\nLocation: In the fertile lands of an oasis\n\nName: Frost Vine\nRepresentation: Life, Calmness, Ice\nDescription: Frost vine is a blue vine which grows in very cold climates. It seems to have a need for shade, the most common of places being around trees, leaving long blue spirals in their\n\ntrunks and falling from their branches. The plant is a very good numbing agent, one which can easily be made into a sticky clear substance which numbs pain and loosens stiff muscles. Too much of it and it may affect the limbs, making them completely numb\n\nfor some time. One must always be sure to wash the area after applying the substance. It is by some considered a good alternative to a stronger but also more dangerous rubbing agent, Blissfoil, and with some other good qualities in the mix.\n\nThe vine is quite cold to the touch, which means it may also be further used to better the effect of numbing and treating wounds and pains. With these traits, it is no wonder that this plant represents life, calmness and cold.\n\nLocation: Shaded areas in cold climates.\n\n          Strong\n\nName: Athin\nSymbol: Freezing, Ice, Life, Balance\nDescription: Athin is quite a potent Water Symbol that one finds in ice lakes and rivers of fresh water, which are normally frozen. It is a blue algae, sometimes quite hard\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nto find if the ice is too thick or the snow stops vision to the unfrozen water under the ice. One particular difficult to harvest plant, not due to the how you take it, but because of the danger associated with it. It is taught to all alchemists that it\n\ntakes two people to harvest Athin. This is because it grows under ice, on very cold water. The ice breaking and someone getting stuck is a high probability, but either way you will have to get into the water to collect it. Sometimes people get stuck\n\nunder the ice, not knowing where the hole they did was, sometimes they do not take precautions with heating before entering the water and freeze in the cold wind when out. Such dangers do come with reward, as Athin is one of the materials there are that\n\nare fireproof. It has a great representation of ice and freezing, and an alchemist that carries around alchemist fire, or simply makes it, always have some athin around with them as well.\n\nLocation: Under the ice of frozen lakes and rivers.\n\nContinues in part 4\n\nWritten by Daniel Nodal\nThe Grand Harvest, 1480", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy", "§bLemik Rextiureus", "Dear Roopak,\n\nI wish to place a very specific order. I am going to create an area where you or your company shall be gavering Alchemecila ingredients. We shall have an are where you break the grass and till the land continously. This is the fastest-\n\n-way to find alchemical ingredients as many are buried jus underground. \n\n-Lemik Rextiureus", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy:Botany 2", "§bSarrion", "Alchemy: Botany II\n-------------------This glossery is based off a tale written by an unknown alchemist of the alchemists guild.\n\nRe-Written by:\nSarrion di' Pascali\n\n=[Fire Symbols Continued]=\n      Moderate\nName: Flametongue’s roots\n\nRepresentation: Heat, Anger, Destruction\n\nDescription: Flametongue’s roots is a difficult reagent to collect.\n\n It’s location of growth already makes the task hard, having to find it extremely sunny locations in forests, either in burnt areas of it, or on hillsides. If you’re not lucky to find a spot which has burnt down, and if you don’t burnt the forest down\n\nto gather this reagent, then most likely you will have to climb a cliff or hold yourself in one’s edge to gather it. The second great problem with it, is that it’s sharp brambles release spores which with contact with the skin\n\n cause hypersensitivity to pain. Gloves have to be used when harvesting, and I would suggest a face mask as well. If you know where to look, it is not so hard to notice the reagent, as it has an almost rose like appearance. The roots\n\nare the part of the plant to use, being bright red colored, which when used on wounds causes coagulation. So to harvest it, one has to sometimes dig into the side of a hillside. Use it only when in a life or death situation,\n\nas the pain you will fill when using it, will most likely be far worse than waiting for the wound to heal naturally. Such strong traits bring this plant representations of Heat and Anger.\n\nLocation: Forest’s hillsides and burnt forests.\n-------------------Name: Dwarf’s Pumpkin\n\nRepresentation: Rage, Envy, Bitterness, Power, Strength\n\nDescription: Although it is called Dwarf’s Pumpkin, this plant is an odious\n\norange-leafed shrub, and one of the most prominent feature of Swamp botany. Quite potent and atrocious smelling, it is easy to find at a distance, when it exists. It has it’s name from the it’s orange color, and the smell quite\n\nfits with the description of dwarves, or perhaps because of the brew which is made by them with this herb. They grow around the muddy waters and while they are not dangerous to gather, you may get your foot stuck\n\nand perhaps even fall into the disgusting mud, and unless some boots are used, your shoes will become horrible smelling. The whole plant may be used for the brew made by the dwarves,\n\nyet only the brightest of orange leaves need to be collected for the alchemic use.\n\nLocation: Swamplands next to muddy waters.\n-------------------\n\nName: Blood Lotus\n\nRepresentation: Hope, Courage\n\nDescription: This vine of the deserts is one of my personal favorites to use outside of alchemy reactions. Perhaps a weird trait of mine considering\n\n what it’s properties are. The plant is of a rusty color, growing on the fresher entrances of caves in the desert. They fall from the ceiling and become like a red curtain that separates the cave from the hot exterior. These vines are easily harvested,\n\nand it’s main use outside of alchemy is mostly in culinary and brewing arts. A diet with consumption of Blood Lotus is one quite nutritious. The plant itself has a peculiar taste, one of iron, making the broths and stews created to appear to\n\nhave been done with blood of an animal. Blood Lotus can also be fermented to create strong alcohols of red color. It’s representation as a fire symbol comes down to the emotions spectral. It is a plant that grows in a hot climate\n\nand finds the perfect ideal place of freshness and light. As such, it represents hope, but as well courage.\n\nLocation: Entrance of caves in the hot desert lands.\n-------------------\n\nName: Elrow Berries\n\nRepresentations: Rage, anger, burning\n\nDescription: It isn’t common to find a fire symbol in cold climates, Elrow berries are of the few that exist. Elrow berries\n\nwas named after an alchemist, probably in mockery, as it is also known to some as the Hellfire fruit. It grows in bushes and has a great red color to it, easy to spot sometimes in the white wastelands of the arctics.\n\nThe properties which make it a fire symbol are achieved when one is unfortunate enough to eat it. Unless for a single child and her mother with a great taste for spicy food, I have not met a single person which did not feel like their throat was on fire\n\nafter tasting it. It starts as a spicy feeling in the mouth that spreads through the throat, where a soreness starts coming with it. From there, the whole body starts to heat up, and even in the most cold days, the whole body\n\nstarts to sweat, and the body starts aching. It lasts for a few minutes, many grabbing hands of snow and placing them in their mouth, others stripping almost down to their loincloths, and a few even faint. I don’t recommend eating\n\nElrow berries unless perhaps you are dying of cold frost, or teaching an alchemist class and want a laugh. It’s normal uses are in poisons, especially those that work with emotions, and it represents the more\n\ndestructive side of Fire. It has been added to brews on some occasions as well, perhaps to heat up the body in cold winters.\nLocation: Bushes in cold climates.\n-------------------\n\n           Strong\nName: Crimson Vase\n\nRepresentation: Heat, Warmth, Love, Passion, Compassion\n\nDescription: In my opinion, this flower is the most beautiful that can be found in the forests. As it names suggests\n\nit has a strong red color, crimson. This is the color that the large petals of the flower have, and the form of which they take is of an elegant vase. It’s beauty is also enhanced by it’s rarity, as finding one in a forest is an extreme delight.\n\n It appears at the base of young trees, growing almost clinging to them. When the sun is high up, the plant starts almost glowing, as a beam of light passes through the small leaves and shines on it. Such combination of sunlight and protection\n\ncan not be found on older trees. The plant splendor does not end here, as my favorite trait is that the flower is warm to the touch, lovingly so. There are not many fire symbols which have representations\n\nof the ‘good’ side of fire, yet this one seems to have most of them. Warmth, Love, Passion, Compassion, this plant can be used for most emotion changing concoctions. Of course it’s\n\nvery important to fire based potions as well, such as Alchemist’s fire, but with it poisons of envy and rage, and potions which bring focus, courage, and warmth can as well.\n\nLocation: At the base of young trees in forests.\n-------------------\n\nName: Drake’s Tail\n\nRepresentations: Heat, Destruction, Chaos\n\nDescription: Of the herbs and plants of which I speak of in this volume, Drake’s Tail is the strongest and probably the rarest symbol of Fire.\n\nA rare sight in the deserts, almost a legend sometimes, this plant is probably the best you may find without having to slay a beast of mythical stories. It grows in the deserts, but even in them the conditions needed for it to grow\n\nare quite scarce and hard to find. This plant’s growth is very dependant on the moisture and temperature in the air, which means in the hot desert wastelands you will not find one growing. Only near Oasis, yet not too close does it grow.\n\nIt needs the moisture that exists in the air because of the Oasis, and the hotness of the desert, so it becomes an area which one might find Drake’s Tail. It seeks shade as well, embarrassing it’s thin and long vines filled with thorns around a rock.\n\nWith a red dusky color it’s easy to spot, when there is one around. To harvest it one has to remove the whole plant, as both the thorns and the roots are the necessary bits that are used by the alchemists in their concoctions. The harvesting is\n\nnot only hard, but also dangerous. If one is struck by one of the thorns, he will gain a strong fever and have a high chance of passing out in the middle of a hot desert, to be food to the coyote’s. It’s characteristics\n\nmake Drake’s Tail one of the best in the fire eccentric representations.\n\nLocation: In deserts close to oasis.\n-------------------\n\n=[Water Symbols]=\n->Weak\nName: Blissfoil\n\nRepresentation: Calmness\n\nDescription: Blissfoil is a plant which has more applications in healing medicine than in alchemical\n\nhealing potions. While it has been found to have a weak potency in alchemy, it’s effects are quite used in healing. I do not believe many good doctors exist which have not heard of the use of this plant, for it is one of the best numbing agents out there.\n\n The greatest problem with it, is the fact that overuse of it causes permanent numbing to an area, and with the power it holds, it happening by someone who does not know of it’s effects is quite possible. The plant itself looks\n\nlike a combination of many small blue flowers with a thick and flimsy stalk. It’s harvesting is as easy as collecting a flower, and it is born and grows under birch trees near grassy areas.\n\nLocation: Under birch trees in grassy areas.\n-------------------          Moderate\nName: Serpent's Stalk\n\nRepresentations: Life, Rebirth, Calmness, Fluity\n\nDescription: This plant is one of the easiest to find in the forest of the realm.\n\nIf one knows where there are small ponds and perhaps streams of water, they would find the Serpent's Stalks. Long Emerald hued stalks, with stripes and ridges of a lighter green and brown along its length, almost like a serpent.\n\nSometimes simple mistaken by normal sugar canes by those unknown of this plant, and to collect it is the same as well, cutting it at the bottom. Another attribute it holds similar to a serpent’s it’s the poisonous nature it has. Specific insects\n\n that come in contact with it are normally found dead all around them, yet from all it’s use, no other creatures seem to die from it. It is actually used many times as a healing agent, specifically to heal and treat burn wounds. A normal\n\noinkment from simply crushing the plant and mixing it with some water may smooth the pain and keep the wound safe. It is as such a good water symbol that can be used for antidotes, oils, balms, healing potions and potions of calmness,\n\nas well as fire extinguishing potions. Those are good to have when preparing alchemist fire.\nLocation: Grows near small bodies of water in forests.\n===================To be Continued...\nSeek:\nAlchemy: Botany III", ""),
        StaticBook("Elem. Alchemy", "§bRosso", "The Tales of a Travelling Alchemist\n------------\nElements of Alchemy\n\nVolume I-2 of V\n\nThe Elements:\n--------\nFire\n--\nEarth\n--\nWater\n--\nAir\n\nSkeptics often approach me with a wholly ignorant conundrum, and that is “how can you trace every spell and element back to these four basic elements.” But what they do not realize is that an understanding of what is meant by these four elements is\n\nrequired before that question can be answered and in fact, if that understanding is acquired then this question will never be asked.\n\nThese four basic, rudimentary elements, Fire, Earth, Water, and Air, are what every potion, oil, elixir, and\n\nconcept, in Alchemy of course, is based upon. An ignorant outlook on this statement is that what is meant is the physical, literal meaning behind these elements but one must look into the symbolism, they must look deeper than the surface. I shall go into\n\neach of these elements in detail, so that you, and budding alchemist or simply a well-read individual, may understand how Air may both aid someone’s speed, as well as their perception of their surroundings.\n\nFire\n===\nThe Destroyer, the Unmaker, the Cleanser.\n\nWe will begin with one of the most rudimentary and used element, Fire. Fire can represent many a feeling, action, and state and because of this, it is often one of the most useful elements of the Alchemist’s arsenal. Fire can represent rage, anger,\n\nhatred, as well as bravery, courage, and gusto. Through this a brew can be crafted to make someone unquestionably and savagely furious, but it could also craft a brew to strengthen one’s will, and allow them to do what other men would shrink away in fear\n\nat. These feelings, these emotions can be invoked by the element of Fire within a potion, but feelings are not the only thing that fire can produce, actions are another.\nActions are often the most recognized aspect of fire, and by this I mean the action\n\nof burning, consuming, and destroying. Alchemist’s Fire, a handy inferno in a bottle, is an example of this aspect of Fire, for within it is the action of destruction. This action can be further expanded upon with burning oil placed upon a weapon, but if\n\none were to use this example they would also have to include the element of pain, another feeling that fire an invoke. State is aspect that Fire can take one within an alchemist’s potion and this is often represented as Chaos. It is true that Chaos is\n\noften something alchemist’s fear, for it is something athat often ruins a potion, but it is yet another aspect of Fire that must be recognized. Chaos, being the state that it is, may seem like it has little place in a potion but brilliant minds and\n\npowerful reason can bring it to use just the same as the first farmer realized a stone and a stick, two completely different objects, could help them till their fields by putting them together. As this is not a book on the philosophy of alchemy, or\n\nsolely on the element of fire, I will not go into depth on how Chaos can be used. The teacher must leave something for the student to ponder.\n\nEarth\n===\nThe Enduring, the Lifegiver, the Fortifying\n\nWith the conclusion of Fire, the other elements will not have as long as description for most of the fundamentals of the elements are covered in how they can represent different things, I will simply go over what they may represent, starting with Earth.\n\nEarth is a truly powerful element for it may represent metals, strength, the notion of being stalwart, solidity, firmness, flora, animals, and countless other, quite obvious, subjects. Earth is often used for potions that increase one’s endurance, or\n\nstrength, and it is a must in potions that toughen one’s skin. In fact, a strong series of representations of Earth are required to perfect a barksin, or stoneskin potion. Protecting oneself is not the only use for the element of Earth, for it is also\n\nused in antidote potions, disease-curing potions, and, of course, fertilizers. Antidotes use this element for it can represent animals, and among those elements are snakes and spiders, often associated with poison. Of course a symbol of Water is also\n\nrequired for a proper antidote, but this will be addressed later on. Many of the uses of the element of Earth are quite obvious, but a always, if one wishes to master the field of Alchemy they must learn creativity is their greatest friend, and using\n\nelements in odd and interesting ways can be useful, and quite detrimental, in numerous ways. A final note on the element of Earth is how it is associated with the fable of Transmutation. Now, as a scholarly and skeptic individual, I have doubts upon\n\ntransmutation, its existence, and possibility. This will be described more in depth, but know that the element of Earth is the greatly represented by metals, for metals are merely ores in their purest forms.\n\nWater\n===\nThe Base, the Purifier, the Healer.\n\nWater is the element that seems to get the most amount of use due to how it is a base for many a potion and it is true, for if someone wants a liquid spell they want to use the Element of Water. Often known as the “base” element, it truly lives up to its\n\nname for observations, and personal experience, lead all to believe it is necessary for life. This base element, is often associated with flowing, liquids, balance, peace, calm, order, life, and rebirth. One requires water for any potion that one plans on\n\ndrinking, and any oil that one plans on applying for to have a liquid, one must use the Element of Water. Now that and understanding on how Water is a base, we must go into how it is used beyond simply supporting the other elements.\n\nWater’s uses are varied, for it can be used both in antidote as well as poisons, showing that it is quite the versatile element. One may also use it to create a potion of calmness, or perhaps a potion of healing due to its association with life. But one\n\ncan also use this beautiful element in the creation of an oil to coat a weapon, or to coat and strengthen armor. This is not the same as enchanting armor or weaponry, mind you, for doing that is often permanent and requires magic. Through oils, one may\n\nreap similar benefits of enchantment but instead it is only temporary and anyone with basic sense can apply them. The purest base, and often representation of water, is Aqua Vitae or “Water of Life,” which is best made by distilling wine or other liquors.\n\nAir\n===\nThe Nimble, the Traveller, the All-Seeing.\n\nOur final element, the element of Air, is not the least by any stretch of imagination but is often the hardest one to put into representation and use in a potion. Now this element is often associated with agility, quickness, travel, feet, perception, and\n\neven flight, it can be quite difficult to embody the element of Air into a physical item due to our basic fault of lacking understanding of what we cannot see. But with reason and the opening of one’s eyes, the ways in which to represent Air are quite\n\nobvious in our everyday world.\nItems that can be used to represent air, and subsequently put through your tools to produce a potion, are rather easy to find during your average day but of course, as with all the elements, purer ingredients are often quite\n\nrarer. One representation that you may have already thought of is a mere feather, which is often the most common representation of Air. This, of course, is not the purest representation of the element but it will often due for your more basic potions\n\ncentered around this element. Another, more outlandish representation, are rabbits’ feet. Rabbits are often seen as quick and agile critters, and as Air takes on the aspect of agility and quickness, it becomes quite obvious how the feet of a rabbit and\n\nAir intermingle. With that said, it becomes rather easy to find out representations of Air through simply looking and observing the world around you. But do not fret if you are still hard-pressed to find these symbols, the next chapter will explain\n\nherbs that will best aid you in the process of finding symbols for all the elements.\n\nThe Tales of the Traveling Alchemist, Vol I-2\n\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy: Symbols", "§bSarrion", "Alchemy Basic's\nSub Volume I\nSymbols\n\nBy: An unknown alchemist of the alchemist guild.\n\nRe-written by:\nSarrion di' Pascali\n\nDuring my times as a student, I was very skeptical of most of the theory of alchemy. One of the things I could not understand was the need for Symbols. If the representation and properties of a reagent are what is used,\n\nthe elements they fit in are not necessary to be stated. After a few years of becoming an alchemist I finally realised the purpose, it is because not only do ingredients have perfect representations, but the sibling representations are as well\n\nnecessary to create proper results. Perfect representations would never be able to be properly used and leveled, and no reagent has only one representation. For such, attaching a symbol to an ingredient\n\nfits far better than attaching only the representations. By choosing the representation or symbol you can decide the properties of what you create. One could use the representation of life for healing potions, but depending if it’s an\n\nearth symbol or a water symbol, it will have other smaller scale representations that will distinguish them. A water symbol will provide a more smooth and fluent healing rate, making the wound heal nicely, and the earth symbol will\n\ngive more strength and resilience to the healing process, perhaps making it hard to get the wound reopen. Everything fits into at least one of the four symbols or elements of alchemy, Fire, Water, Earth and Air.\n\nThe way you look at what a symbol represents is looking at the element in question and assessing what properties it has and what it is associated with. You do not look exactly at what the element is, assessing a symbol to a reagent is\n\n more about symbolism than pure correct connection. It’s to aid the alchemist design and investigate the ways of alchemy in a more practical way, without having to reach so fully into the details. Each ingredient or reagent may\n\n also be of different strengths depending on the properties and rarity it holds. Those which are rarer have a greater value than those which are common, and as such they are have as well more strength of use in alchemy.\n\nAn ingredient may be a Weak, Moderate or Strong symbol, those of greater strength required quite more skilled hands, but the payoff might be quite better.\n\n=[Fire]=\n->Starting with the more easily explainable one, fire is the symbol very used by alchemists that focus themselves on improving skills of fighting and giving new arsenal to use in wars. While I don’t find myself using many fire symbols,\n\n they are an absolute in the making of many of the truly fantastic deeds of alchemy. Most of the representations of fire are quite easy to grasp, because they fit perfectly onto what fire truly is. It may represent heat, burning, consumption,\n\ndestruction, warmth, power and chaos. Most of these have as such a great use for damaging others or to give power to oneself or others. Alchemist fire for example, is a great demonstration of what could be done with the use of fire symbols,\n\nfire in a bottle. Devastating and powerful, it is most likely the most dangerous conception that an alchemist can create. The representation of chaos is one more confusing, one that may bring unknown results as controlling chaos is quite\n\nhard job to have. Fire does have another way you may look at it, as many poets might say, “the flames of passion ran deep in his heart, but he acted as in a burning rage.” Fire may also represent emotions, both those\n\nof anger, regret, rage, confusion, and those of love, passion, confidence. These representation are especially useful in potions to improve oneself, and in poisons to bring damage emotionally to others. The mythical love potion though, is one that is\n\nby experience impossible to have in full effect, as love is in it’s own way subjective, and varies from person to person. The closest thing that may be created is one that gives well being and peace of mind, or ecstasy.\n\nMost of those are considered drugs.\nAs for the properties one may find generally in fire symbols, the idea of heat is normally present in them. A flower or plant that is warm to the touch could be a good indicator of a fire symbol.\n\nThe effects it might bring to a person might be associated that way as well, such as becoming feverish, or feeling a burning itchy sensation on touch. And of course the flavour of many of them, which I don’t really advise\n\nto be a way to figure out what kind of symbol it is, is most of the times spicy and strong, sometimes sweet as it goes well with raising emotions.\n\n=[Water]=\n->Sometimes even said to be the base ingredients, a symbol of water is essential to all potions and oils that alchemy can create. This is of course due to it’s liquid and coherent state, or it would not be a potion or oil. The base is the main\n\ncomponent of a potion, where all other reagents are added and mixed in. It is important to refer that it is considered a base only to the creation of potions and oils, but in the other more exotic fields of alchemy, they are not used as such.\n\nNormal water is the most simple of bases to use, not very useful for any powerful potion, as it’s not all that pure, it’s used mainly in minor potions. Distilling it would purify it and make it far more useful in lesser potions.\n\nThe purest form for a base you can find, is aqua vitae. Aqua vitae can also be called Alcohol, or distilled alcohol, it is simply the result of distilling brews such as ale, wine or any other intoxicating beverages.\n\nWith it, the most powerful of potions can be created. The other base one could use is lard, which is used only in the creation of oils.\nNow onto the representations that define a symbol of water. Like when you imagine\n\na stream of water, you imagine calmness, fluidity, peace, order and balance of nature. Continuing with the balance of nature, you have to understand that water is one of the fundamentals of life, and as such, water represents many parts\n\nof it. Life, regrowth, rebirth are the most important of representations, making water symbols sometimes the most important in healing potions, and antidotes. Tasting a water symbol normally brings the other representations that water might have,\n\nfreshness and a minty flavour are normally what you get from safe to eat water symbols, even going so far sometimes descending into cold and ice, another two representations of water. Do not think that just because water symbols\n\nmay represents life and are used in a lot of healing potions that they’re safe to eat, even water symbols might be essential to the creation of poisons, as a stream may be a thought of life and balance,\n\nbut a strong river might be the location of one’s death.\n\n=[Earth]=\n->While the water symbol is considered the base for potion making, I consider earth symbols the bases for other more advanced sections of alchemy, such as Transmutation and the creation of Chimeras. Such is because while potions need\n\nto be fluid and in a liquid state, transmutation is performed mostly on objects, and the creation of chimeras requires great representations of life and connectivity if one is ever to be created. More of the advanced alchemy will be left for another book,\n\nbut I wanted to leave the idea that the selection of a base is not randomly selected but adapts itself to what the alchemist wishes to create. The earth symbols concentrate themselves more on solids and life.\n\nTo better understand the symbol, one must think of the two greatest visualisations one thinks about when the idea of earth comes to mind. Those two visualisations are the rock, and dirt. Such have the representations that will fit with all of the symbols\n\nof earth. A rock is resilient, hard to break and strong. It’s used a lot in buildings creating structure and protection, and all these characteristics are representations of the symbol. A rock may also be associated with minerals and metals, two\n\nrepresentations very much sought out in transmutation. Once one thinks of dirt on the other hand, he does not receive the same thoughts as when with a stone. Dirt is different, dirt is used mostly for the growth of plants, the propagation of life.\n\n This is the second part of the symbol of Earth, one that might not be the most easily noticeable. It represents life, regrowth, rebirth and connection. Plants grow in it, and animals life over it, feeding of the plants, and so fauna and flora are\n\nas well the last representations of earth, making them the most suitable for the creation of chimeras.\nI do not advise trying to eat any type of Earth symbol. While it may represent life as well, it leans more for survival of life, than saving of it.\n\nThis means that normally earth symbols are not suitable for eating, either having extremely bitter tastes, or being straight up poisonous.\n\n=[Air]=\n->The symbol of air is perhaps the hardest to explain in all of the symbols. When one tries to imagine air, he may think of the wind, or of what he breaths, perhaps of smoke. What could one bring from those? These are quite simply almost\n\n impossible to see, you can not see air, you can not see wind, you feel the wind and hear it’s sound, and you breath the air. One of the first representations to speak of would be of perception, without air you would not be able to see\n\nbut it comes from that fact that you do not see air, yet know it’s there, and percy the effects of it. The rest of the representations are around the idea of speed and quickness. Perhaps a thought we all have when we imagine speed,\n\nwe can think of the movements in the air, the fast animals traveling through the air and ground almost provoking a wind in itself. Not the best way to explain it, as those images are quite hard to have. Of the most easy representations to think about,\n\nyou may think of fight and travel, and for such you only need to think of a bird. The palate one gets on these symbols are normally quite bland. Having almost no taste, no aroma, almost like the normal air in the mountains.\n\nA last mention on air symbols, is always it’s representations of fragility and grace. The ingredients of air are ones that in most cases are very thin and weak, easily broken and destroyed maybe simply by the moving of the hand near them.\n\nGrace is a representation I adore to use in potions of healing, and oils of well being, because they bring the condition of the skin to a more natural and graceful way. Yet I consider them hardest symbols to use,\n\nas the harvesting of the ingredients, the handling and the storage has to be extremely precise.\n===================End.\n\nLook for:\nAlchemy Volumes I, II, III.\nRe-Written by:\nSarrion di' Pascali", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy: Potions", "§bSarrion", "Alchemy: Potions\n-------------------Glossery based off\na book written by an Unknown Alchemist.\n\nRe-Written by:\nSarrion di' Pascali\n\n=[Potion of Mending]=\nOften times the most used potions of an alchemist’s arsenal. Mending potions are those to aid the hurt and injured with their effects of repairing flesh and bone, as well as body and mind. Potions of Mending are\n\n normally administered both internally and externally. Pouring it on bandages to place over wounds is a normal way of using it, as well as drank as well. For illnesses it is just drank, and for minor cuts and bruises just\n\nplacing it on the wound is enough. Minor wounds require only minor potions, and no better potion should be used for these kinds of injuries. This is because greater potions, those designed for greater injuries are often\n\n time a way to escape death, or save from devastating injuries, and side effects result from extreme fast regeneration or reconstruction of tissue and bone. Paralysis of the area, scarring, chronic pain and small deformations\n\nmay appear from the greatest of potions, yet their effects can be as great as repairing a shattered bone, returning vision to a cut eye, closing wounds in seconds. Even though those things are possible, sometimes\n\neven faster than with magic, they do not only require the use of the potions, but as well medical expertise. A potion to fix up a shattered bone requires it to be administered directly into the bone, and that means the flesh must be cut to expose it,\n\nwhich to do safely is quite the task. Of course even though the patient will he healed quite fast, he would be in no condition to do much for a few hours, fast healing is tiring for the body.\n\nRecipe:\n \n \n- Minor:\nBase: Water\n1 Symbol of Water (Life)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Life)\n\n- Lesser\nBase: Distilled Water\n2 Symbols of Water (Life)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Life)\n1 Symbol of Air (Quickness)\n\n- Greater\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n2 Symbols of Water (Life)\n2 Symbols of Earth (Life)\n1 Symbol of Air (Speed)\n\n=[Potion of Regeneration]=\nA quite potent potion, it is part of the healing potions, but has a recipe on it’s own due to it’s complexity. It’s recipe is both dark, and it’s effects slow, but with amazing results.\n\nThe potion is able to remove scarring, and most of all, regrow missing limbs. Taking months to fully regrow the limb, they would then have to be retrained, and strengthened. It’s the most powerful healing potion an alchemist can\n\ndo, and to make it one must be quite adept at alchemy making, but as well face a choice which would leave most healers with a sore throat. The base used is not Aqua Vitae or Water, but the blood of the person that requires the potion.\n\nThis means the potion only might work on one person. Mandragora is used for it’s rejuvenation representation, and the other main ingredient is the liver of a mother of the same race. Not using the same race woman will result in horrible mutations,\n\nand weird growths, and not perfectly made potion will as well result in weird growths instead of the required limb. The mix of the ingredients are also quite reactive, which means it will take a skilful alchemists to make it carefully.\n\n It becomes a dark colored liquid when complete, and throughout the making, the heat must be correct and constant. The other ingredients are not always the same, as the recipe like other greater healing potions.\n\nRecipe:\n\nBase: Blood\nMandragora\nLiver of a Mother\n2 Earth Symbols (Life)\n1 Air Symbol (Grace)\n\n=[Potion of Stoneskin]\nA potion that almost seems like a legend come true. This potion is one great in an alchemist’s arsenal. It makes one skin as hard as stone, armor without armor, able to protect itself against blades and arrows quite efficiently.\n\nOf course like hitting stone, some chipping of the skin might happen, and that will appear as wounds once the effects of the potion wears out. There are two major side effects of this potion. One of them is the fact that drinking\n\nit is extremely hard. Some that have drank it consider the taste worse than poison, and I do believe, as they are alchemists who I think would try poisons on themselves. After the effects of the potion the result is also one like being sick.\n\nVomit, extreme nausea are very common effects. The other side effect is what happens if the body can not fully handle the potion. They become like a rock, unable to move, fully paralyzed yet indestructible as well.\n\n It is a rare effect, I would say happening one in five potions drank, but losing agility is a normal side effect of it. There is only one rank of this potion, anything\n\n less and it’s effects won’t be worth, anything more and the body can not take it.\n\nBase: Distilled water\n4 Symbols of Earth (Fortitude)\n2 Symbols of Water (Life)\n3 Symbols of Fire (Power)\n1 Symbol of Air (Speed)\n\n=[Potion of Strength]\nAnother highly sought after potion, this one is capable of enhancing the strength of someone, sometimes making an elf as strong as an Olog for short moments. Making such temporary changes to one’s body may bring quite\n\n the great amount of pain, yet it’s great way to achieve victory in a duel or fight. The major problem, like that of the potion of stoneskin, is that the body is not adapted to this amount of strength. It has been heard about\n\npeople who drink this potion falling in pain as the effects of the potion come, or after the effects end, due to crushed bones, or destroyed muscles. If used in the right dosages and not excessively though, the side effect\n\n is normally tired muscles. The minor rank of this potion is quite good for training, and it’s effects quite safe, considering it only gives one a small power boost, good for lifting heavy weights out of a cart.\n\nRecipe:\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n1 Symbol of Earth (Fortitude, Power, Resilience)\n- Lesser\nBase: Distilled Water\n2 Symbols of Earth (Fortitude, Power, Resilience)\n1 Symbol of Fire (Power)\n\n- Lesser\nBase: Distilled Water\n2 Symbols of Earth (Fortitude, Power, Resilience)\n1 Symbol of Fire (Power)\n===================\n\n=[Potion of Celerity]=\nAlso know as potion of agility and potion of speed, the potion of celerity is the one that raises the movement speed and reflexes of the body. Normally wanted to be used for combat, many find it better to use\n\nwhen following prey while hunting, or even by thieves to quickly escape when seen. The side effects happen as all do when the body is changed temporarily, the body is not made to move faster than it\n\ndoes, and forcing it to do it may bring extreme pain to the muscles after the effects are done.\n\nRecipe:\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n1 Symbol of Air (Speed)\n\n- Lesser\nBase: Distilled Water\n2 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n\n- Greater\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n3 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n1 Symbol of Fire (Power)\n\n=[Potion of Accuity]=\nAnother air based potion like the potion of Celerity, the potion of Accuity is an amazing potion to create and use. This potion provides the drinker with heightened senses, and may be prepared to all senses, or to specific\n\nones for more potency. Often times uses for hunting or for taking a job in hard tasks with require great vision, hearing or touch. In a competition of cooking for example, the judges might drink this potion beforehand for greater ability\n\n to judge the taste, and it is said that with it, someone might smell poison in their drink when there is no smell to sense. The result of enhancing the senses for a short while, is the worsening of them afterwards for a short while.\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n2 Symbol of Air (Perception)\n\n- Lesser\nBase: Distilled Water\n3 Symbols of Air (Perception)\n\n- Greater\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n4 Symbols of Air (Perception)\n\n=[Potion of Wizard’s Wisdom]=\nEver wanted to feel like the great scholars of old? Then this potion is the one to use. A potion able to enhance the mental capabilities of the drinker temporarily. Fantastic even in it’s purple color, it enhances one’s\n\n problem solving, reasoning, and general thinking ability. Of great use to alchemists as well, especially when formulating new potions and you find yourself stuck on a problem. This potion will aid you, not by giving you ideas as soon as you drink it\n\nbut by clearing your mind, making you think fluidly, and giving you a new perspective on the matter. It’s reagents are many, so to create them you would take quite the lengthy time, and the migraines it creates\n\nare long, yet if you enhancing it with purer ingredients will diminish some of the migraines. Another point about this potion could perhaps be it’s effects when dealing with memory loss, it might make the person\n\nhave an easier time remembering, mind invasion from mental magic, becoming fully in control of your own mind placing the mage outside of control, and while being interrogated or lying, you will appear far more truthful in your responses.\n\n- Minor:\nBase: Water\n2 Symbols of Water (Balance, Clarity)\n1 Symbol of Fire (Power)\n2 Symbols of Earth (Endurance)\n1 Symbol of Air (Perception)\n\n- Lesser:\nBase: Distilled Water\n3 Symbols of Water (Balance, Clarity)\n2 Symbols of Fire (Power)\n3 Symbols of Earth (Endurance)\n2 Symbols of Air (Perception)\n\n- Greater:\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n5 Symbols of Water (Balance, Clarity)\n3 Symbols of Fire (Power)\n3 Symbols of Earth (Endurance)\n3 Symbols of Air (Perception)\n\nPlease refer to Part II for the rest of the potions. Thank you.\n-Sarrion\n\n===================To be Continued...", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy: Botany", "§bSarrion", "Alchemy: Botany\n-------------------This glossery is a detailed explaination of the Botany with in alchemy, and was written by an unknown alchemist.\n\nRe-Written by:\nSarrion di' Pascali\n\n=[->Earth<-]=\n          Weak\nName: Goblin’s Ivy\nRepresentation: Resilience, Poison, Life\n\nDescription: One of the most easily found flora of the deserts, this plant is easily identified as a\n\nbrownish green vine with small leaves. It is found mainly clinging onto old buildings or ruins of the deserts, and on the dry cliff sides. It is quite a resilient plant, able to survive in extreme hostile conditions, and\n\nwith a very low or no amount of water close by. Removing it also becomes a problem, as it seems to regrow on the same spot after someone goes to the long labour of removing it all. It is these properties that brings the plant such\n\ngood uses, it’s representation of resilience makes a welcome addition to any stoneskin potion, even while being regarded as a Weak reagent. A few poisons may also be created by use of this reagent,\n\nmainly ones weaker, some to create nausea or ill disposition. Easy to find and prepare, it is normally used by those new to alchemy to learn the craft.\n\nLocation: Clinging on ruins, stones and cliff sides of dry regions.\n-------------------\n\n          Moderate\nName: Saffvil\n\nRepresentation: Resilience, Endurance, Death\n\nDescription: Possibly the most horribly smelling herb in all of swamplands, many alchemists make use\n\nof their nose when trying to find it. This green grass is green and grows on the corpses of animals, on dead wood and plants, and sometimes on the bottom of big trees. This plant is one that is extremely hard to remove from anything,\n\nas it spreads very quickly and strongly. Normally to remove it, you would have to remove the “host”, the plant or animal that it is growing on. If you do not, it will spread all throughout it. This means that the plant has a great representation\n\nof Resilience and Endurance, but this plant, unlike a few other Earth symbols, does not represent Life and Regrowth, but Death. This means this plant is mostly used in the creation of poisons,\n\nand one particular myth of an aging potion.\n\nLocation: On the bodies of dead plants and animals.\n-------------------\n\nName: Alabaster Leaf\n\nRepresentations: Immobility, Perseverance\n\nDescription: This plant is mostly characterized by it’s leaf’s alabaster color. Most often times found in the dryer regions of the forest growing around fallen trees.\n\nQuite small in size, harvesting it may prove a difficulty to the untrained hands. The best method would perhaps be taking the whole plant, with roots, and then remove the leaves as you may use them. The extract one gets by crushing the leaves is what\n\nan alchemist should use. It comes with it’s natural alabaster hue, which serves as paint to many. The extract is mildly poisonous, creating dizziness and nausea to those that consume it. It’s main uses are in antidotes, oils, and of course, poisons.\n\nLocation: Forest dry regions next to fallen trees.\n-------------------Name: Tippen’s Root\n\nRepresentation: Life, Regrowth, Resilience, Strength, Endurance\n\nDescription: Tippen’s roots is one of the most used\n\n reagents in the making of mending potions, it is even said, that it is required to be present in all healing potions, or the potion will be of weak quality. It was named after the botanist that had first discovered it around hills and\n\nin dense forests. It’s location of growing is often the same of that of Alabaster leaf, besides fallen trees, but as well next to carcases of animals and great bugs. It is quite a pleasant sight when compared with the death around it,\n\nas the plant of the herb blooms into purple flowers. What one has to use though, is the roots. They are of a yellow color, with spots accentuated a bit more darker. Many carry these roots around, even those with no knowledge of it’s use in alchemy.\n\nThis is because from the roots one can easily make a remedy which may stop bleeding near instantly. In it’s unprepared it’s strength isn’t as great, but it still may save countless lives. The problem with this root is a simple one, it’s smell.\n\nThe odor that the plant releases when the salve of the roots is spilled is enormous. While using it you may gag and even vomit from thinking that you are placing such foul-smelling balm onto an open wound. After such,\n\nthe smell will not escape you for a few hours. It’s traits in healing bring the herb representations of Life and Regrowth, but it also has great representations of Strength and Endurance.\n\nLocation: Forest, around dead trees and animals.\n-------------------Name: Desert Berry Bush\n\nRepresentation: Protection, Resilience\n\nDescription: The Desert Berry Bush's appearance is that of a withered rose bush,\n\nlong brown/grey stalks with big pointy thorns, yet without any leaves. It doesn't appear to grow higher than four feet, but most of the thorns are longer than an inch. It grows inside cactus forests, as in, where there  is great concentration\n\nof cactus. Never has one been alone in the desert’s sands without a cactus near it. The bush gives Berries of a strong red color every full moon. These don't last long, as animals of the desert appear to enjoy it's taste quite dearly.\n\nThe berries are extremely sweet, something very strange to find in the desert, so no wonder the animals enjoy it. The stalks are not like the berries, if an animal by any chance passed the long thorns to rip and eat the stalk, I believe\n\n would fall dead in less than a day. As you cut the stalk you notice that the insides are green, instead of the dead brown. From it it's juice flows a lot, yet of course it's poison to drink it. Eating the liquid or even touching it causes rashes and all\n\nsomeone wants to do afterwards is scratch the area, even if it’s the mouth. Too much of it and it kills of course, and it sound be painful. The thorns create the same effect, so without gloves, the hand starts itching and becomes red for about a day.\n\nIt doesn't seem deadly to descendents and probably kha's, but it does explain the small rodents found dead near them. A thorn is poisonous, and annoying, just doesn't kill big animals. The stalk seems to have far more uses in\n\nalchemy when compared with it’s berry, especially in oils of protection and potions of stoneskin. It represents protection and resilience, the thorns and it's poison nature being the reasons why. To prepare for it I have found it hard,\n\nas when cut, the juices drip quite fast. You can't remove the bush from the ground, as it's roots are too big, so they have to be cut in the spot. Feet long pieces of stalk are enough, yet one would have to almost tilt the plant\n\ndown so that the cut doesn't face down. Gloves are essential in gathering it because of the thorns, and after cutting, I advise placing it in a long bottle, with the cut end pointing up.\n\nWhen being prepared, the alchemist should be careful as to lose as little bit of the stalk as he removes the seemingly dead outer layer of the stalk, before using it in his potion.\n\nLocation: Desert Cactus Forests\n\n          Strong\nName: Starved Coyote’s Tick\nRepresentations: Protection, Resilience, Connection\n\nDescription: The Tick that I speak of is probably the only non flora which I speak of as an Alchemy\n\ningredient in this volume. Mostly it can be found on coyotes the first ones discovered on the bodies of thin starved coyotes, giving them the name. This does not mean it does not appear in other animals, like desert\n\nhares and rhinos. As long as it is an animal that grows in hot climates, you may be lucky enough to find one. If the animal has a lot of fur, it may be hard to find the tick, but due to it’s size, just passing a hand through the animal may\n\nmake one find the tick. It might get as big as a thumb, very fat of pure brown color. It would seem many times as a lump in the animal’s skin, and trying to remove it is mostly impossible. The Staved Coyote’s Tick, once grabbing onto some meat,\n\nwill never let go unless it wants to. Fire, Ice, or even swings of a warhammer only damage the skin around it, the tick itself resiting more. It’s collecting is done by cutting the flesh that the tick is grabbing, and only when all the meat\n\nis removed from the tick, does it try to find another place to grab to. This is a dangerous thing to do, as if an alchemists isn’t careful, even with gloves the tick may grab onto the skin, and never let go. When I say never,\n\n I truly mean it, no one can make the tick let go, most likely the finder would have to be removed, or at least part of it, that or one would learn to live with the tick, as it doesn’t seem toxic or poisonous to an animal. With characteristics like this,\n\n it is no wonder that it is a strong symbol of earth, representing Connection, Protection and Resilience.\n\nLocation: Bodies of animals of hot Climates\n\n     =[Fire Symbols]=\n->Weak\n\nName: Desert Bush Berry\n\nRepresentations: Empowerment, Overwhelming\n\nDescription: The Desert Berry Bush's\n\nappearance is that of a withered rose bush, long brown/grey stalks with big pointy thorns, yet without any leaves. It doesn't appear to grow higher than four feet, but most of the thorns are longer than an inch. It grows inside cactus\n\nforests, as in, where there is great concentration of cactus.Never has one been alone in the desert’s sands without a cactus near it. The Desert Bush Berries are the berries given by the Desert Berry Bush.\n\nThe bush gives Berries of a strong red color every full moon. These don't last long, as animals of the desert appear to enjoy it's taste quite dearly. The berries are extremely sweet, something very strange to find in the desert,\n\nso no wonder the animals enjoy it. The use of the berries are mostly in culinary, serving as treats, in jams and to bake. As for alchemy uses, it behaves as a fire symbol, as is custom to red ingredients. In this case not because of the color,\n\nbut because of the strong flavor it has, almost overwhelming. On the other hand, it doesn't appear all that useful nor seems to give great use in equivalent exchange. Great to be used by new alchemists in their training, as it quite safe.\n\nLocation: Desert Cactus forests in Full moon days.\n-------------------===================To be Continued...\nPlease look for:\nAlchemy: Botany II", ""),
        StaticBook("Elem. Alchemy", "§bKirrekith", "Elementaly Alechemy\n\nThe Tales of a Travelling Alechemist\n-------------------Notice, this book is a rushed copy of tomes in the library of the Ork capital, as made during the raid of 16th of the Grand Harvest, 1532. This is not a perfect copy.\n\nElements of Alchemy\nVolume I-II of V\n\nFire\n-------------------Earth\n-------------------Water\n-------------------Air\n\nMost people believe that you cannot trace a spell back to the IV key elements. However a grasp of these elements can show this, however without understanding will make this impossible to know.\n\nThese IV elements, Fire, Earth, Water and Air. These are what\n\nevery potion, oil, and elixir is based upon. People often see a potions physical state, and so not of the IV, however look to the potions symbolism and it will become clear. Look further than the asthetics to understand this, hopefully this will guide any\n\nalchemist or student in understanding how air can assist in someones ability to move and perceiving someones surroundings.\n\nFire\n---\n---\nThe Destroyer, the Unmake, the cleanser. \n\nStaring with one of\n\nthe key elements; Fire. Fire can be shown in feeling, movement, and in its form. Due to this it is key to an alchemist to have on their person. Fire represents many forms such as rage, anger, hatred, as wel as bravery, courage and speed. With this a\n\nsolution can take form to influence someone to be savegely furious, or even make someones will be strengthened, making them do as other men would buckle to fear over. This can in turn be a catalyst to bring out the element of fire inside a potion. But\n\nfeelings are not the only result the element fire can produce, actions as well.\n\nPotions are often the most known form of fire, this is meant in the form of burning, consuming and destroying. A key named potion would be alchemists fire, a brutal\n\nflame withstrained by a vial. Living destruction within the vials confines, a good metaphor for fire aspect. This result can be amplified by catalysts such as oil placed on a weapon. This potion invokes the feeling of pain, and so can be branded as a\n\nemotion fire causes. It is often viewed to be chaos within a potion due to aspects of fires illtempored nature. It is true the chaos of fire is something alchemists fear, as the very element can spoil potions. Chaos has little place in potions to the weak\n\nminded, but to the adept it is seen as powerful reason to be brought to use. This can be compared to iron and wood to make a woodsmans axe, two different materials to form a new and more efficient purpose. This is not a book on the philosiphy of alchemy,\n\nor solely made for the element of fire. I will not indulge you into how chaos can be mastered in alchemy. This is the place of the student to discover. \n\nEarth\n---\n---\nThe Enduring, the Lifegiver, the Fortifying\n\nThe following elements are not as key as the previous elements, and so will not be explained in long detail, however they may be mentioned later on. I will go over what they mean, beginning with Earth. Earth is truly a strong element, as it is shown in\n\nmetals, strength, solidity, firmness, flora, animals, with many other forms also being part of it. Solutions in its form can be used to amplify ones endurance, strength, and is a key catalyst to be used when toughening ones skin. A continuous series of\n\nelements or used to fortify and perfect barkskin or stoneskin solutions. Protection isn't the only use for the earth aspect, it can be used to fend of poisions, or disease curing alchemy, fertilizers. Antidotes represent animals, more directly snakes and\n\nspiders, as they often are in association to poison. A symbol for water aspect is also required within a antidote, but we'll discuss this further on in this book. Earth aspects have many obvious uses, though as with mastering any field, creativity is key\n\nto when using elements as it can allow for odd and interesting results. Quite detrimental in varioys ways. Earth aspect is also believed to be key in transmution, however I possess a skeptical view in this matter, and have doubts whether it is possible.\n\nThis will be described in more detail later, but understand that metals are often linked to earth aspect. \n\nWater\n---\n---\nThe Base, the Purifier, the Healer\n\nWater is the base to most potions, being a\n\nkey aspect to all of them. If a person needed a water based spell, they'll want to Element of Water. This is well known as the \"Base\" element, it does truly live up to the name. This element is shown to be in liquids, flowing, balance, peace, calm, order\n\nand rebirth. Potions require water in all forms to be usable for drinks or rubbing oils/liquids. Understanding that water can be a base is simple, however we must learn how to use it without it simply being a supporting agent. Water has a variety of uses\n\nIt can be used as an antidote or the counter poisons. Or it can be used to form potions of calm and healing. This element can also be used in item strengthening or maintaining of it. However this is not the same as enchanted or runecrafted armour, the\n\ndifference being that the water application is for the most part a non-permanent solution. One of the purest bases, and often a representation of water is Aqua Vitae, or Water of life. Made by distilling wines or liquors.\n\nAir\n---\n---\nThe Nimble, The Traveller, the All-Seeing\n\nThe final element to discuss is air, often the hardest to present and use in a potion. This element represents agility, travel, feet,\n\nperception and evend flight. It becomes an issue to harness this as how can one embody air in a potion? Essentially something we cannot see with our eyes. But with study one may find represented catalysts. \n\nItems that can be found\n\nin everyday life, purer materials for air are rarer. One such material would be feathers as they're a good representation to air. However it is not a pure form of air, however is often used in the common air based potions. Another material would be a\n\nrabbits foot due to its deft nature. There are other more forms you'll see in your daily life, if you cannot see them the next chapter will further explain this in herbs that represent the elements.\n\nThe Tales of the Traveling Alchemist, Vol I-II.", ""),
        StaticBook("Fund. Alchemy", "§bRosso", "The Tales of a Travelling Alchemist\n------------\nThe Fundamentals of Alchemy\n\nVolume I of V\n\nForeword:\nI have heard many a man ask why one, such as myself, would take such interest and dedication in the art of alchemy when there are ready-made spells that could do just the same, if not better, a job than an apothecary’s potion. My response to\n\nthis statement is rather simple, whereas one must have a certain gift for spell-slinging, rune-craft, and spirit-speaking, one mustn’t need these frivolous commodities to use a balm to mend a wound, shroud themselves in a nigh-invisible cloak\n\nwith the swig from a bitter bottle, or set someone ablaze with bottled flame. Alchemy is not merely reproducing a wizard’s spell, but it is shaping the spell in a way for the common man’s use. While magic still has its place in our world, Alchemy allows\n\nthose without the gift to reap the same benefits as any mage might.\n\nChapter 1: The Fundamentals of Alchemy\n------------\nThe Principle and Law of Equivalent Exchange:\n\nAs I was providing my services to a rather quaint hamlet, which also provided me information of the local flora and more of the same legends I always here of, I was approached by a man of common repute, and he produced a sarcastic, yet thought provoking,\n\nquestion. This question was, and I will attempt to reproduce the dialect as best as my phonetic ability will allow, “ ‘ow come, Mister, ‘hat ye’ can make all ‘dis ‘onderful stuff outta nothin’?” Now as shameful as his speech may be, the question remains\n\nthe same. How is it, that alchemists are capable or reshaping the spells of sorcerers for the common folk out of, seemingly, nothing? The answer to this question is simple to state, but fairly difficult to grasp, and this is the Principle of Equivalent\n\nExchange. Much like the money one gives to the merchant for the apple or slab of meat, one cannot have something for nothing. As the saying goes, “there is no such thing as a free brunch,” there is no such thing as a free potion. This fundamental rule\n\napplies to old alchemy, through the use of symbols and rituals, far more than what I call “Modern” alchemy. Although I will not get into ancient alchemy, I will state why this rule applies to Modern Alchemy. To craft a potion, for example a bottle of\n\nalchemist’s fire, one requires the materials. Much like a spell casted by a wizard, one cannot make something out of nothing. Know that to make a potion, and the possibilities are truly endless, one must take plants, objects, and other representations of\n\nwhat they wish to make. For example, alchemist’s fire could be best produced with an - -, a mixture of -  -, -  -, and a dash of -  -. Why would one need these items?\n\nThe - - provides the basic mixture of what we will make, and of course the better quality of the - - the better quality of the Alchemist’s Fire, but of course we will get further into this in a later chapter. The -  -, a -  -\n\nplant that grows most often in deserts, is often -  - and often associated with -  -. This provides an superb symbol for the element of -  -. The -  -, another powerful symbol of -  -, mixes well with the crushed -  -\n\nto produce something truly strong. The -  -, an optional portion, is another symbol of -  -, albeit both weaker than blaze -  - for both a -  - fire burns far greater than -  -.\n\nNow then, although you have these symbols of flame, rage, and warfare, one mustn’t simply toss objects into the metaphorical cauldron. Precision is necessary, as always. One must measure their mixture carefully, or they will give too much. And giving too\n\nmuch will tip the scales of Equivalent Exchange, causing unpredictable results. Why is this you might ask? When balance is achieved through Equivalent Exchanged, a mixture of the elements for the bottled spell of your desire, than all is well. But when\n\nyou disrupt this balance and push on the world, taking and forcing in too much of one or more element then the world will push back. Of course a word of caution is required, for one may think taking less is the best option when balance is not a certainty.\n\nThis is a common misconception for not providing enough of one element will result in the same conclusion, imbalance and chaos. This can be easily associated with cooking, too much of one ingredient will spoil the recipe, not enough of an ingredient will\n\nproduce similar results. Of course it could also produce blander results, and in that case a potion may not be spoiled but it will simply be weak. This is preferable to exploding, of course.\n\nPreparation and You:\nNovice Alchemists often question me, what advice would you provide the budding alchemist, what sip of wisdom could you provide one who desires to tap your fountain of knowledge? The answer to this\n\nis rather simple, preparation is key to any potion. What is meant by this is that to mix a potion that you have not mixed before, or do not mix often, you must have all the facts and supplies before you begin. This is best provided in a short list\n\nof required procedures if one wishes to keep their eyebrows, fingers, and life.\n\n1. Have all the Facts\n2. Know the Recipe\n3. Clean and check all your instruments are in working order\n4. Ensure you have plenty of whatever ingredients you are using\n5. Keep and Calm Dispostion\n\nI believe the only topic that requires explanation is “Have all the Facts” and by this I mean that one should not mix a potion they know nothing about. Even having the recipe is not enough to craft a potion safely, but knowledge of its origins,\n\ningredients, and uses are crucial. Although budding alchemists often overlook this step, it may save your life one day.\n\nThe Tales of the Traveling Alchemist, Vol I\n\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven", ""),
        StaticBook("AlchemyGuide 2-4", "§bDaniel", "\\_+*+_/\n   Alchemy Guide\n\n\n\nVolume 2 – Reagents\nPart 4/4\n\n\n\n\n-By Daniel Nodal\n\n\n\n\n\n\n       Air Symbols\n\n          Weak\n\nName: Jailer’s moss\nRepresentations: Travel, Movement\nDescription: Quite an annoying plant in many cases, the Jailer’s Moss seems to stick to everything it touches. It is found the caves near or in swamps,\n\ngrowing in water streams from the walls, and sometimes even in small currents of water. It has a very light yellow color making it easily noticeable in the dark caves. It’s main property is the stickiness it has, just a touch and it never seems to\n\nlet go. Of course it doesn’t hold much force, so no one but the small fishes and insects it feeds on get stuck to it, it normally breaks or the whole plant gets dragged with the animal or person. It is from this that the ingredient is considered an air\n\nsymbol, representing the motion of travelling with the other animals, this way spreading. A weak symbol which is great for new alchemists to use and try out.\nLocation: Caves in swamps\n\n         Moderate\n\nName: Swamp’s blossom\nRepresentations: Quickness, speed, Travel\nDescription: This is the flower that would be considered the most beautiful in the awful looking lands of swamp and wetlands. A purplish\n\ngreen flower that grows through the branches of the trees, with a short and thick stem. One might feel bad for removing the little beauty one might find in the swamplands, which is quite understandable. After collecting this flower, you can remove the\n\nnectar which has a very peculiar reaction. This nectar is a clear liquid, but one that acts like quicksilver. Extracting a bit of it and pouring it over your hand, and it will dance gracefully until finding a way to the floor between your fingers.\n\nIt is this nectar that is used in the concoctions of the alchemists, one that represents a clear symbol of air.\nLocation: Growing through the branches of swamp trees\n\n         Strong\n\nlName: Crouching Feather\nRepresentations: Speed, Travel, Gracefulness\nDescription: A graceful and rare sight of the forest and plains. This quite potent reagent can be recognized by it’s\n\nlong three white petals that sweep in a downwards curve to the point where they touch the ground, appearing like feathers. Like the name it has, they are found “crouching” under large fauna, such as big bushes and shrubs. The harvesting of this peticular\n\nplant is one that is quite hard. The flower only blooms at night and a strong wind will blow the petals right off. When finding one, the alchemist or botanist must carefully remove the petals from the stalk, and keep them safe from being blown away, and\n\nfrom being crushed. When finally harvested, this plant is quite a strong symbol of air. The extract of the petals much be collected first, and doing so to a graceful petal is quite hard. It’s use it’s mostly for potions of celerity and when the\n\nrepresentation of speed is quite desired. Yet gracefulness and travel also represent these long petals.\nLocation: Under big bushes and shrubs of forests and plains.\n\nName: Elf’s hair vine\nRepresentations: Travel, Gracefulness, Speed, Flight\nDescription: One of the most beautiful plants of which I speak of in this volume, Elf’s Hair vine is a rare sight in the top of cold mountains. Like the elegant hair\n\nof the nibble elves, the plant has a extreme lightness to it, flowing softly even in the most minute of breezes. The vine is thin and white, seeming to float in the air. Normally protected from the wind, when a stronger gust of air finds it, the small\n\nblossoms that grow from the vines are broken off, and start flying with the wind, travelling together. They have many pure small white petals which gracefully come together to create the flower. These flowers is what an alchemist uses in his concoctions,\n\nwhich makes the collection quite difficult. If a simple breeze can take it away, picking it up or moving it around is an very timely task. The alchemist has to be slow and gentle, as not only would harsh movements send the graceful flower flying away, it\n\nmight also damage the petals of this fragile plant. With a representation of grace of the flower, it’s petals have to be in perfect condition when used, a tear or a part of it damaged, and most of the use is lost. This reagent serves with great use for\n\nconcoctions centered around speed and grace.\nLocation: Covered from the wind at the top of cold mountains.\n\nWritten by Daniel Nodal\nThe Grand Harvest, 1480", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy: Symbols", "§bSarrion", "Alchemy Basic's\nSub Volume I\nSymbols\n\nBy: An unknown alchemist of the alchemist guild.\n\nRe-written by:\nSarrion di' Pascali\n\nDuring my times as a student, I was very skeptical of most of the theory of alchemy. One of the things I could not understand was the need for Symbols. If the representation and properties of a reagent are what is used,\n\nthe elements they fit in are not necessary to be stated. After a few years of becoming an alchemist I finally realised the purpose, it is because not only do ingredients have perfect representations, but the sibling representations are as well\n\nnecessary to create proper results. Perfect representations would never be able to be properly used and leveled, and no reagent has only one representation. For such, attaching a symbol to an ingredient\n\nfits far better than attaching only the representations. By choosing the representation or symbol you can decide the properties of what you create. One could use the representation of life for healing potions, but depending if it’s an\n\nearth symbol or a water symbol, it will have other smaller scale representations that will distinguish them. A water symbol will provide a more smooth and fluent healing rate, making the wound heal nicely, and the earth symbol will\n\ngive more strength and resilience to the healing process, perhaps making it hard to get the wound reopen. Everything fits into at least one of the four symbols or elements of alchemy, Fire, Water, Earth and Air.\n\nThe way you look at what a symbol represents is looking at the element in question and assessing what properties it has and what it is associated with. You do not look exactly at what the element is, assessing a symbol to a reagent is\n\n more about symbolism than pure correct connection. It’s to aid the alchemist design and investigate the ways of alchemy in a more practical way, without having to reach so fully into the details. Each ingredient or reagent may\n\n also be of different strengths depending on the properties and rarity it holds. Those which are rarer have a greater value than those which are common, and as such they are have as well more strength of use in alchemy.\n\nAn ingredient may be a Weak, Moderate or Strong symbol, those of greater strength required quite more skilled hands, but the payoff might be quite better.\n\n=[Fire]=\n->Starting with the more easily explainable one, fire is the symbol very used by alchemists that focus themselves on improving skills of fighting and giving new arsenal to use in wars. While I don’t find myself using many fire symbols,\n\n they are an absolute in the making of many of the truly fantastic deeds of alchemy. Most of the representations of fire are quite easy to grasp, because they fit perfectly onto what fire truly is. It may represent heat, burning, consumption,\n\ndestruction, warmth, power and chaos. Most of these have as such a great use for damaging others or to give power to oneself or others. Alchemist fire for example, is a great demonstration of what could be done with the use of fire symbols,\n\nfire in a bottle. Devastating and powerful, it is most likely the most dangerous conception that an alchemist can create. The representation of chaos is one more confusing, one that may bring unknown results as controlling chaos is quite\n\nhard job to have. Fire does have another way you may look at it, as many poets might say, “the flames of passion ran deep in his heart, but he acted as in a burning rage.” Fire may also represent emotions, both those\n\nof anger, regret, rage, confusion, and those of love, passion, confidence. These representation are especially useful in potions to improve oneself, and in poisons to bring damage emotionally to others. The mythical love potion though, is one that is\n\nby experience impossible to have in full effect, as love is in it’s own way subjective, and varies from person to person. The closest thing that may be created is one that gives well being and peace of mind, or ecstasy.\n\nMost of those are considered drugs.\nAs for the properties one may find generally in fire symbols, the idea of heat is normally present in them. A flower or plant that is warm to the touch could be a good indicator of a fire symbol.\n\nThe effects it might bring to a person might be associated that way as well, such as becoming feverish, or feeling a burning itchy sensation on touch. And of course the flavour of many of them, which I don’t really advise\n\nto be a way to figure out what kind of symbol it is, is most of the times spicy and strong, sometimes sweet as it goes well with raising emotions.\n\n=[Water]=\n->Sometimes even said to be the base ingredients, a symbol of water is essential to all potions and oils that alchemy can create. This is of course due to it’s liquid and coherent state, or it would not be a potion or oil. The base is the mai\n\ncomponent of a potion, where all other reagents are added and mixed in. It is important to refer that it is considered a base only to the creation of potions and oils, but in the other more exotic fields of alchemy, they are not used as such.\n\nNormal water is the most simple of bases to use, not very useful for any powerful potion, as it’s not all that pure, it’s used mainly in minor potions. Distilling it would purify it and make it far more useful in lesser potions.\n\nThe purest form for a base you can find, is aqua vitae. Aqua vitae can also be called Alcohol, or distilled alcohol, it is simply the result of distilling brews such as ale, wine or any other intoxicating beverages.\n\nWith it, the most powerful of potions can be created. The other base one could use is lard, which is used only in the creation of oils.\nNow onto the representations that define a symbol of water. Like when you imagine\n\na stream of water, you imagine calmness, fluidity, peace, order and balance of nature. Continuing with the balance of nature, you have to understand that water is one of the fundamentals of life, and as such, water represents many parts\n\nof it. Life, regrowth, rebirth are the most important of representations, making water symbols sometimes the most important in healing potions, and antidotes. Tasting a water symbol normally brings the other representations that water might have,\n\nfreshness and a minty flavour are normally what you get from safe to eat water symbols, even going so far sometimes descending into cold and ice, another two representations of water. Do not think that just because water symbols\n\nmay represents life and are used in a lot of healing potions that they’re safe to eat, even water symbols might be essential to the creation of poisons, as a stream may be a thought of life and balance,\n\nbut a strong river might be the location of one’s death.\n\n=[Earth]=\n->While the water symbol is considered the base for potion making, I consider earth symbols the bases for other more advanced sections of alchemy, such as Transmutation and the creation of Chimeras. Such is because while potions need\n\nto be fluid and in a liquid state, transmutation is performed mostly on objects, and the creation of chimeras requires great representations of life and connectivity if one is ever to be created. More of the advanced alchemy will be left for another book,\n\nbut I wanted to leave the idea that the selection of a base is not randomly selected but adapts itself to what the alchemist wishes to create. The earth symbols concentrate themselves more on solids and life.\n\nTo better understand the symbol, one must think of the two greatest visualisations one thinks about when the idea of earth comes to mind. Those two visualisations are the rock, and dirt. Such have the representations that will fit with all of the symbols\n\nof earth. A rock is resilient, hard to break and strong. It’s used a lot in buildings creating structure and protection, and all these characteristics are representations of the symbol. A rock may also be associated with minerals and metals, two\n\nrepresentations very much sought out in transmutation. Once one thinks of dirt on the other hand, he does not receive the same thoughts as when with a stone. Dirt is different, dirt is used mostly for the growth of plants, the propagation of life.\n\n This is the second part of the symbol of Earth, one that might not be the most easily noticeable. It represents life, regrowth, rebirth and connection. Plants grow in it, and animals life over it, feeding of the plants, and so fauna and flora are\n\nas well the last representations of earth, making them the most suitable for the creation of chimeras.\nI do not advise trying to eat any type of Earth symbol. While it may represent life as well, it leans more for survival of life, than saving of it.\n\nThis means that normally earth symbols are not suitable for eating, either having extremely bitter tastes, or being straight up poisonous.\n\n=[Air]=\n->The symbol of air is perhaps the hardest to explain in all of the symbols. When one tries to imagine air, he may think of the wind, or of what he breaths, perhaps of smoke. What could one bring from those? These are quite simply almost\n\n impossible to see, you can not see air, you can not see wind, you feel the wind and hear it’s sound, and you breath the air. One of the first representations to speak of would be of perception, without air you would not be able to see\n\nbut it comes from that fact that you do not see air, yet know it’s there, and percy the effects of it. The rest of the representations are around the idea of speed and quickness. Perhaps a thought we all have when we imagine speed,\n\nwe can think of the movements in the air, the fast animals traveling through the air and ground almost provoking a wind in itself. Not the best way to explain it, as those images are quite hard to have. Of the most easy representations to think about,\n\nyou may think of fight and travel, and for such you only need to think of a bird. The palate one gets on these symbols are normally quite bland. Having almost no taste, no aroma, almost like the normal air in the mountains.\n\nA last mention on air symbols, is always it’s representations of fragility and grace. The ingredients of air are ones that in most cases are very thin and weak, easily broken and destroyed maybe simply by the moving of the hand near them.\n\nGrace is a representation I adore to use in potions of healing, and oils of well being, because they bring the condition of the skin to a more natural and graceful way. Yet I consider them hardest symbols to use,\n\nas the harvesting of the ingredients, the handling and the storage has to be extremely precise.\n===================End.\n\nLook for:\nAlchemy Volumes I, II, III.\nRe-Written by:\nSarrion di' Pascali", ""),
        StaticBook("Alc. Potions II", "§bSarrion", "Alchemy: Potions\nPart II\n-------------------Please read Part I.\n\n-Sarrion di' Pascali\n\n=[Potion of Smoke Whispers]=\nike in the magical stories where the hero throws something into the floor and a cloud of smoke is formed, this potion creates the same as the vial shatters. Creating black smoke which releases fast, it allows the\n\n thrower a distraction or the ability to temporarily incapacitate the adversaries. Brewed over the course of four weeks, this potion is literally smoke in a bottle, and like it, it creates irritation when breathing and to the eyes.\n\nRecipe:\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n4 Air Symbols (Flight, Transport)\n1 Fire Symbol (Combustion)\n\n=[Potion of Sleeping]=\nUsed by people of all kinds, this potion’s objective is placing someone to sleep. It might act fast, it might act slow, it might be to help with sleeping at night, or it might be to knock someone out. The use will dictate the power\n\n the potion has to be made. Minor potions will have more of a relaxing result, making resting far more easier to achieve. Lesser ones have to be drank, and soon after the potion will take effect and the one to drink it will not even\n\nnoticed he passed out. Greater potions on the other hand, might not even need to be drank, only smelled. This is particular good for doctors when needing to operate, as it does not force the patient to drink something.\n\n To force it on someone might not have some good results, as the potion does take it’s time to give it’s effect, enough to fight back. It is worth noticing, that if the recipe is only read by the symbols needed, the lesser potion\n\nof sleeping is the same as the lesser potion of mending. Always be careful with the representations of the symbols you use.\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n2 Symbols of Water (Calmness and Relaxation)\n1 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n\n- Lesser\nBase: Distilled Water\n2 Symbols of Water (Calmness and Relaxation)\n1 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Resilience)\n\n- Greater\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n2 Symbols of Water (Calmness and Relaxation)\n2 Symbols of Air (Flight and Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Resilience)\n\n=[Potion of Freezing]\nA peculiar potion, which it’s only intent is freezing or cooling something, or perhaps to just keep cold. I invented one of the potions, the potent one which quickly freezes water on contact, making it something impossible to drink\n\n and dangerous to the touch. This potion must actually be kept in a thick glass vial, and it then must be surrounded by fur, or something of the like as to keep it your hands from freezing and stopping water from getting\n\nstuck to the vial. Another recipe was made by my student, it is far weaker, and more for a use in someone’s body. To put it simply, it is a protection of a person’s cold, which seems quite horrible for most times.\n\n Yet if you’re travelling into very hot climates, it could save one’s life.\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n1 Symbols of Water (Cold)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Resilience)\n\n- Lesser\nBase: Distilled Water\n2 Symbols of Water (Ice, Cold)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Protection)\n\n- Greater\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n2 Symbols of Water (Calmness and Relaxation)\n1 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Hardiness)\n\n=[Waterbreathing Potion]=\nA remarkable potion which allows someone to breath underwater for long periods of time. The biggest side effect of this potion thought, is that you stop breathing air. Made of rare ingredients,\n\nmainly the liver and gills of a mermaid, it turns someone temporarily into a sort of human fish. Your skin between the toes and hands starts webbing, only falling out a few hours after the effects of the potion are gone. Being too long inside\n\n of the water also makes it so that when you finally leave, your ears seem to pop, and you can not hear for a few minutes. The most important part of the potion, is the creation of gills on the side of your neck, it is these that allow you to breathe\n\n underwater, yet not above. For the first few moments of the transformation, someone can not breathe in or out of water, it may create panic in some people, yet they must remember to go into the water, because they will not be able to breath out of it.\n\nRecipe:\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae mixed with a bit of seawater\nMermaid’s Gills and Liver\n1 Symbols of Water (Fluidity)\n1 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Resilience)\n\n=[Frost Oil]=\nAs an oil, this concoction has an external use and is not to be drank. As I move on to the oils, the type of base fixes itself to only lard. Lard has a more viscous consistency which allows it not to drip when coated on something.\n\nThe Frost oil, or Oil of frost, is the first I will speak of. An oil which has similarities with the potion of freezing, but whose effects last longer, enabling it to be coated in weapons, armor or clothes. It’s effects are quick cooling what it touches,\n\nbut it reacts weakly to air, meaning it’s effect happen mostly through contact. Using such an oil on clothing or armor might make the wearer cold, but such could be the objective if one is travelling into hot deserts or inside volcanos.\n\nOn the other hand, while it might make the cloth or armor it was placed cold, it also offers a protection to the same cold, as something already cold, can’t get colder. As such, if one would use the oil of frost in cloth,\n\nhe would find himself getting colder, yet if the oil was used on the outer layer of plate armor, it would assist with protecting against cold winds. If placed on weapons, it has the most use on swords. A slash of the weapon on\n\nsomeone’s skin would slightly freeze the area. You might think a frozen cut would mean that the blood loss would be lower, and it’s true, yet freezing the area will make the skin turn slightly blue, and the opponent will have difficulties moving\n\n the area, this way slowing him down. The cut makes the cooling enter more into the flesh, perhaps even forcing someone to have to remove the frozen flesh affected, perhaps even amputating a limb. The biggest worry of using it on tools\n\nand armor is that it also cools it down substantially, and when badly brewed will make metals very easily shattered, when well brewed they will still suffer some problems in lesser effect.\n\nRecipe:\n\nBase: Lard\n3 Symbols of Water(Cold, Ice, Frost)\n2 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Fortitude)\n\n=[Flame Oil]=\nThe Oil of Flame does almost the exact same as the Oil of Frost, yet instead of cold, it works with burning. In contact with the skin it will leave huge burns, but of course you do not apply it to the skin. On weapons,\n\nwhen striking the enemy, it will cause great pain a kind to burning. The flesh will become red and start to create puss. While the Oil of Frost might slow the opponent, the oil of Flames inflicts greater damage on him. The weapon does not escape\n\ndamage, while with the Frost Oil the weapon may shatter, with the Flame Oil it corrodes it. While the effects do seem like those of a flame, it does not actually heat an object up. It actually make it more flame repellent and heat resistant.\n\nOf course the resistance isn’t perfect, and a wooden sword coated with the oil will still catch ablaze, it does tarden the effects. This means if applied to armor or cloth that is not touching the skin, it creates a sort of heat and flame\n\nprotection. In the recipe it is found the use of a water symbol, and one might ask why such a representation is required in such a fire centric concoction. The explanation is rather simple, it calms the fire symbols of destruction and power, balancing\n\nit to not create an extreme reaction, which normally is represented as an explosion while brewing.\nBase: Lard\n3 Symbols of Fire (Power, Destruction, Burning)\n2 Symbols of Earth (Endurance)\n1 Symbol of Water (Calmness, Balance)\n\n>=[Alchemist’s Fire]=<\nOne of the greatest means of attack from an alchemist, their creation of fire. Many times referred to as ‘fire in a bottle,’ a more correct term would be ‘liquid fire.’ The alchemist is able to access the properties of fire and\n\nadd them into a liquid, making it highly volatile and dangerous. Getting hit by a bottle of it would result in being completely covered in flames. Of course it’s not always used for combat, and many times it is used to cleaning metal utensils,\n\nsuch as cauldrons and knives from contaminants and impurities. Some may say it’s a waste to use such a powerful potion on cleaning, but it’s use that way may also be associated with it’s danger. Alchemist’s fire is highly dangerous,\n\nboth while brewing and after. To brew it, not only must the temperature of the concoction remain constant, that temperature must be almost freezing. The room must be made almost specifically to create it,\n\nas it must be freezing cold. Some alchemists have started using Thanhium to have a cold room in their labs instead of heading to the tall mountains, or the cold Tundras. In those conditions the alchemist’s fire is made, and even brewed in freezing\n\ntemperatures, it still can burn your hand. The bottles must be made of glass, and to fill them, thongs of sorts must be used, as if your hand touches the liquid it will be burnt, even will gloves. Never should an alchemist fall\n\n into the mistake of using a wooden cork, as the liquid will burn right through it and most likely blow in the hands of the handler, they must always be made of glass. Now onto the dangers of the bottles. If they are kept stationary,\n\nthey won’t be dangerous, it’s problems start when they are moved quickly or hit. Some alchemists keep them in Athin made sacks to prevent some of it’s force if the bottle is broken, yet even so the alchemist would be burnt. Falling\n\nwhile carrying these bottles, or being pushed, punched, thrown to the ground may result in a bottle breaking. For them to be useful they must break, but that is the bane of the carrier as well.\n\nThe safest way to use the bottle is to drop it, it results in it blowing up when hitting the ground in almost all cases. While it does explode, it’s explosion is not one like tnt, but more of many tiny shards of glass and fire sent flying everywhere.\n\nRecipe:\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n4 Symbols of Fire (Destruction, Power, Consumption)\n2 Symbols of Water (Fluidity)\n1 Symbol of Air (Speed)", ""),
        StaticBook("AlchemyGuide 2-1", "§bDaniel", "\\_+*+_/\n   Alchemy Guide\n\n\n\nVolume 2 – Reagents\nPart 1/4\n\n\n\n\n-By Daniel Nodal\n\nIn the process of equivalent exchanged, as explained in a previous volume, is one that requires things of value to create the alchemical results of the same value. For such concoctions, various ingredients, or better called reagents, can be found and used\n\nall throughout the realms. The rarity of some are great, and many of those listed here have even other properties and uses besides the ones that are used in alchemy. The list of reagents is endless, as even dirt may be considered an ingredient, even if\n\nnothing worthy can be created from it.\n\nI have created a list of all the ingredients knowledged to me in with a pronounced alchemy use, in which I explain how to collect it, where to find it, and what symbols it represents. While I do detail mostly the\n\nplants and botany with use in alchemy concoctions, a good alchemist must also learn from examples and from the theory itself how to find new ingredients, and how to access what type of ingredient one holds in their hand. Besides flora, parts of animals\n\nand beasts are also highly used, many times as more powerful or stronger symbols than the plants, yet they are also rarer, and harder to collect. I do not mention the powerful symbols, yet many of them are of easy imagination. A feather of a giant bird,\n\na fin of a siren, the heart of a great fire beast, a bone of a troll, are just examples that may be used to then further develop your imagination.\n\nTo new alchemists I advise the use of Weak reagents, as they are far easier to use,\n\nyet almost all times don’t result in great potions. These are not to be ignored though, as even the strongest of potions may require Weak reagents, it comes down to their representations, and what the alchemist requires in his concoction. Pay attention\n\nto the characteristics of the plants and how they fit with the symbol and representations. Where they grow, if they are poisonous, what nutrients they need, how to harvest them, what part of the reagent to use, and many more details.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    Earth Symbols\n\n          Weak\n\nName: Goblin’s Ivy\nRepresentation: Resilience, Poison, Life\nDescription: One of the most easily found flora of the deserts, this plant is easily identified as a brownish green vine with small leaves. It is found mainly\n\nclinging onto old buildings or ruins of the deserts, and on the dry cliff sides. It is quite a resilient plant, able to survive in extreme hostile conditions, and with a very low  or no amount of water close by. Removing it also becomes a problem, as it\n\nseems to regrow on the same spot after someone goes to the long labour of removing it all. It is these properties that brings the plant such good uses, it’s representation of resilience makes a welcome addition to any stoneskin potion, even while being\n\nregarded as a Weak reagent. A few poisons may also be created by use of this reagent, mainly ones weaker, some to create nausea or ill disposition. Easy to find and prepare, it is normally used by those new to alchemy to learn the craft.\n§\n\nLocation: Clinging on ruins, stones and cliff sides of dry regions.\n\n        Moderate\n\nName: Saffvil\nRepresentation: Resilience, Endurance, Death\nDescription: Possibly the most horribly smelling herb in all of swamplands, many alchemists make use of their nose when trying to find it. This green\n\ngrass is green and grows on the corpses of animals, on dead wood and plants, and sometimes on the bottom of big trees. This plant is one that is extremely hard to remove from anything, as it spreads very quickly and strongly. Normally to remove it, you\n\nwould have to remove the “host”, the plant or animal that it is growing on. If you do not, it will spread all throughout it. This means that the plant has a great representation of Resilience and Endurance, but this plant, unlike a few other Earth\n\nsymbols, does not represent Life and Regrowth, but Death. This means this plant is mostly used in the creation of poisons, and one particular myth of an aging potion.\nLocation: On the bodies of dead plants and animals.\n\nName: Alabaster Leaf\nRepresentations: Immobility, Perseverance\nDescription: This plant is mostly characterized by it’s leaf’s alabaster color. Most often times found in the dryer regions of the forest growing around fallen trees. Quite small\n\nin size, harvesting it may prove a difficulty to the untrained hands. The best method would perhaps be taking the whole plant, with roots, and then remove the leaves as you may use them. The extract one gets by crushing the leaves is what an alchemist\n\nshould use. It comes with it’s natural alabaster hue, which serves as paint to many. The extract is mildly poisonous, creating dizziness and nausea to those that consume it. It’s main uses are in antidotes, oils, and of course, poisons.\n\nLocation: Forest dry regions next to fallen trees.\n\nName: Tippen’s Root\nRepresentation: Life, Regrowth, Resilience, Strength, Endurance\nDescription: Tippen’s roots is one of the most used reagents in the making of mending\npotions, it is even said, that it is required to be present in all\n\nhealing potions, or the potion will be of weak quality. It was named after the botanist that had first discovered it around hills and in dense forests. It’s location of growing is oftenthe same of that of Alabaster leaf, besides fallen trees, but as well\n\nnext to carcases of animals and great bugs. It is quite a pleasant sight when compared with the death around it, as the plant of the herb blooms into purple flowers. What one has to use though, is the roots. They are of a yellow color, with spots\n\naccentuated a bit more darker. Many carry these roots around, even those with no knowledge of it’s use in alchemy. This is because from the roots one can easily make a remedy which may stop bleeding near instantly. In it’s unprepared its strength isn't\n\nas great, but it still may save countless lives. The problem with this root is a simple one, it’s smell. The odor that the plant releases when the salve of the roots is spilled is enormous. While using it you may gag and even vomit from thinking that you\n\nare placing such foul-smelling balm onto an open wound. After such, the smell will not escape you for a few hours. It’s traits in healing bring the herb representations of Life and Regrowth,\nbut it also has great representations of Strength and Endurance.\n\nLocation: Forest, around dead trees and animals.\n\nName: Dersert Berry Bush\nRepresentation: Protection, Resilience\nDescription: The Desert Berry Bush's appearance is that of a withered rose bush, long brown/grey stalks with big pointy thorns, yet without any leaves. It doesn't appear to grow\n\nhigher than four feet, but most of the thorns are longer than an inch. It grows inside cactus forests, as in, where there is great concentration of cactus. Never has one been alone in the desert’s sands without a cactus near it. The bush gives Berries\n\nof a strong red color every full moon. These don't last long, as animals of the desert appear to enjoy it's taste quite dearly. The berries are extremely sweet, something very strange to find in the desert, so no wonder the animals enjoy it. The stalks\n\nare not like the berries, if an animal by any chance passed the long thorns to rip and eat the stalk, I believe would fall dead in less than a day. As you cut the stalk you notice that the insides are green, instead of the dead brown. From it it's juice\n\nflows a lot, yet of course it's poison to drink it. Eating the liquid or even touching it causes rashes and all someone wants to do afterwards is scratch the area, even if it’s the mouth. Too much of it and it kills of course, and it sound be painful.\n\nThe thorns create the same effect, so without gloves, the hand starts itching and becomes red for about a day. It doesn't seem deadly to descendents and probably kha's, but it does explain the small rodents found dead near them. A thorn is poisonous,\n\nand annoying, just doesn't kill big animals. The stalk seems to have far more uses in alchemy when compared with it’s berry, especially in oils of protection and potions of stoneskin. It represents protection and resilience, the thorns and it's poison\n\nnature being the reasons why. To prepare for it I have found it hard, as when cut, the juices drip quite fast. You can't remove the bush from the ground, as it's roots are too big, so they have to be cut in the spot. Feet long pieces of stalk are enough,\n\nyet one would have to almost tilt the plant down so that the cut doesn't face down. Gloves are essential in gathering it because of the thorns, and after cutting, I advise placing it in a long bottle, with the cut end pointing up. When being prepared,\n\nthe alchemist should be careful as to lose as little bit of the stalk as he removes the seemingly dead outer layer of the stalk, before using it in his potion.\nLocation: Desert Cactus Forests\n\n         Strong\n\nName: Starved Coyote’s Tick\nRepresentations: Protection, Resilience, Connection\nDescription: The Tick that I speak of is probably the only non flora which I speak of as an Alchemisy ingredient in this\n\nvolume. Mostly it can be found on coyotes the first ones discovered on the bodies of thin starved coyotes, giving them the name. This does not mean it does not appear in other animals, like desert hares and rhinos. As long as it is an animal that grows\n\nin hot climates, you may be lucky enough to find one. If the animal has a lot of fur, it may be hard to find the tick, but due to it’s size, just passing a hand through the animal may make one find the tick. It might get as big as a thumb, very fat of\n\npure brown color. It would seem many times as a lump in the animal’s skin, and trying to remove it is mostly impossible. The Staved Coyote’s Tick, once grabbing onto some meat, will never let go unless it wants to. Fire, Ice, or even swings of a warhammer\n\nonly damage the skin around it, the tick itself resiting more. It’s collecting is done by cutting the flesh that the tick is grabbing, and only when all the meat is removed from the tick, does it try to find another place to grab to. This is a dangerous\n\nthing to do, as if an alchemists isn’t careful, even with gloves the tick may grab onto the skin, and never let go. When I say never, I truly mean it, no one can make the tick let go, most likely the finder would have to be removed, or at least part of\n\nit, that or one would learn to live with the tick, as it doesn’t seem toxic or poisonous to an animal. With characteristics like this, it is no wonder that it is a strong symbol of earth, representing Connection, Protection and Resilience.\n\nLocation: Bodies of animals of hot Climates\n\nContinues in part 2\n\nWritten by Daniel Nodal\nThe Grand Harvest, 1480", ""),
        StaticBook("Arcane Evocation", "§bKirrekith", "Arcane Evocation\n\nTranscribed by the Farseer Thurak, 19th of the Great Harvest, 1529.\n\nOne: What is Arcane Energy?\nTwo: Arcane Energy.\nThree: Aura.\nFour: Beginner steps.\n\nChapter One\nWhat is Arcane Evocation?\n-------------------\nArcane Evocation is the art of drawing pure magical energy from the void. It can be shaped as anything the mage can think of.\n\nThis is a creative form of magic with the mostly\n\nartistic uses. While it is possible to use this magic in combat, only a skilled arcanist can do so, and even then it is not as strong as the other types of evocation. If your intention is to aquire a means of dealing damage, then this is not the magic for\n\nyou.\n\nChapter Two\nArcane Energy\n\nArcane energy is a rare element. In fact, it does not exist naturally in the world. It is an ethereal substance almost exclusive to the void. Because of this, Arcane Evocation is\n\nthe most difficult type of evocation to learn. As with all other types of evocation one must fully understand the element that is being evoked. The only way to study arcane energy is by observing an evocation of someone who already knows this magic. This\n\nmeans it is practically impossible to learn without a teacher.\n\nNow for those already have a teacher and seek to complement their lessons with further knowledge, the arcane element  can be described as follows. \n\nIn its purest form,\n\narcane energu is invisible, shapeless, odorless, completely inert and very volatile. It can be compared to air or smoke, having similar composition to most gaseous substances. \n\nThose who have observed evocations my have noticed a fine, thin mist\n\nsurrounding them. This is because it isn't a single mass. It is, in fact, a conglomeration of countless tiny wisps of energy that together give the illusion of being a single object. \n\nSmoke is perhaps the thing that visually resembles arcane energy the\n\nmost in our world. Studying its shapeless and erratic nature may be of big help to understand arcane energy.\n\nChapter Three\nAura\n-------------------\nAura is a field of mana that surrounds living beings. More complex beings, such as\n\ndescendants, have a colour associated to their aura. Each individual has their own colour for their aura. \n\nSome believe that the colour depends on one's personality. This isn't always the case, however, and anyone may have any colour. This colour doesn't\n\nchange, unless other forces somehow manage to tamper with your aura, which is usually very bad. Auras are very relevant to Arcane Evocation in particular. \n\nWhen raw arcane energy is drawn from the void, it instantly mixes with the mages\n\naura, making it visible and giving it colour. It is a common misconception to assume that arcane evocations manipulate aura itself. In truth, it is simply the product of mixing aura with arcane energy that is being manipulated, not the actual aura.\n\nAnd no, arcanists cannot see other people's aura or make it visible. \n\nAura is a very personal thing, like your own soul.\n\nThis means your evocations can never mix with someone else's or other types of evocation.\n\nChapter Four\nBeginner Steps\n-------------------After you have managed your first evocation, there is not much you can do. You will only be capable of producing a small wisp and hold it for a short time. The best thing to do is to keep practicing. With\n\neach successful attempt you will be able to hold the wisp a little longer. Make sure to have rested enough to be completely recovered after each attempt. \n\nEventually you will be able to hold your wisp for about five minutes.\n\n[!] A Detailed Drawing of a wisp hovering over an open hand occuies most of this page.\n\nAt that point you'll get enough time to figure out how to get your wisp to do things. You are in control of your ecovation, but must learn to control it properly. Try\n\nmoving it around slowly. Think clearly of the movements you wish it to make. \n\nAt first a few inches is all you can manage at a time, but with practice it'll get better. Another good exercise for beginners is to make it shrink and grow slightly in size.\n\nAdding and removing the amount of energy is an evocation is a basic tool that is key for the later stages of learning.\n\nOnce you can comfortably manipulate the size and movement of the wisp with relative freedom, you can start to attempt shaping it.\n\nSimple geomertical shapes such as cubes, spheres and cylinders are the easiest to achieve and you should start there. Imagine that arcane energy taking the desided shape wit has much detail as possible. Do not lose focus once the evocation has taken the\n\ndesired shape or it will become amorphous again.\n\nThis is the end of this book. Hopefully this will help many students to complement their learning of arcane evocation. \n\nAs book dedicated to advanced students and even novice arcanists\n\nbe written in the future. Thank you very much for reading my book and I hope you found it helpful.\n\nWrote by Farseer, re-wrote by Kirrekith during the raid on the Orc Capital, on the 16th of The Grand Harvest, 1532. \n\nSigned Kirrekith", ""),
        StaticBook("Translocators", "§bArchmage Haadi", "~*~\n      On the creation\n    of translocators\n                  -\n\n\n\n              \\o/\n              -O-\n              /o\\\n\n[!] Along the page you see many strange markings and drawings. They look to be rough copies, at best. There appears to be some sort of archway built of stone, below it, several odd circular schematics, or perhaps a map. Next to the map, the phases of the\n\nmoon trail along.\n\n[OOC] View in browser:\n\n tinyurl.com/slatrop\n-------------------\n\nAn odd name, indeed. Though, the actual device is far more mysterious and eldritch in nature. Translocators, Lori Oathcast refers to them as. Crumena took to calling them closets, as per their birthplace, the closets within Rasmot's magical porta-potty.\n\nRegardless, the process for creating these translocators is a fairly complex ritualistic one that involves both a skilled Void Translocationist with knowledge of the subject, and a fairly powerful enchanter capable of binding the spell to the locations\n\nin question.\n---\n\nCreation\n\nAn enchanter holds the magic to the spot. One who knows how to send matter to the Void and bring it back again must have learnt to create what is like a 'gateway' into the Void. More than just the item itself, but anything that touches it. Even\n\nair. Then again you do it on the other end and the mage attempts to pull the block back through the Void and the enchanter binds that to a doorway or pillar or something. In short, one must reach into the Void and pull out an open space.\n\nMaintenance\n\nAfter time, it will need a mana supply or one end will fizzle out before the other and create an endless hole. It's advised then that you have the enchanter available fairly often to refill whatever large mana gem or crystal you wish to power\n\nthe translocators' magic with. As far as i'm aware, this would be the only option, unless one would be able to get ahold of Kalameet's notes on the mana obelisk. For the ignorant, this is large pillar in which sums of mana are stored for use. The mana\n\ncannot be pulled out by any means by a magi to fuel their own spellcasting, but instead it exerts energy needed to power enchantments in the area. I'd elaborate further, though i'm only going off of what i've seen myself.", ""),
        StaticBook("Electric Evo.", "§bHaadi Mubdee", "=--=--=--=--=--=--=            Electrical\n           Evocation\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=\n\n\n     -< ^v^v^v^v^\n\nThere is an old saying that humans (mortals) fear most other humans. I believe, however, that those who say that have never looked outside the confines of the human world. If anyone has come face to face with the power of nature, they will consider the\n\nprevious proverb a joke. If this sounds like nothing more than nonsense coming from an old fool, then answer this next question. If you can possess any form of power, what would you choose? Would you choose the power of mankind, or that of a wild beast?\n\nWould it be the roars of powerful men, or the sound of thunder that shakes the heavens? Is it the limited life of men, or nature's continuous cycle of life? Whichever way you look at it, the power of man is feeble compared to the grandeur of nature.\n\nThroughout history, all of mankind has admired nature's power and though they had feared it, they tried to deny that. This is one of man's many follies. They neglect nature's power, which does not discriminate, solely because they are a part of nature.\n\nIn particular, as humans learned of Mana and started seeing a glimpse of nature's other realm, they began neglecting nature's power even more. They didn't realize that their \"magic\" was actually an imitation of nature's power. This is what worries me.\n\nThere are many students who are Wizards and Druids in training who do not understand that every magic's starting point is a natural phenomenon. I, who also boasted in my knowledge of Electric magic , did not learn this until I saw lightning strike down at\n\nthe Crown Lands Plateau of Oren. In hopes that those who come after me will not make the same mistake, I decided to write this book. In other words, I hope that beginner wizards will learn the laws of nature and have a healthy fear of it as well.\n\nAlthough this book only deals with lightning power, which is only one of nature's awesome power, if you are wise, you will take the moral of the story to heart and apply it when training other magic as well.\n\nThe Principle of Electricity series Magic Skill\n                -=-\n\nIn order to understand the principle of Electric magic, you must first learn its natural counterpart: Lightning. If you've seen anyone use Electric-based magic, you probably noticed a close resemblance to a lightning strike. The lightning strike occurs\n\nwhen Mana, the energy that surrounds us, splits into two polarities of energy for reasons unknown. These split polarities each continue to accumulate energy, when, in an instant, an explosion of energy flow occurs between heaven and earth.\n\nThe intensity of the light and heat during this process contains tremendous power. Also, because of its unpredictability of where it strikes, it has been an object of fear to mankind. There are many opinions as to when and why Mana, splits into two\n\npolarities of energy. However, before anyone could come up with any concrete evidence, the debate's focus shifted because of the ‘great’ Wizard Ambrosia, who at times lacked common sense. He claimed that he was able to stabilize his Mana to split the two\n\npolar energies in a controlled manner, which caused all the scholars to shift their focus from the conditions in which the energy splits to the process of it. (It is a shame that scholars today are not interested in insightfully studying natural\n\nphenomena, and rather just want to uncover it just for the sake of using it. This is quite foolish.) Once Mana is split into two polar energies, each polarity begins accumulating energy at a rapid rate and causes an electric discharge. Then, when the\n\nbalance of energy between the two polarities is broken, a transfer of energy occurs from the one with more energy to the other. So imagine what would happen if one could embody either of the polarities and designate his or her target as the other\n\npolarity. Then, using Mana, if he or she polarizes the Mana and also raises his/her own energy level to be higher than their target, what would happen?\nExactly. This is what is known as Electrical Energy Magic.\n\nHow to use the Lightning Bolt\n                -=-\n\nThe Lightning Bolt attack is a magic that uses the mechanic of creating static electricity, which in turn utilizes the characteristics of the negative charge for a sudden electrostatic discharge. During the energy transfusion, a bright explosion\n\nresembling a lightning strike, rushes toward your target as Mana is depleted into replicating this phenomenon. Once all the Mana is depleted from the bolt and the surrounding elements reach equilibrium, the flow of energy ends. (Just like a lightning\n\nstrike in nature.) There are many questions surrounding this magic, such as \"How powerful is it?\" and \"How easy is it to use?\", which is technically asking how much energy it needs and how long it takes. To give you an answer, there is a limit to how much\n\nenergy you can gather, and you can only maintain the energy for about 15 seconds until later coming to a greater grasp of the art. You can charge your energy multiple times to gather more energy, but it is reported that it can become highly unstable after\n\nafter the third charge which is already risky enough. Also, you only have about 15 seconds to charge all your energy. This is because of several factors, such as the difficulty of maintaining the Mana's focus, and also maintaining the polarized energy at\n\nthe same time. (Even if you have plenty of Mana, it is difficult to charge for an extensive electrical bolt). The ‘Lightning Bolt’ attack is used to strike your enemy with electricity . Once your opponent is struck with this attack, they will be in a\n\nstate of shock due to the electricity surging within them as it an energy. Not a tangible object. Since this attack is based on an instant energy explosion between you and your opponent, the lesser the energy gap between you and your opponent, the great\n\ndamage it will inflict as the longer the distance the surge of electricity must travel, the more it discharges. Wasting precious Mana in the long run.\n\n              Simple\n              Usage\n                -=-\n\nOnce you've carefully read through this book, and understood the principles behind the Lightning Bolt attack and are ready to use it, you can do so by following these simple steps. First, a connection to the Void is a must. As any form of magic. The\n\nsimplest manner in which to begin evoking such an element is to picture the empty black abyss that is the Void. A sleet of pure nothingness. Void of any life as the name suggests. Now one must mentally picture lightning crashing down upon the Earth. But\n\nstrictly the lighting. If one were to picture the scene, one could possibly border the realm of Pseudomancy (Illusionary magic) or if too much detail is placed on the Earth, soil or stone may be produced. Then you will see the static discharge enivision\n\nsuch an occurring unfolding before your palm you calm your mind to allow your Mana to polarize the energy and fuel it. Once the preparation is finished, lightning-like strands of light energy will flow around you. This indicated you have successfully\n\ncalibrated yourself with the Void and established an affinity to Electrical Evocation.\n\nThough mentioned before, I want to remind you that the intense light and heat from this attack can significantly startle your opponent.\n\n       Application:\n    Chain Lightning\n               -=-\n\nBefore we move on, I need to reiterate and explain in detail regarding multiple charges. As you know by now, the Lightning Bolt attack is very similar to the actual lightning strike that happens in nature. Does this mean that this magic is capable of\n\nimitating the continuous strikes that occur in a natural lightning strike?\n\nThe answer is yes. Scholars call this ‘Chain Lightning’. By charging the magic multiple times, you are able to hurl this energy to your target back to back.\n\nHowever, there is a limit to how many times you can strike in a row. As previously mentioned, since you can charge up to about three bolts, yet the risk increases with each charge. One interesting fact is, these bolts of energy attract to metallic\n\nobjects. Thus when in the vicinity of metal objects such as the bands of a barrel, swords and armor, iron grating, and other Ferrum or Arcarum apparel, the bolt will change it’s path of perjectory and curve to meet said object.\n\n  Continuous Attack\n    Between Lightning\n         Bolt Users\n               -=-\n\nWe looked at the principle behind the Lightning Bolt, the Chain Lightning attack, and the application of these attacks in the previous chapter. If you think you've mastered everything through these two attacks, you still have a lot to learn from mother\n\nnature. If anyone has studied lightning strikes carefully, you know that lightning does not only occur in single, or multiple chain of strikes. Sometimes, lightning strikes attract other lightning and fuse together. Thus, where there are multiple\n\nLightning Bolt users, they can fuse each other's energy to form a collaborative attack.\n\nThis is a unique trait of Electric energy based magic that other ‘generic’ elemental based magic do not possess. When a user is charging his or her Lightning bolt\n\nattack, you can add on to their energy and instantly form a Chain Lightning attack. In other words, if you have charge your Lightning Bolt attack near another user that is charging, your charge will add to their charge, increasing their total number of\n\ncharges.\n\n        The Effects\n                of\n        Lightning Bolt\n               -=-\n\nSo, when is it a good time to use the Lightning Bolt attack? If you are wise, you should be able to figure it out yourself.\n\nFirst, the Lightning attack is good when facing multiple targets at once. Try it out yourself.\n\nUse skill of Electrical Evocation and attempt to fire off a bolt at a grouping of targets.. You will be amazed at how effective and convenient this property can prove to be.\n\nHowever, there is something you must remember: Chaining charges  inflicte damage which subsequently decreases with each hit. Beginners, unaware of this, might overuse the attack and find themselves out of Mana and getting attacked by the opponent.\n\nEven when you successfully use the Chain attack, the opponent might not have received as much damage as you think. Thus, the first target of the Chain Lightning Skill is very important. This is something every good wizard should be mindful.\n\nAuthor: Archmage Crumena V. Illwindior [HeeroZero]\n\nConsoliant: Electomancer Ceruberr Asul'Ailer [CaptainSheepy]\n\nCopier:\nGuildmaster Haadi Mubdee [Eladriendil]", ""),
        StaticBook("Enchanting Glyph", "§bLorien Vesitza", "[!]The book seems burnt badly, as if someone had tried to dispose of it in a fireplace, but then decided against it and pulled it out of the fire...too late, however, as half of it is burnt away[!]\n\nEnc__nt__ G-_ph\nF_r Unb__-k__-\n\n[]  []\n  []\n_--__---|__|]----___\n\n[!]The rest of the pages are burnt away[!]", ""),
        StaticBook("Sorcerer Types", "§bGerald Elgan", "===================                                                                                             Sorcerers: Recognizing the Various Varieties\n\nTranscribed by\nGerald Elgan\n\n==================\n\nAmongst those who possess distinctly closer bonds to the void, a few different kinds have been recognised. This short excerpt will attempt to bring the reader through these varied distinctions, and hopefully provide aid in attaining some small measure of\n\nenlightenment in the subject matter.\n===================Types outlined:\nThe Common Robed Mage: Page 4-7\nHousehold Mage:\nPage 8-13\nBattle Mage:\nPage 14-22\n===================\n\nThe Common Robed Mage\n==================\nThe typical conjurer, often accompanied by a staff- draped in long cloaks, adorned in all manner of strange runes and odd colours, is often depicted as an old man with long, grand beards and a vast\n\n\n==================\n-reservoir of knowledge esoteric. There is no doubt that this variety is the most renowned and favored. The Common Mage is often the most well-versed in the forbidden art of magical combat- with stronger spells and\n\n\n===================\n-hexes, and (depending on the proficiency of the caster) all the more perilous.\n\nHowever, with these strengths, come weaknesses, for the Common Mage is often by tradition, frail and\n\n\n===================-lacking in physical prowess- endurance, hardiness and such- due to the almost fanatical obsession that they so often possess with the search and acquisition of more knowledge, and in turn, more power of origins profane.\n\nHousehold Mage\n===================The common Mage's humbler counterpart of lesser renown, the Household Mage could very well be the lowly servant a small measure too efficient at the task of cleaning, or the unassuming cook who heats the furnace a moment-\n\n\n===================\n-too quickly.\n\nThe subtlety of the magic these beings possess and and put into practice makes them quite difficult to discern. One could, perhaps rightly, assume that such beings might hardly\n\n\n===================-pose any considerable amount of threat, due to their lack of experience and utility in the field of combat.\n\nHousehold Mages can be either shunned members of the secluded Mage's Guild\n\n\n===================-or apprentices who, having acknowledged their inferior ability or lack of talent, had decided to follow an entirely different path, and seek a livelihood in a different environment. \n\nTheir spells are most\n\n\n===================\n-often simple and pose little to no danger- these sorcerers are seen as more benevolent than the common Mage, and physically more robust; though such things are quite difficult to confirm, given the elusive and subtle-\n\n\n===================nature of the mages in question.\n(Witch Maid/Mage of Cleaning.)\n\nThe Battle Mage\n===================The rarest and least liked of the known types- The Battle Mage is seen as the bane of chivalry and honorable duels, with little care for learning more of their art. The Battle Mage is more often spotted in places where\n\n\n===================-war and conflict are as commonplace as frost on a cold winter's morn. He is often the result of a fighter who, through rigorous and excruciating training, has learnt both the way of the sword and the art of magic-\n\n\n===================-applying both theories and practices in harmonious synergy. The existence of this being suggests the existence of some clandestine groups or orders that have handpicked and set aside some select candidates for the\n\n\n===================-rigors required to achieve this balance.\n\nThe training of a Battle Mage is the stuff of tales and legends- often known to be one of, if not the most dangerous path to the art of the arcane. Apprentices that were\n\n\n===================-found to have been fit candidates were often heard to have died of various accidents-including but not limited to: Death by fire and cinders, of spell failures and eternal sleep, of ice and water, and of broken shards of\n\n\n===================-marble or glass. The fortunate few- if one could call them such- that did manage to live through the horrors of their training and ire of the common folk, quite often found themselves unable to resume the lives they one led, now bent\n\n\n===================-purely on suffering and death as heralds of doom and ruin.\n\nBattle Mages are often not as proficient in the art of magic as their more focused counterpart: the common Sorcerer. However, their skill-\n\n\n===================with and mastery of the blade may, at times, more than compensate for this lack of further magical prowess and potency. Hence, it would be most unwise for the regular mage to step within range of the Battle Mage, for the Battle-\n\n\n===================Mage, neither frail nor unfit, could quite as easily close the distance, and deal a devastating blow.\n(Warrior-Witch/ Spellblade/ Witchman /Mystic Knight. )", ""),
        StaticBook("Bardmancy", "§bOx Druid Popo", "The Art of Bardmancy is forming noises from the Void into the sound of words, and bringing it into our world. Through this act, the Bardmancer can influence (not force!) the emotions and feelings of those around him or her. It is important to note, while\n\nBardmancy is NOT in any way shape or form used for combat, it does require mana, as it requires a connection to the void. It should also be noted that an instrument can be enchanted with Bardmancy to amplify the Bard's magic, much like a staff to a mage.\n\nTo perform bardmancy, one must meditate while listening to any manner of music that involves words. Focus on how it makes you feel, what makes the sound, and how one moves their mouth in order to form these word. The next step is to connect to the Void.\n\nWhile Bardmancy does not require an awful lot of mana, connecting to the void regardless takes much time, and most of your time studying will be spent trying to connect to the void. Once you have said connection, the first thing one must to on the\n\nbeginning of their journey with Bardmancy is to make any noise come out of their mouth. Truly, any noise you can hear is suitable. Imagine drawing out from your mouth with an invisable hand. While this takes several tries to correctly perform,\n\nit does come easier with practice. The next step is to attempt to form this sound into a word. This is easier said than done, as it requires your mind to focus greatly on turning the sound into the sound of your very own voice.\n\nHowever, as you continue, connecting to the void and forming and singing the words, as well as putting emotions behind them, will become much easier. At the beginning, your sentances will have long pauses in them, taking a lot of concentration, and may\n\neven force you to quit your song lest you pass out due to using up your mana. However, as Bardmancy is not /too/ complex, it does not require much mana once you learn how to bring the words into our plane of existance. However, any Bardmancer, no matter\n\ntheir skill, can sing forever.", ""),
        StaticBook("Telekinesis Prog", "§bGallidron Elindrow", "Telekinesis§0\n§0  A Guide to Progress§0\n§0\n§0\n§0             By§0\n§0\n§0   Gallidron Elindrow§0\n§0\n§0\n§0   8th of Amber Cold,§0\n§0           1613\n\nTelekinesis, as a voidal magic, progresses in a not dissimilar fashion to other voidal magics. One starts their journey as an apprentice, struggling to accomplish even minor feats.\n\nEventually, however, skill is acquired and more and more can be drawn forth from the void.\n\nThis guide is to help the prospective student, the new caster or even the fledgling instructor to create a framework for safe and reliable progress. In essence a lesson plan.\n\nTo begin we shall simplify this progression by creating an arbitrary ranking system. We’ll say that a novice is rank 1 and a master is rank 5. Ideally as a teacher one should be a master of the magic being taught and telekinesis is no different.\n\nA rank 1 student will begin by being unable to do much at all. I was only able to barely move a feather on my first attempt and I had already mastered Transmutation before taking up Telekinesis.§0\n\n (It should help to note that if a student takes up Transmutation before other forms of magic, it tends to deepen their mana reserves.)\n\nAfter some practice however a rank 1 telekinetic will be able to reliably manipulate up to 4 or 5 small objects, each weighing no more than half a kilogram, or 1.1 pounds each.\n\nBe careful though as their focus can be easily broken. This progression should last 1 or 2 elven weeks.\n\nMoving on to a rank 2 telekinetic, the student can begin to test themselves against more difficult tasks. Whether they focus on moving more items at once or simply on heavier items is up to you the teacher,\n\nbut I would not ask them to lift anything heavier than 12 kilograms, or 26.5 pounds, at once. The strain may prove impossible for them and set them up for failure.\n\nThis progression should last about 2 or 3 elven weeks, making the student ready to begin rank 3 training after about an elven month of total training.\n\nRank 3 will see the student’s still increase in very noticeable ways. Their speed and dexterity with the objects manipulated will show a great deal more prowess.\n\nItems of slightly weightier can be lifted though I would only expect them to lift up to 50 kilograms, 110.2 lbs., reliably.\n\nHeavier objects can be moved, though with some difficulty I would wager. This progression usually lasts around 1 or two elven months.\n\nAs a Rank 4 telekinetic your student will truly begin to come into their own. They are less an apprentice at this stage, than a mage who is honing a hard won skill.\n\nMuch of their training can now be performed on their own, with only the odd bit of wisdom or bit of advice coming from you, their once all important teacher.\n\nUpon reaching rank 4 I was able to lift 80 kilograms or so, 176.4 pounds, with only a moderate amount of focus. The telekinetic’s skill will greatly increase, should they practice, over the next 2 elven months.\n\nIt is not uncommon to see them jump from 80 kilograms to around 200 kilograms of movable weight in that time.\n\nRank 5 is the rank of the master. Your skill and your knowledge are truly formidable and you know well the uses of this arcane art.\n\nAround 300 kilograms, 661.4 pounds of weight is the average for new telekinetic masters but this will increase as you ply your craft.\n\nA rank 5 who uses their skill regularly can expect to see a 5 kilogram, 11 pounds, per elven week increase in their weight maximum.\n\nIt is important to note that throughout these progressions, a telekinetic will find their ability to focus, even through numerous and raucous interruptions, increase.\n\nOn the average, about 1/5 of a mage’s total weight limit can be manipulated with true speed and dexterity, and 1/10 of their maximum weight can be manipulated with dazzling speed and prowess once one has achieved real skill.\n\nI hope this book has been of use to you the reader and I encourage you to always seek to improve your knowledge.§0\n§0 §0\n§0-Gallidron Elindrow§0\n\nEasy Reference Guide.§0\n§0\n§0Rank 1: up to five objects each weighing no more than ½ kg. 1-2 weeks§0\n§0\n§0Rank 2: 12kg. 2-3 weeks§0\n§0\n§0Rank 3: 50kg. 1-2 months\n\nRank 4: Progression from 80kg to 200kg. 2 months§0\n§0\n§0Rank 5: 300kg starting. 5kg gain each week§0", ""),
        StaticBook("Arcane Puppetry", "§bNienna Calm", "Arcane\n     Puppetry\n            \n            O\n           -|-\n            ^\n           ***\n\n\n\n\n\nBy: Nienna Calm\n\n    A Foreword\n-------------------Dear Reader,\nMagic is an incredible thing, a wonderous art form through which the imaginary becomes real. However, there are many who would rather only use it as a tool to advance their own power. They are only-\n\n-Half right. Magic, especially Void magic, is indeed a tool to be used in the same way a carpenter would use a saw, but no true power will ever come of it. We live in a world where the mage is distrusted in every corner of the globe. We are Discriminated,\n\n-hated for our unique persepective on reality. It is a touched life we walk; Do not embark on this trek lightly.\n\n             O\n            -|-\n             ^\n\n      What Is\nArcane Puppetry?\n-------------------*Everything on the topic is listed here*\n\n((Link: https://goo.gl/9qEdyA ))\n\n*You should definitely read this to learn the magic*\n\n   Creating Your\n  Arcane Puppet\n-------------------A properly made Arcane Puppet is a complicated piece of machinery, on par with a Golem or a Obelisk in complexity. In order for a puppet to function, every piece of the Marionette's makeup\n\nis specially enchanted with telekinetic mechanisms that allow it to move of its own accord. This process might seem straightforward at first, but as the puppet becomes more lifelike, hundreds of small enchantments will end up overlapping and at times\n\nconflicting with one another just to operate simple movements. More than ten enchantments may be working in unison for an arcane puppet to simply walk on a flat plane.\n\nNaturally, because of this complex design, only a skilled Enchanter\n\ncan create a puppet for you. If you know the magic yourself already, well, this portion of learning just got easier for you. If not, expect to be indebted to one handsomely quite soon.\n\nHere is a list of what Enchantments Any Arcane puppet Should Have:\n\n@-A telekinetic Enchantment for every component of the puppet.\n\n@-A special visual enchantment that allows one to see(*)\n\n@-(Optional) An illusionary enchant to allow one to speak through the puppet.\n\n@- An overall Alterational modification to make the Clay puppet light enough to use.\n\n(*)- Creating the visual enchantment can be achieved through a cursory study of Soul Puppetry. Since the heart of Arcane puppetry is the connection of the soul to an enchanted puppet, some special enchantments like this one can be preformed, where\n\notherwise it would be impossible for a void mage to enchant such.\n\n Connecting to the\n  Arcane Puppet\n-------------------\n\nAs with all arcane magic, first, connect your mind to the void.\n\nThrough the void, connect to your Arcane puppet, and imagine yourself inside of it. Imagine that you-\n\nyourself /are/ the puppet, with a whole seperate mind, body, and life. Through this method, one is able to connect to a completed Arcane Puppet.", ""),
        StaticBook("ELECTROMANCY", "§bonm224", "ELECTROMANCY\n         GUIDE\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBy: Richard Tarus\nAdapted: Amras Lúinwë\n\nWhat is electric evocation?\nElectrical Evocation is the arcane magic form whereby a mage creates Electrical bolts for his or her purposes. Unlike other types of Evocation electrical bolts is intended to be much more difficult to control due to its\n\nspeed and high energy nature. Mages who specialize in electrical evocation will be able to form electrical bolts and streams of electricity to electrocute their targets.However, electrical evocation requires much more energy than for example\n\nfire and therefore a single electrical bolt requires much more energy than a fireball. This however is made up for by the fact the lightning bolt is somewhat equal in power to the two fireballs which could be made in the same time period.\n\nStrength\nAn electrical evocationist is capable of producing powerful electrical bolts\nBolts may travel from any point to point with speed and reliable accuracy.\nWeakneses\nElectrical evocation takes a greater amount of time to produce a bolt\n\nthan other types of evocation\nElectrical evocation is dangerous to learn and during some phases of learning the mage can be incapable of producing any spark which will not injure themselves.\n\nProcess\nReaching Tier 1:\nWhen you begin to learn magic, you will start a Tier 1. At that point in time, you are unable to cast any form of magic unless you have some sort of experience and have connected to the void.At this tier you will learn\n\nhow to connect to the void, and then research electricity as an element itself. Connecting to the void will take perhaps even years of practice, and once completed,you may still have some difficulty. Researching electricity would consist of practically\n\nfinding any sort of detailand then taking note of it.\nReaching Tier 2: \nAfter their advancement from tier 1, and their connection to the void, they can now create a multitude of sparks and perhaps an even minor bolt,albeit difficult to\n\ncontrol till towards the end of Tier 2.  Even though they can create sparks, users at this tier may have an overload in which the sparks will practically burst outwards towards the conjurer.In this tier they will need heavy loads of practice in order\n\nto become more proficient with a minor bolt.\nReaching Tier 3:\nIn this tier they can finally create a full powered bolt withought major troubles, and have full proficiency. At this point they can now create streams of electricity,\n\nthough they will simply spark and singethe target, rather then cause major damage. With this tier, they are novice mages. \nReaching Tier 4:\nNow, the electrical evocation users can create the \"call-down\"of electricity, aka a bolt from a high point\n\nin the sky. They can create multiple bolts in a shorter time, although a fire evocationist could create fireballs in an even shorter time.The call-down is not very proficient, and will rarely land on the user's target.  In this tier,they can create great\n\nstreams of electricity that will do some good damage (burns, etc.) They are very proficient with the bolts now.\n\n For more information go to this link, there is the same info + RP guide:\nhttp://www.lordofthecraft.net/topic/88498-improved-electricity-guide/", ""),
        StaticBook("Electric Evo.", "§bHaadi Mubdee", "=--=--=--=--=--=--=            Electrical\n           Evocation\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=\n\n\n     -< ^v^v^v^v^\n\nThere is an old saying that humans (mortals) fear most other humans. I believe, however, that those who say that have never looked outside the confines of the human world. If anyone has come face to face with the power of nature, they will consider the\n\nprevious proverb a joke. If this sounds like nothing more than nonsense coming from an old fool, then answer this next question. If you can possess any form of power, what would you choose? Would you choose the power of mankind, or that of a wild beast?\n\nWould it be the roars of powerful men, or the sound of thunder that shakes the heavens? Is it the limited life of men, or nature's continuous cycle of life? Whichever way you look at it, the power of man is feeble compared to the grandeur of nature.\n\nThroughout history, all of mankind has admired nature's power and though they had feared it, they tried to deny that. This is one of man's many follies. They neglect nature's power, which does not discriminate, solely because they are a part of nature.\n\nIn particular, as humans learned of Mana and started seeing a glimpse of nature's other realm, they began neglecting nature's power even more. They didn't realize that their \"magic\" was actually an imitation of nature's power. This is what worries me.\n\nThere are many students who are Wizards and Druids in training who do not understand that every magic's starting point is a natural phenomenon. I, who also boasted in my knowledge of Electric magic , did not learn this until I saw lightning strike down at\n\nthe Crown Lands Plateau of Oren. In hopes that those who come after me will not make the same mistake, I decided to write this book. In other words, I hope that beginner wizards will learn the laws of nature and have a healthy fear of it as well.\n\nAlthough this book only deals with lightning power, which is only one of nature's awesome power, if you are wise, you will take the moral of the story to heart and apply it when training other magic as well.\n\nThe Principle of Electricity series Magic Skill\n                -=-\n\nIn order to understand the principle of Electric magic, you must first learn its natural counterpart: Lightning. If you've seen anyone use Electric-based magic, you probably noticed a close resemblance to a lightning strike. The lightning strike occurs\n\nwhen Mana, the energy that surrounds us, splits into two polarities of energy for reasons unknown. These split polarities each continue to accumulate energy, when, in an instant, an explosion of energy flow occurs between heaven and earth.\n\nThe intensity of the light and heat during this process contains tremendous power. Also, because of its unpredictability of where it strikes, it has been an object of fear to mankind. There are many opinions as to when and why Mana, splits into two\n\npolarities of energy. However, before anyone could come up with any concrete evidence, the debate's focus shifted because of the ‘great’ Wizard Ambrosia, who at times lacked common sense. He claimed that he was able to stabilize his Mana to split the two\n\npolar energies in a controlled manner, which caused all the scholars to shift their focus from the conditions in which the energy splits to the process of it. (It is a shame that scholars today are not interested in insightfully studying natural\n\nphenomena, and rather just want to uncover it just for the sake of using it. This is quite foolish.) Once Mana is split into two polar energies, each polarity begins accumulating energy at a rapid rate and causes an electric discharge. Then, when the\n\nbalance of energy between the two polarities is broken, a transfer of energy occurs from the one with more energy to the other. So imagine what would happen if one could embody either of the polarities and designate his or her target as the other\n\npolarity. Then, using Mana, if he or she polarizes the Mana and also raises his/her own energy level to be higher than their target, what would happen?\nExactly. This is what is known as Electrical Energy Magic.\n\nHow to use the Lightning Bolt\n                -=-\n\nThe Lightning Bolt attack is a magic that uses the mechanic of creating static electricity, which in turn utilizes the characteristics of the negative charge for a sudden electrostatic discharge. During the energy transfusion, a bright explosion\n\nresembling a lightning strike, rushes toward your target as Mana is depleted into replicating this phenomenon. Once all the Mana is depleted from the bolt and the surrounding elements reach equilibrium, the flow of energy ends. (Just like a lightning\n\nstrike in nature.) There are many questions surrounding this magic, such as \"How powerful is it?\" and \"How easy is it to use?\", which is technically asking how much energy it needs and how long it takes. To give you an answer, there is a limit to how much\n\nenergy you can gather, and you can only maintain the energy for about 15 seconds until later coming to a greater grasp of the art. You can charge your energy multiple times to gather more energy, but it is reported that it can become highly unstable after\n\nafter the third charge which is already risky enough. Also, you only have about 15 seconds to charge all your energy. This is because of several factors, such as the difficulty of maintaining the Mana's focus, and also maintaining the polarized energy at\n\nthe same time. (Even if you have plenty of Mana, it is difficult to charge for an extensive electrical bolt). The ‘Lightning Bolt’ attack is used to strike your enemy with electricity . Once your opponent is struck with this attack, they will be in a\n\nstate of shock due to the electricity surging within them as it an energy. Not a tangible object. Since this attack is based on an instant energy explosion between you and your opponent, the lesser the energy gap between you and your opponent, the great\n\ndamage it will inflict as the longer the distance the surge of electricity must travel, the more it discharges. Wasting precious Mana in the long run.\n\n              Simple\n              Usage\n                -=-\n\nOnce you've carefully read through this book, and understood the principles behind the Lightning Bolt attack and are ready to use it, you can do so by following these simple steps. First, a connection to the Void is a must. As any form of magic. The\n\nsimplest manner in which to begin evoking such an element is to picture the empty black abyss that is the Void. A sleet of pure nothingness. Void of any life as the name suggests. Now one must mentally picture lightning crashing down upon the Earth. But\n\nstrictly the lighting. If one were to picture the scene, one could possibly border the realm of Pseudomancy (Illusionary magic) or if too much detail is placed on the Earth, soil or stone may be produced. Then you will see the static discharge enivision\n\nsuch an occurring unfolding before your palm you calm your mind to allow your Mana to polarize the energy and fuel it. Once the preparation is finished, lightning-like strands of light energy will flow around you. This indicated you have successfully\n\ncalibrated yourself with the Void and established an affinity to Electrical Evocation.\n\nThough mentioned before, I want to remind you that the intense light and heat from this attack can significantly startle your opponent.\n\n       Application:\n    Chain Lightning\n               -=-\n\nBefore we move on, I need to reiterate and explain in detail regarding multiple charges. As you know by now, the Lightning Bolt attack is very similar to the actual lightning strike that happens in nature. Does this mean that this magic is capable of\n\nimitating the continuous strikes that occur in a natural lightning strike?\n\nThe answer is yes. Scholars call this ‘Chain Lightning’. By charging the magic multiple times, you are able to hurl this energy to your target back to back.\n\nHowever, there is a limit to how many times you can strike in a row. As previously mentioned, since you can charge up to about three bolts, yet the risk increases with each charge. One interesting fact is, these bolts of energy attract to metallic\n\nobjects. Thus when in the vicinity of metal objects such as the bands of a barrel, swords and armor, iron grating, and other Ferrum or Arcarum apparel, the bolt will change it’s path of perjectory and curve to meet said object.\n\n  Continuous Attack\n    Between Lightning\n         Bolt Users\n               -=-\n\nWe looked at the principle behind the Lightning Bolt, the Chain Lightning attack, and the application of these attacks in the previous chapter. If you think you've mastered everything through these two attacks, you still have a lot to learn from mother\n\nnature. If anyone has studied lightning strikes carefully, you know that lightning does not only occur in single, or multiple chain of strikes. Sometimes, lightning strikes attract other lightning and fuse together. Thus, where there are multiple\n\nLightning Bolt users, they can fuse each other's energy to form a collaborative attack.\n\nThis is a unique trait of Electric energy based magic that other ‘generic’ elemental based magic do not possess. When a user is charging his or her Lightning bol\n\nattack, you can add on to their energy and instantly form a Chain Lightning attack. In other words, if you have charge your Lightning Bolt attack near another user that is charging, your charge will add to their charge, increasing their total number of\n\ncharges.\n\n        The Effects\n                of\n        Lightning Bolt\n               -=-\n\nSo, when is it a good time to use the Lightning Bolt attack? If you are wise, you should be able to figure it out yourself.\n\nFirst, the Lightning attack is good when facing multiple targets at once. Try it out yourself.\n\nUse skill of Electrical Evocation and attempt to fire off a bolt at a grouping of targets.. You will be amazed at how effective and convenient this property can prove to be.\n\nHowever, there is something you must remember: Chaining charges  inflicte damage which subsequently decreases with each hit. Beginners, unaware of this, might overuse the attack and find themselves out of Mana and getting attacked by the opponent.\n\nEven when you successfully use the Chain attack, the opponent might not have received as much damage as you think. Thus, the first target of the Chain Lightning Skill is very important. This is something every good wizard should be mindful.\n\nAuthor: Archmage Crumena V. Illwindior [HeeroZero]\n\nConsoliant: Electomancer Ceruberr Asul'Ailer [CaptainSheepy]\n\nCopier:\nGuildmaster Haadi Mubdee [Eladriendil]", ""),
        StaticBook("L.R. Trans.", "§bKirrekith", "Long Range\nTranslocation Device\nInvestigation.\n\nFrom what I can gather from\nArchmage Haadi the long range translocator is developed through a use of 'Void Translocation' magics which establish small pockers within the\n\n- Enchanter. The void translocationist must take a object and place it within the\nvoid, and then a enchanter must co-operate in keeping such a object within the void without the\noriginal void translocationist present. The void translocationish must\n--->\n\n- then re-locate himself and in the\narea he wishes to designate the other opening pull forth the object from the void again, which the Enchanter had held there. This enables a hole to open between the two locations and stays open when powered.\n--->\n\nWrote by Lori Oathcast and re-wrote by\nKirrekith Reffahc\non the 8th of the\nDeep Cold, 1532.", ""),
        StaticBook("ELECTROMANCY", "§bonm224", "ELECTROMANCY\n         GUIDE\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBy: Richard Tarus\nAdapted: Amras Lúinwë\n\nWhat is electric evocation?\nElectrical Evocation is the arcane magic form whereby a mage creates Electrical bolts for his or her purposes. Unlike other types of Evocation electrical bolts is intended to be much more difficult to control due to its\n\nspeed and high energy nature. Mages who specialize in electrical evocation will be able to form electrical bolts and streams of electricity to electrocute their targets.However, electrical evocation requires much more energy than for example\n\nfire and therefore a single electrical bolt requires much more energy than a fireball. This however is made up for by the fact the lightning bolt is somewhat equal in power to the two fireballs which could be made in the same time period.\n\nStrength\nAn electrical evocationist is capable of producing powerful electrical bolts\nBolts may travel from any point to point with speed and reliable accuracy.\nWeakneses\nElectrical evocation takes a greater amount of time to produce a bo\n\nthan other types of evocation\nElectrical evocation is dangerous to learn and during some phases of learning the mage can be incapable of producing any spark which will not injure themselves.\n\nProcess\nReaching Tier 1:\nWhen you begin to learn magic, you will start a Tier 1. At that point in time, you are unable to cast any form of magic unless you have some sort of experience and have connected to the void.At this tier you will lear\n\nhow to connect to the void, and then research electricity as an element itself. Connecting to the void will take perhaps even years of practice, and once completed,you may still have some difficulty. Researching electricity would consist of practically\n\nfinding any sort of detailand then taking note of it.\nReaching Tier 2: \nAfter their advancement from tier 1, and their connection to the void, they can now create a multitude of sparks and perhaps an even minor bolt,albeit difficult to\n\ncontrol till towards the end of Tier 2.  Even though they can create sparks, users at this tier may have an overload in which the sparks will practically burst outwards towards the conjurer.In this tier they will need heavy loads of practice in order\n\nto become more proficient with a minor bolt.\nReaching Tier 3:\nIn this tier they can finally create a full powered bolt withought major troubles, and have full proficiency. At this point they can now create streams of electricity,\n\nthough they will simply spark and singethe target, rather then cause major damage. With this tier, they are novice mages. \nReaching Tier 4:\nNow, the electrical evocation users can create the \"call-down\"of electricity, aka a bolt from a high point\n\nin the sky. They can create multiple bolts in a shorter time, although a fire evocationist could create fireballs in an even shorter time.The call-down is not very proficient, and will rarely land on the user's target.  In this tier,they can create gre\n\nstreams of electricity that will do some good damage (burns, etc.) They are very proficient with the bolts now.\n\n For more information go to this link, there is the same info + RP guide:\nhttp://www.lordofthecraft.net/topic/88498-improved-electricity-guide/", ""),
        StaticBook("Sorcerers", "§b[Ghost] The Eternal Librarian", "Sorcerers:\n  Recognizing the\n Various Varieties\n\n  by Arther Versluis\n\nAmoungst those who possess distinctly closer bonds to the void, a few different kinds have been recognized. This short excerpt will attempt to bring the reader through these distinctions, and hopefully provide aid in attaining some small measure of\n\nenlightment in the subject matter.\n\nThe Common Robed Mage:\nSorcer(ess) - Magician - Conjurer - Wizard - Robe\n\nThe typical conjurer, often accompanied by a staff and draped in long cloaks, adorned in all manner of strange markings, runes and often odd colours, is\n\noften depicted as an old man with a long, thick beard, and possessing a vast reservoir of knowledge esoteric.\n\nThere is no doubt that this variety is the most renown and favoured. The common Mage is often most well-versed in the forbidden art\n\nof magical combat- with more powerful spells and hexes, and, depending on the proficiency of the caster, all the more perilous.\n\nHowever, with these strengths come weaknesses, for the common Mage is often, by tradition, frail and\n\nlacking in physical prowess, endurance, hardiness and such due to the almost fanatical obsession that such ilk often possess with the endless search and acquisition of more knowledge, and in turn, more power of origins profane.\n\nThe Household Mage\nWitch Maid - Hearth Mage\n\nThe common Mage’s humbler counterpart or lesser renown, the Household Mage could very well be the lowly servant of a small measure too efficient at the crude task of\n\nsweeping the kitchen floors, or even the unassuming cook who heats the oven furnace a moment too quickly.\n\nThe subtlety of the magic these beings possess and put into practice makes them quite difficult to discern to those\n\nwithout the knack for spotting such things. One could, perhaps rightly, assume that such beings might hardly pose any considerable amount of threat, due to their lack of experience and utility in the field of combat.\n\nHousehold Mages can be either shunned members of the secluded Mage’s Guild or former, disgraced apprentices who, having in the past acknowledged their inferior ability or lack of talents, had decided to follow an entirely different path, seeking a\n\nlivelihood in a different environment.\n\nTheir spells are most often simplistic in nature and effect, and pose very little danger- if any at all. These mages are seen as more benevolent than the common Mage, and physically more robust; though such\n\nthings are quite difficult to confirm, given the elusive and subtle nature of the mages in question.\n\nThe Battle Mage\nWarrior-Witch - Spellblade - Witchman - Mystic Knight\n\nThe rarest and least liked of the known types. The Battle Mage is seen as the bane of chivalry and honourable duels, with little regard for learning more of this\n\nart. The Battle Mage is most often spotted in places where war and conflict are commonplace as frost on a cold winter’s morn. He is often the result of a fighter who, through rigorous and excruciating training, had learnt both the way of the blade and the\n\nart of the Arcane- applying both theories and practices in harmonious synergy. The existence of such beings suggests existence of some clandestine groups or knightly orders that had handpicked and set aside some select candidates to undertake the rigors\n\nrequired to achieve this balance of spell and blade.\n\nThe training of a would-be Battle Mage is the stuff of tales and legends- often known to be one of- if not THE most dangerous path to the art of magic. Apprentices that had been found and\n\ndeemed fit candidates were often rumoured to have died of various accidents- including , but not limited to: Death by fire and cinders, of spell mishaps and eternal sleep, of ice and water, and of broken shards of marble and glass. The fortunate few - if\n\none could even call them as such- that did somehow manage to live through the horrors of their training, and the ire and malice of the common folk, quite often found themselves unable to resume the lives they had once led, some distant time ago, now bent\n\npurely on suffering and death as heralds of doom and ruin.\n\nBattle Mages are often not as proficient in the art of magic as their more focused counterpart: the common Sorcerer. However, their skill with and mastery of the sword may, at times,\n\nmore than compensate for this lack of further magical prowess and potency. Hence, it would be most unwise for the regular mage to step within range of the Battle Mage, for the Battle Mage, neither frail nor unfit, could quite easily close the distance,\n\nand deal a devastating blow.", ""),
        StaticBook("Laws of MotionII", "§bLorolith Iira", "Laws of Motion §0Vol.II§0\n§0-----=====+=====-----§0\n§0\n§0By Lorolith Iira\n\nIn this second volume I will be drawing the conclusions of our observations and experiment from volume one. §0\n§0\n§0If you have not already read through the first volume of this work, it is very important that you do in order to understand the ideas here.\n\nContents:§0\n§0\n§01) Conclusions from further experimentation.§0\n§0\n§02) Useful descriptions of motion.§0\n§0\n§03) Formal Laws of Motion.\n\nChapter 1§0\n§0-----==========-----§0\n§0\n§0Conclusions from Further Experimentation§0\n§0\n\nIn the last chapter of the previous volume we experimentally explored the relationships between quantities such as force and acceleration and mass and acceleration. §0\n§0\n§0Our results demonstrated an inverse proportionality between the mass of an object\n\nand the acceleration resulting from an applied force. Simply, the more 'stuff' there is in an object, the more that object will resist a force and therefore resist acceleration. §0\n§0\n§0It also demonstrated a proportionality between the net force on an object\n\nand the acceleration of that object. We must consider this the 'net' force because our previous experiment aimed at eradicating all resistive forces (one should be careful in the construction of their wooden trolley- a skillful tinkerer would be best for\n\nthe construction of almost-frictionless wheel components). Also because force is a quantity (like velocity) that specifies a direction, so any resulting acceleration *must* come from the net force or after accounting for any resistive forces. §0\n§0\n§0We could\n\ntherefore write a very simple expression for this reliationship:§0\n§0\n§0a=k(f/m)§0\n§0\n§0where a=acceleration,§0\n§0f=net force, m=mass and k= the constant of proportionality. §0\n§0\n§0We could also write this as:\n\nF=kma§0\n§0\n§0to show us the expression for the net force acting on any object.§0\n§0\n§0To make this equation much more sensibly appealing, we could remove that constant 'k' by defining a unit of force (as is not yet defined)\n\nas that force which is required to accelerate one unit of mass to one unit of acceleration. In my case (having used the standard metre and kilogram units), it would be the force required to accelerate one kilogram of mass to one meter per second squared.\n\nHowever, if one wanted to use grams and inches, you could. But always defining this force unit as accelerating one mass unit to one acceleration unit will make this constant 'k', 1. Meaning it no longer needs to be in the equation. §0\n§0We can now make\n\nprecise measurments of 'force unit's or even predict the acceleration of objects given the applied forces and mass. §0\n§0\n§0I would go further to point out the equivalence between the idea that force is proportional to the product of mass and acceleration and\n\nthe idea that force is also proprtional to the rate of change of momenta. §0\n§0\n§0Since a=dv/dt (as defined in volume one), we could consider:§0\n§0\n§0F=m*dv/dt,§0\n§0\n§0assuming 'k' to be removed.\n\nSince 'dv' (or, change in velocity) could also be expressed using:§0\n§0\n§0dv=u-v §0\n§0\n§0(where u=initial velocity and v=final velocity), §0\n§0\n§0it would be right to say:§0\n§0\n§0F=m(u-v)/t.\n\nIt would then also be right to say:§0\n§0\n§0F=mu-mv/t.§0\n§0\n§0'Mv' (or 'mu', for that matter) represents the known quantitiy of 'momenta'. This (the product of mass and velocity) describes the 'quantitiy of motion of an object'. More mass in motion means a greater\n\nquantity of motion and a higher velocity, the same. This final equation would overall describe the translation of force for an object moving at a constant velocity and could also be used to predict the behaviour of objects that change in their mass (for\n\nexample, an open bottle of water that is thrown). It would also explain why we experience less force upon bending our knees upon impact with the ground. Doing so increases the time that the momenta of our bodies is changed over, reducing the force.\n\nChapter 2§0\n§0-----==========-----§0\n§0\n§0Useful Descriptions of Motion\n\nSince we have come to understand the causes of motion in the universe, we could even try our hand at complex and detailed explainations of the behaviour of physical objects. The ideas expressed in the chapter 'first principles' of the first volume contain\n\nall we need to know about simple motion to describe the motions of objects in straight lines. We might similarly develop these ideas to describe motions along a flat plane if we can use trigonometric proportions to treat these motions as independant,\n\nstraight-line motions. I will perhaps go into this kind of description of motion in further detail in furture volumes. This hinges, however, on being able to measure the acceleration of the ground force. I have measured this to be around 10ms^-1, using\n\nthe equation:§0\n§0\n§0a=2s/t^2.§0\n§0\n§0The concepts that have been better concreted in this volume can also be used to describe the motions and forces in many (now simple) situations such as in objects moving along uniform circular paths and in paths where multiple\n\nobjects may cross paths and interact, applying forces to one another.\n\nChapter 3§0\n§0-----==========-----§0\n§0\n§0Formal Laws of Motion\n\nBefore truley formally stating these laws (most of which I have previously discussed in a very round-about way), I would like to mention one thing that comes implied by the ideas expressed in this volume.§0\n§0\n§0When two objects interact (a brick wall and arrow\n\n, for example, where one is massive and more-or-less stationary and the other is much lighter yet in motion), we'll often see objects come to dead stop. Or even when objects come to stop due to resistive forces of fluids like air or water (as discussed in\n\nvolume I). This could only happen if it were that another force were applied to the object in motion to bring its acceleration to zero. If there was no force opposing whatever driving force is behind it (in the case of coming to a stop whilst trying to\n\nwalk through water), then the object would continue to accelerate rather than be stopped. Similarly, it may even drive the obstacle out of its way. §0\n§0\n§0We must, then, posit the existence of theoretical forces that come in reaction to the interactions\n\nbetween physical objects. If you are pushing onto a brick wall, the only reason you do not accelerate through that wall is because it is also applying an opposing, equal force. Though, if you interact with and push an object that *does* move out of the\n\nway, this is because the object has a low enough mass to gain a significant enough acceleration from the force you have applied. §0\n§0This is why, sitting in a boat, shoving a ledge will actually move *you*, rather than the ledge. The reaction force from the\n\nledge is applied to you and the water that you then push against doesn't have enough mass to keep you in place (essentially). §0\n§0\n§0This, of course, must be included in our laws.\n\nChapter 3§0\n§0-----==========-----§0\n§0\n§0Formal Laws of Motion\n\nI§0\n§0\n§0The natural state of any object is constant motion. An object will stay in this constant motion until some kind of external force acts upon it.\n\nII§0\n§0\n§0The force of an object is proportional to the rate of change of momenta of that object. Or, the net force of an object is proportional to its acceleration and is inversely proportional to the object's mass.§0\n§0\n§0F=mu-mv/t§0\n§0F=ma\n\nIII§0\n§0\n§0The object applying a force will become subject to an equal (in magnitude) and opposing (in direction) reactionary force.\n\nConclusion§0\n§0-----==========-----\n\nThe information shared within these pages only describe the very basics of the mathematical uniformity of our universe. There is much more work to be done in implementing these laws to describe the simple workings of our universe. We can use them to\n\npredict and understand. What I have described is only the most simple expression of these ideas that can be built upon. If further inquiery is required regarding my own methodology or any of the more nuanced concepts involved in these works, I can be\n\ncontacted directly.", ""),
        StaticBook("Laws of Motion I", "§bLorolith Iira", "Laws of Motion Vol.1§0\n§0-----=====+=====-----§0\n§0\n§0By Lorolith Iira§0\n§0\n\nIn my previous work, titled ‘On Eternal Laws’, I spend the majority of my time explaining the necessity of physical laws in the past (as many philosophers have done before me) and even explained how the idea of mana links with the concept of ‘energy’ and\n\nhow it all ties into the motion and interaction of physical objects. §0\n§0 §0\n§0Briefly, at the end, I discussed the possible existence and form of motional laws. In this work, I aim to explain these laws in greater detail, as I have recognized them.§0\n\nThis first volume will cover basic observation and experiments that we can use to understand simple axioms relating to nature. Then I will move on to describing more complex experimentation aimed at explaining the relationships between different physical\n\nquantities before drawing these ideas together into rational laws in the next volume.§0\n§0 §0\n§0Contents:§0\n§0 §0\n§01) First Principles of Motion§0\n§02) Development of Simple Axioms§0\n§03) Further Experimentation§0\n\nChapter 1§0\n§0-----==========-----§0\n§0\n§0First Principles of Motion\n\nBefore we start discussion motion through space, we should first discuss very simple mathematical descriptions of everyday quantities. This is the (simple) mathematics of change with time.§0\n§0\n§0During this section (and moving forward) we will be using the\n\nDistance unit of ‘1 metre’ as our standard. This measurement is also standardized in construction industries and is used to measure quantities in the productions of masons, woodworkers and builders alike. You should be familiar with this unit. §0\n§0\n§0Similarly\n\n,our standard unit of time will be the ‘second’, as measured by the earliest watch inventors to occur 86,400 times in one day. A painstaking task, I’m sure. As a result, this is now the standard, minimum unit of measurement on most mechanical watches,\n\nClocks and timing devices.§0\n§0\n§0-One familiar with simple mathematical descriptions of change may want to only skim over this chapter. The more complex explanations of these ideas will essentially be left out.\n\nSpeed:§0\n§0 §0\n§0This quantity is intuitively understood to be the ‘distance covered per defined unit of time’. Or the ‘rate of change of distance’. The ‘distance’ is the length (in metres) of the path of the object is moving along. §0\n\nIt can also be understood with the following expression, simply:§0\n§0 §0\n§0s=x/t§0\n§0 §0\n§0Where s=speed, x=distance and t=time. §0\n§0 §0\n§0Although I won’t be using this quantity very much, it will aid in an understanding of the next.§0\n\nVelocity:§0\n§0 §0\n§0This, like speed, is a measure of the rate of change of a length measurement. Except, instead of ‘distance’, this uses ‘displacement’. This will be a new concept to many.§0\n§0\n\nWhereas ‘distance’ is the length of a path of motion of an object, the ‘displacement’ is the length of the line that connects the object and its starting position.§0\n§0\n§0Velocity also defines a direction. Meaning that, when you aim to review the velocity of an\n\nobject, you must define the ‘positive’ direction. Movement in the opposite direction is considered ‘negative’ velocity.§0\n§0\n§0v=ds/dt§0\n§0 §0\n§0Where v=velocity, s=displacement, t=time and d=’change in’.§0\n\nAcceleration:§0\n§0 §0\n§0Like velocity is related to displacement as it changes with time, ‘acceleration’ is related to velocity as the change in velocity with time. As a result, acceleration also expresses a direction and there can be negative acceleration.§0\n\nThis represents nothing other than the ‘speeding up’ or ‘slowing down’ of an object.§0\n§0 §0\n§0a=dv/dt§0\n§0 §0\n§0where=acceleration, v=velocity, t=time and d=’change in’.§0\n\nChapter 2§0\n§0-----==========-----§0\n§0\n§0Development of Simple §0\n§0Axioms\n\nIn this chapter I will discuss a handful of conclusions that can be made from simple observation of the natural world and some not-so complex experimentation. I hope that the reader can use these axioms a foothold to understand ideas expressed later in\n\nthese works (as I did in conducting these experiments).§0\n§0 §0\n§0I would first like to note that, upon close examination of the nature of falling objects, one can in fact conclude that they accelerate during their descent. One experiment I conducted to examine§0\n\nThis phenomena simply required a ball and a straight, slightly inclined track. Upon releasing the ball at the top, it is quite easy to observe this acceleration. The greater the inclination of the track, the greater the acceleration (according to rules\n\nof trigonometry, interestingly enough). We could, therefore, conclude that, if the track were to be inclined indefinitely (vertical, as in free-fall), this acceleration would continue and be greater still. This is the phenomena we observe with falling\n\nobjects. The pull of the ground imposes motion on them throughout their fall (as it does on everything), so it does not stop. Another observation to note would be the effect of resistance on motion. For example, if one submits their arms to motion in air,\n\nyou feel an element of resistance to that motion, just as you might feel resistance to walking against the direction of the wind. Similarly, if you move through water, it is noticeably harder. It requires more force to move through.\n\nForce is currently defined as the ‘imposition of movement on another object’. Shooting an arrow through water will result in a greater decceleration than through air. We could, therefore, consider this resistance as imposing an acceleration on this object\n\nin the direction opposite to its motion. Resistive forces hold them back. We could imagine, then, that an object set into motion where no resistance existed at all, would stay in motion forever. The natural state of an object is not ‘motionless’ (as many\n\nmay believe), but is actually be be in ‘constant motion’, even if that constant is zero.§0\n§0 §0\n§0We can conclude from these observations (among others) that when motion is imposed on a physical object (driving or in resistance), that object actually enters§0\n\nAcceleration. Its velocity becomes subject to change Now we can define a force as something that accelerates another object, gravity included. §0\n§0 §0\n§0It should now be clear that we have some simple and general understanding of what happens when objects§0\n\ninteract and experience forces. It is not necessary to conduct experiments of greater control to examine the exact relationships between quantities like force and accelerated motion, if it is true to say that such relationships exist.\n\nChapter 3§0\n§0-----==========-----§0\n§0\n§0Further Experimentation§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0First Variation\n\nTo conduct the experiments that will be described in this chapter, one only needs a long table (the longer, the better), some light masses, a small wheeled trolley of a known mass, a connecting thread or wire and a pulley wheel. You must attach the§0\n§0 §0\n\npulley to one end of the table and the connecting wire, attach to the trolley on one end and run over the pulley to connect to the hanging masses on the other end (using any kind of hooked mass holder or tray of *also known mass*). Then, have some kind of\n\ndistance measuring tool run across the side of the table. It would also be best to have the trolley roll along a straight track during this experiment.§0\n§0 §0\n§0One should also use the most precise timing device to hand (using an imprecise time measuring device§0\n\nwith a short table simply won’t give you reliable results. §0\n§0 §0\n§0My own timing device was something I built. Very similar to the mechanism of a metronome, except it had a small clamp to stop the metronome in place. Using this, along with a slightly§0\n\npreemptive grasp allowed me to make the following measurements with some accuracy, even accounting for my own reaction time. I will leave further details of this device out, also perhaps for some future writing).§0\n§0 §0\n§0To start, one must apply masses to\n\nthe wire, hence applying a set force. This will cause the trolley to travel forward. Upon releasing these masses, you should set off your timing device (if using a less accurate pocket watch, you should make a note of the position of the second hand).\n\nThen, once the trolley stops in its place after being set into motion, stop your timing. Record this time and the distance traveled by the trolley.§0\n§0\n§0-Note that it is okay for the trolley to reach the end of the track, as long as the measurements are as\n\nprecise as possible.§0\n§0\n§0Now, you should *double* the applied mass to the trolley and make the same measurements. You could continue to apply *triple* and *quadruple* mass but, as you go on and the trolley begins to move fast and faster,\n\nmeasurements will become less reliable and this isn’t necessary to observe the desired effect.§0\n§0\n§0One should repeat at least the first part of this experiment several times to make sure that measurements you take are as reliable as possible. It would\n\nbe very reasonable to ignore measurements that come out *very* different to those previous because they are likely due to a mistake in the set-up.§0\n§0\n§0Using these same results, you can actually calculate the acceleration of the trolley using the equation:\n\na=2d/t^2§0\n§0 §0\n§0(where a=acceleration, s=displacement and t=time).§0\n§0\n§0This can be done for all of your readings. You will notice that (if conducted correctly), the acceleration appears to more or less double when the mass applied to the wire (or\n\nforce applied to the trolley) is doubled. If you continue this experiment to triple and then quadruple the force applied, you will also notice the acceleration more or less triple and quadruple. §0\n§0\n\nSecond Variation:§0\n§0 §0\n§0This experiment requires the same equipment but, instead of varying the mass applied to the wire, you would actually lay masses *on* the trolley.§0\n§0 §0\n§0You know the current mass of the trolley. Apply a set force to it by applying a§0\n\nmass to the other end of the wire. This will again set the trolley in motion and you can calculate its acceleration using the previous equation. §0\n§0 §0\n§0The second time around, you should double the total mass of the trolley itself by laying masses equal to§0\n\nthe mass of the trolley, onto the trolley. After securing them, apply the same force as before and set it in motion. You can again calculate its acceleration with  the aforementioned equation. You could again continue to *triple* or *quadruple* the\n\nmass. You should also repeat this experiment many times to ensure reliable results.§0\n§0\n§0Wou will notice that, when the mass of the trolley is doubled, its acceleration is halved. And if the mass is tripled, then acceleration is one third. And if the\n\nmass is quadrupled, the acceleration is quartered and so on.§0\n§0\n§0In the second volume of this work I will be using these observations and results from our experiment to even further develop the axioms that were created earlier in this volume. I will also\n\nfurther explain how we can use these observations and conclusions to our advantage in describing the behaviour and cause of motion in nature into the future.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n-Note that, if any of the details of these experiments are too nuanced in my description or you simply wish to inquire further, you can contact me personally.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Pure Truth", "§bLorolith Iira", "On Pure Truth§0\n§0\n§0-----=====+=====-----§0\n§0\n§0By Lorolith Iira\n\nMany scholars and philosophers (and laymen) alike are rather flippant in their disregard for theories of knowledge beyond or outside of pure empiricism. For example, Farley III Stafyr boldly states in his writing 'Knowledge and Experience', \"The origin of\n\nknowledge is clearly, through the experiment and therefore experience\". Although I don't contest this conclusion, it's painful to see such a complex matter taken as lightly as to assert that it is 'common sense'. In this work I hope to analyse the process\n\nof forming beliefs from experience of the universe and I hope to demonstrate that, truley, only experience can ever be considered the arbiter of 'knowledge'.\n\nContents:§0\n§0\n§01) Meaning of 'Knowledge' and 'Truth'.§0\n§0\n§02) The Nature of Experience.§0\n§0\n§03) Quirks of the Universe.\n\nWhen we talk about 'knowledge', what exactly do we mean? This is an important question to answer before we move forward. I would define it as \"am affirmed claim which is 'true'\". This definiton comes with a set of criteria, however. There are some\n\ncases in which a 'true' assertion could not be considered by that definition. If I were to claim \"on that hill, there is a small, white Dog\", we could evaluate this claim by moving closer to the hill to see. If we move closer and it turns out that this\n\napparent small, white dog is actually a lamb, I was wrong. However, if we move further and *also* find a small, white dog on the other side of the hill, am I still wrong? I would argue that, although I was not wrong, I did not *know* that this dog\n\nwas on this hill. It was but a lucky guess. This is most likely uncontraversial. Perhaps a better definition for knowledge would be 'an affirmed concept that, which reason for affirmation, is 'true' '. §0\n§0\n§0Though, we must also now define the word 'true'.\n\nI must now define base on how I think people most value these terms. It would certainly seem that a 'true' assertion is valued to mean 'an assertion that correlates with the reality of the world'. I.e. a true assertion is one that describes the world\n\naccuratley. §0\n§0\n§0So, how is it that we can achieve 'true' knowledge? Or 'pure' truth? One might be tempted to argue that experience is *not* the way to do this. If we accept our previous definitions, it would become apparent that experience is subject to\n\nextreme change or fault. For example, in dreams or vivid hallucinations we see things that contradict everyday experience. And, if we were to ever accept experience as the arbiter of pure truth, we would never have grounds to choose between these\n\nexperiences.§0\n§0\n§0However, this is not an accurate assessment of the situation. When we go about our daily lives, we find ourselves only ever subject to two mind-states. Impressions (information we feel is coming to us through observation from an\n\napparently external world) or imagination (ideas created by the mind itself). I would ask the reader to note that the imagination can never be as vivid as impressions. When you read a compelling novel or tale of battle, the words themselves are never\n\nenough to pierce through your imagination to create an experience. You can never feel the true rush of charging into battle or taking a wound just from a story. It may tickle these impression somewhat but it has never reached the same level as actually\n\ndoing it. But we should also note that our imaginations are subject to our impressions. By this I mean, our imagination can only ever conjure that which we are familiar with through impressions. Yes, we can imagine fantastical new creatures and universes\n\nfor the sake of story telling but these things are essentially re-allignments of things we have previously experienced. For example, one cannot imagine a new colour. Try to do this. It is simply not a part of our faculty. We *can*, however,\n\nimagine a brand new shade of a colour. But this is only because we recognize the difference between darker or lighter shades of other colours from previous impressions of them. These examples illustrate this point very well. §0\n§0\n§0It would appear, therefore\n\nthat we are actually slaves§0\n§0to the experience all together. Our passions are dictated by interactions between ourselves and what we experience. As are our thoughts in general. Simlarly, when those effected by the likes of opium, mental deficiency or\n\ninsanity claim to see a universe beyond *our* experience, we can see the cause related to their difference in opinion (though, I will discuss the nature and problems with causal links soon enough). §0\n§0\n§0Having accepted this, it is only one step to accept\n\nappealing to knowledge beyond our experience is meaningless. At this very moment I may be dreaming. But this claim is meaningless because this dream-state that is external to my experience may never effect the experience itself. Therefore, I would have no\n\nway of knowing this. It is an empty claim all together. The uncertainty still remains. I may very well be dreaming. But we must essentially ignore claims like this, even if we do have to accept them as possibilities. Even the laws of reason seem to come\n\nfrom a mixture of developed intuition and direct experience (noting that even our intuition is likely governed by past experiences, as I will also discuss in detail soon). We see a universe in which things rarely contradict one another (unless, like I\n\nmentioned previously, we're effected by substances or madness. In which case, we have a non-contradictory understanding of these apparent contradictions). We also see a universe where the past always seems to resemble the future. We can use maxims like\n\nthese to make accute deductions. Many of which turn out to be right. §0\n§0\n§0Finally, I wish to discuss several problems with our often taken for granted experiences. Although it may appear to be reasonable to make deductions about the future by\n\nremembering the past, this actually makes little sense. Nothing assures us that the apparent order in nature will continue. This is the problem with 'causal' links that I described previously. §0\n§0The proper way to understand causality (or so it would seem)\n\nis to realize that if one thing truley is the result of another, they will *always* correlate. For example, forceful contact with a ball will always set it in motion. This is because there is some mechanistic link between them. We can therefore, you would\n\nthink, deduce that strong correlation is evidence for causation (even if it is not conclusive evidence). We can see that the pregnancy of a woman will undoubtedly follow the fertalization of a man. Therefore, we can deduce that these things are very\n\nlikely causally linked. Even if we don't understand underlying mechanisms. §0\n§0\n§0But this is a product of our intuitions and intuitions alone. Infact, beliefs based on these intuitions aren't neccessarily that reasonable. But they are neccessary and\n\npractical. They have always worked. We can never know if they will work the next time. But may judge it as worth trying. §0\n§0\n§0Perhaps the only way to posit absolute continuity in the order of the physical world is to posit the existence of a creator. Though,\n\neven the nature and being of that deity is subject to the same problems. We cannot reasonable deduce continuity in the existence of that being, even if we have once found reason to believe it exists. §0\n§0\n§0We must just plow forward, continuing to make these\n\ndeductions, trying to at least acknowledge this discrepancy. §0\n§0\n§0To conclude, we can only really know how the world 'seems' to behave, moment-to-moment. But anything else is meaningless, due to our reliance on our sensible faculties. And there is little\n\nreason to believe that the universe is any more ordered than the void. Although it has continued to be for all of its previous existence. And I would place a bet on this order remaining forever onwards, even though I am in no place to make such a claim.\n\nIt is a task for myself and future scholars to attempt to find a reasonable explaination from our reliance and past experience to deduce that of the future. And if anyone can really find this explaination, feel free to contact me. I would gladly help you\n\npublish such a work in writing.", ""),
        StaticBook("Research I", "§bArchmage Crumena", "Fourth of the Sun's Smile, 1497\nCrumena Ilwindior \n\nAn extreme oddity this is. The transporting closets, whatever they are, are beyond mind boggling. Elindor and myself sat down and attempted to unravel the enchantments\n\n\nthat create them. It is safe to say we were unsuccessful. By the title of Rasmot it is no surprise that this entire compound would be so sporadic and deranged. Although everything, even the tiles, are imbued with magics that have never\n\n\nto the likes of myself or even Elindor. For the transports we are still questionable of them. Possibly they have another purpose? Is it possibly some side effects occur from usage? I still recall the first time we used the closets. It\n\n\nto another one in the same closest. Both Elindor and myself felt a daunting pain in our stomachs. While the feeling has passed whenever I use the transporters it does make me wonder. What if on the first attempt Rasmot’s\n\n\nfrom the both of us?", ""),
        StaticBook("Notes II", "§bArchmage Crumena", "Seventh of the Sun's Smile, 1497\nCrumena Ilwindior \nI looked back to my notes and scribblings when Elindor and I attempted to decipher Rasmot’s enchantments on the closest. Nothing was comprehensible. It was only until\n\n\ndiscover something almost like a thread. Though this thread wielded unbridled power. We could not trace its origins but we realized that thread was the source of power for each transporter.", ""),
        StaticBook("Notes III", "§bArchmage Crumena", "Eighth of the Sun's Smile, 1497\nCrumena Ilwindior \nI took the liberty today to carry out an experiment for I could not quell my curiosity. It was early in the morning as I did not want to disturb anyone nor do I want Lotheriel\n\n\ncoming across my studies. I traversed the halls of this tower with my Shade trailing behind. The damned mist expanded its reaches outward and clutched any it could. Finally at the garden between Ceruberr’s Arch-Mage quarters and the meeting\n\n\nhall I had discovered something. Something pulsated above. For one second I was certain it was just above the ceiling and then without worry it circling about. Like a roach skidding away for dear life. Quickly its height altered with every moment.\n\n\nHigher and higher and then it dropped. Right before my Shade lost its grasp of the anomaly it seemed like an ocean away. I truly wonder if we should further dwell in Rasmot’s domain.§§", ""),
        StaticBook("On Eternal Laws", "§bLorolith Iira", "On Eternal Laws\n\n-----=====+=====-----\n\nAeternum Iuris\n\n-By Lorolith Iira\n\nJust as the reasonable mortals of this universe have confined their societies to the order of rule of law. This is moral and legislative law. Similarly, it would appear that the universe itself has (at least in the past, leading up to now) confined itself\n\nto working 'eternal laws'. These laws, like moral and legislative laws, regulate behaviour. But instead of regulating the behaviour of conscious agents, they regulate the bahviour of nature itself.\n\nWithin this book I aim to dicuss why I think these laws\n\nprove to be persistant as well as my own understanding of existing laws of the physical universe. This piece is an essential companion to anyone wishing to delve into the field of natural philosophical study.\n\nContents:\n\n1) Persistence.\n2) The law of equivalent exchange. \n3) Laws of interaction.\n4) How we may hope to understand these laws in the future.\n\nChapter 1\n-----==========-----\n\nPersistence\n\nAs it would seem, at the very moment I write these words, the world is 'ordered'. Meaning, it seems to continue to be stable (upon reflection of past events) and similar causes produce similar effects. Thing I drop fall down. When my skin is cut, I draw\n\nblood. When a moving object interacts with another stationary object, the stationary object recieves some part of the first object's speed. I could go on. However, it follows that these results have only been observed in controlled circumstances. Outside\n\ninfluences can always alter observation. As is a pain in formal study. But still, these rules persist. I would also argue that these laws have *always* persisted. There would not have been any time after the birth of the material world that these rules\n\nwould not have existed. \n\nI say I know this because it would seem that the order of the present is contingent on the order of the past. I.e. if things didn't always fall down, we would observe strange chaos in the world around us as some part of our world\n\nbe absent, sent off into the abyss of the heavens. There are also rules of existence that our very lives are contingent on. If the rules of nature did not persist in our past, little is to stop nature itself destroying the lives of all descendants through\n\napocolyptic storms and quakes. Yet, most likely due to the natural restrictions on these powers, we are still here to discuss. This is the simple reason that I beleive it is necessary that these laws exist.\n\nChapter 2\n-----==========-----\n\nThe Law of Equivalent Exchange.\n\nThe law of 'equivalent' exchange is, I would argue, the longest known physical law. Not only is it extremely intuitive but it has become clear within the practice of alchemy since the ancients first tried their luck within the art. Simply, the law is a\n\nprinciple that states that you can never get more 'out' than you put 'in'. Most alchemists (however effective in their trade) who haven't done extensive study will know this law to mean that you cannot recieve more from the properties of any final\n\nproduct than from the properties of composit parts. A potion created for healing will only be able to do so because their composing parts had properties that, once taken together, allow for this to happen. Essentially, there are set properties allowed to\n\ncome from certain types of ingredients. However, this law, as far as I can see, applies universally. Eternally. For example, when we alchemists create these new substances, we can weigh them and find that the final weight is equal to the total weight of\n\ncomposit parts. Nothing extra has been 'created' and nothing has been 'destroyed'. \n\nThis concept also exists within magic, it would seem. It's well known to all mages that, once something is 'evoked' from the void, it will *always* return. This is\n\nessentially a 'magical' embodyment of the law of equivalent exchange. You cannot *end* with more than you started with. No matter how long it sticks around. \n\nHowever, one problem I found myself with when considering this was the idea that although the\n\nphysically evoked substance of any spell cannot stick around, its effects on our physical universe *does*. \nI will explain this problem after first defining the term 'energy'.\n\n'Energy' as it is commonly used, is vauge in its definition. I have only\n\never heard it comprehensively a few times. However, here is my own: \n\n'Energy' is the property or state of being able to impose physical change. \n\nFor example, a vibrating ball would have a high energy. This is because, when another object comes\n\ninto contact with it, it would impose motion on that other object. Most (if not all) of the physical 'change' I will be referring to will be to do with the idea of physical motion. \n\nNow, the idea that a fire can be evoked and have it's physical\n\nchanges remain, seems to involve having physical change come from nothing. \nAllow me to illustrate:\n\nWhen a normal fire is lit, the energy from the fire (heat could indeed be considered energy since it has the capacity to burn and break down matter as\n\nfuel, hence imposing change) comes from originally striking the flame. An evoked flame essentially comes from nothing and, once it has done its work, goes back into nothing. If the results of this burning fire remain, the universe is in a state equivalent\n\nto having a fire burn without a starting flame. \n\nMuch like the existence of physical material, 'energy' (as previously defined) cannot come from nothing or simply disappear, it would seem. All motion and change in the universe appears to come from\n\npreceding motion and change. Set up a line of balls. Roll one towards them and watch the chain reaction of transfer of motion. The energy of each ball comes from the energy of the last.\n\nNow, if magic itself were to follow the principle of equivalent\n\nexchange, as I am to argue, it takes the mana of the 'evoker' to actually keep this change in existence. This makes sense because, unlike the material substance of a spell (such as earth evoking), energy transfered from this material into the physical\n\nworld *does* exist before the spell is cast. In the form of mana. This is what mana truley is. It is pure energy, as I have defined. Having mana is identical to having the potential to cause physical change in the universe *through* access to the void.\n\nSo, if I am right, we can conclude that the mana used in evoking some kind of magical substance from the void is in some way equal to the mana/energy required to actually drag this substance into the material world and the energy 'created' by this magical\n\nsubstance.\n\nThis would explain why maintaining a spell without properly casting would cost the caster much less mana. It would still cost some as this magical substance would interact with the physical world by emitting heat or interacting with the airs\n\nand materials around it whilst being maintained. Most of the mana would have to come from the real interactions between that magical substance and its target. Then, once that substance has returned to the void, the energy it has 'created' remains. This\n\nenergy is acutally sapped from the caster's mana. \n\nThis rational definition of 'mana', to my knowledge, has never existed before. We can think of it as essentially equivalent to the actual heat of a fire, or the motion of the winds. It is just as much an\n\nimposing force of nature as anything else.\n\nThere are some ways I can test these assertions soon enough. I plan on seeing the relationship between consumed mana and imposed motion at some point in the future with the help of a telekenetic mage in a\n\ncontrolled environment.\n\nI plan on releasing one or several volumes titled 'Science of Magic' that explore how would consider the laws of magic and the various mana to energy transfers that happen in different areas of the magic arts.\n\nIf anyone has found the contents of this chapter of this tome particularly confounding, yet they wish to inquire further, you may feel free to contact me directly to ask questions or even help with this area of research.\n\nChapter 3\n-----==========-----\n\nLaws of Interaction\n\nThere are, similar to the law of 'equivalent exchange', laws that govern the interactions between physical things, it would seem. Not only their existence. \n\nI wish to briefly discuss these along with what has been discussed in the previous chapter.\n\nThese come as a mixture of my own findings in research and my further reading.\n\n----------\n\nIt is clear, as I have explained previously that energy can be transfered between physical bodies. Although I have no way to quantify\n\n'energy' at this time, we can observe the transfer of motion upon collision of physical objects.\n\nNot only this, but we can observe that, once an object has recieved a forceful impulse, it begins to slow down. I beleive (although I cannot at this time,\n\ndescribe mathematically) that what truley slows this object down is the influence of surroundings like air and wind. Really, if you were to set an object in motion and nothing were to collide with it or slow it down, this motion may continue constantly\n\nforever. Simply put, if you try to move your hand through water (a thicker fluid), you experience more resistance than through air (a thinner fluid). It's harder to do so. You can therefore imagine that, if these mediums were to be removed *all together*,\n\nnothing would stop motion as nothing resists it. \n\nIt would follow, actually, that imposing motion on an object actually causes an increase in its speed, rather than giving it a set speed, as many may believe. Especially since, without this imposition,\n\nspeed can still be maintained.\n\nChapter 4\n-----==========-----\n\nHow we May Hope to Understand These Laws in the Future\n\nI hope that, upon further reflection on the details and observtaions I have discussed in this book, I cand find ways in greater detail to describe the course of motions in our universe. I have yet, as I have said, to collect detailed observations to test\n\nmy theories about energy transfer from mana. Which itself may be difficult to do without detailed mathametical basis. \n\nSoon I wish to be able to calculate the motions of all objects in our universe. If not in great detail, at least enough to have be\n\nable to truley watch the work of whatever gods lay beyond the heavens.", ""),
        StaticBook("Redstone Studies", "§bGallidron Elindrow", "Day 1\n\nI, Gallidron Elindrow, have begun my study of Redstone. I intend to gain greater understanding of this substance that I may learn to manipulate it to more fascinating purpose, perhaps even that of an arcane one.\n\nTo this end I am beginning with basic observations of the material in question. Redstone is found in the earth as an ore much like iron, however it is fragile in nature and breaks down into a dust when extracted. Not unlike Thanhium when it is extracted,\n\nwhich causes me to think the two might have a connection, but this is just a musing of mine.\n\n The dust is coarse to the touch and grainy, but gives off a slight shine. It is capable of being compressed into a block and then seems to be much more dur\n\nthan in its natural form as an ore. Hence it does not break apart when struck. The compressed block is decidedly smoother than the dust or the ore, but this is just a product of the refinement process.\n\nThis concludes my observations of its physical nature.\n\nDay 2\n\nRedstone is primarily used for its conductive nature. My experiments today are centered around this aspect. Redstone is so conductive that it can be laid in a line as a powder and still hold an electrical current.\n\nThis is a common way to use the material, as it is not necessary to enclose redstone while transferring power through it. The current will eventually taper off however, thus showing that while uninsulated some of the power is indeed lost in transfer.\n\nA compressed block of redstone can obviously hold more energy and act as a greater conduit due to its increased mass. More water flows through a bigger pipe, the same is true of energy it would seem.\n\nAs well, a compressed block of redstone seems to produce and electrical current all on it’s own. Whether this is the result of some electrical energy generation that occurs within the higher density of the block or just latent charge held within its form\n\nis a matter for debate.\n\nRedstone is used for transferring electrical energy and can be used as a conduit for errant electrical static, especially if one has a large quantity of it available in the open air.\n\nDay 3\n\nI have enlisted the aid of a Tier 5 Electromancer in this next phase of my studies. It was my intent to record the behavior of redstone when encountering measured amounts of electrical charge. Here are the results.\n\nControl: The electromancer is set up 15 meters from a stone target. Upon firing a single bolt of lightning of what I was assured is “medium strength.” They hit the target at it’s center.\n\nExperiment 1\nPurpose: To determine if the presence of uncharged redstone can alter the course of an electrical discharge.\n\nSet up: A stone target set 15 meters from the Electrical source, I.E. The Electromancer. Between the source and target are 5 lines of redstone powder, each one separated by 1 meter of distance, the lines are connected on the sides and hold no charge.\n\nResult: The source fires a singular bolt of electricity at the target and the bolt curved down to strike the base of the target.\n\nExperiment 2\nPurpose: To determine if the presence of charged redstone can alter the course of an electrical discharge.\n\nSet up: A stone target set 15 meters from the Electrical source, I.E. The Electromancer. Between the source and target are 5 lines of redstone powder, each one separated by 1 meter of distance, the lines are connected on the sides and hold a charge.\n\nResult: The source fires a bolt of electricity which immediately arcs down to the red stone. Both the bolt and the red stone are consumed entirely.\n\nExperiment 3\n\nPurpose: to determine the effect of a condensed block of redstone upon an electrical discharge.\n\nSet up: A stone target set 15 meters from the Electrical source, I.E. The Electromancer. Approximately halfway between the source and the target and a meter to the right (as reckoned by the source) is a single block of condensed redstone.\n\nResult. The source fires a bolt of electricity which veers directly to the redstone block. The Block takes the charge and remains intact, but does give off a somewhat brighter glow.\n\nConclusion: Redstone is perfectly capable of affecting the trajectory of an electrical discharge, magical or otherwise. It seems that Redstone that is already laden with a charge is more effective at doing so. It is my understanding That the Redstone\n\nlines were consumed in experiment 2 because their mass was not great enough to take the energy of the bolt. The block of Redstone in experiment 3 however, was both more dense and possessed over all more mass and so could withstand the sheer voltage.\n\nThis concludes journal 1 of my findings on Redstone and its properties. \n\nGallidron Elindrow\n\nPublished\n9th of Sun's Smile\n1600", ""),
        StaticBook("Enchanting", "§b[Ivae'Fenn Guard] Lady Nessa Anarion", "((I'm a tad bit too lazy to actually write anything in here, but this is a link to the \"book\" just type it in))\n\nhttp://tinyurl.com/oz9bvn8\n\nOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOO", ""),
        StaticBook("College Conduct", "§bGallidron Elindrow", "College Of Sutica§0\n§0Code of Conduct§0\n§0-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-§0\n§0\n§0Every student and Professor must follow these guidelines, failure to do so could lead to termination from the college and access to the campus.\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-Behavioral Rules§0\n§0-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-1. Every student and staff is expected to act mature and serious about their work.§0\n§02. Rudeness and tormentation of others is not tolerated, you have a right to be at the college like everyone else.\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-Class Rules§0\n§0-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-1. Everyone must take their class seriously, goofing off and being a distruption during class time can result in expulsion from the class.§0\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-Dormatory Rules§0\n§0-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-1. Students must keep their dorm space clean and tidey. Failure to keep a clean sleepspace could result in the loss of it.§0\n§02. No Girls in the boys dorm, Vice Versa.\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-Library Expectations§0\n§0-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-1. Respect the books you sign out, you can and will be charged if a book is lost or destroyed.§0\n§02. Keep a low voice in the library, people may be studying for a final exam, or presentation.\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-Campus Rules§0\n§0-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-1. No using dangerous magic unsupervised§0\n§02. No practicing the latest fighting tecnique on other students.§0\n§03. The campus courtyard has training dummies for fighting practice and magic, no explosive magic.", ""),
        StaticBook("Philosophorum", "§bLanavia Zytiaear", "An Introduction to the\nPhilosophorum Society\n------------------------------\n\nThe information provided within these pages are to simply help you understand what the Society is and what exactly we stand for.\n\nIf you still have questions contanct\n\nSarrion (Toxzero), Elric (Jacey) or Lanavia (Creete).\n\n\nEnjoy~\n\n-\nLanavia\n\nWhat is the Society? \n\nThis has to be the most asked question we get here in the Society - at the same time it is the easiest to answer.\n\nThe Society is more a community than a guild.\n\nWe are a gathering of like-minded individuals that have a thirst for knowledge.\nIt is this thirst for knowledge that has pulled us all together and has led to the creation of the Society, and is the thing that still keeps us together.\n\nAre there any fees?\n\nNo. We have never charged new members any type of mina fee to join our ranks.\n\nThe only thing we ask is that if you stumble upon a book we might be interested in that you would donate it to the Society.\n\nWhat does it mean to be a member of the Society?\n\nThis one is also easy to answer. To be a member of the Society simply means you are a part of something more – you are trying to better yourself and the world around you.\n\nYou are not wasting the most precious thing you own: your mind.\n\nBeing a member of the Society means you are searching to answer the unknown and learn from others who have already started their personal quests to do just that.\n\nWhat’s with the jackets?\n\nThis is one thing we share with a lot of guilds or schools, we have a matching uniform of sorts. The navy colored jackets are a symbol of your ties to the Society – and thus your eagerness to learn and spread knowledge.\n\nA fact about the jackets that you need to know is that the colored trimmings do mean something. A student with gold trims on his jacket with a student of magic, whereas a student with a jacket that has a silver trim is a student of science.\n\nMany students study both science and magic – the jacket they decide to wear is up to them but should be trimmed with the colors associated with whichever of the two they focus more heavily on.\n\nThe best way to know what a student studies is to ask them.\n\nWhat is the purpose of the Society?\n\n\nThe Society’s purpose is simple. We are here to gather, document, and share knowledge – that is it.\n\nDo you need to be an Elf to be a member?\n\nNo. We do not care what race you are, so long as you are like-minded you are welcome here.\n\nThis is the basis of the Society put into the simplest terms I could. If you have any other questions about it feel free to ask any member you see – if they can’t answer your questions they can point you in the right direction. \n\nBe well~!", ""),
        StaticBook("The Kal'Varak", "§bArchmage Crumena", "The Kal'Varak\nRescribed by Arch-Mage Crumena V. Illwindor on the 2nd of the First Seed, 1473.\nThe Kal'Varak, which roughly translates as 'Mind City', is an ancient crystalline artifact. It was dug up by the dwarves of Karik just prior to\n\ntheir unearthing of the hibernating dragons, but the faded scratches on the side suggest it has been discovered at least once before this, perhaps by the original inhabitants of Karik. It seems to be some sort of powerful magical nexus, exerting a\n\nsignificant magical influence without appearing to draw magical power anywhere, as if it somehow generates it itself. Despite this, the Kal'Varak is useless as a source of magical energy. All of its power is turned inwards on itself, giving the\n\nKal'Varak the bizzare property that gives it its name. When a living being touches the Kal'Varak, their mind and soul are forcefully drawn from their bodies into the Kal'Varak itself. Once inside, they find themselves in a strange dreamworld, often\n\ntaking the form of a maze, populated by their memories, dreams and nightmares. In the Kal'Varak, people appear as they would wish to be, some see themselves as stronger, taller, more attractive, sometimes as a different race\n\nentirely. While the ability to walk through one's mind and the minds of any others currently 'in' the Kal'Varak may seem enticing, the dreamworld is dangerous. The dreamworld responds to moods, fears and doubts, often turning a\n\nguilty memory into a full-blown nightmare in an instant. The more complex the mind, the more labyrinthian the maze, and those with dark pasts they'd rather forget are often forced to face them within the Kal'Varak. Even more dangerous is\n\nwhen the mind and crystal form a world that one would not want to leave. The collapsed body outside the crystal still needs food, water, and is still vunerable to harm. If the body dies, the mind cannot return to it. Somewhere in the Kal'Varak maze is\n\na door, and those more experienced with the dreamworld can find it more quickly than others. The door is always relevant in form to the person it belongs to, often impossible to mistake for one's own. Sometimes the door is not even a door, but it is\n\nalways clearly an exit. Passing through your door returns you to your body, but if you pass through the wrong door, you may find yourself in the wrong body entirely.", ""),
        StaticBook("Gemstones", "§bAnwyn Izalith", "~*Gemstones*~\n\n     A copy written by:\n        Anwyn Izalith\n\n                ~*~\n\nThis book has been created in order to spread the cultural significance of gemstones.\n\n-Written in 1542\n\n-Ruby\nIt gives people increased wisdom. It is particulary useful for rash.\n\n-Emerald\nIncreases the fertility of a person, both during conception and bearing.\n\n-Diamond\nThe most powerful of all gems. It amplifies all other traits.\n\n-Amethyst\nCalms the nerves. Useful for study or meditation.\n\n-Sugilite\nThe healer's stone. It is used in healing arts, both magic and item.\n\n-Carnelian\nImproves analytical skills.\n\n-Garnet\nThe bloodstone. It is used to draw toxics from the body and aid in blood clot.\n\n-Onyx\nUsed to obtain enlightenment.\n\n-Hematite\nReduces stress.\n\n-Sapphire\nEnvokes peace and happiness.\n\n-Topaz\nIncreases both metal and physically stamina.\n\n-Tiger's eye\nDestroys anxiety. It promotes non-reckless courage.\n\n-Peridot\nUsed for growth.\n\n-Pearl\nUsed in chakra. Improves the connection with both mind and body.\n\n-Soladite\nAdsorbs negative thoughts from both the wearer and those around them. This negates the thoughts.", ""),
        StaticBook("MonkResurrection", "§bKirrekith", "Monk Resurrection\nBy Jistuma\nPages 23\n\nNotice, this books is a rushed copy of a tome inside the Library of the Orkish capital, as made during the raid of the 16th of the Grand Harvestm 1532 The following is no way a complete nor perfect copy\n\nMonk Resurrection\n\nIt is the salvation of those lives, but it is pondered why nothing has been wrote upon it. The monks are able to and well known for being able to save those on the brink of death or those who've always passed the thresshold. While at the grounds of the\n\nsanctuary manning a stall I have been able to witness of these happenings, or so what I believe. \n\nThis magic, unlike others, is one we know little of and myself. Like most holy forms of magic as it takes the form of a bright light, used to heal woun\n\nBesides this light, the monks act is to pray to the three known as the Triumvirate. It would appear to be that of clerical healing, but uses other properties not like those of clerical magic. Monks returning to the sanctuary can come back wounded,\n\nhaving been attacked by bandits proberly, showing peoples lack of care for whom they attack on the roads, especially when these individuals will often bring them back from the brink. Monks are not ordinary people, they heal faster than others from\n\nobservation. This is likely from their magic. often I witnessed light coming from they're wounds and surrounding it. Monks can also bring back the once deceased fully mortal once more. \nThis isn't in which to say they are necromancers, those that would\n\nbring back the dead, and remain in a state of limbo between death and life. Monks unlike them are able to bring back body AND soul unlike those of the necromancer, and bring them back to a near perfect condition from before death. Talking to those at the\n\nsanctuary, there is always an effect of being saved. A present residing condition is the loss of memory that occurrs after ones life being taken to the brink or past it and brought back. I have witnessed those effected having no memory of the passing or\n\nthe run up to it. Other common effects that linger is the scarring, revealing how the soul nearly passed the brink permanently or the tortures it passed. Often what worries me is the effects of change that develops in those that return, in personality.\n\nHaving talked to those if proves my worries. It is my belief that death is a terrible experiences, and part of it remains after the salvation. \n\nAnother fact I have witnessed, is that the deceased do not wish passively return to their prior resting p\n\nThis came to mind when I witnessed one of the saved hugged dearly by a beloved. Before this a friend has carried his friend, the two of us talked about what had occurred. Knowing the location of his end, the twos route home took them through the place,\n\nand upon discussing this he began to want to take other ways home, and a argument erupted when the woman just wanted to move home swiftly, much to the mans reluctance. He could offer no explanation as to why his mind has been set to not travel that way.\n\nAs well this this the other strange behaviours of those visiting the sanctuary became more apparent, such as staying there durations. As well as this not using normal routes out of the temple, or swiftly returning to the santuary after having left with\n\nfriends. Its my assumption that those who meet their death are faced with a pain too horrible to face, what could be worse than seeing your own dead body?\n\nOn the talk of the bodies, I have a state as well, it is not necessary for the body to be brou\n\nback to the monks for the resurrection. I've witnessed them bring bodies back to the sanctuary, so I believe possessing them makes the process easier or favourable. In the lands of Anthos there are many evils, you could be decapitated, easten, thrown in\n\nlava, cut into pieces and drowned in the deepest oceans. Your body may disappear and never be found once more, and even without it can still be saved. Resoration of the body is beyond my comprehsion, and monks refuse to answer the question. They do\n\nhowever answer a few questions. I learned the monks are neutral, and never take sides in politics and conflict. They also are neutral in those they save, murder or saint they are rescued. This has brought them into conflict as some are desired to be dead.\n\nMost of the stories of those rescued begin with them explaining their awakening. Waking to the monks care. Soon come back swiftly after their demise, often before family and friends arrive. Others can take many elven days. They're magic is not all\n\npowerful, as monks say they can't save all those who come to them. If the deceased does leave the monks rooms within a elven week after their death it is to be presumed them to have passed to the beyond without the monks being able to act. The same occurs\n\nto those who take their own life in an attempt at life to end. If they no longer wish to live, their is nothing the monks can do to bring them back. \n\nThe monks are a blessing to mortals. They are those thay save many from their shortened lives.\n\nWrote by Rosso, 15th of Malin's Welcome 1452, copied by Kirrekith on 16th of the Grand Harvest, 1532 during the raid on the Ork Capital.", ""),
        StaticBook("Immortal Spirits", "§bThurak'Yar", "Immortal Spirits\n\nA list of the Greater Spirits inhabiting the Immortal Realm and what they represent. This may change, given time, and will be updated as new spirits come to power.\n\nVotar- The spirit of the hunt, commonly shown as a giant, bloodied wolf. Praised by the Phrase Glougaz Votar.\n\n      /\\_==_/\\\n      | /o   o\\ |\n      \\         /\n       \\       /\n        \\ *.* /\n          \\ /\n\nEnrohk- The spirit of bloodlust, savagery and war, often shown as a bastard sword covered in blood. \n        \n         /\\\n         |  |\n         |  | \n         |....|\n         |. .|\n      `==8=='\n          ||\n\nShezept- The spirit of revenge, plots and stealth, depicted as a woman with a lower body of a snake. \n      \n        O\n       -|-\n   ^\\_\\\\_\n   \\____/\n\nJevex- Spirit of order, hard work and self sacrifice, shown as a giant hammer. He's a deadly enemy to the spirit Shezept.\n \n     [======]\n     [==|  |==]\n         ==\n         ==\n        |.--.|\n         ==\n\nIxli- The spirit of forbidden knowledge, truth and judgement. Depicted as an eye, he provides knowledge as the cost of sanity. \n\n       |/  \\|\n      |  (.)  |\n       |\\  /|\n\nOgrol- Spirit of despair, sapped strength and entrapement, shown as a cage full of writhing bodies. He tries to lock up all who summon him.\n\n       ______\n       | | |  | | |\n       =<< >>=\n       | | |  | | |\n      ========\n\nKor- The spirit of the dead, depicted as a cloaked man with an open hand.\n          _\n          |O\\\n           |-''\n          /\\\n\nAnkrus- Spirit of the sea, fishermen and safe travels, often shown as an aquatic woman wielding a spear. \n\n           () |\n           oo/\n           \\/\n           /\\\n           \\/\n\nArwa- Spirit of fertility, harvest and farming, commonly shown as a plump female with over seven hundred breasts.\n \n           ()\n         /.oo.\\\n        /o.oo.o\\\n        |o.o.o.o.o|\n        \\_____/\n         [|]    [|]\n\nAnyhuluz- Spirit of destruction, malicious intent and internal strife, shown as a warrior covered in open wounds.\n\n         O   ^\n       /(`)-`|\n         [|]\n         /\\\n\nAkezo- Spirit of health, vitality and healing, depicted as a winged whale surrounded by a blue aura.\n\n   (\\./)\n     \\\\_/--^--.\n      =.    \"~\"   )\n        \\______/\n\nPaxahru- Spirit of arrogance and stupidity, depicted as a boasting fool.\n\n        /**\\\n       ( ^^ ) \n        /--\\ \n        |`~~'|\n        ====\n        \\/\\/\n         |   |\n\nUblulhar- Spirit of hope an new beginnings, shown as a tree sapling bursting from the ground. \n\n       _____\n      {~~~~}\n     {~~~~~}\n      {~~~~}\n       {~| |~}\n          | |\n       ~~~~~\n\nGhorza- Spirit of travel, depicted as an ever turning wheel.\n\n       /----\\\n      / /--\\ \\\n      |  |<:::::)|  |\n      \\ \\--/ /\n       \\----/\n\nRolfizh- Spirit of assassinations and poison, depicted as a dagger with poison dripping from it.\n\n        /\\\n        |  | \n        |  |\n       =||||=\n         ||\n\nTrokorl- The spirit of engineering, machinery and building. Depicted as a goblin with a goblin with goggles and a hammer.\n\n       {o o}\n        \\-/P\n         |. |/\n         |. |\n         ==\n        /  \\\n\nLuara- The spirit of the moon, often shown by the symbol of a pale, white wolf.\n\n        \\___(>\n         |===/\n         |    |\n\nRamakhet- Spirit of the desert, barren lands and sand. He is never seen, and therefore is rarely ever depicted as something other than his name. He has sworn to scour the earth of all life for all time.\n       ~~~~~\n      Ramakhet\n\nKrathol- The spirit of pain, suffering and starvation. Depicted as a pile of dry bones.\n   \n      (------)\n      (------)\n\nVeist- Spirit of illusion, tricks and thievery, depicted as a goblin with four eyes.\n\n      (:_:)\n      \\| |/\n       /\\\n       |  |\n\nFreygoth- Spirit of the wind and wild animals. He takes any form which he may desire. \n \n       ~~~~~\n        ~~~~\n         ~~~\n\nGlutros- The spirit of greed and obesity. He is depicted as a cake.\n   \n        , , , ,\n       |====|\n       |====|\n\nThulezia- Spirit of lust, pleasure and beauty, depicted as a naked slave girl. \n\n        (_)\n        |00|\n      ,/|   |\\,\n        !__!       \n       /   \\\n\nIsuz- Spirit of love, caring, and tranquility, depicted as an open-armed woman.\n\n       { . }\n       \\| |/\n         ||\n         ||\n\nOrgon- Spirit of Disease, Pestilence, and failure. Depicted as a festering, starving man with six arms, each holding a rusted and broken weapon.\n            (.)\n           |-||-|\n           i-||-i\n           !-||-!\n            /\\\n\nUrin- The spirit of rain and hail, depicted as a dark rain cloud.\n\n         *****\n       ********\n         *****\n         ` ` ` ` \n          ` ` `\n           ` ` `\n            ` `\n\nKesaroth- Spirit of envy and jealousy, usually shown as a golden bull.\n \n                 |__|\n                 ('')\n     /`-------\\/\n    / |         | |\n  '~  | |-----| |\n      ^^       ^^\n\nLeyd- Spirit of dominance and physical strength, symbolised by a closed fist.\n\n       ()()()\n        (   )\n         |**|\n         |**|\n\nScorthuz- The spirit of cleansing and purging, depicted as a vial of clear water.\n\n          (-)\n           | |\n         /--\\\n         |~~~|\n         |~~~|\n         ----\n\nTheruz- Spirit of intelligence and learning, shown as a wise old teacher. Brother to Betharuz.\n\n        ----\n       /     \\\n       | _  _ |\n     ('| O  O |`)\n       |  (.)  |\n       `/=.=\\'\n        \\/\\/\n\nBetharuz- Spirit of alcohol, cactus green and celebration, often depicted as a young dim man, in direct contrast to his brother, Theruz.\n\n       *, (.) U\n        \\_||_/\n           ||\n          /\\\n\nGentharuz- Spirit of smithing, smelting, industry and forging, depicted as a man with no legs, hammering an anvil.\n\n          (,)\n           ||--P\n         || ||   <==->\n         ||===| |==|\n         ||    || |++|\n\nDrelthok- Spirit of sleeping and dreams, often shown as a dark cloud.\n\n        %%%%%%\n       %%%%%%%\n        %%%%%%\n\nEathruz- Spirit of dawn and dusk, shown by the symbol of a red sun.\n\n       . .       . .\n       .\\       /.\n         \\\\ //\n         /|/\\|\\\n         \\|\\/|/\n         // \\\\\n      . /       \\ .\n      . .         . .\n\nKotrestruu- The spirit of memory and the recording of knowledge, depicted as an endless book.\n   \n       ______\n       ||=====/|\n       ||=====/|\n       ||=====/|\n       ||=====/|\n       ||=====/|\n       ||=====/\n\nLetrothak- The spirit of hindsight and excuses, shown as a goblin with eyes in the back of his head.\n\n        /----\\\n     \\\\| |  ||  | |//\n      \\,| (i)(i) |,/\n        \\      /\n         |      |\n       /        \\\n   __/ |", ""),
        StaticBook("Yemekar", "§bHogarth Irongut", "IV-Yemekar's Balance\n\nWritten by Morgrim Grandaxe and copied down by Hogarth Irongut, tennant from Da Ord Nar Kadrin.\n\nFrom the dawn of time it was said to be the creator, Yemekar, who selected the Dwarves as the chosen amongst all his creations. However, with this great honour, there too came responsibility. Yemekar commanded of Urguan that he and his kin would forever\n\nemsure that none of his creations would ever bear the power to completely destroy another. \"Go forth my children, and preserve the balance of my creation. For you are the pillars that uphold the world.\" Though these words held many different meanings\n\nthroughout various ages of Dwarven history it is generally accepted by most within the Dwarven Clergy as a matter that remains relevant to this day. Urguan accepted his creator's bidding and thus the Dwarven race, throughout the vast majority of its\n\nexistance, remained vigilant in the event that an empire were to rise. This has in practice become better known amongst the Dwarves as Yemekar's Balance. For if ever a nation were to cast aside its morality in favour of the pursuit of power, the\n\nprosperity of other nations would surely suffer at its hands. \n\nThe concept of Yemekar's Balance therefore revolves largely around a fierce opposition towards policies of expansionism and the invasion of another nation's sovereign\n\nterritory without a cause deemed righteous in the eyes of the gods. Furthermore, in the majority of circumstances, wars that are declared in the name of Yemekar's Balance are almost exclusively made in the defense of nations deemed to lack the\n\ncapability to defend themselves. However, there are a few unique examples that can indeed be to seen to differ from this norm. If a nation is found to be harboring a capability that is deemed wither unholy or dangerous in the wrong hands, the Dwarves may\n\nchoose to act against them. For example, golemancy, gifted unto the hands of the Dwarves by Yemekar himself, is considered a very real threat in the hands of those who are not to be trusted. \n\nToday, we may take no extraordinary effort in viewing tha\n\nDaemon, Khorvad, was once one amongst the Brathmordakin, represented by a will for ambition. Yet with ambition, came the everlasted desire for more. It was thought that plagued the Ironborn ruler, Velkan, as he departed from the ways of Yemekar and\n\ninstead opted to follow in Khorvad's footsteps. So too was it a driving force behind the Holy Oren Empire and its reign of terror upon the Princedom of Malinor. For this, Thorin Grandaxe, former Grand King of Urguan opted to be commended for his actions,\n\neven in going up against a power that  for a period of its history, held the capacity to conquer much of the world. For when the Empire fell, Thorin had within his grasp the opportunity to take both Malinor and Oren for himself, he did not give in to such\n\ntemptations. If we are indeed to tkae one lesson from Thorin's actions, it is that even when we are seemingly on the verge of victory, it is never in any case wise to forsake Yemekar's will. Though greed is a natural blessing, bestowed upon Dwarves by the\n\nBrathmordakin, Grimdugan, ambition and a thirst for power are what drives mortal beings mad. Consequently, it is by no coincidence that empire building is deemed an act of sin in the eyes of many of Urguan's descendants.", ""),
        StaticBook("Deity Magic", "§bGallidron Elindrow", "I I\n             I I\n       ___\\I I/___\n       ----I I----\n         `~/I I\\~'\n             I I\n             I I\n             \\/\n            The\n   Power  of  Prayer\na guide to deity magic\n             By\n   Gallidron Elindrow\n\nDeity magic, unlike arcane magic, is highly contingent upon the relationship between the \"caster\", for lack of a better term, and the source of their power. For example a cleric or an aengulic faith may recieve great power from their patron aengul. Given\n\nthat they are trusted enough and have gained sufficient favor with the patron. Indeed, the practitioner of deity magic must learn to rely almost wholly upon their otherworldy masters and their whims for a scrap of their might.\n\nIn these pages I will attempt to list and off a brief explanation as the to each of the known branches of Deity Magic.\n\n          ~ONE~\n        The Cleric\n-------------------Clerics tend to gain power through the worship of the \"Creator\" and the veneration of His Aenguls. The powers gained depend largely upon the aengul from which they come though are usually\n\ncentered around healing, light and protection.\nThe aenguls are listed here.\nAeriel- Arch~aengul and caretaker of souls\nEshtael-Balance and destiny\nGazardiael-curiosity and new beginings\n\nNemiisae- aengul queen of spiders\nTahariae- purity and patron of clerics\nWandering Wizard- An aengul who wander the seven lands.\nWyrvun- Cold and virtue\nYeu Rthulu- contentment and compassion\nXan- order\n\nMost clerics venerate Tahariae or Xan though worship of the others is not unheard of.\n\n           *TWO*\n           Druids\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Druidism is perhaps the oldest of the deity magics, but this depends on whom you speak with. The druids have, for ages taken it upon themselves to protect nature and to keep its secrets. They order of\n\ndruids is most definately an old one and has survived relatively unchanged. This is due to the certain level of indoctrination that take place amoung the acolytes who seek to join the order. This process is called Dedicancy and is evidently\n\nquite effective. After some time of being given tasks to complete and lectures to attend, the worthy new-comer is led to a pool of attunement and placed in its waters. The high concentration of druidic energies there attune the mind of the individual to\n\n\"The will of nature\", or so it is said. Whether or not this is the case the druid gains impressive control over nature and usually becomes wholly devoted to protecting the natural world.\n\nThe powers of the druid, so far as I have been able to catalog are listed here.\n\nCONTROL OF NATURE-\nA druid can coax life from even the driest ground. They can cause trees to spring immediately from tiny saplings. They can\n\ntie their foes in vines and mold trees into new shapes. The Druid is a master of manipulating nature in almost endless ways.\n\nBLIGHT HEALING- When necromancy is used there is often an occurance of blight. The land becomes cursed and all life in it withers\n\nIt is within the druids power to remedy this, though depending on the strength of the blight it may take time and more than one druid.\n\nHEALING- Druids are talented healers that seem to increase the bodies natural ability to heal itself. It is said that\n\nonly monks and clerics are better at it.\n\nSHAPE SHIFTING- This is perhaps the most facinating branch of their magic (no pun intended). The Druid, when powerful enough, can change their very physical and biological make-up and become a mariad of different\n\nthings. Not much is known about this abillity, in fact very few even know about it. Even I, who have read all I can find on the subject, am not sure if it is even a reality or just a rumor. One thing remains certain, that this is one of the most closely\n\nguarded secrets of the druids.\n\nCOMMUNION WITH NATURE- This is perhaps the most important of the druid's abillities. Not because it is the most powerful or awe inspiring to witness, but because this is from where all their other powers stem.\n\nIn truth the druid does not command nature. They speak with it and ask it to do things for them, and if nature is willing it complies.\n\n          %Three%\n          Shamans\n-------------------It is believed that Shaman originated from the orcs. As the story goes, thinner, smaller orcs were thought to be more apt at intellectual persuits than the brutal aspects of a stronger orc's\n\nlife, and so the art of Shamanism was born. These orcs learned to commune with spirits of various natures and there by gain magical power of various natures as well. The entire practice of Shamanism has to do with the contact and appeasment of spirits.\n\nThe spirits are catagorized into three different types.\n\nELEMENTAL- These are spirits of the elements and embody the aspect that they represent.\n\nIMMORTAL- These represent various aspect of life. These spirits are not so flashy\n\nbut are highly sought for various reasons. For examply, one might commune with the spirit of the hunt if they wish to be \"blessed\" in their hunting efforts. There other Immortal spirits of course, each having to do with different aspects; Honor, strength,\n\nhusbandry, battle, wisdom, ect...\n\nANCESTRAL- These are the spirits of the dead. Those who came before and might be called upon to offer wisdom and proctection. They are often refered to as Ancestrals rather than spirits.\n\nGREAT SPIRITS- While not exactly one of the three base distinctions, it is wise to denote that a Great Spirit is of some import. They are the most powerful of their kind. The is a great fire elemental spirit as there is a great spirit of fertility, and so\n\non. These are akin to kings over their kind of spirit.\n\nShamans call upon these spirits to gain different effects though most develope an affinity for one or another.\nThese shaman subtypes are as follows.\n\nELEMENTALIST- A caller of elemental spirits to devistating effect.\n\nFARSEER- One who communes with Immortal spirits to gain blessings that increase strength, prowess, healing, ect...\n\nLATAUMAN- Uses Ancestrals to increase their combat prowess.\n\nWITCHDOCTOR- These interesting shamans use potions and brews to gain the effects and help of all types of spirits.\n\n       CONCLUSION\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nThese are the main kinds of deity magic users. There are also The Temple Monks and the Muun'Trivazja, but these two sects are not covered in this work.\nIt is my hope that the subject of deity magic has been illuminated\n\nsomewhat for you and that this book has left you, if not wiser, then more educated at least.\n\n-Gallidron Elindrow\n\nPublication date\n11th of Sun's Smile 1518\n\nPlease look for my next book!\n\nARCANISTS AND WORSHIPERS\na comparison of magics.", ""),
        StaticBook("A found Journal", "§b:", "An Itharel's journal:\n\nIt's interesting really, even after ascending to a height of Demi-Godhood that many a Valah would dream of and many a Mali, Uruk or Bortu might envy...I feel little difference and yet I still fear it. The loss of my soul...not the literal soul I remind\n\nyou...you being me in the future. I fear it because...well look at my cousin...I hardly see a scrap of him left in that hollow golden shell...\n\nSo starting from this day I'll note the simple things...the taste of bread, the trees...\n\nthe coitus, the masonry, the books I read and poems that made me cry...that way I'll have memories to cling to...the pile of experiences that define us as people and thus will save me. \n\nI pray my mortality does not leave me too quickly...I must also\n\ntell Mia...and Skale...and those ignorant of this matter. It's a saddening truth and a cruel one for them and myself...but alas it is so...\n\nI am bound by the chains of service until I'm cast aside.\n\nYet another is lost. I can't blame him, the stress is damaging and the sights can drive any man past sanity...or good...\n\nIt would have been me had I not *several words have been erased as if the reader couldn't decide*\n\nwell...if I wasn't what I am...\n\nSeems as soon as we lose one, we recieve a new one. One of Katari's creatures...now one of Hesh's....I named him grandmaster.\n\nThe new Cleric is Luna. Impure but...pretty...\n\nalas the frailty of mortality. Like a small sculpture of glass and china. Fascinating yet...so delicate.\n\nSo this Rose tavern is clearly going to be nutcase central, perhaps I can fix that....maybe not.\n\nI see interesting folk here, the Gwyn individual appears again...she seems less...brute-ish.\n\nAll sorts in here actually. Old faces and new...one of Ivanus' past.\n\nSo for those interested, this is how you can be an Itharel:\n1. Man up- Ivanus.\n\nWell...I would note that usually sitting on a Shrine's altar would be...most...cruel.\n\nHowever...this particular one is in Lenia's name...so I doubt she'd mind.\n\nBesides I'm a walking steamboat of Tahariae's favour\n\n\n-------------------", ""),
        StaticBook("Navitas I", "§b[Child] Silus Hightower", "The Book of Nativitas\n\nThere were no planes of existence. There was nothing. Just the Void. And a bright shining light. It was not a light we could see, it was not really white either\n\n but it would be the only way to explain the existence of such a Deity in contrast to the emptiness of the Void. None knows why, how or when this Deity existed. It is, in-fact, heresy to speculate on such matters. The all knowing God was this Deity.\n\nTruly none can describe the majesty of the Creator, the one and only. The Merciful Creator of the Seven Skys. With his intent, and only his intent, the World of Aegis was created.\n\nAll he had to do was simply want it to occur and it happened. The rolling seas, proud mountains and vast landscapes, all in a second, created by God.\n\nThe World was not created in play or for fun, but as a theater for the inhabitants of the World to be tested in. And tested for what? Well, that is for us to speculate.\n\nThough I believe it is to test us for purity, how hard we can fight temptation, and how clean and good can our actions be.\n\nThe first mortal being to be created was a man. This man was sculptured with clay and water, but left hollow. Soulless, God's creation lay there for the other immortal beings to see.\n\nThe Aengul and the Daemon, the two spirits created before the mortal beings and before the creation of Aegis. Not much is known about these two spirit races, but hopefully in the future we may know more.\n\nGod then blew his own breath of existance into the hollow shell and the Man lived. God then took the man and put him in the centre of all of Aegis, the same spot where The Cloud Temple now stands.\n\n God also made the Man a partner, from the exact same clay and the exact same breath, she was the first Woman. These simple beings could not talk, nor did they know much about the World. They roamed the world,\n\n enjoying its pre-historic beauty. Untouched, the fresh glades and dark thick forests were a wonder to set eyes on. Soon they had four children in the four corners of Aegis.\n\nMalin was the first of their sons, born in the deep forests of Aegis, he had blonde hair and sharp ears. Standing tall, he was the father of the Elves of Aegis. Krug was born in the scorching deserts of Aegis\n\n fierce and relentless, he was the father of the Orcs. In the fresh meadows of Aegis was born Horen, father of Humans. And lastly, in the deep underground caves of the World, Urguan was born, master of the Dwarves.\n\nWe do not know how they fathered sons and how their lines continued but perhaps God created a partner for each Son to continue their lines? Soon the first Man and Woman created by God departed Aegis\n\nand climbed the seven skies to meet with God their creator, for they had achieved their goal. The Sons began to explore the World of Aegis and each was stricken with grief at the loss of their parents,\n\n seeking to fill the chasm of emotion inside them they began to create civilisation.\n\nAlthough none of the descendants of each Son had neither their long lives or splendour,\n\nthey did carry their forefathers looks and traits. The Elves with Malin at their head travelled the lands and began naming all the inhabitants of this world, they were the first to create a tongue\n\nand began to name the trees and grass, the mountains and valleys across all of Aegis.\n\nAt this time, Malin, Urguan, Krug and Horen were not far apart in looks. Horen and Krug had darker hair than the other two and Krug was a bit larger in build\n\nMalin had blonde hair and Urguan had red. They were all close companions, friends and had much love for each other and each others people.\n\nUrguan travelled into the deep underground of the earth, naming the beautiful ores and gems, sharing it with his brethren. Krug travelled the harsh deserts, testing his stamina and strength against the environment of Aegis, and Horen began building\n\nthe first squalid huts and villages in the valleys. The people lived for hundreds of years, in harmony and peace. Until the day where one of the great Daemons, great worshipers and squalid protectors of the Void, kin spirits to the Aengul, defected.\n\nIblees, who was once the greatest of all of God’s worshippers, was cast down into the mortal world, and so he was about to destroy the tranquility of Aegis forever. He came in the shape of a man.\n\n A gentle being who loved things delicate, he was quickly befriended by Malin, Urguan and Horen. Krug had a great distrust of him but after many years of being convinced by his brothers, he finally accepted his company.\n\nLittle did Iblees know that this was all part of the Great God's plan to test the descendants of Man and Woman. Iblees wanted to destroy everything that God had designed and created,\n\n still not in a powerful physical form he began sowing seeds of evil in the minds of the Descendants. Prying on their needs and lusts.\n\nTo Malin, lover of nature, he promised him many children.\n\nHe promised him that their laughter would resonate across their forest homes and that he would be content. Malin did not want anything more in this world than a bountiful amount of young to occupy the barren forests.\n\nTo Urguan, he promised infinite wealth of Gems and Ores, colours and crystals he could never imagine. To this, Urguan’s greed failed him.\n\nHoren, was anxious he would not live long enough to see his city completed and his lands increased. He needed more time, and Iblees struck the chord and promised immortality.\n\nKrug was the last to be tricked, Iblees tempted him with wealth, with food, with power. None interested Krug. Iblees was furious, he then challenged Krug in open combat in fury.\n\nThe whole world witnessed Ibleeses transformation into a hideous beast of the void. His skin turned into scales, fangs erupted and dark horns exploded from his head. He grew large wings and began to breath terrible fire.\n\n All stood in shock and horror. The beast terrified all inhabitants of Aegis, animals fled and the Sons of the Descendants were too weak of blood to stand in front of such evil fury. Only Malin, Urguan, Horen and Krug stood in front of the terror.\n\nIn Iblees’ right hand, a sword wrought in flame. Krug, naked and unarmed charged at The Daemon, throwing himself against the scalding scales of Iblees. He screamed in agony as his skin burned and decayed,\n\nbecoming green and molten. Iblees was knocked off his feet, for the last time. He stood up, heaving in rage and fury, flames exploded from the pores of his skin, he roared in anger and where he struck his sword the land collapsed and the ground erupted.\n\nAnd Lo, great was the Valour of Urguan and the Strength of Krug as they fought the beast. Powerful were the arms that carried Krug’s Axe and strong was Horens’ heart as they fought in open combat upon the plains of Aegis\n\n Malin’s sword was fast and swift, smiting Iblees upon his breast and sides.\n\nFor the thirty years the Descendants fought Iblees, the world became a war torn mess\n\nThe dead were defiled, summoned from their graves to follow Iblees’ mastery. The more mortal descendants died, the greater Iblees’ armys grew.\n\nIt was a sad chapter in the World of Aegis, the brothers threw up their hands to God in prayer and in agony. Their creations destroyed, their children massacred.\n\nWoe to the Sons of Man and Woman. Krug grew bitter, his resentment of his brothers increased for befriending such a beast. Urguan grew greedy, he fought valourously but began to hide his treasures underground.\n\nMalin feared for the lives of his children and did not leave his forest sanctuary. It seemed the world was destined for ruin. Iblees grew in strength and the Descendants weakened. Until one day.\n\nOne day where the sound of horns could be heard from the far east. Beautiful horns echoing all across the skies of Aegis. God had heard their prayers.\n\nAn army of Aengul and Daemons from the seven skies had appeared on the grand shores of Aegis. Their majesty and beauty so strong that all The Descendants could see was a flash of power white light erupt from the east\n\nIblees’ soldiers burned where they stood, The thousands of skeletons and cadavers burst aflame and his living servants fled. There in the right middle of the world, he was cast down and chained by the Aengul and Daemon.\n\nA robed figure with brown hair and a black wreathed apparation stood before him. They were a terrible image to look at, all shyed down and bowed in their majesty and glory. They stood before Iblees,\n\nknowing he was immortal and could not be killed, sentencing him to banishment to the void where his dark soul would be forever trapped in torment.\n\nBut..\nBefore they could complete the sentence, Iblees stood tall for one last time.\n\n\n“You believe you may simply banish me from this plane and that my taint shall not last?\n\n I have touched this world with evil and it shall forever be part of the souls that inhabit it.\n\nMalin, I find no need to curse you, for so many of your kind have already willingly submitted to me now and will on my return in\n\nthe distant future. The curse you will bear, is not of my doing, but of your own. Even you have done my work, if you realize it or not.\n\nUrguan, your greed and lust shall overcome you, you are not worthy of the height God gave you,\n\nHoren, the immortality you hope for will never arrive. Your longevity has already been stripped away thanks to the labors of the twisted heart of your brother Malin. You will age quickly and die before you ever taste the fruits of your pointless labors.\n\nAnd you Krug, the most hated of the Descendants, you shall always have the lust of war. You are strong? Well the strength shall be used against your brothers, used to pillage and murder!\n\nYour lust for battle shall be unsatiated and your descendants shall grow ugly and heartless.”", ""),
        StaticBook("Immortal Spirits", "§bThurak'Yar", "Immortal Spirits\n\nA list of the Greater Spirits inhabiting the Immortal Realm and what they represent. This may change, given time, and will be updated as new spirits come to power.\n\nVotar- The spirit of the hunt, commonly shown as a giant, bloodied wolf. Praised by the Phrase Glougaz Votar.\n\n      /\\_==_/\\\n      | /o   o\\ |\n      \\         /\n       \\       /\n        \\ *.* /\n          \\ /\n\nEnrohk- The spirit of bloodlust, savagery and war, often shown as a bastard sword covered in blood. \n        \n         /\\\n         |  |\n         |  | \n         |....|\n         |. .|\n      `==8=='\n          ||\n\nShezept- The spirit of revenge, plots and stealth, depicted as a woman with a lower body of a snake. \n      \n        O\n       -|-\n   ^\\_\\\\_\n   \\____/\n\nJevex- Spirit of order, hard work and self sacrifice, shown as a giant hammer. He's a deadly enemy to the spirit Shezept.\n \n     [======]\n     [==|  |==]\n         ==\n         ==\n        |.--.|\n         ==\n\nIxli- The spirit of forbidden knowledge, truth and judgement. Depicted as an eye, he provides knowledge as the cost of sanity. \n\n       |/  \\|\n      |  (.)  |\n       |\\  /|\n\nOgrol- Spirit of despair, sapped strength and entrapement, shown as a cage full of writhing bodies. He tries to lock up all who summon him.\n\n       ______\n       | | |  | | |\n       =<< >>=\n       | | |  | | |\n      ========\n\nKor- The spirit of the dead, depicted as a cloaked man with an open hand.\n          _\n          |O\\\n           |-''\n          /\\\n\nAnkrus- Spirit of the sea, fishermen and safe travels, often shown as an aquatic woman wielding a spear. \n\n           () |\n           oo/\n           \\/\n           /\\\n           \\/\n\nArwa- Spirit of fertility, harvest and farming, commonly shown as a plump female with over seven hundred breasts.\n \n           ()\n         /.oo.\\\n        /o.oo.o\\\n        |o.o.o.o.o|\n        \\_____/\n         [|]    [|]\n\nAnyhuluz- Spirit of destruction, malicious intent and internal strife, shown as a warrior covered in open wounds.\n\n         O   ^\n       /(`)-`|\n         [|]\n         /\\\n\nAkezo- Spirit of health, vitality and healing, depicted as a winged whale surrounded by a blue aura.\n\n   (\\./)\n     \\\\_/--^--.\n      =.    \"~\"   )\n        \\______/\n\nPaxahru- Spirit of arrogance and stupidity, depicted as a boasting fool.\n\n        /**\\\n       ( ^^ ) \n        /--\\ \n        |`~~'|\n        ====\n        \\/\\/\n         |   |\n\nUblulhar- Spirit of hope an new beginnings, shown as a tree sapling bursting from the ground. \n\n       _____\n      {~~~~}\n     {~~~~~}\n      {~~~~}\n       {~| |~}\n          | |\n       ~~~~~\n\nGhorza- Spirit of travel, depicted as an ever turning wheel.\n\n       /----\\\n      / /--\\ \\\n      |  |<:::::)|  |\n      \\ \\--/ /\n       \\----/\n\nRolfizh- Spirit of assassinations and poison, depicted as a dagger with poison dripping from it.\n\n        /\\\n        |  | \n        |  |\n       =||||=\n         ||\n\nTrokorl- The spirit of engineering, machinery and building. Depicted as a goblin with a goblin with goggles and a hammer.\n\n       {o o}\n        \\-/P\n         |. |/\n         |. |\n         ==\n        /  \\\n\nLuara- The spirit of the moon, often shown by the symbol of a pale, white wolf.\n\n        \\___(>\n         |===/\n         |    |\n\nRamakhet- Spirit of the desert, barren lands and sand. He is never seen, and therefore is rarely ever depicted as something other than his name. He has sworn to scour the earth of all life for all time.\n       ~~~~~\n      Ramakhet\n       ~~~~~\n\nKrathol- The spirit of pain, suffering and starvation. Depicted as a pile of dry bones.\n   \n      (------)\n      (------)\n\nVeist- Spirit of illusion, tricks and thievery, depicted as a goblin with four eyes.\n\n      (:_:)\n      \\| |/\n       /\\\n       |  |\n\nFreygoth- Spirit of the wind and wild animals. He takes any form which he may desire. \n \n       ~~~~~\n        ~~~~\n         ~~~\n\nGlutros- The spirit of greed and obesity. He is depicted as a cake.\n   \n        , , , ,\n       |====|\n       |====|\n\nThulezia- Spirit of lust, pleasure and beauty, depicted as a naked slave girl. \n\n        (_)\n        |00|\n      ,/|   |\\,\n        !__!       \n       /   \\\n\nIsuz- Spirit of love, caring, and tranquility, depicted as an open-armed woman.\n\n       { . }\n       \\| |/\n         ||\n         ||\n\nOrgon- Spirit of Disease, Pestilence, and failure. Depicted as a festering, starving man with six arms, each holding a rusted and broken weapon.\n            (.)\n           |-||-|\n           i-||-i\n           !-||-!\n            /\\\n\nUrin- The spirit of rain and hail, depicted as a dark rain cloud.\n\n         *****\n       ********\n         *****\n         ` ` ` ` \n          ` ` `\n           ` ` `\n            ` `\n\nKesaroth- Spirit of envy and jealousy, usually shown as a golden bull.\n \n                 |__|\n                 ('')\n     /`-------\\/\n    / |         | |\n  '~  | |-----| |\n      ^^       ^^\n\nLeyd- Spirit of dominance and physical strength, symbolised by a closed fist.\n\n       ()()()\n        (   )\n         |**|\n         |**|\n\nScorthuz- The spirit of cleansing and purging, depicted as a vial of clear water.\n\n          (-)\n           | |\n         /--\\\n         |~~~|\n         |~~~|\n         ----\n\nTheruz- Spirit of intelligence and learning, shown as a wise old teacher. Brother to Betharuz.\n\n        ----\n       /     \\\n       | _  _ |\n     ('| O  O |`)\n       |  (.)  |\n       `/=.=\\'\n        \\/\\/\n\nBetharuz- Spirit of alcohol, cactus green and celebration, often depicted as a young dim man, in direct contrast to his brother, Theruz.\n\n       *, (.) U\n        \\_||_/\n           ||\n          /\\\n\nGentharuz- Spirit of smithing, smelting, industry and forging, depicted as a man with no legs, hammering an anvil.\n\n          (,)\n           ||--P\n         || ||   <==->\n         ||===| |==|\n         ||    || |++|\n\nDrelthok- Spirit of sleeping and dreams, often shown as a dark cloud.\n\n        %%%%%%\n       %%%%%%%\n        %%%%%%\n\nEathruz- Spirit of dawn and dusk, shown by the symbol of a red sun.\n\n       . .       . .\n       .\\       /.\n         \\\\ //\n         /|/\\|\\\n         \\|\\/|/\n         // \\\\\n      . /       \\ .\n      . .         . .\n\nKotrestruu- The spirit of memory and the recording of knowledge, depicted as an endless book.\n   \n       ______\n       ||=====/|\n       ||=====/|\n       ||=====/|\n       ||=====/|\n       ||=====/|\n       ||=====/\n\nLetrothak- The spirit of hindsight and excuses, shown as a goblin with eyes in the back of his head.\n\n        /----\\\n     \\\\| |  ||  | |//\n      \\,| (i)(i) |,/\n        \\      /\n         |      |\n       /        \\\n   __/ |       | \\__", ""),
        StaticBook("Zpirit Buuk", "§bShreck", "DUH ZPIRIT BUUK\n\n   rulg tew awl ub duh shomoz whu halped mi ryte diz buuk.\n\n  ELEMENTUL ZPIRITZ\n\n*Skathach-Zpirit ub fiya\n\n*Akathro-Zpirit ub watur\n\n*Bregthar-Zpirit ub earth\n\n*Fiarza-Zpirit ub wind agh air\n\n*Kulthark-Zpirit ub metul\n\n*Neizdark-Zpirit ub sturm\n\n*Binazdar-Zpirit ub lite\n\n*Radzanib-Zpirit ub darknuzz\n\n*Fanzra-Zpirit ub chaoz\n\n*Arznaf-Zpirit ub ordur\n\n*Ringat-Zpirit ub lief\n\n*Tagnir-Zpirit ub death\n\n*Omnizan-Zpirit ub zpace, intertwindud wif etz partnur, Sandru\n\n*Sandru-Zpirit ub tyme\n\n  IMMORTUL ZPIRITZ\n\n*Votar-Zpirit ub duh hunt\n\n*Enrohk-Zpirit ub bluudluzt, zavagery agh war\n\n*Shezept-Zpirit ub revunge, plotz agh stelth\n\n*Jevex-Zpirit ub ordur, hard wurk, agh zelf zakrifize\n\n*Ixli-Zpirit ub forbidun knowludge, truuf agh judgemunt\n\n*Ogrol-Zpirit ub dezpair, zapped strength agh entrapmunt\n\n*Kor-Zpirit ub duh flat\n\n*Ankrus-Zpirit ub duh zea, fishurmen agh zafe travulz\n\n*Arwa-Zpirit ub furtiluti, harvuzt agh farmun\n\n*Anyhuluz-Zpirit ub deztruction, malishush intunt, internul stryfe\n\n*Akezo-Zpirit ub helth, vitality agh heelun\n\n*Paxahru-Zpirit ub stewpudity agh arroganze\n\n*Ublulhar-Zpirit ub hope agh new beginunz\n\n*Ghorza-Zpirit ub travul\n\n*Rolfizh-Zpirit ub azzazzinashuns agh poizun\n\n*Trokorl-Zpirit ub engineerun, mashinery agh buldun\n\n*Luara-Zpirit ub duh moon\n\n*Ramakhet-Zpirit ub duh dezurt agh zand\n\n*Krathol-Zpirit ub payn zuffern agh ztarvashun\n\n*Veist-Zpirit ub illushun, trikz agh theivury\n\n*Freygoth-Zpirit ub duh wild agh wild animulz\n\n*Glutros-Zpirit ub greed agh beun phat\n\n*Thulezia-Zpirit ub luzt, plezure agh beautifulnuzz\n\n*Isuz-Zpirit ub lub, carun agh tranquiliti\n\n*Orgon-Zpirit ub dezease, pestilence agh failur\n\n*Urin-Zpirit ub rain agh hail\n\n*Kesaroth-Zpirit ub envi agh beun jelluz\n\n*Leyd-Zpirit ub dominanze agh strength\n\n*Scorthuz-Zpirit ub kleansing agh purgin\n\n*Theruz-Zpirit ub intelligunze agh lernun\n\n*Gentharuz-Zpirit ub zmithun, smeltun, induztry agh forgin\n\n*Ikuras-Zpirit ub fear agh inzanity\n\n*Drelthok-Zpirit ub zleepun agh dreemz\n\n*Eathruz-Zpirit ub dawn agh duzk\n\n*Kotrestruu-Zpirit ub memury agh duh rekordun uf knowludge\n\n*Lethrothak-Zpirit ub hindzite agh excuzuz\n\n*BETHARUZ-ZPIRIT UB BEER, GRIIN AGH CELEBRASHUN!!!!!!!\n\n   ANZESTRAL ZPIRITZ\n\n*Krug-Duh firzt shomo evur agh duh hozhuzt ork tew evur lib. Hi iz duh hozhuzt zpirit\n\n*Lur-Hozhuzt hunter en hiztury\n\n*Rax-Hozhuzt warriur ub awl tyme\n\n*Gorkil-Duh hozhiezt militari stratiguzt agh tactishun\n\n*Braduk-Ah faymed hozh chieftun\n\n*Dom-Duh zecund hozhezt shomo\n\n*Ugluk-A kub ub Gorkil, hozh warriur\n\n*Azog-Duh hozhezt smif en hiztory\n\n*Kurak-Alzo grukd az duh howlur ub Lur, wen zummond et kauzuz duh shomo tew tranzform intew ah half ork, half howlur, dey luze der mynd wile dey ar tranzfurmd\n\n*Tor-Knewn fur hiz kalm, kollective intellekt agh stronkth\n\n*Trickster-Knewn fur hiz zubterfuge\n\n*Bralkor-Knwen fur hiz rezuztunze tew physikul damag\n\n*Jarkun-Knewn fur hiz dexterity agh zpeed\n\n*Yar-Knewn fur hiz hozh wizdum agh zpirituality\n\n*Yaggo-Duh hozhezt ridur ub awl tyme\n\n*Dregort-Duh hozhezt boat buldur agh kaptun tew evur lib\n\n*Grumbah-Ah hozh buildur\n\n*Vargo-Knewn az beun ah veri hozh alchemizt\n\n  HOZH URUKZ\n\nShreck-Wun ub duh hozhiezt urukz tew lib, hi iz blu agh stronk agh zmart agh handzum agh kewl agh stronk\n\nReynuh-Ah hozhie peepun uruk whu iz veri stronk agh zmart\n\n*Wun-Ah veri stronk ogre\n\n*Glumpuzz-Ah nub'hozh uruk whu iz rehd", ""),
        StaticBook("Laws of Aym", "§bIrrin'Athri", "Laws of Aym\n\n1.Love\nLove is allowed between Aym and any follower of Aym. Marriage is allowed between any two people as long as you attempt to convert your spouse into believing upon Aym.\n\n2.Stealing\nStealing and looting is not allowed unless the one that you're stealing from one that's life has already been taken from them, but besides looting a corpse no stealing is permitted to any followers of Aym.\n\n3. Death\nDeath to any living being is permitted to all and any followers of Aym. If you're dying, then be happy about it, it is just a greeting from Aym. If you are to provide death upon another you are helping that being understand and believe in Aym\n\nthe only difference is that by providing the death upon a being will provide more of a challenge on their way to Aym.\n\n4. Respect\nYou are to respect Aym, his ways, and his followers and to disrespect the ways or followers of Aym is to disrespect Aym, if you're found disrespecting Aym you shall be punished. . .\n\n5. Pain\nPain is a blessing from Aym and if you are injured you shall not be healed unless the injury affects your ability to worship Aym. If you happen to be in pain you're allowed to request death and you can choose who you would like to be killed by\n\n6. Other Laws\n-No belief in any other god\n-Don't murder other followers\n-Attempt to help and provide for followers and Aym\n-Do not let  non-followers corrupt or convert your faith\n-Do be friendly and kind to all other followers", ""),
        StaticBook("On Shamanism V2", "§bThurak'Yar", "Volume Two of \nOn Shamanism, Scribed by the Farseer Thurak, 14'th of Sun's Smile, 1487.\n\n... The more they do this, the more word of their feats will spread among the Ancestral Plane and the more likely it is for these Spirits to accept their deals.\nWitchdocters go about things a bit differently than others. During the rituals,\n\nthey’ll present certain objects which hold relevance to the Spirit they are trying to evoke. For example, should they be trying to create a potion which causes the drinker to have insatiable hunger, they would perhaps need something\n\nsuch as the stomach of a large animal, such as a bull, or they may burn and char excessive amounts of savouries in hopes of gaining the attention of the spirit of Greed and Gluttony. Should they do so, they’ll begin to call to their desired\n\nspirit in the tongue of Old Blah, asking for their aid and promising that the attributes that they represent will somehow be more prominent after the use of said potion. Should the spirit agree, the mixture will be cursed, and the Witchdoctor can use\n\nuse it as he will on his unfortunate victims. However, these Shamans must be careful, as it’s sometimes not apparent how a spirit has cursed the potion, and unintended effects are rampant in the brews of unexperienced Witchdocters.\n\nFinally, the Farseers’ blessings are actually quite similar to that of the Lataumans’, in the way that it requires sharp tongue and wit to convince the spirits to give them their boon. When a Farseer decides he wishes to bless something, he must travel\n\nto the Spirit Realm and begin calling the name of the spirit that hold the attribute he needs for the blessing. Should the spirit answer, then the Farseer must try and convince it that the blessing will, in some way, benefit the spirit.\n\nIf this outright fails, and the Spirit refuses, a Farseer may do something else that no other Shaman may do; that being fight the spirit for his blessing. This is very dangerous, and is usually done only in times of desperation, as when a Shaman\n\ndies or is injured in the Spirit Realm, he dies or is injured back in the mortal realm. Once he’s either convinced the Spirit of the need of the blessing or beaten him in battle, it will then begin to take over a Shaman’s body. Back in the Mortal Realm\n\na Farseer’s limbs may start to move under its will, and words in Old Blah will be said to give the blessing direction. A mist, usually hued the colour that best represents the spirit doing the blessing, will pour from the Shaman's orifices\n\nand encompass the person, animal or plant he wishes to bless. When its finished, the Shaman need only to return his connection to his body and admire his handywork; the fabric of the mortal world being shifted in his favour, albeit in only a small front.", ""),
        StaticBook("Aengelic Theory", "§bDauth Sundavar", "The Aengudaemonic\n          Theories\n\n\n\n\n   By Dauth Sundavar\n\nTheory: The Aenguls and Daemons exist in realms beyond our own. Us mere mortals, possessed of magick, are able to breach those confines and access these realms. \n\nDear reader, I pray that you continue you on with this book and begin to speculate as\n\nI have, and not just discard it back to the shelf from whence it came. \n\nI shall discuss this theory in relation to the Aspects, and perhaps these ponderings may perhaps be applicable to other Aenguls known to us mortals.\n\n Chapter One: Aspects\n\nFrom my readings in the libraries of the Mali'aheral and the Druidic Order, I have come to the understanding that the Aspects exist within a dimension of existence beyond our own. Considering that the commune between the Aspects\n\nand their Druids is possible, I believe that there is some physical or metaphysical link between our world and theirs. \n\nBut where does this link manifest? Is it only possible through Druidic means, or is there a physical location where the barrier\n\nbetween our world and theirs is at its weakest? Is it possible the breach this barrier?\n\nIf there was to be a physical landmark, I would believe it to be what we regard as 'fairy rings'. In the writings of Kyral, a person unknown to me,\n\nthey note on their meditations within the aforementioned fairy rings, describing distinct humming sounds and sensations that came from both within and without. \n\nBased on this evidence, I believe that this is where the realms of the Aspects and the\n\nDescendents collide. \n\nBut is it possible to access this inextricable link? If so, can it only be done through Druidic means, or is it possible for those possessed of voidal magicks to harness this potential?\n\nI would like to believe that it is the\n\nlatter. However, it would be more than likely that if someone who possessed any access to the Void were to actually attempt this feat, they would be destroyed by the Aspects within an instant.\n\n Chapter Two: The Void\n\nTo my understanding, the Void is another dimension beyond our own, that is composed of completely limitless potential. It is, in essence, completely everything, yet nothing at the same time.\n\nWhilst this concept is difficult\n\nto comprehend, I would like to make the conjecture that considering the Void is everything, it is the crossroads in which all planes of existence align. \n\nTo my understanding, there is a voidal magic known as 'Voidal Shifting'. Through my readings, I have\n\ncome to understand that it grants the wielder of the magic to transport from one place to another, via the Void. \n\nMy theory is that if an individual is exceptionally talented in this field of magic, it could be perfectly possible for them to access the\n\nAengelic realms through the Void.\n\nAnother possibility that I have considered is that they are able also to breach the confines of these worlds through the links found in the fairy rings - the landmarks where the Aspects are linked with our reality.\n\nIf the one who attempts this feat has enough understanding of the realm of the Aspects, and is open to this concept, I have no doubt that they would be able to reach beyond this plane of existence and reach those of the Aspects, and by extension, the\n\nAenguls.\n\nHowever, this is only broad speculation, and is just a manifestation of my overactive imagination.\n\nIf you did read the entirety of this book, I commend you for your perserverance and tolerance towards my erratic thoughts.\n\nMay fate bring you an everlasting peace, and may you never have to suffer at the hands of the cruel injustices that reality may thrust upon you.\n\nWrit this day,\n\n22nd of The First Seed, 1534.", ""),
        StaticBook("Holy Scrolls - I", "§bJacob Chapel", "The Holy Scrolls\n\n     The Book of\n     Nativities\n\nTransscribed by Jacob Chapel during the Deep Cold, 1470.\n\nUse these words to keep true to your faith and to find strength and knowledge.\n\nThere were no planes of existence. There was nothing. Just the Void. And a bright shining light. It was not a light we could see, it was not really white either, but it would be the only way to explain the existence of such a Deity in contrast to the\n\nemptiness of the Void. None knows why, how or when this Deity existed. It is, in-fact, heresy to speculate on such matters. The all knowing God was this Deity.\n\nTruly none can describe the majesty of the Creator, the one and only. The Merciful Creator of\n\nthe Seven Skys. With his intent, and only his intent, the World of Aegis was created. All he had to do was simply want it to occur and it happened. The rolling seas, proud mountains and vast landscapes, all in a second, created by God.\n\nThe World was not created in play or for fun, but as a theater for the inhabitants of the World to be tested in. And tested for what? That is for us to speculate. Though I believe it is to test us for purity, how hard we can fight temptation, and how\n\nclean and good can our actions be.\n\nThe first mortal being to be created was a man. This man was sculptured with clay and water, but left hollow. Soulless, God's creation lay there for the other immortal beings to see. The Aengul and the Daemon,\n\nthe two spirits created before the mortal beings and before the creation of Aegis. Not much is known about these two spirit races, but hopefully in the future we may know more.\n\nGod then blew his own breath of existance into the hollow shell and\n\nthe Man lived. God then took the man and put him in the centre of all of Aegis, the same spot where The Cloud Temple now stands. God also made the Man a partner, from the exact same clay\n\nand the exact same breath, she was the first Woman. These simple beings could not talk, nor did they know much about the World. They roamed the world, enjoying its pre-historic beauty. Untouched, the fresh glades and dark thick forests were a wonder to\n\nset eyes on. Soon they had four children in the four corners of Aegis.\n\nMalin was the first of their sons, born in the deep forests of Aegis, he had blonde hair and sharp ears. Standing tall, he was the father of the Elves of Aegis. Krug was born in the\n\nscorching deserts of Aegis, fierce and relentless, he was the father of the Orcs. In the fresh meadows of Aegis was born Horen, father of Humans. And lastly, in the deep underground caves of the World, Urguan was born, master of the Dwarves.\n\nSoon the first Man and Woman created by God departed Aegis and climbed the seven skies to meet with God their creator, for they had achieved their goal. The Sons began to explore the World of Aegis and each was stricken with grief at the loss of their\n\nparents, seeking to fill the chasm of emotion inside them they began to create civilisation.\n\nAlthough none of the descendants of each Son had neither their long lives or splendour, they did carry their forefathers looks and traits. The Elves with Malin\n\nat their head travelled the lands and began naming all the inhabitants of this world, they were the first to create a tongue and began to name the trees and grass, the mountains and valleys across all of Aegis.\n\nAt this time, Malin, Urguan, Krug and\n\nHoren were not far apart in looks. Horen and Krug had darker hair than the other two and Krug was a bit larger in build. Malin had blonde hair and Urguan red. They were all close companions, friends and had much love for each other and each others people.\n\nUrguan travelled into the deep underground of the earth, naming the beautiful ores and gems, sharing it with his brethren. Krug travelled the harsh deserts, testing his stamina and strength against the environment of Aegis, and Horen began building the\n\nfirst squalid huts and villages in the valleys. The people lived for hundreds of years, in harmony and peace. Until the day where one of the great Daemons, great worshipers and squalid protectors of the Void, kin spirits to the Aengul, defected.\n\nIblees, who was once the greatest of all of God’s worshippers, was cast down into the mortal world, and so he was about to destroy the tranquility of Aegis forever. He came in the shape of a man. A gentle being who loved things delicate, he was quickly\n\nbefriended by Malin, Urguan and Horen. Krug had a great distrust of him but after many years of being convinced by his brothers, he finally accepted his company.\n\nLittle did Iblees know that this was all part of the Great God's plan to test the\n\ndescendants of Man and Woman. Iblees wanted to destroy everything that God had designed and created, still not in a powerful physical form he began sowing seeds of evil in the minds of the Descendants. Prying on their needs and lusts.\n\nTo Malin, lover of nature, he promised him many children. He promised him that their laughter would resonate across their forest homes and that he would be content. Malin did not want anything more in this world than a bountiful amount of young to\n\noccupy the barren forests.\n\nTo Urguan, he promised infinite wealth of Gems and Ores, colours and crystals he could never imagine. To this, Urguan’s greed failed him.\n\nHoren, was anxious he would not live long enough to see his city completed and his lands increased. He needed more time, and Iblees struck the chord and promised immortality.\n\nKrug was the last to be tricked, Iblees tempted him with wealth, with food,\n\nwith power. None interested Krug. Iblees was furious, he then challenged Krug in open combat in fury.\n\nThe whole world witnessed Ibleeses transformation into a hideous beast of the void. His skin turned into scales, fangs erupted and dark horns exploded\n\nfrom his head. He grew large wings and began to breath terrible fire. All stood in shock and horror. The beast terrified all inhabitants of Aegis, animals fled and the Sons of the Descendants were too weak of blood to stand in front of such evil fury.\n\nOnly Malin, Urguan, Horen and Krug stood in front of the terror.\n\nIn Iblees’ right hand, a sword wrought in flame. Krug, naked and unarmed charged at The Daemon, throwing himself against the scalding scales of Iblees. He screamed in agony as his skin\n\nburned and decayed, becoming green and molten. Iblees was knocked off his feet, for the last time. He stood up, heaving in rage and fury, flames exploded from the pores of his skin, he roared in anger and where he struck his sword the land collapsed and\n\nthe ground erupted. And Lo, great was the Valour of Urguan and the Strength of Krug as they fought the beast. Powerful were the arms that carried Krug’s Axe and strong was Horens’ heart as they fought in open combat upon the plains of Aegis. Malin’s sword\n\nwas fast and swift, smiting Iblees upon his breast and sides.\n\nFor the thirty years the Descendants fought Iblees, the world became a war torn mess. The dead were defiled, summoned from their graves to follow Iblees’ mastery. The more mortal descendants\n\ndied, the greater Iblees’ armys grew. It was a sad chapter in the World of Aegis, the brothers threw up their hands to God in prayer and in agony. Their creations destroyed, their children massacred. Woe to the Sons of Man and Woman. Krug grew bitter,\n\nhis resentment of his brothers increased for befriending such a beast. Urguan grew greedy, he fought valourously but began to hide his treasures underground. Malin feared for the lives of his children and did not leave his forest sanctuary. It seemed the\n\nworld was destined for ruin. Iblees grew in strength and the Descendants weakened. Until one day.\n\nOne day where the sound of horns could be heard from the far east. Beautiful horns echoing all across the skies of Aegis. God had heard their prayers.\n\nAn army of Aengul and Daemons from the seven skies had appeared on the grand shores of Aegis. Their majesty and beauty so strong that all The Descendants could see was a flash of power white light erupt from the east. Only Malin with his strong sight\n\ncould see the figures of light as they sped across the skies towards the armies of Iblees.\n\nIblees’ soldiers burned where they stood, The thousands of skeletons and cadavers burst aflame and his living servants fled. There in the right middle of the\n\nworld, he was cast down and chained by the Aengul and Daemon. A robed figure with brown hair and a black wreathed apparation stood before him. They were a terrible image to look at, all shyed down and bowed in their majesty and glory. They stood before\n\nIblees, knowing he was immortal and could not be killed, sentencing him to banishment to the void where his dark soul would be forever trapped in torment.\n\nBut before they could complete the sentence, Iblees stood tall for one last time.\n\n“You believe you may simply banish me from this plane and that my taint shall not last? I have touched this world with evil and it shall forever be part of the souls that\n\ninhabit it.\n\nMalin, I find no need to curse you, for so many of your kind have already willingly submitted to me now and will on my return in the distant future. The curse you will bear, is not of my doing, but of your own. Even you have done my work,\n\nif you realize it or not.\n\nUrguan, your greed and lust shall overcome you, you are not worthy of the height God gave you, your descendants shall be short, squalid and ugly. You will always seek to find Gold and Gems in the deep underground of the earth,\n\nyour hunger never satisfied.\n\nHoren, the immortality you hope for will never arrive. Your longevity has already been stripped away thanks to the labors of the twisted heart of your brother Malin. You will age quickly and die before you ever taste the\n\nfruits of your pointless labors.\n\nAnd you Krug, the most hated of the Descendants, you shall always have the lust of war. You are strong? Well the strength shall be used against your brothers, used to pillage and murder! Your lust for battle shall be\n\nunsatiated and your descendants shall grow ugly and heartless.”\n\nAs soon as his words were uttered, the world grew suddenly dark. A great roar erupted from Iblees’ mouth and in a blast of fire he was banished from the Mortal World. The Descendants stood\n\nstill for several moments, pondering over the words of Iblees. Malin, Krug, Urguan and Horen all knew naught of what the great Devil had spoken of when concerning Malin’s betrayal. Dismissing it as the lies of a demon propagated to corrupt their souls,\n\nthe brothers thought nothing further of Malin’s supposed treason.\n\nThe robed figure stood before the four brothers.\n\n“There is nothing in my power that may undo what that evil did. There is one thing I may do however, and that is to bless you.\n\nMalin, may your forests be a sanctuary of peace and your children long lived.\n\nUrguan, may your descendants be hardy, strong hearted and strong of mind.\n\nKrug, may your people show valour and honour where none exist.\n\nAnd Horen, may your people one day explore the Seven Skies.\n\nI can do no more, for all my power is spent. We will go back to our master now, I believe your children may see us once more but long in the future.”\n\nAnd with that, the great host of Aenguls and Daemons disappeared into thin wisps in the air.", ""),
        StaticBook("Dwed Clergy V1", "§bMiscellus", "THE FIRST MEETING OF THE DWARVEN CLERGY\n\n9TH OF THE SUN'S SMILE, 1520\n\nHigh Prophet Hogarth Irongut began the meeting by speaking of uniforms. He stated concisely that Demagol Doomforged is able to supply\n\nuniforms & robes to any who might need them, as a unifying symbol is key. The robes themselves are symbolic of your patron god, and of your own personality; the trim differs from Dwed to Dwed, allowing a bit of a look into their own state of mind.\n\nFor a quick note:\nOgrahad is green. \nWyrvun is teal. \nDungrimm is dark red.\nGrimdugan is gold.\nArmakak is also gold, as they both represent merchants. Yemekar is also gold, though his gold represents divinity rather than wealth.\n\nAnbella is orange. \nBelka is the same red as Dungrimm. \n\nThe next matter of business was the newly constructed temple. It was confirmed that all future sermons and meetings would be held here, directly above Kal'Akash.\n\nA road was confirmed to be in planning for construction, though the date is unknown for when it shall be finished, as the hike up the mountain is steep and inhospitable for a well-paved road. \nIn normal Dwarven fashion, the material of the road was argued\n\nover vehemently; the king yelled gold, a man yelled blackstone, and another simple lapis. One dwarf even dared to recommend simple gravel! There was no conclusion met by the end of the topic. \n\nDemagol and Conan were assigned to work on the road, and the\n\nrest of the volunteers were set to construct vast shrines to the Brathmordakin. The Doomforged's patron, Dormarr, has already been constructed as an effigy; the rest shall be scattered around the kingdom in places that exude their greatness.\n\nGauldrim Irongut was then promoted to the status of priest due to his help in construction of the church and the altar of Ograhad. \n\nWe then went towards the ritual site; hidden under the arena, this water-based altar was filled with\n\nglowstone, fire, and a mouth to the ocean, allowing water to flow in freely. Its isolation shows the introverted and introspective power of Ograhad; its fire shows the hidden passion and ambition of the scholar-god. Magical glowstone represents his arcane\n\narts, and runes flow throughout the walls, though I know not their meaning. \n\nHogarth assured that this was a great altar, though we should not copy it in making our own: it was the only one he's ever seen hidden from the public eye, and each\n\ngod should have individual artifacts and runes inscribed for their offerings. We were told to speak to him if we ever had an idea for our own places of worship, or sermons to spread to the people of Urguan. \n\nHe ended the meeting by bringing up an\n\nunnerving topic: the ex-High Prophet, Beros Oathcast. He has been attempting to start his own church, to undermine Dwarven authority and religion; Hogarth, obviously, does not like this, and wishes to put a stop to it, for he will not come and debate or\n\nspread his ideas with honor. He says to contact him if Beros ever tries to speak to any of us and to let him handle it, though I wonder if that will truly work. From what I understand, Beros has quite the silver tongue... \n\nAlgoda Frostbeard recommended\n\nHogarth make an official statement, a first move against Beros and the heretics. The Irongut was hesitant, but assured us that he would figure out a solution soon. \n\nThe meeting ended with a reminder to start our assigned temples and not to linger.\n\nThe first meeting of the Dwarven clergy was a success, and I hope each meeting afterwards can be just as productive!", ""),
        StaticBook("30 Strictures", "§bQuavinir", "Stricture 1: One must never find joy in the death of any man, woman, or child. \n\nStricture 2: It is right for one to be bound in matrimony as to create lifemates, but one must never force such a sacred act on another.\n\nStricture 3: One must never make love outside of marriage.\n\nStricture 4: One must restrict themselves from gluttony and drunkenness.\n\nStricture 5: One must restrict themselves from un-marital lust and intercourse.\n\nStricture 6: One must restrict themselves from taking what is not theirs.\n\nStricture 7: One must restrict their tounge from lashing words, for words are made to build up, not harm.\n\nStricture 8: One must restrict themselves from needlessly striking out against someone.\n\nStricture 9: One must not allow one's lust for revenge overtake them. Just retribution may be needed, but vengeance is reserved for the Gods.\n\nStricture 10: One must not deceive or harm one another with words.\n\nStricture 11: One must not abandon those whom trust one for support.\n\nStricture 12: One must allow their hearts to not be apathetic to their brother's burdens.\n\nStricture 13: One must respect their family, for they're of same blood.\n\nStricture 14: One must not show hatred to anyone due to their race, their age, or gender.\n\nStricture 15: One must restrict from the use of substances which muffle and cloud the emotions as to use to deal with their pain.\n\nStricture 16: One must support all whom are widowed, lame, or otherwise unable to support themselves.\n\nStricture 17: One must not harm any man, or woman without the cause of just retribution.\n\nStricture 18: One must be submit under the discernment of others as to have a understanding of what is brought by justice, and what is brought by anger.\n\nStricture 19: One must keep themselves accountable to their spouses. And likewise hold their spouses accountable.\n\nStricture 20: One must not allow one's anger to provoke trouble, and pain.\n\nStricture 21: One must allow themselves to be counted as equal unless proven otherwise by content of character.\n\nStricture 22: One must not challenge or disobey those whom are appointed to authority.\n\nStricture 23: One must find mercy within themselves to be patient with the mistakes of others.\n\nStricture 24: One must find joy in whatever he has, whether little, or great.\n\nStricture 25: One must find the purest of joys from their spouse.\n\nStricture 26: It is in due right, and in way of nature that those bound in marriage should enjoy the privileges of sexual intercourse and relations.-\n\n-Yet, it is to be kept between the 2 lifemates, not extending to others and creating such things as orgies or brothels, for they are detestable and disgusting.\n\nStricture 27: One must support and nurture their spouse as they would themselves.\n\nStricture 28: One must love their spouse more than any worldly thing, or person.\n\nStricture 29: One must not hide their true nature from their spouse. For it is the true nature of lifemates that must be bound together.\n\nStricture 30: One must honor and follow the strictures if they wish to live a pure life, contented, and compassionately.", ""),
        StaticBook("Brathmordakin", "§bMiscellus", "THE BRATHMORDAKIN: A GUIDE\n\nThe Dwarven Gods are diverse and plentiful. This book is an attempt to catalogue their various powers, domains, and personalities for all to read. \n\nBrathmordakin, roughly\n\ntranslated, means House of the Gods. The exact translation is unknown.\n\nAt the head of this pantheon is Yemekar, the creator. He smithed the Dwarves in his image, created the other races, and is the patron of smithing and creation. Very few living\n\nDwedmar (Dwarves), if any, have spoken to him. It seems that they are mostly intangible figures that only the greatest of clergymen can truly speak to.\n\nWyrvun is a god of frost, virtue, and righteousness. He takes the form of a frost drake when on\n\nthe mortal plane, and is one of the only examples of a god with a mortal avatar. He is also what the other races know as an Aeungul, though the Dwarven clergy does not seem to believe that all Aenguls are gods or vice versa. \n\nThe third of the\n\nBrathmordakin is Anabella. She is the mother figure of the gods, with domains of peace, nature, and nurturing. She values the home and comforting and brings rest to restless dwarves. \n\nDungrimm is the \"Guardian of the Dead.\" He is what\n\nmost men would call a Daemon, though he is still good: his domains are of battle, valor, bravery, courage, defense, the dead, and the moon, and he never waivers in the face of adversity. He believes in the power of life and all that it brings, and,\n\nbeing a god of death, disdains the needless throwing away of life. He is a good man, and I believe in his tenets. \n\nBelka is the capricious Lady of Passion. A loving and romantic god, she believes in mercy, safety, passionate emotion, and full\n\nembracement of life through courtship and marriage. \n\nOgradhad is a master of knowledge: god of scholars, invention, discovery, creativity, and magic, he displays the industrial mind the Dwarves are so well-known for. He is the god of\n\nmages and tinkers alike, admiring a diverse mental portfolio and the preservation of history.\n\nArmakak is the merchant. He is a god easily seen within the roots of the Dwedmar: the patron of merchants and thieves alike,\n\nhe embodies avarice, greed, ambition, and a need for more. He both represents strength and stealth, and will do anything to get what he wants. Wealth, to Grimdugan, is paramount. Always pay your debts back to a Dwarf.\n\nFor more, read Da Ord Nar Kadrin, found in the Dwarven cities.\n\n(( https://www.lordofthecraft.net/topic/131576-da-kirkja-dverga-the-dwarven-clergy/\n))", ""),
        StaticBook("Wilven Monks", "§bKirrekith", "Wilven Monks\nBy Argyll\nPages 50\n\nNotice, this book is a rushed copy of a tome in the Library of the Orkish Capital, as made during the raid of the 16th of the Grand Harvest, 1532. The following is no way a complete or a perfect copy of said book.\n\nPart I\nThe History of Keldrith\n\nLord of the Sun, this story did start like most stories of mortals. He was the child of the King, King Celetiman of Celestrian, this was a kingdom in Asulon, the cities name being Arethor. This made him prince to\n\nthe throne. The story starts with Keldrith had become a teen (13). Thousands of villagers lived and thrived inside the kingdom, and all did so love Keldrith. This was due to him being kind and a charasmatic individual. With time the prince in turn found\n\nlove in a princess.\n\nKeldrith's Love\nThe princess was known to be Rellenia, of a small city-state outside the Kingdom. He saw her when she wasn't attending a political debate. He saw her supposedly purchasing wheat from a farmer for twice the\n\nvalue? (Questionable at best) The prince became fascinated by her beauty and heart. In turn he went to the king to arrange a marriage, his name being Taron. This was approved and marriage set between Rellenia and Kledrith. It would be assumed the princess\n\nwould find distaste with this, however she did not, quite the opposite, as she had loved him in a similar manner since meeting him. With that they were happily wed, and Kledrith became King. When time was right they went into the world, leaving their\n\nmilitary leader in charge. Time proceeded, Keldrith was 26, and Rellenia 24, now visited the hamlet of Flowershire, and met a man called Tariel who fell into friendship with the farmer, who was a man of kindness. As they left Tariel joined them on their\n\ntravels. The trio travlled the length of Asulon, and became close friends in their many adventurers. There story did meet a sour end as they reached a wasteland in Asulon, described to be scorched mountains. A great deal is not known about this mountain\n\nor valley, besides a farmer who lived close by, claiming a flash of light occurred after they entered. In truth they died here or were murdered, their items stolen besides one left by each. It was claimed that the Creator (false) took pity, binding them\n\ntogether by important minerals. Rellenia was a diamond, Keldrith a bar of gold and Tariel was lapis lazuli. The farmer waited as he believed he knew what occurred to the trio. The wasteland took life aroun their stones. Water flowed, grass grew and it\n\ntook to life. The grass was a island in the centre, with stones laying upon the shore. The creator (false) ensured it would be enjoyed by people, and so two arrived, a woman and man. The man was called William Alerus, and woman Vera Alerus. The duo never\n\nknew of the stone, and not until their child went to the shore was it discovered. Neither could the parents touch it, so the child Amanda did so by scooping them in her arms. Light erupted from them, thrown into the skys matching their colours in nature.\n\nThe gold bore the sun, lapis the stars and diamond the moon. The creator admired the friendship of the former three. With this the knowledge of them was left onto the family living on the shore. The creator (false) told them to build a temple in the trios\n\nhonour, once a bird was trained they sent word to their family and friends. They spoke of the paradise and the price to pay. They brought over all the materials they could carry for the Temple of the Triumvirate. The work was soon progressed with it\n\nand became a great library. These first residents were known as the Monks of the Triumvirate. Though the first trio were known to be Keldrith, Rellenia and Tariel and so called the Celestial Triumvirate. With this they became Demi-gods to their cult. Gods\n\nto the sun, moon and stars. They gave unto their followers the ability to heal those when invoking their names in prayer. Those most dedicated to their religion and false deities could heal those through force of mental focus. The Templed became blessed\n\nwhen it was completed, in the hopes that it would be void of disease and illness and forever a safe haven. With time the monks forgot the ways of the first, and became lazy and ignorant to the needs of others besides their own. The original three became\n\nangered by this, angered enough to banish the monks from this temple, magic preventing them from entering the valley for as long as they bore life. People were kept from entering until an adventurer known as Wilven Adonan.\n\nWilven Adonan\n\nA young human adventurer was to be the first to originally come to the land through the portal he did become afflicted with a curse. With determination he set out to cure himself of the ailment. He travelled through swamp, mountains and\n\nforests. With this did arrive at the mountain range and valley. At the start of the mountain he found a collapsed tunnel, and so made his way through it (Error?) and discovered what lay on the otherside.  This place was different to the world before, and\n\nhis affliction didn't bare weight in this place. Trees and grass met him, waterfalls and mountainscape. The structure was alien to him, yet even in their destroyed state held beauty. Meeting several larges arches he felt himself feeling in better health.\n\nHowever, soon after reaching the arches he discovered a hidden tunnel behind a wall, following it he found a large library, nearly untouched by natures wrath outside. Reading the tomes he swiftly found that this was built a great deal of time ago. He\n\nsoon found this was the place of worship for the Celestial Triumvirate which were Rellenia, Keldrith and Tariel and watched over the valley. Wishing to find out more he delayed himself he returned to those ill from the curse. \n\nBy the time he returned he\n\nwas met with a great deal of ill, more so than he had prior witnessed. Gathering those with the ability to move he brought to the valley, sending word then to the Orcs, Elves and the Dwarves to aid there ill. However when they returned they brought fewer\n\nstill.\n\nThe survivors for many years tried to make the valley a home, and restore it to its previous form. When Wilven neared his end he told the people of the celestrial trio, and emphasized they build a shrine to worship them.\n\nBecoming a monastic order the group came together to keep from the rest of the world. They chose Wilven to be there Abbott, and with time continued to live in peace in the valley. The monks continued in they're prayer in the hopes the world would be\n\ncleansed of its curse, and with Wilven's death the cult honoured his life by naming the valley after him, naming it the Wilven Sanctuary.\n\nPart II\nLaws of being a Monk.\n\nI. A monk must be neutral in all actions, including war.\nII. Monks remain kind and happy in the face of anger and ones own anger.\nIII. Monks robes must remain unaltered in appearance beside if trimming the robes\n\nlength, the colour may be changed to that of the trio.\nIV. A monk may furnish his room, but not anothers (Possible error, if not a pointless rule). \nV. A Monk may never sell goods for personal gain or profit unless in the case of benefitting the Sanctuary\n\nand not individual.\nVI. Monks are obligated to give food, clothing and shelter to those in need.\nVII. If a argument arises and no answer can be found, the Elder is to be consulted and his word is to end the argument.\nVIII. If one breaks the code of the\n\ncult, the individual must have his robes taken from him and his duties removed.\n\nPart III\nHealing\n\nThe healing is performed through prayer to the three. Those who lack faith may not call upon this. Following on from prayer, the lengthier the prayer the stronger the result.\n\nAnother way to call\n\nupon this power is to chant the name of the three like a cult may they're prophet or deity, though this is low in power. \n\nThe hardest way to call upon the power is through silence, and a artform of a master. The healing conducted normally comes through\n\nthe one they're praying to.\n\nIf one followed Keldrith, the glow would follow his mineral and be golden in nature.\n\nOne of Tariel would be the colour of purple (possible error? his mineral was lapis a blue).\n\nNo record shows evidence of Rellenia's powers effects or colour, likely not mentioned as of her gender and would give power to the women. \n\nResurrection is an obscure branch of healing, but to monks is key. It is difficult to master, and requires the most\n\neffort of all prayer based healing. \n\nOnce a monk has mastered silent healing, he may sense the souls of those inside the temple. And with this, a monk can revive these souls. Outside of the temple resurrection becomes much more difficult and requires a\n\nvast ammount of ones energy (do they mean prayer?). It usually means a great many sessions of prayer.\n\nPassive healing is the least known arts of a monks healing. When a monk achieves resurrection they can achieve self healing, often without noticing it.\n\nIf a fatal wound was to occurr sparks of the trios power will hue from the wound and bubble from it until healed. (Dependant on which of the trio is chosen.)\n\nChoosing a Triumvirate\nNone of the trio have a greater deal power than the next, however\n\nthe choice of ones deity does often show ones personality (Likely error as the three show no sign of being any different from the next.) However one effect is the colour of ones prayer and power, Keldrith being gold, Tariel lapis purple (error as stated\n\nbefore) and Rellenia a diamond blue (Now the book mentions her influence in the faith, possible error in the first writing of it).\n\nFinally the achievement of ones robes occurs once a initiate becomes a monk. To become a monk, they first must be a\n\ninitiate and often requires them to study the trio and farm. Once a initiate appears ready the Elder Monk is to tet the individual in their knowledge. If they pass they receive their robes and become full monks. \nAs written by Monk Argyll.\n\nRe-wrote by Kirrekith Reffahc of Grandaxe, the documentation is different to the first book, and has been annotated in places where the information is questionable.\n\nAs recorded and documeted by Kirrekith.", ""),
        StaticBook("Holy Scrolls -II", "§bJacob Chapel", "The Holy Scrolls\n\n     The Book of\n     Horen\n\nTransscribed by Jacob Chapel during the Amber Cold, 1471.\n\nUse these words as a source of strength and widom.\n\nThe years passed and went on and on, as time is wont to do. The Decendant, after being cursed by the Great Devil, adapted their lives to suit their new roles. There was, for some time, peace and prosperity amongst the original realms created by the\n\nCreator. One day, upon the cusp of which a red sun rose from the east, King Horen of humanity was approached by an Aengul, a divine messenger of God. This Aengul shone magnificently, illuminated by the light of God, its shades and colours twisting\n\naround one another as it addressed Horen. Its wings swathed in blinding white, it opened its mouth and through it the Creator spoke.\n“Horen of Man. God has chosen you as his divine prophet, as his mouthpiece on this world, until the day of\n\nyour ascent into the Seven Skies at his order. You are the almighty king, the Creator’s chiefmost servant on this hallowed earth. Be jubilant, for you are second to none but me! Humanity is the holy divine being of God, the human body remaining the most\n\nunadulterated, original form of the Creator’s design, not possessing of neither corruption nor mutation. You are the chosen people, and you shall carry out my commands, and your people alone, not your brothers and their kin, shall swear to my commandments\n\nThey are as follows!\n\n-I believe with perfect faith that the Creator, Blessed be His Name, is the Creator and Guide of everything that has been created; He alone has made, does make, and will make all things.\n\n-I believe with perfect faith that the Divine\n\nare the Creator’s regents in this realm and that their obedience is worship to the Creator.\n\n-I believe with perfect faith that the Aenguls and Daemons are the Creator’s servants.\n\n-I believe with perfect faith that the Creator\n\nBlessed be His Name, is One, and that there is no unity in any manner like His, and that He alone is our God, who was, and is, and will be.\n\n-I believe with perfect faith that the Creator, Blessed be His Name, has no body, and that He is free from all\n\nproperties of matter, and that there can be no comparison to Him whatsoever.\n\n-I believe with perfect faith that the Creator, Blessed be His Name, is the first and the last.\n\n-I believe with perfect faith that the Creator, Blessed be His Name,\n\nand to Him alone, it is right to pray, and that it is not right to pray to any being besides Him.\n\n-I believe with perfect faith that all the words of the Divines are true.\n\n-I believe with perfect faith that the\n\nprophecy of Horen I our teacher, blessings be upon him, was true, and that he was the chief of the Divines, both those who preceded him and those who followed him.\n\n-I believe with perfect faith that the entire scrolls that is now in our possession is the\n\nsame that was given to our Divines, blessings be upon them.\n\n-I believe with perfect faith that the Holy Scrolls can not be corrupted\n\n-I believe with perfect faith that the Creator, Blessed be His Name, knows all the deeds\n\nof beings and all their thoughts.\n\n-I believe with perfect faith that the Creator, Blessed be His Name, rewards those who keep His commandments and punishes those that transgress them.\n\n-I believe with perfect faith that there will\n\nbe a revival of the dead at the time when it shall please the Creator, Blessed be His Name, and His mentions shall be exalted for ever and ever.”\n\nAnd so Horen listened, and he repeated the Creator’s words, and he carried the word\n\nof the Creator to his people, who listened as he did. They kneeled before Horen, first of the Divine, and pledged the commandments and supremacy of humanity over all but God. The realmn of man rallied underneath the Regent of Heven, arming themselves and\n\nerecting great fortresses, beginning the golden age of the Horen dynasty. For truly, it was decreed that humanity was second to none but the Creator, and the chose people would carry out his command until the end of time.", ""),
        StaticBook("Basic Ethics", "§bGlacio Nereus", "Motivism, Deontological, and Consequence theories of ethics.\n\nFirst, the consequence theory dictates that an action is either right or wrong based on the consequences of any given action. An example is if a man kills another man.\n\nman. The action would be right if the consequences were good, such as saving the lives of five good people, or bad, such as if the man who was killed were to have saved the world. Utilitarianism is based off of consequentialism,\n\nand the basic point of utilitarianism is an action is good if it brings about the greatest amount of pleasure, and the least amount of pain, for the largest number of people. Issues with this type of utilitarianism though are that one can not see the\n\nthe consequences of the future until they have occured, and one can commit heinous evils if others were to be pleasured by the action. An example of the first argument would be if a man is in a comatose state, and others work to keep him alive,\n\nalive, killing him would be what a utilitarian \nof this type would argue to be the good action. However, the effort that is no longer keeping him alive \ncan be used to aid others, but this condones murder of of the comatose person. The other argument\n\nis the \nsoldier analogy. A soldier fighting in a war decides to spare a fleeing enemy since the enemy is \nunarmed, wounded, and in no state to fight back. However, this soldier later kills fifty innocent men. \nAlthough the motive for sparing\n\nsparing the man was a good motive, the consequence in the long run was ill, \ndespite being unable to foretell such a result. This would make the soldier who spared the enemy guilty of what he could not foretell.  Another type of utilitarianism is rule\n\nRule utilitarianism. Rule utilitarianism is different in how it attempts to reconcile consequentialism and deontology. This formsays an action is morally justified if an act conforms to a rule, and leads to the greatest good.\n\nFor example, if a man follows a rule of to not murder, then his act is good, even if one he may have murdered will do so himself. So, two forms of consequentialism are utilitarianism and rule utilitarianism.\n\nContinuing the discussion of ethical theories is deontology. The deontological theory states an action's is based not on the consequences or the motives of an action, but rather the action in it of itself. An example would be if a man were to fight\n\ncriminals. criminals. This man's actions may be fighting for good, but he is bad due to the act of fighting being morally wrong. An example of a good action would be being charitable. charitable. If a man were to donate money to a man who uses money for\n\nhedonistic acts, it would still be a good action, and the man a good man. This is because deontology states that the act, \ncharity, is good, so the man doing the act is good. There are more nuances within this theory however, but they will not be\n\ndiscussed in this book. Finally, motivism is the theory that an action is either morally permissable or morally wrong based on the motives of an action. An example would be if the previously mentioned soldier spared his foe. The action would be considered\n\nconsidered morally permissable since the act of not killing him, and the reason for it, is good. A good. A more direct way to follow motivism is the idea to universalize a given maxim, and see if it can be ordered as a universal law. An example would be\n\nif the soldier spared the enemy once more. The action would be considered morally permissable since the act of not killing him, and the reason for it, is good. A more direct way to follow motivism is the idea to universalize a given maxim, and see if it\n\ncan be universalized as a universal law. law. An example would be the action of stealing. The maxim would be 'I should steal.' If one universalized the law, then everybody should steal. If everyone were to steal, the idea of stealing itself collapses,\n\nfor stealing is the act of taking something that is not rightfully yours. Now, there is no object or thing that is rightfully anyone's since all is being stolen, and all is gained by stealing. Therefore, this view of motivism states\n\nstealing is never morally permissable. Other actions with this include murder, lieing, suicide, and many more. An example of a morally permissable act by this view of motivism would be any action that does not defeat itself when universalized.\n\nFor instance, the soldier that spared his foe is doing the right thing since the idea of not killing does not defeat itself, and it can be universalized. However, a flaw in this argument would be conflicting duties.\n\nFor example, if a general ordered the soldier to kill the surrendered foe, the man's two duties would be conflicting. He has the moral obligation to not kill the man, and the moral obligation to obey his superiors due to the idea that\n\nboth maxims can be universalized. This argument is against this type of motivism.\n\nIn conclusion, the three types of moral theories include consequentialism, deontology, and motivism. All\n\nthree have their independent benefits, and their independent flaws.", ""),
        StaticBook("Dominus Tecum", "§bGuru Pinya", "In this book I shall write of the Order of St. Amyas and their error. The Creator teaches Divine love and mercy, yet these men give no unbeliever the chance to convert or see the truth. These men are HERETICS, they claim that they are setting an example\n\npersecuting pagans, and heathens! What fools! I have been following them and making religious observances. One accused me of being a sodomite! They do not even realize their error of their religious fallacies and indoctrinaztion! Another moment\n\nI listened to a conversaion of theirs! Two soldiers called a church 'the house of God' when it is merely a house of worship! These foul mouthed, idiotic hypocrites don't even realize the blasphemies they utter. They suggest that God is incompotent and\n\nis limited to such a small building! On several occasions I was forced to correct them in their ways IN FRONT OF OTHERS. What an abomination. They also do not realize the meaning of Church! I have spoken that I wait for the Church, and do you know what\n\nthey replied? 'The Cathedral is in the town square!'. The Church is not a building! It is the mystical body of the Creator and it is made up of all the believers! The fools even tryed to take away my Creator-given freewill! They use FORCE to spread the\n\nlove of the Creator! A farmer of hate will never sow a seed of love! It is impossible and contrary! To the foolish men in the Order of St. Amyas, change your ways before God's wrath is brought about for your hypocracy.", ""),
        StaticBook("\"A Time Before\"", "§bMaehr'Celia", "This book has been written by Maehr'Celia, by Memory once again.\n\n Before time itself there was darkness, a evil like no other capable of destorying any life that may have formed. But for every evil there is good, a light appeared in the darkness, a good capible of creating any life. The two opposite forces battled for\n\nwhat have been in our time, milleniums. But the light pulled through against the darkness and arose the first life, Time. Time is a life unkown to many, thought to be nothing, but it was Time that created all of what we know today. Time was only one\n\ncreature, the only living thing in our world, but Time grew weary and grew more lonely so it created us. Every living thing in our world was created by Time. Time controled everything, it controled our lives, it allows to live in its world for a period of\n\ntime in exchange for on thing, our will to overcome the temptation of the darkness, so the world shall remain in peace. But like all good things, it will eventually come to an end. Watching Time created all this peace and happiness, The Darkness grew sick\n\nof it. It grew jealous of Time. So The Darkness created something to tempt us to join him, it created sin. The Darkness too form of one of us and tried to tempt us with sin, but we all refused, until one child met it. The Darkness told the child that he\n\nhave more then this world of peace, he could have the power to control it, so the boy accepted the temptation of sin and became the first evil, The Void. The Darkness named this bou levithan. Levithan tempted others to sin and to join the Void, those who\n\ndid join became the first beasts, the slaves of the Void. No one could tell who joined the Darkness because those who did appeared to be so normal, except for one small thing, there eyes where a pure white. The Void spread Terror through out this peaceful\n\nworld for years. Until Time caught up with them.\n-----\n Time soon discoverd the Void and spent years trying to find a way to fight them, until he soon decieded to create his own warriors, the Hope, few us joined with the idea that if they joined him the\n\nworld would be at peace once more. That was all that Time promised, and so few joined. The Hope was described to wear a pure white robe with blue lines around the neck, arms, and feet. After the Hopes creation, the first war begin, the war for peace.\n\n Hundreds, upon hundreds of years of fighting took place. The Hope fought as best they could, but they were nearly destoryed. Just as soon as the Void declared its victory, the people stood up, fought back the Void, wiping it out from this world. The\n\nDarkness retreate to a different world, leaving this one to Time. But Time went after the Darkness, leaving the Hope to protect the land from any new evil that may arise, but they slowly diminished and disappeared in Aegis, leaving the people vaunible.\n\nSoon a new evil arose, led by one they called Iblees. Iblees tried to conquer the ways of the Void, raising the beast of the dead, but he had much he did not learn. Soon the sages came to fight Iblees, using the ancient ways of the Hope, but they to had\n\nmuch they did not learn. As they fought and time went by, the Darkness grew stonger and soon will return to our world with the stongest evil ever known, the Void. But it is said that when the Void returns, so shall the Hope, lead by Time himself, to fight\n\nthe Void once more, But the propechy of the tomes leaves one mystery, which shall win, Good of Evil?\n\n*sign*\n -Maehr'Celia \n  Follower of Time", ""),
        StaticBook("Pursuing Purity", "§bAshanaak", "------=+=------\nFor Those Who Pursue Purity...\n------=+=------\n\n\n\nA Guide for Those Who Pursue Knowledge of Tahariaean Clericism\n\n------=+=------\nMasjid al Nur al-Haqq\n------=+=------\n\nThe Masjid al Nur al-Haqq is the building before you, serving as a gathering place for the clerics of the Aengul of Purity, Tahariae. Tahariae has given us the abilities to establish Purity in this\n\nrealm, which comes in two forms. The first is the eradication of injuries, blemishes, and sickness from the mortal races. The second is the eradication of the servants of darkness throughout the realm, such as dark mages, tainted beings, and those with\n\nfraternal relations with such beings.\n\n------=+=------\nThe Five Tenets of Tahariae\n------=+=------\n\nWhen one becomes a cleric, they must follow a set of five tenets, or five main rules, told to the Prophet Braxis in Anthos by Tahariae Himself. The rules are to ensure the cleric knows what he\n\nor she must do for Tahariae and His duty. Breaking even one of these rules will mean forceful disconnection from Our Lord:\n\n[*] Shelter and protect thy brothers and sisters in Tahariae. [*]\n\nThe other clerics are much like your brothers and sisters, a family. You must always be ready to come to their aid, and to allow them to die intentionally is impure.\n\n[*] Do not stray in thy dedication to Tahariae. [*]\n\nNever stray from your duty, your duty is to heal those in need and to purge the darkness that roam around Vailor. To allow a simple matter to draw you away from such duty is impure.\n\n[*] Guide and protect the Descendants of the Four against the Impure. [*]\n\nNo matter their race or personal prejudice, cast all hatred aside, as we aid all. Help guide them onto the right path and ensure they are healed and protected from Impurity.\n\n[*] Do not spill the blood of the innocent. [*]\n\nDo not attack or kill an innocent person, even if they have insulted you or another. We clerics are the healers of the innocent, not slaughterers of the innocent. Such is impure.\n\n[*] Do not use Tahariae's blessing for anything other than His will. [*]\n\nTahariae’s power is only for His Mission. To use it to harm those innocent of crimes or for your own selfish needs is impure.\n\n------=+=------\nWalking the Path of Purity\n------=+=------\n\nWe are willing to take in those willing to become a cleric, although becoming one is not a simple matter of gaining His power and using it of your own free will. One must be willing to undergo\n\ntrials and be prepared to put Tahariae’s duty before all, including what is most precious to you personally. Most of all, you must follow the Tenets without hesitation; failing to do so will mean permanent disconnection.\n\nIf you truly have desire to bring\n\nLight and Purity into this realm, you shall meet with one of our masters and put through a series of trials that the master decides. These trials will help demonstrate to us your devotion and dedication, as well as help you understand the roles of the\n\nclerics. Once your trials are complete and the master deems you ready, you will be brought before Tahariae Himself. If He deems you ready, He will grant you His blessing of Light, and your duty as a cleric shall begin under the guidance of the master who\n\nconnected you to Tahariae.\n\nIf you wish to walk the Path of Purity, leave a letter explaining who you are, why you wish to serve Tahariae, what skills you have, and if you have any questions regarding us. Simply leave the letter in the chest at the\n\nMasjid al Nur al-Haqq, in the Caliphate of Khalestine, and await our response.\n\nIf you require healings, protection, or blessings, do not hesitate to send a bird to one of the Four Archons of the Masjid al Nur al-Haqq.\n\n------=+=------\nThe Four Archons\n------=+=------\n\n-[ The Mind ]-\nRamza Mantisuku\n((Zeeer0))\n\n-[ The Mouth ]-\nAshanaak\n((Ventusyr))\n\n-[ The Heart ]-\nAndriel Z'lorn\n((jlyoko))\n\n-[ The Arm ]-\nElvira Mantisuku\n((Farryn))\n\n[!] This page is dedicated to a map of the Caliphate, with a small, simply-drawn temple symbol in one of the corners of the city. [!]\n\n((-2197, 1061))", ""),
        StaticBook("MonkResurrection", "§bKirrekith", "Monk Resurrection\nBy Jistuma\nPages 23\n\nNotice, this books is a rushed copy of a tome inside the Library of the Orkish capital, as made during the raid of the 16th of the Grand Harvestm 1532 The following is no way a complete nor perfect copy of said book.\n\nMonk Resurrection\n\nIt is the salvation of those lives, but it is pondered why nothing has been wrote upon it. The monks are able to and well known for being able to save those on the brink of death or those who've always passed the thresshold. While at the grounds of the\n\nsanctuary manning a stall I have been able to witness of these happenings, or so what I believe. \n\nThis magic, unlike others, is one we know little of and myself. Like most holy forms of magic as it takes the form of a bright light, used to heal wounds.\n\nBesides this light, the monks act is to pray to the three known as the Triumvirate. It would appear to be that of clerical healing, but uses other properties not like those of clerical magic. Monks returning to the sanctuary can come back wounded,\n\nhaving been attacked by bandits proberly, showing peoples lack of care for whom they attack on the roads, especially when these individuals will often bring them back from the brink. Monks are not ordinary people, they heal faster than others from\n\nobservation. This is likely from their magic. often I witnessed light coming from they're wounds and surrounding it. Monks can also bring back the once deceased fully mortal once more. \nThis isn't in which to say they are necromancers, those that would\n\nbring back the dead, and remain in a state of limbo between death and life. Monks unlike them are able to bring back body AND soul unlike those of the necromancer, and bring them back to a near perfect condition from before death. Talking to those at the\n\nsanctuary, there is always an effect of being saved. A present residing condition is the loss of memory that occurrs after ones life being taken to the brink or past it and brought back. I have witnessed those effected having no memory of the passing or\n\nthe run up to it. Other common effects that linger is the scarring, revealing how the soul nearly passed the brink permanently or the tortures it passed. Often what worries me is the effects of change that develops in those that return, in personality.\n\nHaving talked to those if proves my worries. It is my belief that death is a terrible experiences, and part of it remains after the salvation. \n\nAnother fact I have witnessed, is that the deceased do not wish passively return to their prior resting place.\n\nThis came to mind when I witnessed one of the saved hugged dearly by a beloved. Before this a friend has carried his friend, the two of us talked about what had occurred. Knowing the location of his end, the twos route home took them through the place,\n\nand upon discussing this he began to want to take other ways home, and a argument erupted when the woman just wanted to move home swiftly, much to the mans reluctance. He could offer no explanation as to why his mind has been set to not travel that way.\n\nAs well this this the other strange behaviours of those visiting the sanctuary became more apparent, such as staying there durations. As well as this not using normal routes out of the temple, or swiftly returning to the santuary after having left with\n\nfriends. Its my assumption that those who meet their death are faced with a pain too horrible to face, what could be worse than seeing your own dead body?\n\nOn the talk of the bodies, I have a state as well, it is not necessary for the body to be brought\n\nback to the monks for the resurrection. I've witnessed them bring bodies back to the sanctuary, so I believe possessing them makes the process easier or favourable. In the lands of Anthos there are many evils, you could be decapitated, easten, thrown in\n\nlava, cut into pieces and drowned in the deepest oceans. Your body may disappear and never be found once more, and even without it can still be saved. Resoration of the body is beyond my comprehsion, and monks refuse to answer the question. They do\n\nhowever answer a few questions. I learned the monks are neutral, and never take sides in politics and conflict. They also are neutral in those they save, murder or saint they are rescued. This has brought them into conflict as some are desired to be dead.\n\nMost of the stories of those rescued begin with them explaining their awakening. Waking to the monks care. Soon come back swiftly after their demise, often before family and friends arrive. Others can take many elven days. They're magic is not all\n\npowerful, as monks say they can't save all those who come to them. If the deceased does leave the monks rooms within a elven week after their death it is to be presumed them to have passed to the beyond without the monks being able to act. The same occurs\n\nto those who take their own life in an attempt at life to end. If they no longer wish to live, their is nothing the monks can do to bring them back. \n\nThe monks are a blessing to mortals. They are those thay save many from their shortened lives.\n\nWrote by Rosso, 15th of Malin's Welcome 1452, copied by Kirrekith on 16th of the Grand Harvest, 1532 during the raid on the Ork Capital.", ""),
        StaticBook("´`", "§bCorvus Keint", "Short stories series: \n\n           RAVEN\n\n\nShe had her mission. As Always.\n\nQuickly, she transformed herself for the third time that night and flew off into the stormy sky.\n\nWhen she neared the castle, she swooped down to the throne room and entered through an open stained glass window depicting a phoenix.\n\nShe landed on the red carpet in front of the king. Heavily armored knights stood on each side of the room.\n\nThey snickered at the \"clumsy\" bird. Inclunding the king who slouched in his red-cushioned seat of gold.\n\nSuddenly, a strong, silver wind whirled around the bird as it grew to the size and shape of a human, clothed in a long black cloak.\n\nShocked, the guards flinched and the king sat up in his throne, his hands gripping the armrests. Before him stood a black figure with a hood. All he could see were the tender lips on its face. \nInhaling deeply, she let her ebony hair tumble down her back\n\nas she removed her black hood.\n\n\"I am Raven\", she proclaimed, her forest green irises glaring straight into the stunned eyes of the malicious king. \"And I have come bearing a message. Step down from your throne and\n\nand crown the rightful ruler of this kingdom King Phoenix. If you do not, you shall be cursed for the rest of your days.\n\nKing Vulture's eyes stormed with fury. Raven could see lightning flashing in them.\n\n\"Never! Guards! Kill her! Now!\"\n\nThe knights charged at her, shouting. But Raven simply transformed back into a small raven. She lunged at the king. He blocked his face with his arms, but it was no use. The raven's claws\n\nhad touched the top of his head. The curse had been cast.\n\nFor the rest of his days, King Vulture was known as the Beggar Serpent. He could do nothing but crawl for his life, struggling to escape the phoenixes ready to devour him.\n\nHe lived in the holes of the Phoenix Palace, determined on getting revenge on Raven.\n\nRaven, she had returned to Phoenix to tell him the good news. He returned to his palace, defeated the army of Vulture's remaining men and took back his throne and\n\nand kingdom. As he sat in this throne, one day, gazing at the scarlet and orange stained glass windows, he noticed a little black bird at his side.\n\n\"Raven? Why are you still here?\"\n\nI am waiting for you to tell me what to do next.\n\n\"You don't need to do anything else for me. You have saved the kingdom. You are free\"\n\nAnd that lifted the curse on Raven.\n\nPhoenix was the first human being to ever say that to Raven, instead of throwing at her more orders.\n\nShe had been cursed to do whatever she was told and many took advantage of that. But Phoenix was selfless. Instead of flying away to do whatever she pleased, Raven used her freedom to stay with Phoenix. They were soon wed and lived merrily all their days.\n\n\n\n\nWritten by:\n\n\nCorvus Keint,\n\n\nLord Merentel's squire.", ""),
        StaticBook("I Have a Dream", "§bTyrone Agashi", "§l§nI Have a Dream§0\n§0\n§0-Tyrone Agashi§0\n§0\n§0\"Faith is taking the first step even when you don't see the whole staircase.\"§0\n\n§l§nExperiences§0\n§0\n§0I sat in a puddle of my own excretment on a bench. §0\n§0My hands raw with blisters, and the skin on my back ripped open from a whip.§0\n§0Without proper food I knew I'd be gone within a Saints Week.\n\nWas I that expendable?§0\n§0\n§0Was I as easy to replace as a damaged tool?§0\n§0\n§0The shackles around my ankles were heavy, and after thirty hours I'd been sat there to work, no sleep permitted.§0\n\nMy thoughts became random as as the delirium set in and I lost track of where I was and the consequences for misbehaviour. §0\n§0I stood up abruptly, and struck my fist at an overseer. He crumpled to the deck, and I stepped back bewildered and unsteady on my\n\nfeet. §0\n§0\n§0In minutes the other overseers trudged over, and rained down blows on me, before gripping the scruff of my neck and dragging me down to the lowest deck.§0\n§0\n\nWere they to come to me as a friend, I would give them everything. My heart, my labour, my soul. §0\n§0Instead they tied me to a chair littered with thorns.§0\n§0They beat with a stick that which I would have willingly surrendered with a loving heart.\n\nYou think it foolish to stand meek, but you are wrong, for it is we that inherit the earth.§0\n§0\n§0As they each took turns burning me, cutting me, whipping me. I could only feel sorry for them. §0\n§0\n\nThey treated us no better than cattle or pigs. They'd encourage us to have children and then rip the family apart, selling the family members to different plantations across the isles of Axios. §0\n\nOnce I became independent, and had escaped from my overseers.§0\n§0\n§0I was awoken from a sleep by hoarse cackles, and yelling coming from outside. §0\n§0My fingers became entwined in the woollen blanket, gripping it as if it were more\n\nthan a square of yarn. §0\n§0I built up the courage to scramble to my feet, and peered outside. §0\n§0\n§0A mob swarmed my home, torched in their hands and pitchforks. §0\n\nI watched as flames engulfed my cottage, scorching my skin and singeing the surrounding trees. §0\n§0Wreathes of smoke filled my nostrils as I stumbled my way towards the door. §0\n§0I managed to swing open my door, only to be met with the crowd.\n\n\"Leave Darkskin! We don't want your kind here!!!\" One of them bellowed.§0\n§0\n§0My feet slip outwards on the wet leaves as I round the corner, the cold evening air shocking my throat and lungs as I inhale deeper, faster.\n\nBehind me I could hear the baying howls of the dogs and jeering laughter of the mob. §0\n§0\n§0I eventually lost them, and found upon a town: Sutica, which is where I now reside.§0\n§0The attitudes here are mixed, but far better than Johaneesburg. §0", ""),
        StaticBook("Warrior", "§bDra'Khataba", "Woke up early with the bright rising sun. Picking up and cleaning a well-worn axe. The wounds are deep and cause much pain. Stumbling along the road as it starts to rain.\n\nPainfully thinking of all the battles long past. Time starting slowly to slip from his grasp. Throat burning and so dry he cannot even talk. Slowly moving forward he continues to walk.\n\nMemories pull a single tear that rolls from his eye. Finally realizing that now he will surely die.\nNow, Beaten he stops and in the grass does he lay.\nAnd, waits for the release of death to come to his this day.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tomb Secrets", "§bLemik Rextiureus", "{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nPrologue\n\nThe veil fizzled out and Seisi became visible just as the last of the dark priests about to leave. \n\nAss Dung. Seisi thought, Seisi turned and ran towards the River. He sensed a disturbance behind him and threw himself to\n\nthe ground. A bile green torrent of light shot over his head, thundered into a palm grove, disintegrating the trees into powder.\n\nThat had been a Tail of Sobek, one of the forbidden spells. A spell, that also required divine energy, which meant... Seisi\n\nshot up from where he was lying and zig-zagged thorugh the reeds. He heard whomping sounds and his inner eye beheled green coulds behind him. His pursuer was getting closer. \n\nSomething silvery screeched past his nose, burying itself into another palm\n\ntree.\n\nA Dagger of Khons! One of the other dark priests has returned. Seisi focussed on his sun-flow, ripped an amulet from his collar, rasped an invocation to Hapy from his burning lungs and races across the spongy surface of the river.\n\n\"Have to reach the other side...\" He thought to himself\n\nHe sensed movement on the bank, twisted-an invisible fist slammed nto his back, the spell broke and he plunged into the river. \nNot again! Seisi thought as he sank through the murky green depths of\n\nHapy's domain. His consciousness waas fading fast, yet he knew he ould awaken again. But then it would be too late to pass on what he had learned. Too late for Metjen, too late for his love-and too late to protect the people of Caliphate from annihilation", ""),
        StaticBook("Forty Thieves", "§bArnorian Elendil", "Ali-Baba and the\nForty Thieves\n\nA long time ago there were two brothers named Cassim and Ali Baba. They lived in a far off land. Cassim, the older of the two brothers, had married a rich wife. He had a fine big home and did not\n\nneed to work for a living. Ali Baba, the second brother, had married a poor woman. He worked very hard indeed. Each day he went away from home to cut wood, which he sold in the city. \n\nOne day Ali Baba was at work. Suddenly, he\n\nheard the sound of horses' hoofs. He was frightened, and hid behind a tree. There were many bad men n the land, and he knew that it was wise to keep out of their way!\n\nAli Baba just had time to hide before a group of men rode up. They\n\nwere the forty thieves who had been stealing gold and rich goods from the countryside for many years. The leader of the thieves stopped his horse at the side of a big hill. \"Open, Sesame,\" he cried, so loudly that Ali Baba could hear\n\nhim. \n\nNo sooner were the words out of his mouth, than a magic door opened in the hillside. There was a deep dark cave behind the door. The forty thieves went into the cave and the door shut behind them. Ali Baba waited\n\nfor what seemed a very long time. At last the door opened, and the forty thieves came out again.\n\nThis time the chief of the band called out \"Shut, Sesame!\" The door closed, and the men rode away.\n\nAli Baba wanted to know what was in the cave. After a minute or two, he ran up to the side of the hill. Just as he had heard the leader of the thieves do, Ali Baba cried out, \"Open, Sesame.\" Once again, the hidden door opened. He went through the\n\ndoorway. The door closed behind him. \n\nAli Baba found himself in a huge cave. It was filled with rich goods and gold which the thieves had carried there. Ali Baba trembled with fear. What if the thieves should find him!\n\nQuickly, Ali Baba cried \"Open, Sesame,\" and ran out of the cave. He found the old horse he had left in the woods and led him back to the cave. There he loaded four big bags of gold on the poor beast. Then he hurried out of the cave.\n\nHe called out, \"Shut, Sesame,\" and went home as fast as his horse would travel. His wife could hardly believe him when Ali Baba told her that they were rich. Her eyes sparkled with joy when she saw the gold. \n\nNow Ali Baba was\n\nso happy that he just had to talk about the gold. That was a big mistake. He told his brother, Cassim, where he had found the gold. Ali Baba was so kindhearted he even told Cassim the words that opened the magic door. And, he said that he would give\n\nhim half the riches in the cave.\n\nAli Baba's brother was a mean and selfish fellow. He did not want half the gold in the cave. He wanted all of it. Early the next morning, long before the sun was up, Cassim took ten horses.\n\nThen he set out to the cave. He planned to load all the riches onto his animals and leave none for Ali Baba.\n\nWhen he got to the hillside, Cassim cried out, \"Open, Sesame!\" When the door swung open, he led his horses into the cave.\n\nThen the opening in the hil closed behind him. Cassim hurried to load his horses. He knew it would be dangerous if the fourty thieves found him in their hide-out. When he had taken all the gold that his horses could carry, Cassim\n\nled the animals to the door.\n\nSuddenly, Cassim turned white with fear! He had forgotten the magic words that made the door swing open! He could not get out of the cave!\n\nIn a few hours the\n\nthieves came back to their hide-out. There they found the rtembling Cassim. \"Do not kill me!\" he cried. \"It was not I who found your cave. It was my brother, Ali Baba, who did it. He sent me here to take your gold.\"\nThe fierce leader of the thieves\n\nlaughed. \"That is all I wanted to know!\" he said. \"We will soon visit this brother of yours.\" Then the chief ordered his men to beat Cassim with heavy sticks. They beat Cassim until they had killed him. The leader next told his men of a plan to kill Ali\n\nBaba because he had found their cave. \"In a few weeks, Ali Baba will think that he is safe,\" the chief said. \"He may guess that we have caught his brother, but he will not know that his brother told us that Ali Baba found our hiding place.\"\n\n\"We will get a string of horses,\" th chief went on. \"On the horses we will put forty oil jars, each one big enough to hold a man. You will get into the oil jars. I will lead the horses to Ali Baba's house. I will tell him that I am a travelling oil-seller\n\nand that there is no room for me to sleep at the inn. Ali Baba will then let me stop for the night at his house. \"Later, when Ali Baba sleeps, I will call you,\" the chief said. \"You will cut your way out of the jars with your knives. Then we will pull Ali\n\nBaba from his bed. We will kill him and his wife. Then no one will know about our cave.\"\n\nEverything happened just as the leader of the thieves had said. When Cassim dissapeared, Ali Baba thought his brother had gone\n\nto the cave and been caught. But when he neither saw nor heard of the thieves, Ali Baba decided that he himself was safe.\n\nWith hte four bags of gold he had taken from the cave, he had bought a new house\n\nand fine clothes. People who had once passed Ali Baba without a word now treated him like an old friend. He was happy indeed. Then, late one afternoon an oil-seller came by Ali Baba's new house. He led a long string of horses. Each one\n\nwas loaded with large leather oil jars. Of course, the oil-seller was really the leader of the thieves. Ali Baaba had seen him at the cave. But he did not know that his visitor was the same man. Ali Baba invited the chief to come in when he heard that\n\nthere was no room for him at the inn. \"Please come in,\" said Ali Baba. \"You can have supper with us, and a warm bed to sleep in until tommorow.\" Now it happened that Ali Baba had a wise and beautiful servant girl. She worked as a cook in his house. While\n\nshe was making supper, she found out there was no cooking oil in the kitchen. \"There is plenty of oil in the jars in the yard,\" the girl said to herself. \"I think it will be alright if I take just a little. Then I can cook the supper.\" And off she went to\n\nget some oil in a boil.\n\nWhen the girl drew near to one of the jars, she was greatly surprised to hear someone inside. It was one of the thieves, who said, \"Is it time for us to climb out and kill Ali Baba?\" The girl thought\n\nquickly. \"Not yet,\" she said, making her voices sound deep as if the chief of the thieves were talking. Then she ran to tell her master that there were strange men in the oil jars.\n\nAli Baba guessed that the thieves were hiding in the\n\noil jars. \"Run and bring the soldiers!\" he ordered. \"They will be happy to meet our visitors.\" \n\nIn a few minutes the soldiers came on the run, led by the servant girl. Then, very quietly they crept into Ali Baba's house. There they caught\n\nthe surprised leader of the thieves. When the leader had been tied up with rope, Ali Baba led the soldiers to the yard where the oil jars stood along the wall. \"Come out! Come out! It is time!\" Ali Baba called. Out popped the thieves -\n\nright into the hands of the awaiting soldiers. Ali Baba was so pleased with the little servant girl that he gave her his son's hand in marriage. There was a great feast. Everyone in the city came. And why should they not, for Ali Baba\n\nwas now rich and well known for catching the thieves!\n\nAnd whenever Ali Baba, his son, or even his grandson needed gold, there was always the hidden cave to keep them rich to the end of their days.", ""),
        StaticBook("BalladofEdgardo", "§bFranswaar Kellian", "From the depths Oren old \nThere came a man, eyes bright and fists bold,\nPast shadowed figures of loners he strolled,\nAnd the man said, \"My name is Edgardo.\"\n\nScoffing and scowling met his shining heart,\nWith shining swords they ordered, \"play the part,\"\nAnd defiance was met with death by despot,\nBut the man said, \"My name is Edgardo.\"\n\nIn the wilderness where shadows lay,\nThere was another, cast out from the day,\nA man named Sid, smile kind and gay, \nAnd the man said, \"My name is Edgardo.\"\n\nThey were forced to travel, to escape their cage,\nBut though they won over the orc brigade,\nBanishment was sustained and gods were enraged,\nSo the man said, \"My name is Edgardo.\"\n\nDays and nights they spent sparring to train,\nMonsters hovering, seeking meals in vain,\nUntil one day they thought up a campaign,\nAnd the man said, My name is Edgardo.\"\n\nAround the towns and o'er gorges they fled,\nBut the great Gilden Jarl could not be mislead,\nWith forceful grace and honer he had lead,\nBut the man said, \"My name is Edgardo.\"\n\nUnparalleled servant destroyed and disgraced,\nMilitant Heroes  prepared armies to race,\nAcross the mountains to Haven's grace,\nBut the man said, \"My name is Edgardo.\"\n\nOverflowing spirit the fountain gave,\nEdgardo defeating dark Khaine with it's aide,\nTeleportation completing the charade,\nThe man howled, \"My name is Edgardo.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Poems", "§bThe Donkey Man", "A Collection of Poems\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nby the donkey\n\nSunshine\n\n\nFlowery birth\nRebirth of sunshine\nRise above the mountaintops\nThe night is not fine\nCruel darkness fails\nSuccumbed to good like wine\nHorses neigh and donkeys bray\nWith sunshine rebirth\n\nLove\n\n\nRestricted with chain\nBleeding with pain\nBroken heart\nA failed start\nA love without hope\nMen sit around to mope\nLove is but a joke\nAnd marriage a cruel elope\n\nSodomy\n\n\nBanished love\nBanished hearts\nFailure lips\nFailure minds\nLaw-alienated\nNever reinstated\nExecution\nLynching\nAnd love\nBuilt to die\n\nDye\n\n\nDye the world\nThe sky is blue\nThe earth is green\nThe hearts are red\nThe world is colored\nThe ocean is blue\nThe dirt is brown\nThe tears are colorless\n\nDonkey\n\n\nA donkey understands\nAn outcast\nLabeled an ass\nCalled a fool\nA donkey understands\nWorking for others\nA slave to his master\nA heart and mind like ours\nA donkey understands\n\nFlowers\n\n\nAre flowers happy?\nPretty in all shades\nPretty in spring and in fall\nBut they are not always happy\nSome lay at churches\nAnd we think of flowers at weddings\nBut they lie more at graves\n\nCrazy\n\n\nCrazy\nAre we all crazy?\nAre you crazy?\nAm I crazy?\nAre bad people crazy?\nAre good people crazy?\nOr is crazy what we just call those\nWhom aren't lazy?\n\nBook\n\n\nWhen I was six\nMy father said he'd teach me to read\nWhen I was seven\nHe said it again\nWhen I was eight\nHe repeated his claim\nWhen I was nine\nIt was off with his head", ""),
        StaticBook("A tale", "§bAlan Marlsbush", "Once upon a time there was an elf. The elf wasn't very happy with its life, they had no reason to be. They grew up alone, and after a while their parents left them. They couldn't understand why, the only logical conclusion was that they were unwanted.\n\nThey wandered until they were found by a young elven woman who raised them as their elder sister. She was away often, and was not a soft kind person, however she did her best to care for the child. The elf rarely spoke, when they did it was uncomfortable,\n\nstiff, and they cut out as many words they could. They communicated the best they could through hand gestures and expressions, and found games to play by themself. They were ill often, but it didn't bother them. They were used to being ill and being\n\nalone. They continued to play alone and read. They loved most to make small snowmen and rabbits, then they could make as many friends as they wanted. Their sister took care of them the best she could, and they were the closest to happy they could have\n\nbeen, but it couldn't last. They were frightened, so frightened that their sister would leave them too. So they ran away. I could not hope to explain their motivations, the elf was still a child at the time, and a frightened child is not the most logical\n\nbeing. So they left. However their illness caught up to them. There was no way they could take care of themself. They wandered until they were found by a woman who offered to take them in. However not long afterwards the woman disappeared, leaving\n\nthem alone once again. They grew up eventually, however they had no idea how to interact with other people, and even when they were seen as a friend by another, they could not trust these people not to leave them too. They were alone, whether surrounded\n\nby others, or isolated in their home it made no difference. Then, one day, they fell in love. She was a beautiful elf, with vibrant red hair and green eyes, and she loved them as well. It frightened them. They couldn't bear the thought of losing her. She\n\nmade them happier than they knew was possible. Being seperated from her frightened them, perhaps she would grow bored of them. They did lose her, but not how they expected. She died. They didn't know how to cope, and completely shut down. They wouldn't\n\ntalk, and wished they'd never met her. For now that they knew what it was to be so happy, living without it seemed unbearable. They didn't know what to do anymore. It was not enough to just be alive anymore. They had been aware of their lonliness before,\n\nhowever the grief with no one to comfort them made it as sharp as a knife. They grieved and wandered and then they found someone else. He swept through the elf's life like a windstorm. To say they loved each other would be inaccurate. He loved the elf's\n\nbeauty, and they desperately needed someone to notice them. However he left as quickly as he came, and died as well. The elf resigned themself to remaining alone, as painful as it was they did not know what to do aside from accepting it. Just when they\n\nfelt that it was such they met someone. A cousin they had not known about. They quickly grew to adore her, she was kind and an accomplished doctor. However they found it difficult to talk to her about anything. She was significantly younger than them, and\n\nthey did not want to burden her. They couldn't. They cared about her too much, and trusted her too little. It was enough to keep them going. They pushed through day to day. Their cousin fell in love, and married someone whom the elf had been well\n\nacquainted with for a very long time. They found themself able to talk to him, if just a little. After a long while it was too much, and they grew apart from the small amount of family they'd found. They couldn't trust her or their friend enough, and\n\ncould truly believe themself to be loved. They tried, they really tried, however they did not know how. They did not know what they should do, what they could do. They had never been taught how. They got a job at a tavern as a chef, and once again met\n\nsomeone. They fell deeply in love with him, and he loved them in turn. Once again they felt happy. Their companion seemed to genuinely care for them, and wanted to be with them. Which made it all the more painful when he too passed away. They did their\n\nbest to carry on living, and for a few years they could. But now, maybe they should just give up? No one will miss them if they're gone. If they die, who cares? No one will mourn them. What is the point of continuing to fight, fight their poor health,\n\nfight their own thoughts? They may just give up.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Flower Boy", "§bFrugo Peregrin", "The Story of the\n  Boy and the Flower\n\n        Written by\n     Frugo Peregrin.\n\nOnce in a meadow, there was a boy. He loved running in meadow. He ran everyday. But one day, his favorite flower wasn't there. He looked to his side and saw a girl who plucked the flower and is twirling it in her hand. He was enraged. He ran over to the\n\ngirl. He told her that was his favorite flower. The girl said that she didn't care. The boy was enraged. He plucked a flower that the girl liked. And so did the girl. This went on and on until there were no flowers left. Both, the boy and the girl were\n\nsad. They caused all of their flowers to be dead. They soon realized that being mad doesn't help and that they should have made a comprimise.\n\n\n\n\n\n        The End.\n\n\n\n       Written by\n    Frugo Peregrin.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Darkened Sleep", "§bWalter Kyroc", "The Darkened\n         Slumber\n\nA deepened rest, caused by pain.\n\nA darkened past, a tyrant in reign.\n\nA desired friend, a horrendous deed.\n\nBut not a single friend, came in time of need.\n\nThe best of friends, they thought they were.\n\nBut all of their past, turned into a faint blur.\n\nAnd in his life, the pain would be known.\n\nAnd cause his strife...\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n   ...Over a throne...", ""),
        StaticBook("-=Promise=-", "§bKlaudia Crast", "-------------------\n\n     ---=()()()=---\n\n          Promise\n\nBy Lady Klaudia Crast\n\n-------------------\n\nHis steps pounded the pavement with a steady beat; a paradox of the quickened rhythm of his heart thrumming against the walls of his chest like a jackhammer.\n\nSix long years had passed since he last laid eyes on her and their home.\n\nThey had become nothing short of a dream that both haunted and soothed his soul, wishing with every desperate breath he took for his dream to become a reality. \n\nIt may have taken six years, but his wish was finally granted, and the elation he felt -\n\ncouldn’t overpower the anxiety and uncertainty that raided his heart and mind. It threatened to reduce him to the memory of what put him in this situation to begin with, and although he tried not to think the worst, he'd witnessed more than any twenty-\n\neight year old man should. Yet, despite his seemingly positive aura, doubt shadowed his presence. \n\n He carried on, taking a moment to revel in the memories he had once created with her, even in their short time together.\n\nIt had been the last night before his departure. He’d lain there, his body entwined with hers, inhaling the scent that clung to the warmth of her skin, the scent of the lavender fields that she’d lain in just hours before he claimed every inch of her.\n\n“I’m going to miss this,” he whispered, kissing the top of his wife’s head. She snuggled in closer, tracing the pad of her finger along the contours of his chest and stomach.\n\n\n[!] A drawing of the lady and man resting would be here in great detail.\n\n“I wish you didn’t have to leave.” Her breath tickled the hair on his chest. “Just promise me you’ll return to me?” Lifting her head up to meet his eyes, she quickly added,\n\n“I know that’s a silly request, and I shouldn’t have asked that of you, -\n\nit’s just…I can’t bear the thought of anything happening to you.”\n\n“Nothing is going to happen to me,” he’d reassured her. “I wont be over there for very long. Before you know it, I’ll be back, and we can start the family we’ve always wanted.”\n\n“Oh?” She questioned, her eyebrows raising in amusement. Dragging her bottom lip between her teeth, she said, “If we have a boy, I want to name him Michael.”\n\n“My middle name?” He questioned, taking a mental note of how soft her skin felt against his -\n\n and the fullness of her lips that beckoned his to caress. Her smile was his answer, and he couldn’t find it within himself to argue. He would give her anything she desired.\n\n“Then, we name our baby Adalind if it’s a girl.”\n\n“No,” his wife protested. “You can’t do that.”\n\n\n“Do what? Use your middle name if it’s girl, just like how you want to use my middle name if it’s a boy? A little hypocritical, don’t you think?”\n\nHe watched in amusement as his wife’s jaw dropped, words caught on the tip of her tongue, unable to form a rebuttal.\n\n\n\nSo beautiful....\n\n“It’s such a strange name, David.”\n\nHe laughed. “Since when did strange phase you? You’ve never been one to settle for ordinary.\n\nBesides, I love your name. I love everything about you.\n\nAnd when we do have a baby, that baby will be a part of you, and I love the thought of naming her after the one woman who stole my heart the day she purposely dumped her tea on me.\"\n\nHis wife looked up into his eyes before snorting in laughter,\n\nburying her face into his chest. “You deserved it! You shouldn’t have been such a pompous arse.\"\n\nBrushing her golden waves aside and tucking them behind her ear, David took a deep breath in, revelling in his wife’s perfection.\n\n“This pompous ass got what he wanted. You’re mine, Quinn. Always and forever.”\n\n“Promise?”\n\n“Promise.” He meant it, with every breath he took, every beat of his heart, and within that moment as he covered her body with his-\n\nhe had never felt so sure of anything in his life.\n\n------------------- \nShall the tale end here? \n\nShall it move forth and elaborate upon their future? \n\nFind out soon! Promise 2 COMING SOON!", ""),
        StaticBook("The Martyr", "§bSporan Kyroc", "If I could bear your sadness, I would.\n\nIf I could, for a minute, take away your pain and make it mine, I would.\n\nIf I could tell you there is a reason for this, I would.\n\nI will do anything to take away your pain.\n\nBut sometimes life makes some unexplainable twists and unfortunate turns\n\nAnd the world seems cold and unfeeling...\n\nI can't tell you how sorry I am.\n\nThat I cannot shelter you from this.\n\nBut I will leave you with this thought...\n\nI am here.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nIf you want to talk, if you find comfort in sharing silence with me. I am here.\n\nYou are important to me...\n\nI care.", ""),
        StaticBook("Two Doors", "§bDolan Velethen", "Two doors, both opaque in feature, stood in front of two staggly men. On the left stoff Veren, a human whose life was reached half. To his right stood Hijol, a wood elf younger than a fox out of season.\n\nIn between them was a sign that read \"Choose carfully\". Hijol inspected the sign and claimed that the sign was closer to the right door than the left one.\n\nAfter some long discussion the two decided that they must both choose the same door. They felt tired and rested.\n\nThe next marrow Hijol woke to fine two sleeping children. Both girls of unrecognizable race. He awoke one and quickly told her his tale. \"The right door is correct\" He notes that the sign has dissappeared. \"There was a sign that read 'There must be only\n\none right door'\".\n\nMeanwhile Veren awoke to find the other girl. Afyer he told her she must not trust Hijol, the two groups conjoined.\n\nFighting words came out. In one corner stood anger and spite.\n\nThe other stoff curriosity and fear. The winner, only the one brave enough to turn a knob,\n\nThey grew weary and slept another night. The next marrow Hijol's girl and Veren's girl awoke to find themselves alone and, adults.\n\nIn Hojol's girl's mind she had two facts, she must choose a door with Veren's girl, and it has to be  the right one. Veren's girl was to scared to speak to Hijol's girl.\n\nHijol's girl only had one option, in her mind, she must take out veren's girl to\n\ncontinue...\n\nHer fists hit her face. Her blood tained her tunic. Fist meet fist, and death was quick.\n\nHijol's girl turned the right knob slowly. On the other side was horrer. Both doors went to the same place. A small\n\nbedrock room with a sign that read \"Life isn't going to bend it'self to your perceptions.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Thomas Tantrum", "§bBeelzebub", "There was once a boy, he was named Thomas.\n\nThomas had a hot temper.\n\nThomas' mother warned him.\n\n\"Thomas, if you continue to be so wrathful, your head will pop like a balloon!\"\n\nThomas yelled at his mother.\n\n\"I do not need your advice! I do not have the patience for it!\"\n\nThomas stomped and yelled and walked away.\n\nHe was one angry boy.\n\nThe next day, Thomas came upon a boy playing with a toy.\n\nHe grew naughty.\n\n\"Your toy is dumb! And so are you!\"\n\nThomas knocked the toy out of the young boy's hand, breaking it.\n\nHe was one bad boy.\n\nThomas' head grew rounder.\n\nIt became a shade of red.\n\nHis voice became squeakier.\n\nThomas' mother warned him once again.\n\n\"Your head will pop, Thomas!\"\n\n\"That is what happens to mean boys!\"\n\nBut Thomas did not listen.\n\nHe ctomped and yelled and walked away.\n\nThe next day, Thomas came upon a boy waiting in line for something to eat.\n\nHe grew naughty.\n\n\"Give me your money!\"\n\nThe boy gave Thomas all he had.\n\nThomas was one big bully.\n\nThomas went home.\n\nHe looked in the mirror.\n\nHis head was a balloon!\n\n\"Help! My head, my head is a balloon!\"\n\nThomas called out. But his voice was too squeaky.\n\nHe stomped and yelled and stomped and yelled.\n\nUntil his head went 'pop'!", ""),
        StaticBook("Seebo the Large", "§bFranswaar Kellian", "This is the Legend of Seebo the Large\n\n\n\n\n\n         Written By:\n            Franswaar\n\nThe raggedy old man known as Galendal the pink was in possesion of an evil artifact. Galendal knew an item such as this must be destroyed, but he alone could not do it alone. Galendal sought help from the person best suited for the task at hand, Seebo\n\nGalendal entered the young the halfings house, amazed that such a small creature could have so much clutter. Seebo was found at his desk reading poetry written by famous writer. As Galendal unfolded his tale of the artifact it filled Seebo wonder and\n\nadventure. He thought 'How dangerous could the artifact be.' 'I surely must destroy this so no harm could come to the world.' But during supper Seebo thought he could sell the artifact for quite a fortune. So in the night while Galendal slept peacefully,\n\nSeebo smuthered him whilst grining with his idea in mind. Unfortunatly Seebo made no profit from selling the ring and threw it in a now covered well. Seebo in his annoyance, proceeded to join the Orenian military, where had killed many dwarfs and orcs.\n\nThrough sheer luck and willpower (and the occasional ankle biting) Seebo vanquished his foes and earned the self imposed title of Seebo the Large, not for his size (he was quite the small halfing), but for the size of the piles of burning, rotting,\n\ncorspes in his wake. On the battlefield on brisk morning, Seebo found the small portrait of a dwarven family. Grief overcame the small halfing as he realized he had slain a member of a loving and happy family... and had not slain the rest of the family\n\nSeebo then dedicated the rest of his life to hunting the family. He deserted the Orenian military and massacre anyone that steped in his way. One such group was a tribe of Ologs. Seebo the Large came during the darkest of the blackest night and poured\n\nalchemist's fire down the throat of all the Ologs, except the young prince, Char'Goth, who had been fabled amoung the tribe as the strongest warrior they had ever seen. But with his home destroyed, his family burnt to a crisp, and his titled striped from\n\nhim, Seebo beat the Olog into eternal submission, even death shall not free him of Seebo's wraith. The 9ft Olog, Char' Goth, was taken away by Seebo to be his eternal slave.  With his new found ally, it is said that Seebo still searches for the dwarven\n\nfamily that had escaped Seebo's grasp, for now.\n\nIf you ever happen to pass the fabled Seebo the Large, be very wary, and keep your ankles covered.", ""),
        StaticBook("Adunian Poems", "§bHamish", "Open your eyes and watch the sky,\nto see the blue and the white,\nand view the stars that roam up above\nand watch the birds in flight.\nTo see the seasons change with time\nand the people grow and age,\n\nto see the kindness in their hearts\nand their dark eyes that hold their rage.\nOpen your eyes to notice the difference\nbetween the rumours and praise,\nfrom the people who honestly care\nto those that could not be more blasé.\n\nTo notice the change from black to grey\nfrom purple to blue, orange to red,\nand see green turn to yellow,\nthe rich comforted and the hungry get fed.\nOpen your eyes to watch the pain\nthat others hide from the rest.\n\nThey might be the man on the street\nor the masquerade, best dressed. \nNotice the seasons wash the air new\nto make room for their colours, \nand make the people change their clothes\nas well as watch out for others.\n\nOpen your eyes to their lives going by,\nto their hopes flickering and fading,\nthe things they want most leaving\nand their dreams gone from trading.\nWatch out for a puddle of tears\nnear a gravestone of the loved,\n\nand see those who longed to be touched\nas they keep their fingers gloved.\nOpen your eyes to see the compassion\nthat seems to flow in this realm,\nfrom the caring and the kindness\nto the comfort that can overwhelm.\n\nBut also take note of the depression\nthat sits at the bottoms of the lakes, \nthat rots on the tree branches\nand in the corners of the crates.\nOpen your eyes to see what I see, \nthe final realisation;\n\nthat with every new begining\ncomes yet another civilisation.\nBut just rest and take it all in,\nwith your eyes wide open\nand see this shattered realm like me,\nwith my heart completely broken.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nShattered,\nThe fact that I am broken,\nBroken,\nEven with what I said;\nTattered, \nMy heart is thankful for use,\nSmashed,\nEven though all out my heart bled.\nHalting,\n\nMy world, but seems to keep spinning,\nCrushed,\nAs my mind searches for support;\nImperfect, \nHe is gone forever as they said,\nUneven, \nAnd I did not have the energy to retort.\nDisturbed,\n\nThey told me, when I asked what happened,\nSnapped,\nThey said, when he fell with my heart,\nDefeated,\nHe was, as he was carried outside,\nCracked,\nThey described he was not that smart.\n\nStopped, \nas I cannot hear it anymore,\nDestoryed,\nmy heart to the very core,\nAltered, \nas I think its beat has changed,\nDefective,\n\nand it has made me beyond poor. \nIs,\nwhat a time that this has been,\nWhat,\nI have been through is too much,\nI,\nonly wanted to see him again,\nFeel,\n\nhis heart and his touch.\nMy,\nThree, dear Three, bless him,\nElton,\nfor he was my net under the ledge,\nIs,\nhe really, truly with you now?\n\nDead,\nand gone, straight over the edge?\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nHis green eyes never leave my gaze\nand his beard will always be brown,\nhis kilt never lost its colour\nas he kept not a trace of a frown.\nHis high stature has yet to be forgotten\nwhat with his boom laughter and grin,\n\nand his highspirits not only with his family\nbut also in the tavern with his gin.\nHis comforting words kept me going\nas his form took shape in my mind,\nfor it was I who kepts his memories,\nit was I that he never left behind.\n\nI cannot say I enjoyed all of it\nfor that man saw too many wars,\nbut he also had his moments of triumph,\nand those he loved dearly of course.\nHe kept his mind straight and level\neven under the most pressure,\n\nhe always knew where his place was\nand respected those who were lesser.\nSometimes he forgot right from wrong,\nbut then again, we all to at some point,\nand yet he knew how to keep people motivatied\neven though he was hard to dissapoint.\n\nAnd though I miss him still to this day,\nwith years gone since he passed,\nI still wish Hanrahan was here with me,\nbut for now his memories will stay glassed.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nI tried to paint you with the finest paints,\nto bring your green eyes and brown hair,\nbut the picture simply slipped from my mind,\nand on the canvas you were not there.\nI had the colours perfectly mixed,\nmy brush poised and ready,\n\nbut when it came to shaping your face,\nI could not keep my hand steady.\nI ended up painting a landscape;\nit was one that I knew all too well,\nit had trees in the back and buildings around,\nbut little did I know that I fell.\n\nI watched my hand with curiosity,\nnot knowing what it was doing, \nand suddenly I forgot where I was,\nand even the tea I had brewing.\nI forgot my life and all my memories,\nI forgot who I had lost,\n\nI only knew about the place at hand,\nand I could not see the lines I crossed.\nI backed into my own painting\nof the trees and of the buildings,\nand backed right into the old Clinic of ours\nand I found the stash of old kindlings.\n\nI relit the fire just behind the counter,\nand leaned on it as I looked towards the door,\nI had washed the floors and that silver bell,\nand waited for your arrival once more.\nAs I stood in the Clinic of Memories\n\nI seemed to trick myself,\nbut at the time I could not see you\nas I restocked the shelf.\nYou walked in quietly as you always did,\nand I hung my coat on a hook.\nBut I could tell that I was not alone\n\nas you stood in that corner and looked.\nI turned around to see your expression,\nthose green eyes sunked into your face,\nand when you told me simple words that calmed me,\nthey helped me find my place.\nYou stepped closer and hugged me,\n\nyour words were soft and kind,\n\"You will be all right, I promise,\" you whispered,\nand suddenly it was I who was hard to find.\n\"Can't I just go back to the Clinic?\"\nI almost cried as you came near,\n\n\"Yes, anything you want, I promise,\nbut for now, Arial, you must stay here.\"\nSomething happened just then, \t\nwhen you promised I would be okay.\nI knew that the person I was then\nwould not be the person I am today.\n\nFor now I know that Arial\nwas the one who you wanted to protect,\nso she took her things and kept them safe,\nleast she be the one I forget.\nAnd now your dreams can come true,\nfor your daughter now walks these lands,\n\nand I can assure that Arial will be all right, \nbecause she is in your own hands.\nShe may call herself Annabelle,\nbut that is only for your sake,\nbecause she knows your daughter is in her\nand her soul was always yours to take.\n\nBut I did lose myself in the Clinic that day,\neven though it was just a painting.\nAnd even though it was all in my mind,\nI know it was real and Arial is still waiting.\nShe will wait for you until the end of time,\nuntil the brush is steady,\n\nuntil your face can be painted again\nand until you think she is ready.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nPostscript.\n\nA collection of poems\nwritten by a famous Adunian poet in the late Anthosian period.\n\nLet her memory be charished by all those who read her delightful words.\n\n-Hanrahan Brae", ""),
        StaticBook("The Journies", "§bTyrell Blavier", "-_-(The \n         Journeys)-_-\n\nAthera in the eyes of\n     Tyrell Blavier\n\n-Reserved Page\n\nSribbles from long\nago that I've kept.\n\nThe true journey is how i've come so far from so little.\n\n       /Foreword/\n\nWell I don't know how to start here honestly, the world seems so big yet so litte. Diverse people are everywhere and I feel that the common man, elf-olog or what have you should have a piece in this book about our world.\n\nChapter One\nWith Humble Beginnings\n-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-\nI've half-rounded the world once but now plan to go as far and wide as a human possibly can, i'm in search for knowledge, a purpose- I want a feel for this world and perhaps abit of fame and money...\n\nHow i'll find all of this is anyones guess but with how I imagine i'll be searching it must be possible. Emphasis on must. I *the word stand is crossed out here* currently sit here writing at the Viridian Pub some distance away from Petrus-\n\nmy only armament being a book, a quill and my way with words. Of course if I ever get in a scuffle i'd find myself pulling out this fantastic wooden dirk at my side, lets just hope that never happens as for splinters are known for being a pain in the ass.\n\nForward along the road I travel and I get to be greeted by Petrus for perhaps the 1000th time. It's a city I take for granted now, and I shouldn't. Imperials have much hatred going their way (for which I will not digress into.) but I must give them...\n\n...credit for upholding their traditions with a strong government, excellent craftmanship and a tax system. All civilized nations must have some kind of tax system. Petrus is large and sits largely surrounded by a moat and mountian. Such a well-\n\n-planned city. It rises almost in three tiers, starting from a common area with a church, bazaars, housing and other storefront then going into the more governmental buildings such as the regients castle and a extensive barracks.\n\nAt the time of writing a thread of undeath lurks not to far from the city further along the Peaks of Godfrey but a fort has been enstablished to help strenghthen the protection of this already bear of a city. Undeath cannot dapen the souls of the-\n\n-citizens of Oren though as a wedding is to take place here soon. A wonderful ceremony, with a- *The words are scratched out here.*\n\nWhat has become of me? I have forgot all I was. Sometimes I wish I was still young.\n\nChapter Two\nSwordhand\n-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-The wedding was a bust, poor damsel wasn't there. Best to hope she wasn't off with another man. Oren shows to be a kingdom with a *The next words are scratched out and the pages after ripped.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Love's Ending 2", "§bFranswaar Kellian", "Love's Ending 2\n\n               By: \n   Franswaar Kellian\n\nThis Book is Dediciated to those who have lost everything\n\n3 years have passed since I learned. \n\nWe barely speak, though you don't have too. Whenever I see you, memories flood my mind. When we were inseperable. When we had our lives planned out. \n\nI will never undstand.\n\nYour daughter has grown  so much. She has your eyes, your nose. When I see her playing in the street, I see you in your youth. When we would play jokes on others. When I first learned that you were the love of my life, my angel\n\nI will never understand.\n\nEveryday since I have returned I have thought of no one else but you. \n\nYou were everything I had. Everything I was. Everything I wanted to achieve. I am in constant pain, though I do nothing. Tears seep down through my heart, though men are told not to\n\ncry. If this is so, I am no man.  \n\nI am a coward. I am afraid. I was born to say I love you. Every Breathe you ever took next to me, filled me with life. \n\nI will never understand.\n\nI am tired being so alone, but I am not welcome. \n\nNothing can mend my wounds, for time caused them. \n\nOnly myself is blame, I left. I told you I had too, but that is not true. I only wished to view the world. I guess my ambitions are my demise.\n\nRegrets and Guilt fill my mind as my heart is posioned by the prick of your quick removal from my life. I long to be with you again. I spend most my time overlooking the city a top the mountains unable to move, so I unwillingly climb down.\n\nSo I guess I will live alone. In your shadow. \n\nThough I have nothing to live for, nothing to hope for. No one else saw me the same as you did. \n\nI can't keep my eyes off of you. Words can't describe how I am feeling. Though I must turn away.\n\nI crawl home, in tears. There is nothing left for me on this planet. \n\nI climb apon the mountains again. The trail leads to the waterfall. \n\nPeaceful\n\nElegant \n\nBlissful\n\nSerenity\n\nI am at peace, the thoughts of you leave. I can finally go. \n\nA hand is placed on my shoulder. Darkness masks their face, but their touch is all but to well known.\n\nWhen I finally found peace to let go, you return into my life\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nI will never understand", ""),
        StaticBook("I have seen", "§bDra'Khataba", "I have seen the sun set in the frozen valleys of Kal'Akash.\n\nI have seen it rise in the burning dunes of Gundz'Koth.\n\nI have held a new born child as it breaths its first breath.\n\nI have held a dying soldier as he breathed his last.\n\nI have tried to give life and I have taken it.\n\nI have felt the seething anger deep inside.\n\nI have lost love and cried.\n\nDo thse actions make me a God or a Devil?\nOn my judgment day I will reveal.\nBecause the truth will be known.\n\nAnd maybe, just maybe I will finally find a home.", ""),
        StaticBook("Champion", "§bOberdiktator Frederick II", "===Heil Vandaloria===\n\n\n    History of the\n Valued Champion of\n    The Hallowvale\n\n Adil, the Eagle-Eyed\n\n\n   Approved by the\n   Ministry of Truth\n\nBorn in the hold of\nAl Dirahk, Adil was fathered by Amir Athir al-Hashim, the Amir of the Mubarizun. Adil had with him three brothers and a valued sister, Aisha, though he himself was destined to be the next Amir. Being so, he and Aisha were sent away.\n\nThey were sent to a mosque called Elspeth to train, Aisha in the arts of diplomacy, Adil in the arts of the duties of an Amir. For years they trained in their arts but, alas, when the time came for them to be called upon, they were abandoned.\n\nTheir father never showed up to greet them. Distraught, they left Elspeth to go adventuring... Roughly the same time large worms engulfed the continent. Assuming their family and friends were now gone, they stormed onward- together.\n\nFor ten years Adil and his sister wandered the lands, Adil playing his Oud with deft hands... And crashing bottles into the faces of ruffians alike. However as fate would have it, his wanderings rewarded him. He stumbled upon Al Wakrah, the Caliphate.\n\n... And his family. And though tearful reunions were in order, they found another member of their party with them;\n\nTheir father.\n\nAs the Ducal War had just begun, Adil and Aisha were both drafted.\n\nThe war changed Adil and his sister. The details of his battles are not important, all that is is that Adil was a man. When he and Aisha returned to Al Wakrah, they came to a breaking surprise;\n\nTheir father had committed suicide.\n\nThis event caused the two to break apart. Aishra persued knighthood in the Order of St. Lucien. Adil... Turned to becoming a slaver, and his soul began to rot. The years of slaving turned to years of drugs... Years of sin and whoring.\n\nIt was one fateful botched slave trade that landed Adil in prison. As he rotted away behind iron bars, a revelation came to him; he was a monster of pure sin. And so, upon his release, he went to find peace and forgiveness... In the Order of St. Lucien.\n\nWhile forgiveness may of been found, peace was not. In the order, Adil found him self constantly tormented by St. Lucien's leader at the time, Grand Master Baudric. Extreme training and constant beatings rendered Adil near whittled.\n\nThis was, of course, until the entire restructure of The Order. In one knight, St. Lucien had become Hallowvale Contracting, all thanks to Nafis Yar (whom Adil now calls his battle-brother). Peace and brotherhood, Adil found.\n\nBut his journy was far from over. In order for ome to be truly accepted into the Hallowvale they must overcome two challenges; kill a vile creature and beat Oberdiktator- or our glorious Kaiser- in a brawl.\n\nThe first task came when Adil was celebrating with his brothers after a battle won over a group of snowelves. During the celebration the encampment was attacked by a wendigo! The brothers took their place, Adil armed with an arbalest on a balcony.\n\nThe beast clawed it's way through the camp and made it inside. As it dangled from a shandelier, Adil fired a well-and-true shot into the creature's eye. This epic shot is what deemed him;\n\nAdil, the eagle-eyed.\n\nHowever, the arrow only made to anger the creature. It lashed out at Adil, mauling him well and- ironically and much to Adil's amusement- taking his eye as well. Though wounded, Adil's brothers revered him a hero. He was promoted to Vandar Council.\n\nCheif and budding advisor to Nafis Yar (whom was the Vandalore at the time), he continued his work as a mercenary for some time after his slaying of the beast. This was, of course, until the Orenian and Dwarven war began. Hallowvale had to choose a side.\n\nIn the end, Oren's coffers proved too tempting. Hallowvale was paid to fight orcs, snow elves, and dwarves alike. As Adil himself words it;\n\n\"I wasn't bias, I just liked money in all honesty.\"\n\nNo truer words, Adil.\n\nThe Hallowvale prospered well in this war. So well in fact, that Adil was invited on a secret mission launched toward the Caliphate. Led by our glorious Kaiser, the Hallowvale proposed to Faiz Charadeen... A union between the Caliphate and the Hallowvale.\n\nThe union was accepted of course, so birthing the Second Reich, successor of Aesterwald. Adil himself stood right behind the glorious Kaiser as the union was officially passed. In fact, the Glorious Kaiser became very close.\n\n... But let's save that for later in this story.\n\nShortly after this unification, Vandalore Nafis Yar retired, Frederick II, our glorious Kaiser, taking his place. And so a new Champion was required... \n\nAdil was chosen.\n\n\"He was my Champion, the man all Vanders of Hallowvale looked up to and had to beat in a slobber knocker fist fight wrestling match to be oathed,\" Adil had to say of the glorious Kaiser. He was awestruck in his choosing to become the new Champion.\n\nAnd so, comfortable in his new position behind the walls of Al Wakrah, Adil was of course admitted into the town's new Caliphate/Hallowvale garrison... Where he met his lovely court, Rajiya. Adil had little to say, just that he hopes to marry soon.\n\n\n\n\n\nAdil, Champion of Vandaloria, shall truly be remembered as a proper hero and Champion by all.\n\nThe Kaiser himself ahd this to say of the man;\n\n\n\"Adil is headstrong, but valiant. Simple words for a simple man, I knew him before his eye incident. He's the best archer I know and a Master of Filth.\n\nAdil and I were battle brothers before politicans and I don't think I can be very poetic with my words, but he's a good soldier and an even better man.\"\n\n\n       Heil Kaiser!\n   Praise be to Adil.", ""),
        StaticBook("Traveler Of Seas", "§bDorin Starbreaker XII", "Traveler Of Sea's\n\n A Tale Of A Dwarf at               Sea\n\n\n\n\nBy:Dorin Starbreaker\n\n\n\n           1288\n\nThe year was 1160, and the time was of winter. The fire was roaring and the crys could be heard all throughout the village. But not every cry was coming from screaming villagers, but one coming from a female dwarf, Summer Starbreaker, giving birth to a\n\nnew dwarf. Along side her, holding her hand was Gherren Irongut. The baby was almost out when a orc raider came in and charged her, but he got no where near close as Gherren threw a fireball at his face, killing him. The baby was almost out and Gherren\n\ngrabed the babys body as it came out.\"w'at is it, a boy or girl?\". asked Summer \"A boy, w'at s'ould we call 'im 'oney?\" said Gherren \"I don't kn'w, 'and 'im over and i'll dicide.\". Gherren hand over the newborn, still in his birthday suit. The mother\n\nlooked at her new child, and said \"You know, i t'ink 'e look's like a Dorin... Yes Dorin Starbreaker.\" Don't ye t'ink 'e s'ould be called Irongut?\"asked Gherren, \"No... i want him to have my last name and be a excelent magic user... just like you!\".\n\nGherren though for a second, and how this could effect the baby in the future, but he did'nt think long as he had to get the baby to safty, He looked over to his wife, something was wrong however... He did not notice it untill now, but the baby had been\n\ncrying still in his mothers hands. Gherren knew she was having trouble breating but he did not think it was this bad... She had died. A tear droped from his face, but he did not think long, for the baby still had to be taken to safety... The docks!\n\nGherren grabed the baby and wraped him in a blanket, then started to run for the docks. While he was runing, he decided to hold the baby in one hand and focused for a moment, with his right hand up, a few seconds of running and a flame appeared in his\n\npalm, lighting the path that was dark untill now. He ran, and he could see the docks and all the light's coming from the now leaving boats's. He was just about to get off of the path onto the dock when a orc shaman appeared, and he shouted at Gherren\n\nwho stayed his distance as the orc shouted in a accent he could not understand. After a moment, the Shaman lifted his hand and the sky darkend up with cloud's, lighting started to spark through them... Gherren had to think fast.\n\nHe closed his eyes, the lightnig flaring in the backround, he concentraded and everything became quite. He stuck his hand up the fire going out, and water started to spout around Gherren and the baby, and as fast as the water had appeared, lightning\n\nflashed and struck the water, and the water fizziled and started to buzz, Gherren quikly made the water disapper along with the lightning. He looked up at the Shaman, who game a look of impress, but he still wanted to kill them, and he raised his hand\n\nagain. Gherren took fast action and put his hand up as well, and a blue flame appeared, small at first, but it quikly grew to the size of a softball, then it grew to a purple state, and the heat grew, wind started to form around it as it comsumed the air.\n\nHe looked to his foe, and smirked, throwing the ball, the shaman tried to run, but the Evocationest moved his hand, the fire following his command. The ball struck the orc's back and he fell down, his cloth's burned off and his skin singed to the bone.\n\nGherren smirked and then started to run again, he passed the dead orc and smirked. Gherren had gotten to the dock in time for the last boat, and handed the newborn over to the captin of the boat, he told him his name and said he was to be taken to his\n\nGrandfather, at the dwarven capital and taught magic when he get's old enough. Before Gherren could climb upinto the boat however, a arrow stuck him in the back and he fell slightly. Not even a second later, 3 more arrows hit him in the back, he fell, and\n\nwith his last dieing breath he managed to say \"Go!\". The captin looked and understood... He was not going to make it, he turned around and told his crew to put the sail's up and they pushed off the dock, sailing away as arrows flew at\n\nthem, the newborn baby crying in the distance.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    75 Years later\n\n\"Are ye sure ye don't want te come wit' me grandfat'er? I could use t'e 'elp wit' finding me master...\" Dorin said to his grandfather, both f them sitting around a fire, behind Dorin a bag filled with food and fire wood, a pouch set aside with about\n\n500minas. \"no...no ye need to go on t'is journey yerself. lot's of exsperince you will get from doing t'is on yer own... yer already almost 100... ye need te' proove yerself, so don't come back if ye 'avent masterd any magic!\". The young dwarf looked to\n\nhis grandfather, he was not surprised, after all, his grandfather had always been this stubern, why would it change now? Dorin was 75 now, and it was time for him to go look for a master to teach him the ways of the fire and water evocationist.\n\nHe had beed given food and fire wood for his travel's, and 500 minas to travel on the boats. He had everything he needed now and he was ready to set off. \"Alrig't grandfat'er, 'ave it yer way. W'en i get back ill be a Evocationist and ye will be proud!\"\n\nDorin said. \"Well boy, i 'ope yer rig't, because if ye don't t'en i will surely be disapointed in ye, so make me proud! Now ye better get goen, yer boat leave's at newn does it not?\".\"Aye, it does, well ill be off now.\"Dorin got up from his chair, took\n\none last look at his grandfather and then walked out the door, heading strait for the dwarven docks, but he was not going for just a ride... But instead a job. He had not told his grandfather that realy he was going to pursue his dream of becoming a\n\nsailor and crossing the seas, exsploring.\nDorin arrived at the dock and looked for the boat he had been hired to sail on for the next year, taking cargo to the distant islands. He found the ship and looked at the sun, it was newn, and it was time to leave\n\nhe was late! Dorin started to run for his ship hoping the captin would not yell at him for his tardyness. Dorin quikly found the boat and got aboard.\nThe captin had beed waiting for him. He was a tall man, large muscles, he must have been doing this job\n\nyears, as his hands were coverd in cysts, and they cracked every time he moved them. He also had a very large beard, almost as large as Dorin's beard, which went down to his chest, although it would be larger if he hadnt shaved it. \"Reporting for duty\n\nCpt.Theodore.\" The captin looked at the dwarf, \"you can just call me Teddy, your one of my sailors now, and we're in it togeather in the long run, so we might as well get better aquanted. You have anything you might want to be called beside's... What was\n\nit again? Dorin,Right?\"\nThe Cpt. said \"Aye sir, t'at be my name Dorin Starbreaker, and nay, i don't have anything id like to be called sir.\" Said Dorin. \"You can drop the formalitys, like i said, justcall me Teddy. Now then, your quarters are down stairs\n\n3 doors to your right, it has a bed, a desk and chair with a window, and a chest. You can get settled in and we will launch off at about 6. So get settled, maybe a nap, and wait untill i say otherwise.\n\n      Later That Day\n\nDorin was settled in, and started sleep, he woke up about 4 hours later, when he could hear yelling from the captin. It was time to leave and he could hear the other sailors rushing down the hall to the main deck. Dorin got up with excitement, and ran to\n\nthe main deck along with the other sailors. The captin called them to attention, 6 sailors standing in a row. \"Listen up! We have a newcomer to our ship. His name is Dorin, Dorin why don't you step out now, and introduce yourself to everyone?\"\n\n\"Aye Teddy.\" Dorin said as he stepped out to introduce himslef to the other 5 sailors, 3 other dwarfs, and 2 humans. \"I am Dorin Starbreaker, and i am hoping to become a member of this crew for a very long time, i 'ope we will all bec'me very well\n\naquainted on 'ur journey, and i lott forward to many adventures.\" One of the crew members, the one closest to him, put thier hand out and introduced herself. She was a thing shorter dwarf, with brown hair and a mole right by her lip.\n\nShe was dressed in a leather armor of sorts. \"Nice te meet a fellow dwarf sailor. My name is Diana.\" She said, her voice in a high pitched manner. \"aye nice to meet you as well.\" The captin did'nt want to waste much time on aquatinces and so he introduced\n\nthe others for them.\n\"from left to write this is Maveric, Sally, and Loki, and Diana has already introduced herself to you.\" Teddy said as he was slowly looking back and forth between them all. \"Aye! Nice te meat ye all, i 'ope we all become great friends\n\n.\" The captin raised his hand and everyone moved out to the sail's and started to unravel them, letting them loose. Dorin looked to the sun, and before he knew it the captin had already pushed them o the dock nd they were floating to the ocean.\n\n       1 Month later\n\n\"Land ahoy!\" Yelled Maveric atop the crows nest. \"We finaly made it then? How long until we dock?\"Yelled Dorin to the man. \"About 1/2-1 hour until we dock, so get ready, and wake the captin!\" Dorin took note and then went into the\n\nunderdeck, heading for the captins quarters. He knocked on the door and said \"Captin, you up? We have seen land, we will be ready for docking in about an hour.\"Dorin said. Over the month that Dorin had spent with the crew, he had merged accents\n\nto fit thiers, and now he sounded as a human. \"Aye, im up. Tell the rest of the crew to get ready, we have a lot of work today.\"\nLater that hour, the boat slowly rocked as it floated ever closer to the dock of the island. It was filled with jungle trees\n\nand there were lots of cat people, all of them stairing as the boat arrived. The boat finaly touched down and some of the cat people ran up, puting a wooden pallet across the water to the boat as a little bridge. \"Dorin, get that rope and tie us up.\"\n\nsaid the captin. \"Aye sir!\" Dorin replyed, quikly grabing the boat's rope and tieing them to the dock. \"Get the cargo, we have to take it all the way to the palace up the road. Dorin and the other crew member's, including the captin went down into the\n\nhull, each coming out with a crate. They took the crates up the road where they finaly got to a palace. They stoped outside the gate where a man walked up and asked them to take them to the celler. They did as the man asked but when they got down into the\n\nceller, the door shut, they were traped! And they stayed traped for 315 years, at least as slaves that is.\n\n\n\n\n      315 Years later\n\n\"Get up! you need to get up, get your things and run for the docks!\" Diana shouted \"I can't just leave you! Besides they killed our son!\" DOrin shouted back. \"Aye, but it will take one of us to hold them off for the other to exscape, and you still have\n\nfamily! I don't!\" Diana shouted back. \"What about me?\" \"yes you are my family, but as i stated one of us has to get of this island, and i could'nt live without you, you can!\" She shouted \"Now go!\"Dorin had to make a desision, but he did'nt want to leave\n\nher. He decided it was best to leave her and ran, he got to the docks, that had been plated with iron since the last time he had seen it. He got aboard a boat and pushed off, a pair of cats running up yelling at him to give it back. He did not and a tear\n\nfell from his eye as he heard a scream from the night.\n\n\n\n\n   Dorin Starbreaker\n\nBook Two Coming Soon", ""),
        StaticBook("The Lonely Cow", "§bKarter", "The Lonely Cow\n\n   There once was a cow that was in a pen with many other cows. The cow lived a short life of watching his friends get murdered and breeded over and over and over again. He needed to stop it, all of it. But he couldn't do anything, so he\n\nkept waiting to get murdered and either get eaten or have his skin put on others. He waited a lot more but never got murdered. He just sat in the pen, but eventually he was set free! He escaped and ran as far as he could then a man named Archrim walked to\n\nthe cow, he pulled out his bow and arrow and shot the cow in the face and ate it with his son later. \n\n\n\n        THE  END!", ""),
        StaticBook("Dark Shamans", "§bHektor Blackwood", "A new foul being has inserted itself into our Lands. This being is extremely resiliant, capable of great feats of magic. It is no Wraith, Archon, or Lich. In fact, it is not of Light or Dark. It is of a Spiritual Nature. They are, The Dark Shamans.\n\nDark Shamans are known as practitioners of a vile art, not their own Creature or Race. Your writer understands this, though your writer also has faced a new breed of Dark Shaman. It all started in Courland. I encountered a skeletal being wielding\n\nscorching hot air that singed flesh upon contact. I made battle with this being, with the help of brave Men and Elves. Eventually we toppled this foul creature, and I recovered some of the ash left behind when it dis-integrated. It may have been a Lich,\n\nthough Liches cannot combine Evocations. They also cannot be unimpacted by Clerics, which this being was during the fight. I had a compulsive need to fully understand this creature (Which to this day, I do not). A Druid appraised the Ash I collected, and\n\nstated that it was of a Shamanistic nature. I met some Orcs in Sutica. One of them was kind enough to tell me that the creatures were Dark Shamans. I was quite puzzled. I've read of brief bits and pieces of the Dark Shamans from Athera. They looked\n\nnothing like the ones from the days of Athera. I theorize that perhaps this is caused by the source of the Vile Being's magic. Dark Shamans back in Athera derived their power from enslaving Spirits. Perhaps these Skeletal Shamans interact with the\n\nSpirit-Gods of the Mythical Realm in a different way. In a way that truly saps and stretches out their very being. Even with someone as such a keen mind as your Writer, I cannot even fathom how these Dark Shamans interact with the Spirits.\n\nI still keep the Ash with me to this day as a means of both symbolism and practical use. To me, it symbolizes knowledge and it's practical use. If the men and women of Axios can probe the secrets of these horrific beings, we can surely defend ourselves.", ""),
        StaticBook("Sunless Sanctum", "§bKass", "===================\n\n  The Cloud Temple\n        Of Aegis\n===================\n\nThe Cloud Temple of Aegis was once a great place some of the eldest living members of society have fond memories of. But that place is no longer a place of light and hope. It is now the Abyss or Sunless Sanctum, and it is the place of darkness and\n\ndesolation. The only way to get to the Sanctum is to find or have someone who knows where it is, take you there. The portal is larger than a small house, maybe even a medium sized one.  But one thing is for sure, Ghouls, Wraths, and other Dark Creatures.\n\nMost will not like you, some will simply see you as a meal, and some may simply want to make you a slave of their dark purpose. I write this book so that no one will need to go there themselves. All that enter, will not leave the same. The weight of what\n\nis seen, is worse than the weight on your soul that is gained while there. This is the first and final warning to you, the reader. Now, to carry on.\n\n___________________      Observations\n___________________ \nThe Abyss is a place seeming to exist in it's own dimension of darkness, no light from anywhere but that seen from the lights around it. Warped pillars are thrown about randomly about, except on\n\nthe dark paths, leading to the different places of residence each species takes. In the center of the Abyss, and first structure to come into view, is the ruins of the Cloud Temple Of Aegis.\n\n*A sketch in red ink, or blood. a dragon eating it's tail lies upon the page. no eye defined as if it lies elsewhere....*", ""),
        StaticBook("Glory to Norland", "§bVykk Volaren", "==Glory to Norland==\n\nAn official song of Norland written by Vykk Volaren, a Thane of Clan Thorrson\n\nStrength and Honor is what we seek!\nOff in our longboats so mighty and sleek!\nThe land of Horen so bland and bleak!\nWeakness and scum is what we reap!\nGold and loot is what we now reek!\n\nBack to Norland so mighty and fine!\nWe wash off the blood, we wash off the brine!\nA celebration with ale and wine!\nBrawling and singing, a merry ole time!\nGlory to Norland so mighty and fine!\n\nIron from ice! is what we shout!\nOren flees with brothers about!\nThey fear death when we charge out!\nHelp! they scream while we make them route!\nWe’ll smack their ass and make them pout!\n\nBack to Norland so mighty and fine!\nWe wash off the blood, we wash off the brine!\nA celebration with ale and wine!\nBrawling and singing, a merry ole time!\nGlory to Norland so mighty and fine!", ""),
        StaticBook("I am Karter", "§bKarter Igne'tahorran", "I am Karter.\n\nMy name is Karter Igne'tahorran I have chosen the fate to be made into a construct and work under Lily of Sutica. I am a Wood Elf with blue eyes and only one ear (my left ear) I now have black air and I am 56 years of age.\n\nAs stated in the first page I am volunteering to become a construct working for Sutica, more precisely under Lily, the princess herself. I understood that I will not remember anything about myself after I have changed into my 'new form'.\n\nAfter I have changed my only wish is to serve for Sutica as long as I possibly can, and assist all of those in need under Lily's command of course.\nI choose to do this because I want to serve Sutica in a more honorable way than most have ever done.\n\n I would like to thank all of those that I have ever met and See you later!   Bye. . .", ""),
        StaticBook("Ascended-Unveil", "§bGranantar Fournier Ateus", "Seeking the Ascended is a daunting task. It requires mental fortitude that rivals that of the hardest steel, tempered by the most fiery inferno.\n\nOn the contrary, what may be home to the Ascended, resides in a harsh wintery, frozen land.\n\nWith Spikes of Ice skewering the chilled air, blade-like glass that sever one's legs and lenghty spires that seem like broken bridges.\n\nAmongst the frosty wrath, is a floating fortress. Gently hovering in the air-less sky. Peerless, but forgotten.\n\nThis frosty land can be found just South of Kal'Agnar, the home of the Dwarves. Beyond the peaked frosty mountains and slightly west of a large lake. But beware, only the dwarves can withstand the chill of this frozen hell.", ""),
        StaticBook("Undru's Notes", "§bThe Lord of Undru", "One will always come.\n\nIt never dissappears as long as the Sildur is up in it's hands.\n\nWe won't die.\n\nIt's not the End\n\nWe are the End.\n\n\n\nDeath is only the start.", ""),
        StaticBook("FTM, Issue I", "§bArchibald", "-+ From the Mouth +-\n\n   Good day, good night, or good evening, or even good morning. I am Archibald Vallero. This book is quite simple. I go around asking other people questions, sometimes deep and personal, and then scribe them down in this tome\n\nfor you all to read and enjoy. I've no bias this time when it comes to who I ask, though keep in mind the demographics of the Descendant races. Enjoy. \n\nI will begin this first issue with an answer from one of my friends, Zeliek, a paladin of Xan.\n\nQI: Do you prefer a dictatorship or a republic? Why?\n\nZeliek: \"Republic. I just picked one at random. I don't even know what you're talking about.\"\n\nQ2: Do you think that crucifying people like the Amyasmen do is worse than torture? If so, why?\n\nShariana: \"Alright, I think that crucifixion is taking the easy way to solve something. Because torture, you have a... Hand on experience with\n\nsomeone. Hm... I think that is everything, really. The Amyas' have no experience with torture, and I doubt that any of them would be man enough to try it.\"\n\nQ3: Do you think that magic, in the future, will cause unforeseen catastrophes? If so, why?\n\nSalendys Doomforged: \"It has in the past and it will keep happening as long as there are irresponsible mages around.\"\n\nQ4: Do you believe that one of the Descendant races should be exterminated from the face of this realm? If so, which one and why?\n\nLyria Serthek:  \"Absolutely not. Because we all have a right to live, and why slay off a /whole/ race? Only a certain\n\nfew should die, and not be compared to their whole race.\"\n\nQ5: Do you believe that one day we will run out of continents and land to return to?\n\nCharles Fournier: \"No, why not?\"\n\nQ6: Thighs or breasts.\n\nForrest Sarr: \"Thighs.\"\n\nQ7: Would you prefer a redhead with green eyes, or a brunette with blue eyes?\n\nJon Snow: \"Brunette, of course! Redheads are usually... crazy.\"\n\nQ8: Do you think that any Descendant race will become extinct within the next 100 years? If so, which one?\n\nExalprick Oxenfeldt: \"Um... Yes I'll smash all their heads, crush 'm against a wall! Put them on spikes!\"\n\nQ9: Is the abundance of mages within the realm causing more or less chaos?\n\nDain Stormcrow: \"There are an abundance of mages? From what I understand of magic, or at least one of its major philosophies is balance. If balance\n\nis not kept preserved, things go out of whack.\"\n\nQ10: Would you rather eat a raw spider, or have a silverfish placed in your ear?\n\nDemagol Doomforged: \"Spideh.\"\n\nQ11: Do you think that necromantic practices are simply misunderstood, or, truly, are they just evil? \n\nAsher: \"Um, misunderstood.\n\nQ12: Are the four Descendant races doing considerably well given their curses?\n\nCheza Stirling: \"Some are, some aren't. Some better than others. Each race is doing 'alright' I suppose. Each has land, but they have problems.\"\n\nQ13: Is there truly such a thing as 'good' or 'evil' in your opinion, or is everything just formed by the aengudaemons above?\n\nGuarger Tsouderous: \"Everything is based on opinion, Archibald.\"\n\nQ14: What would you do if you saw an Orcish cub being bullied by a group of elven civilians? \n\nRyder Sturtart: \"Pick the cub up and take him away.\"\n\nQ15: Would you rather have to assassinate a loved king, or kill a new born child? Which one and why?\n\nSam Beadoin: \"King. Kings are all corrupt, no matter how loved. At least, human kings are...\"\n\nQ16: Do you think that immortality is achievable? \n\nOan: \"Yes, I think it is. What one must ask themselves, though, is this: How much are you willing to give up for it?\"\n\nQ17: Were the 'golden' ages of Aegis overrated?\n\nCharis: \"I wouldn't know, but Mister KAlen speaks of Alras as if it were golden. So, I can't say no.\"\n\nQ18: Would you rather kill a past lover or an old, human man?\n\nLicia: \"I have no past lovers. I am sworn to Xan and the creator only.\"\n\nQ19: Are aengudaemons the cause of all issues in the world, or is it truly us who has caused all these faults?\n\nMithras: \"It's a mix. Aenguls and Daemons interfere and use us because of how flawed we are. All of us differentiate, we all hold differ-\n\n-ent views. It makes us easy pawns in their games, they're merely bored children, you see. It's like when a child plays with a chess set. We're the pieces.\"\n\nQ20: Do you prefer to eat something sweet or sour at night? And why? \n\nCaitlyn: \"Sweet. Sour never tastes nice.\"\n\nQ21: Are we really in control of our own lives? Is our fate chosen by us?\n\nDaemon: \"We are in control of our lives. Fate is for the weak who need something to look forward to rather than doing it themselves.\"\n\nQ22: Who would win in a fight to the death: Blundermore or Archmage Cataris?\n\nLuthar: \"Blundermore. Despite Liches' callous and insidious nature,  our trickery only goes so far against raw holy power.\"\n\nQ23: From Iblees to Setherien, what has been the Descendant race's greatest threat in your opinion?\n\nJa'Mukar: \"Setherien. Because Iblees nevever did anything and he was defeated quite quickly. Where as Setherien showed his face and not hide\n\naway in an unknown location.\" \n\n*Translated by me, his accent was very thick!*\n\nQ24: Will the Human empire of Oren eventually dissolve entirely in the future, in your opinion?\n\nHorik Storasvard: \"Nay! Oren vill be forever! Because dey have da drue faith in da creador! And dey are the stronges der es!\"\n\nQ25: Out of all the four kinds of architecture exhibited by the Descendant races, which race's architecture do you favor the most? \n\nSer Bjorn of Bearhill: \"Human. It makes me feel safe. Other nations buildings just look . . . disgusting.\"\n\nQ26: If you had immortality, what's the first goal you would want to achieve?\n\nBeth: \"Uh . . . Eat a bunch of cake.\"\n\nQ27: From elven women to human, which one would you say is the most attractive, hands down? \n\nOliver Crowley: \"Human. Better build and often faces have more beauty to them.\"\n\nQ28: Where did homosexuality originate, do you think? The high elves, perhaps, or the elven kind in general? Even another race, even?\n\nVuln Shadeleaf: \"I don't know where it originated, but it sure seems likely it started with the mali'ker.\"\n\nQ29: House Chivay or House Horen? \n\nDread Knight Lennox(Dread Knight Noxx): \"I don't prefer either . . . both are idiotic.\"\n\n*I decided to ask him another question!*\n\nQ30: Was immortality worth it?\n\nDread Knight Lennox: \"Indeed.\"\n\nQ31: Would you rather demolish an orphanage or build a brothel?\n\nDorian Hunter: \"Build a brothel. Obviously. It's obvious. Girls.\"\n\nThat's the end of this book. But do not worry, this is only part one! I'm thinking of writing up another quite soon, and there'll be questions just as humorous or serious in the next one as there was in this one. Thank you for reading. Also, I'd rather\n\nnot answer any questions myself until after the end of the second issue, thanks. \n\n       //---\\\\\n      //       \\\\\n     //         \\\\\n    //========\\\\\n   //              \\\\\n  //                \\\\\n //                  \\\\", ""),
        StaticBook("IHBH:SA", "§bSer Paul Ryan", "- - - - - - - - - - - -         It's Hard\n    Being Handsome:\n\n  How to survive in \n  Athera, and other\n        life tips\n- - - - - - - - - - - - \n   By: Ser Pavl Ryan\nWith special foreword         by Emperor \n   Tuvya Carrion I\n\n TABLE OF CONTENTS: \n\n- Foreword\n- Authors Note\n- Chapters I-V\n- About the Author\n\n        FOREWORD\n-------------------\n\nMeeting Ser Paul Ryan, much less becoming good personal friends with him, will most certainly be one of the most defining moments of my life. I've seen much in my time as the Emperor of Athera's greatest nation, but\n\nnever have I seen such strict dedication to greatness as I have in Paul. Whether it was making him part of my privy council, calling upon him to lead a heroic charge in times of war, or simply asking the favor of his attendance to my young daughters\n\nnameday party, I knew I could count on a man as loyal and reliable as Paul. Reading his first book, as well as getting a sneak peak at his upcoming biography, granted me a different outlook on life, and how I live it. Never before have I experienced such\n\na thrilling page turner as I have when reading \"It's Hard Being Handsome\". I recommend this fantastic encyclopedia of majesty to all men and women, regardless of age, race or gender, who one day wish to become sucessful.\n\n     AUTHORS NOTE\n-------------------In my times of travel around the land of Athera, I've come to learn a great deal of things, both through speaking to some of the oldest and wisest masters of \nmany skills, and trial and error as I blundered\n\nmy way through city after city, town after town. I've learnt how to fight great mythical beast, how to drink a \ndwarf under the table, how to hide from halflings, sell my legitimate wares to all kinds of illigitimate folk, and a great deal of other skills\n\n. But if there's \none thing I've learned above all else, it's this; \nIt's hard \nbeing handsome. \n\nHi, I'm Ser Paul Ryan, and this is my book.\n\nMany times through the years, I've been approached by great kings and lowly merchants, all desperate for my advice. I've been the honored guest in many foriegn \ncourts, and handed out justice in my own lands.\n\n I recieved many great awards, both for my continued service to House Carrion and Oren as a whole, and for helping\nout the distressed through all kinds of situations. Yet I soon found out that, no matter how great I was (and trust me, I was great),\n\n my life always seemed....\nempty. By the time I had done all of these great things and achieved knighthood and gentry status, all at the ripe old age of 18, I decided to write down my \nadventures, and, more importantly,\n\nthe rules with which I've come to live life. In the following pages, you too will learn to live by these rules. Good luck, friend,\nand I hope you enjoy it!\n\nCHAPTER ONE (I)\n-------------------Find friends:\nOne of the earliest lessons I learned in life was this; EVERYTHING'S better with friends, friend. Becoming close acquaintance to esteemed men and women like the \nruling class of Oren,\n\nor simply the pal of the local drunk and homeless, friends help you through all sorts of trouble. Need a place to stay? Fine a friend. Need\na loan? Find a friend. Need help breaking Goldsteins' stinking knees, because he still hasn't paid you back\n\nfor that time in the brothel? Find a friend. No matter\nwhere I go in all of Athera, I can always count on finding room and board, courtesy of my good pals. If you really think you're a step above the rest, you can even\ntry to become one of MY friends,\n\nand join Paul's Pals™, my adventuring group of heroes. For better or for worse, helping out someone in need of a hand can result \nin some good luck coming your way down the road. Remember my original and trademarked saying:\n\n A friend in need is a friend indeed, who, in the future, will owe you™. \n\nCHAPTER TWO (II)\n-------------------Find fortune\n\nOne of the greatest assets any man has in lifes' adventure is his coin purse.\n\nMany a schlub can be bribed, and it never hurts to have the pocket coinage to buy that\nfancy new automatic crossbow, or perhaps donate to your favorite local political party. But how does one obtain such vast, impressive wealth like that owned by\n\nthe plutocrats of our age, such as Emperor Tuvya, or Ser Paul Ryan? Well, friend, let me tell you; hard work, creativity, and a good bit of honest borrowing can go far. I myself made my first fortune when I founded CapCo™, Oren's premier\n\nentreprenuerial company, which now is valued at roughly 500,000 minas. By working hard to achieve\nyour dream, and marketing your products correctly, you'll soon build the capital needed to further your endeavors, like\n\nperhaps launching a new, better company. Good\nbusiness partners are a must have, and I myself continue to hold close correspondance with my good friend and investor, Jacob Goldstein, of Goldstein & Company. After I safely handed over CapCo™\n\nto the care of my board of directors, my next startup companies, Paul's Pawn & Picking™, Paul's Tonics & Powders™, Paul's Ghost Hunting™, \nand Paul's Travelling Stall™all quickly flourished under my careful experience. Need some help starting down the\n\npath to vast wealth? Try finding a local investor\nto give you a startup fee, and bleed that sucker dry! If you need some immediate cash, perhaps to pay back the loan shark who threatened to break your fabulous \ncheekbones, consider\nborrwing someone\n\nsomeone elses valuables and using those to pay. Remember, friends: It's not illegal if you're not caught. \n\nCHAPTER THREE (III)\n-------------------Find your talents\n\nIt never helps to have good qualities, friend.\n\n I myself am incredibly handsome and creative, and possess a myriad of skills that would make a jack of all trades cry\nuncle. But how can you exploit the talents you have? Simple; by deceptively manipulating others. Are you a heavy hitter? Consider\n\nstrong arming your way into the \nlocal tavern, and borrowing some of his ale to sell later at a profit. Small and sneaky? Deceptively sneak into a store after hours, and help yourself to the register. \nOr perhaps you're like me,\n\na reknown inventor throughout the lands, sought after for his political savviness and charm. Remember that in this life, NOTHINGS free. Make\nsure you charge the right amount to give that little girl the location you saw her lost dog in, or to hide that\n\nhalfling running from some hungry orcs. If you don't \ncapitalize on all of your chances to make yourself more marketable, you'll never make it in our dog-eat-dog world.\n\nCHAPTER FOUR (IV)\n-------------------Find adventure\n\nSure, friend, you may be able to talk the talk, but can you walk the walk? It doesn't matter if you've accomplished all of these other steps to becoming great, if you don't USE them! Go out on the\n\nroad, and venture into lands previously uncharted. Have a drink at that edgy tavern, or consider stopping by your good dwarf friends\nto see what they've been up to lately. Seeing the world is its own lesson, one that anyone can take, and for free.\n\n Learn how to fight or talk your way out of \nany situation by haggling with the Vanirs over the price of a fish, or wrestling with Orcs in their sand pits. Awe the local milkmaid with tales of your completely\nlegitimate exploits, or spend a night sweeping\n\nat a tavern to cover your room and board. Life in our world is uncertain, friend, but it's always worth checking around the \ncorner to see what kind of trouble you can get into. Fall down and break a leg, or wake up without memory in the temple of those\n\nmonks who definitly enchanted this\nholy water I can sell you at a discount price? Get back up, and try again. If you're ever torn between relative safety, or a possible adventure, ask yourself one \nthing: What would Paul do?\n\nCHAPTER FIVE (V)\n-------------------Putting it all together, and surviving the process\n\nYes, friend, it's challening to learn how to be as great as me, and even more challenging to try, but the results will always pay off. By combining\n\nmy advice with\na little salt-of-experience you gain on your own, you'll quickly find yourself developing into the man or woman you only dreamed of becoming. Go out there, learn\nyour good qualities, get rid of your bad ones, and make\n\nsome good memories while you're at it. Work your way from the gutter to a palace, and go from using that\nrusty dagger to defend yourself to summoning the most powerful of magics. You will fail at first, friend. After all, no one is perfect.\n\n(well, actually, speak for yourself, bub)\nbut the lessons you learn in your failure will help you in years to come. Inevtiably, the great success you'll achieve will make the lesser peons\nof this great realm jealous of you. They'll wish they had your\n\ncreativity, your work ethic, and above all, your dashing good looks. There's a great deal I've learned\nmyself in my many adventures, most of all I've found that, yes, it really IS hard, being handsome. But would I ever give it up?\n\nNot for anything in this world, friend. \n\n- Ser Paul Ryan\n\nSome Quick Tips! \n\nWant to get better at those strange moments of your life, when everyone suddenly has magical symbols above their heads, and people start hitting each other? Try \npracticing in your free time with some pals and a wooden\n\nsparring sword, or, if worse comes to worse, learn how to flee effectively! To fight another day, of course.\n\nDon't try so hard to win all of the time, friend! In gambling, tussling, or even a friendly debate, losing can often yield more than winning.\n\nIf you see Paul or one of his many friends throughout the land, consider stopping for a chat or challenge with them, rather than strangely running and jumping by.\n\nPick a skill or service, become talented in it, and learn how to make a living through it. Sell your paintings or swords in a stall, or on the road, like me! Get a job as a petty laborer to garner experience, before ultimately starting a rival\n\ncompany up with stolen secrets, and crush your former boss!\n\nGo outside every once in a while, friend. And I don't just mean leaving your hovel in Athera.\n\n\nHave fun, friend. What else are we here for?\n\n - About the Author - -------------------Ser Paul Ryan is a successful warrior, statesman, and inventor of great reknown. Founder and CEO of CapCo™, and a number of other popular brands, such as \nPaul's Pawn & Picking™,\n\n Paul's Travelling Stall™, Paul's Pals™, and Paul's World Fair™, he was born in the small village of Wesconsen, before squiring under Boris Carrion III. \nKnighted by the late Emperor Peter Chivay for valor extraordinare in the field of battle.", ""),
        StaticBook("Velu's childhood", "JaketheDog115", "Viscerial:\nMade horrid remarks about\nkilling another Aheral. \nDecapitation then head \nstuffed upon a spike. \nImpurity in violent remarks\nReminded me of a Uruk\nAlso dueled in the middle of the city, more violence.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nKorus: \nChild beater, smacked me and Nemer on the back of our heads without permission, or guardianship of us, mean man. Can't argue for his life.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nBraxus:\nNormal guard, no outstanding information. Lost a hand, haha.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nLelien Lazul: Guardian, great lady. Kind, gentle... enjoy her company very much. Lavender tea friend!\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nNemir: Good friend, close friend. Enjoy her company a lot. She was nice to me, not as annoying as Akaleh\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nAkaleh: Annoying little child, will not leave me alone. Keeps trying to make me join a group. Answer is no.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nAyr:\nNice girl, good guard. We worked on gate duty together, friend for me should I have problems.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nElwyn: Pissy at start till I showed her how well I did gate duty, thanked me for it. Resisted cuteness. Must work harder.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nCael: Stutters like a, well he stutters. Was mean to him at first. Were kinda made up now.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nAdriel: Pulled me off a wall for no reason, hurt a lot.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nAthri:\nDitzy, and strange. Clearly does not understand our ways. Needs education.\nWants to be an Alchemist. Claims to be of pure birth, no signs to say otherwise. Lonely.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nTali: Seems nice, went splat\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nOrlan: New person, seems a little odd.", ""),
        StaticBook("1519 Diary Toren", "§bToren Amroth", "13th of Malins Welcome, 1519:\n\nMight as well start off with who I am. \n\nMy name is Toren Amroth, I have a brother named a stoic and a beautiful girlfriend named Juliel.\n\nFamily:\n\nSo I was the youngest out of me 4 brothers: Brock, Boic, Stoic, George and me.\n\nBrock died when I just maybe coming up to three years old. Didn't remember him that well, he had curly brown hair that ran to his shoulders.\n\nBoic was a tall and sturdy man, us highlanders are known for our height he was 6'6!\n\nHe had a scruffy stubble, and dark brown eyes, his voice was deep and raspy like my fathers.\n\nWhen he was 21 he moved to Petrus and joined the soldiers.\n\nThis was in Athera by the way.\n\nHe was patrolling with his men until a group of savage orcs ambushed them and slaughtered them.\n\nBit's of blood and crushed bones were all of left behind. He wore a gold ring which my brother stoic took for memories.\n\nGeorge:\n\nHe had wavy blonde hair like my mother, bright blue eyes and a skinny build. His face was boneless and his lips were thin.\n\nHe worked as a carpenter until he was 17, and moved out with his girlfriend.\n\nI never saw him again after that.\n\nStoic:\n\nSo that leaves Stoic. He's my best friend, also best brother. He can be harsh but I love him, he's the muscular one, took after me father: Hodrick Amroth.\n\nHe's more of soldier than a...whatever I am.\n\nHe always takes care of me, although i'm smarter than him.\n\nAnd that leaves me: Toren Amroth.\n\nI have navy blue eyes and a slender, skinny build like my mother, I never took notice in war I never liked violence.\n\nI prefered to fish, collect flowers, talk to people rather than join the army.\n\nMe and my girlfriend Juliel met a few days ago, I started speaking to her and offered her into the tavern we began talking for hours. That was also the day I asked her out.\n\nShe's so pretty: She has long brown hair thats silky and smoothe.\n\nHer eyes are wonderful, and she is quite delicate.\nShe looks after me and listens to my complaints.\n\nI love her very much.\n\nWell thats all for now i'll write later.\n\n22nd Of Malins Welcome 1519:\n\nWas approached by a huge beast today, it looked Orcish and some what human.\nDalia said it's name was Gregor it was huge I fell back onto a rock as took a giant step forward.\nIt's large feet sunk into the soil.\n\nHe had long black hair, and wore ripped trousers his body was exposed it had thick, black hairy chest hair on it it's grimy yellow teeth shone in the sunlight.\n\nI fell onto the ground shielding my face from him, he picked me up and bear hugged me.\n\nI could have died.\n\nI have a weird feeling that Dalia may have an interest in me. She says she's married but i've never seen her husband.\n\nSometimes I have to remind her that I have Juliel and I love her very much.\n\nWhat happens if she tries to kiss me, and Juliel walks in?\n\nI'm going to speak to Stoic about Gregor and see if he can do anything about it, because I was petrified about it!\n\nI was strolling around in Felsen, and a guard approaches me I was looking nervous and wiped some sweat off my forehead. He patted me on the shoulder and gave me a leaflet to join the Military. \n\nI rejected it and gave it back to him.\n\nHe called me a coward and a traitor.\nPretty soon we had a big crowd amongst us, I wasn't ready to fight and knew if he punched me he wouldn't be punished.\n\nI left the crowd by pushing past everyone and running back home. \nI locked myself in my cellar.\n\nDalia asked if I could go kill some undead with her as it gets boring doing it alone.\n\nI hesitated but then realised she could die out there, so I came prepared with an iron sword.\n\nIt wasn't hard: just slice and dice the undead bastards.\n\n2nd Of The First Seed 1519:\n\nI spent a majority of my day fishing, catched about three barrels full.\n\nI then went into oren to buy myself some iron for a hook, until I heard a lot of shouting from the tavern and outside it.\n\nI cautiously walked nearer to the crowd all speaking at once staring into the tavern. I walked inside and saw Dalia with a sword up to her throat not sure if she saw me.\n\nI froze in my spot and slowly backed out of the tavern to join the crowd.\n\nI watched from a distance, and saw that Dalia tried to headbutt the man, but he raised his sword and it went straight into Dalia's throat.\n\nI closed my eyes tightly and heard the deceased body collapse onto the floor with a 'thud'.\n\nA lot of people stared inside for a few minutes, children began crying so I left Felsen. Perhaps it is best to avoid Dalia now.\n\nI wont speak to her make eye contact, or even try and see her if possible.\n\nIt could put my life in danger if Guards see me\n\nwith her.\n\nI was thinking of proposing to Juliel.\nIs it still too early, I don't want to ruin what we have now.\n\nI have the ring. I just don't, want to ruin it.\n\n14th Of The First Seed 1519: \n\nSo Dalia is now dead, it's a little bit of a relief because she followed me around a lot. \n\nI always felt as if she was trying to seduce me. But on the other hand she was a good person sort of.\n\nHer sister came up to me today and asked about what happened with her.  I told her that all I know is she slapped a noble in the face, and paid the price for it.\n\nI know I sound harsh as if I don't care which is completley untrue ofcourse I care.\n\nShe was always mean calling me names like: Coward, stupid, lack common sense.\n\nI'm not a strong being, because my father never pushed us to fight he never taught. \n\nMy brothers however apart from George knew how to fight because they trained.\n\nI made a room for me and Juliel today for when I propose, it's a dark room with candles lit making it romantic. \n\nShe'll see when I finally propose.\n\n5th Of The First Seed 1519:\n\nOnce I was done fishing I salted them, and put them in the cooking pot. \n\nIt took a good few hours to cook the buggers but in the end they were all cooked and put on a plate and served to the people.\n\nI was wondering if I should sell this Diary when it is finished?\n\nBut then it could be that some of my secrets are shared to the world so I'll probably just store this one.\n\nLast time I wrote a diary was back when I went on a ship to catch a wale, the trip was a failure but I did get to see a blue wale.\n\nIt almost smashed our boat to pieces when we stuck a harpoon in it. I did suggest we get a bigger ship.\n\nEight of us left to hunt it, only four returned, including me.\n\nI hid in the lower deck, which was slowly filling up with water.\n\nI remember my friend came through slipping in the water which was up to his knees, he was calling my name.\n\nI came out from hiding in a barrel, and went to meet him. I took a step closer about to open my mouth and say \"Here!\" when suddenly, the boat split in two. \n\nA large wooden pillar that held the sails together, collapsed and smashed onto my legs.\n\nI fell on the floor gasping for air. Spitting out bits of sea water I let out a scream and tried to crawl forwards, but I was stuck.\n\nIt was slowly sinking to the bottom, but my legs were trapped.\n\nMy friend slid down the boat, and grabbed my wrist he tried yanking me out of it, but it was no use the pillar was stiff.\n\nMy body was soaked in water, and I could feel the gritty salt in between my teeth.\n\nI told him to go, he hesitated and then hugged me. He ran to top deck which had large holes and cracks in it. I heard his feet run across the deck and a then a large splash.\n\nHe plunged into the sea, and began swimming not knowing where.\n\nI couldn't even tell if anyone else was on the ship with me.\n\nIt fell to a silent yet eery noise. Just the running of water and my heavy breathing with me spewing out sea water everytime it got into my mouth.\n\nI tried to turn around, but my legs were lodged in tight.\n\nI could feel the water coming to my neck, I took one last deep breath and held it.\n\nThe water filled my face pretty soon. I could feel the pillar slowly moving out of place.\n\nIt rolled off my left leg which was now free. I still couldn't get my head high enough to get it out of the water.\n\nI began praying hoping it'd release my right leg.\nI felt it roll off and felt my bone in my right leg\nshatter as it did so.\n\nI let out a high pitched yelp, and swam to the top. Bits of wood and debris filled the ocean top.\n\nEverytime I kicked my leg I would feel an intense burning pain shoot up my leg. \n\nIt was as if someone was trying to stab it.\n\nI found a large and thick piece of debris and managed to climb onto it, I layed on it and used a stick that was floating by to paddle myself. It was an extremely slow pace, but I did manage to find shore.\n\nA crowd was already there it turns out my friend that dived off, made it to the same island I did and was telling the Guards of Petrus of what happened.\n\nThis was back in Athera, only a few stone days we moved there.", ""),
        StaticBook("Journal I", "§bRoopak Khalin", "Hi, my name is Roopak Khalin. This is my journal. The reason why I am writing this is because I wish to document the events that are about to follow. I hope one day my brother finds this, if he's still alive, or my mother, or maybe even my children if I\n\ndecide to have some. \n\nIt all started going downhill about 2 years ago when my brother, Silus went missing, assumed dead. I have no idea what's happened to him, maybe he's dead, maybe he's returned to the life of banditry, maybe he's perfectly fine but is\n\njust ignoring me. I don't know. I send him birds, but I get no reply. Anyways, about a month or two ago the Salvian civil war started. I was casually walking through Salvus when I saw Lily. I turned around to speak to her about the farms moving location.\n\nShe was weeping. I asked her why, and she told me something bad was about to happen. She said she didn't want it to be like this. She pointed behind me, to the horizon. I suddenly knew what was happening. Silv'ar and Ehsan were in danger. I was just about\n\nto set off for them, but then suddenly a Ghoul appeared behind me! The Ghoul tried to attack me, but luckily a kind Elf helped me and shot a arrow in the Ghoul's arm. The Ghoul didn't fall, and sliced my arm. I was bleeding, and it was incredibly painful.\n\nNext thing I know the rebels who attacked Silv'ar and Ehsan walk up to me, and Enrique is with them. He tells me to run, so I do. The rebels were after me, so I ran for my life. Luckily none of them followed me, and I managed to get back to the Caliphate.\n\nA kind Cleric healed my arm, and I told her what had happened. She prayed for my safety, and I left the clinic. I recieved a bird, from David. He wanted to meet up with me. I waited for him at the Caliphate gates. I met up with him, and he told me to stay\n\nout of the war. He said I was like a brother to him, even though he was slaughtering my friends and family. But then again, he saved my life. He told me to give him a list of people I didn't want dead, and I gave him a small list of three people. Lemik,\n\nLinda and Giliath. They were just friends that were on the top of my head. He told me Lemik was already safe, but Linda wasn't co-operating with the rebels. He also said that he would make sure Giliath would be safe.\n\nI still think I will fight in this\n\nwar, I shall not sit idly as I watch my friends and family get slaughtered.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vailor, A Report", "§bFygmor Khen'aum", "The State of Vailor 1557, A journal.\n\nVailor is the place where the decendants live, The Orcs, The Dwarves, The Humans, The Halflings, The Elves, and the Kha. Amoung other magical constructs and creatures. It is a large island where most of\n\nthe cities sit upon plains or mountians, or even smaller minor islands. \n\nChapter 1 : The Monk Temple.\n\nThe main hub to all of vailor is the dock owned by the Monks.\nNo blood can be shed here, because it is under protection.\n\nIt is the hub to all of Vailor, like stated before, because of all of its carriages and boats that lead to the major cities of Vailor. It is a safe place for many and has its own shops and auction areas. \n\nChapter 2: Haelun'or\n\n\nThe large city of Haelun'or, home to the High Elves. A place with winding paths, towering buildings, and many many people. It is where most 'pure' high elven people live, but many high elves live elsewhere. The main area is home to a Tavern, a bank, and\n\nan auctioneer. \n\nAt the time of writing this, their stalls are empty or coming soon. I have yet to go into the walls of the main area of the city. \n\nThe houses in the outside area of the cities follow a road and wind up a hill, these houses at the\n\ntop of the hill have nicer doors, and nicer stone. The stone they use is a nice smooth one. Since I do not have sight I can not tell for certain what it is. \n\nChapter 3: Felsen\n\nPerhaps it self a mini-hub, felsen is home to the humans.\n\nIn it's center, after stepping of the cart. Are many other carts and a rough, coarse dirt ground, mixed in with gravel and some cobblestone. The Orenian people inhabit this land, they are the main leaders of humans, because no other human civilization\n\nis as strong in manpower as the Orenian one. Its rough path leads across a moat, if you could call it that, not of water but of trash. Once you cross this bridge you find yourself in an even rougher, winding, huge city. You would not be surprised to know\n\nthat most of the population is part of the army. So the people here are rough and though. Stalls surround the area along with some buildings. The place seems barren and void of any substance. It is a wonder to me why people live here. To each their own.\n\nBut on that note, it may have to do with loyalty. \n\n\nRacism in Oren:\n\nIt seems some of the people who live in felsen or the areas surronding it have a racial or cultural hate towards a clan of hillmen named the Raevirs. I can not tell\n\nwhy they hate these people, but it has something to do with them thinking they are tribal.\n\nChapter 4: The Caliphate\n\nSandy, desolate, uninteresting. This describes the caliphate. It is under construction, it seems\n\nbut the construction seems to have no workers. But the druid grove near it has people in it, and a nice, but small library. More on librarys next chapter.\n\nChapter 5: The Dragur Library\n\nThe Library of Dragur is one of the biggest\n\nlibrarys in all of vailor, to my knowledge at least. It is filled with books from magic, to fiction, and even history. It is a place where you can learn. But it is a public place so do not expect it to be too quiet. But do not expect it to be too exciting\n\nIt is a place I like to visit. It's 'scholars' are nice and the books are of good quality.\n\nThe library is the biggest in Vailor.\n\nChapter 6: Sutica\n\nSutica is a merchant island that sits upon the sea, to be more precise upon 10+ tiny\n\nislands. You can taste the salt on the air and the smell the scent of mushrooms on the west wind. It is a place where you can buy many things, for example: I recently bought myself a twisted staff. The island runs on trade and economy like this. It is a\n\nfairly peaceful area.\nSutica houses very valuable resources in a vault, inside the bank. About 100 people live in the city of Sutica. The city is said to have been around for a long time now, it had started in \"The Now Lost Salvus.\"\nSources: \nShynys\n\nItesti.\n\nChapter 7: The Iron Uzg.\n\nThe Iron Uzg is the place where the orcs reside. When you come upon the Iron Uzg's Capital, heading up the road will grant you access to a, what I assume is large from the echo, building.\n\nTo get to the Iron Uzg, you must either walk here, or take the boat from the dock. The front path is rough and rocky, but it is not hard to walk past it. When you come to the main path there are some smaller buildings leading up to the large one.\n\nThe Orcs are known for being quite rude to visitors, so I must go! \n\nChapter 8: Kal'Ishtul\n\nKal'Ishtul, home of the dwarves. The dwarven city of Kal'Ishtul is located quite deep in a cavern. If you would like to get into the city you through a short\n\ntunnel, then come out of it, and then you cross a bridge. It seems to currently be in construction but once it's done, it seems it will be huge.\n\nOn the dwarves, they are not seen around the capital nearly as much as you would think! Strange.\n\nChapter 9: Willow Hollow.\n\nWillow Hollow,\nHome of the halfings,\nThe smell of flowers and tea lingers in the air for all to smell. The grass is soft and fertile. Willow Hollow is as small as the halflings, and it does not seem to have such a high\n\npopulation. But I could be wrong, the halflings do usually share a home with their family, it would be selflish in their culture if they did not. The towns citizens are said to be nice. It is like any other town, but seems peaceful and smells wonderful, I\n\n would wager that if I could see, it would be much better! It is a farming town, as the halflings are a farming people. For more on halflings I reccomend the history of halflings in the Monk Literature section.\n\nChapter 10: The Rebelious State of\n\nthe Dreadlands. \n\nA place where it is said, they have no laws and complete freedom. Currently in a war against the orenians, so by the time I am done writing this chapter they may already be gone... It is a dangerous place here, and an ugly looking\n\none too. I think it is a bad idea to have no rules at all, it makes it dangerous and attracts the wrong kind of people. They have rough orenian style buildings, which parallels their former liege. There is only one well in the city, and barely any other\n\nwater... Or anything for that matter. This place is strange, and dangerous.\n\n\nThe End.\n\nWritten By Fygmor Khen'aum.\nSources: Armin Ambertoe, Shynys Itesti, and others.", ""),
        StaticBook("Alsace Catacombs", "§bNoach Hughes", "The excavation of the Alsace Catacombs.\n\n10th of the Grand Harvest, 1532.\n\nAfter days of heavy rainfall, the entrance to the old catacombs is subsiding. Noach Hughes and Father Augustus, Priest of Alsace decided to --\n\nexcavate the catacombs, after slowly digging up the dirt and rubble, a staircase was revealed that lead to the underground system of tunnels and chambers. \nThe two slowly descended further into the catacombs --\n\nrevealing ancient ritual sacrifices, coffins and treasures that the disceased had taken into their graves and burial chambers.\n\n10th of the Grand Harvest, 1532.\n\nNoach and Augustus spent most of their -\n\ntime inside the catacombs, uncovering the long forgotten past. At first the catacomb appeared to be rather small, but after clearing out all the rubble and dirt they uncovered a large tunnel network that was greater than they could ever imagine.\n\n17th of the Grand Harvest, 1532.\n\n7 days had passed since the excavation had started, both Noach and Augustus felt rather sick, exhausted and fatigued. But somehow they were still inside the catacombs, as if a strange power --\n\nhad taken control of them. Slowly they began developing dark intentions and thoughts. Rumour has it that the two have been shackled by the catacomb's magical power.\n\n24th of the Grand Harvest, 1532.\n\nDays slowly passed--\n\nand Noach and Augustus start to act normal again, like if nothing had happend to them. Both went on to continue living their normal lives. \n\nIt would appear that the curse that was cast upon the two had been banished from their souls.\n\n* The pages appear to have been ripped out of the book, leaving only the first 7 remain in the book *", ""),
        StaticBook("Piano Tutorial", "§bRoth Jayamen", "((OOC))\n\nThe noteblocks layout on the floor (3x3 square) goes with numbers. The numbers match the piano in this way.\n\n7 8 9\n4 5 6\n1 2 3\n\nThe 2 through 9 in Real life notation (if you know this) Is C note through C note. or an octave. The Key is C major.\n\nAnother way to think about it is that 2 through 9 is like Do through Do in the phrase 'Do, Re, Mi, Fa, So, La, Ti, Do'.\n\nThe Number 1 of the noteblocks is the Note B. It is basically one white key below C.\n\nThe Piano is based in all white keys.\n\nSongs that I write in-game are based on the 1-9 layout. Just follow the song books numbers in order to play them.\n\nIf you have any questions, Ask me In-Game or through the forum. IGN: Sofetios", ""),
        StaticBook("Creatures by Ork", "§bEirik Ireheart", "Notice: These books are rushed copies of tomes in the library of the Orkish capital, as made during the raid of the 16th of The Grand Harvest, 1532. The following was originally written by an Ork, translated to\nview the modicum of Ork culture inside.\n\n\"Disclaimer: Whomever is reading this must know that this book about the various forms and types of creatures and races. Thank you for reading this book. Also, I wrote a lot of it in a sewer.\n\nChapter One: The Halfings.\n\nHalflings are weird. They are small. You can through them at walls. But, there is more to them than just mutalation. In this chapter, you will learn of the more sophisticated lifestyles of Halflings. The have a complex\n\nthat you wouldn't excpect. Firstly, it turns out that Halflings don't live in holes. Well, they kind of do, but they are called burrows. These burrows are actually quite nice and decorated. This is of course if you can even fit in it. Other than killing\n\nthem, Halflings are useful for trading, as I met many Halfling traders on the roads. They are usually very nice creaturs, but they're so mall that we like to make fun of them whenever we see them. Also, Halflings make for good Bartenders, that is, if they\n\ncan even reach the counter, yes?\n\nChapter Two: The Dwarves\n\nRecently, I learned about the Halflings- Small, ugli, and weak creatures they are. To understand a Dwarf is to understand alcohol.\n\nYou see, these 'Dwarves' fill their holes with beer. Lots and lots of beer. They are a merry race, much so like the Halflings. They are also short like the Halflings. Unlike the Halflings, they are fatter, and they like to fight almost as much as we Orkos\n\ndo. Almost. To be honest, there is much to admire about these creatures. They are a breed of warriors. Of course, the Dwarves can't compare to the strength of an Orkos, but they seem to be stronger than even the biggest human. Much like us Orkos, Dwarves\n\nhold their honor to a huge extent. Both Orkos and Dwarves like to use axes and hammers. To be honest, Dwarves are like tiny, ugly, smelly, dumb Orkos in a way. I think that we should still kill them, but, we need to appreciate why they're so fun to kill\n\nand fight. Now, we're going to shift gears here, and switch to a taller race.\n\nChapter Three: The Elves\n\nThis race is one of a disgraceful sense of honor. The Elves think they're better than us. I mean, so do the Halflings, Dwarves,\n\nand other stuff, but the Elves seem to hate us even more. They are a group of creatures that think they are so beautiful and smart. But, I feel that in some ways, they actually are smart. You see, they may be finer, smellier, and dumber than us, but I\n\nthink that they in some way, deep inside, that Elves have some degree of endulgence. Perhaps Elves can make good weapons or armor, or even good buildings. But, Elves seem to grasp the art of Magic very well. Now, we are going to dive into the specific\n\ntypes of Elves.\n\nChapter Four: The High Elves.\n\nIt is this clan that sucks the most, in my opinion. They're the ones that REALLY think high about themselves. Considering it's in their names and such. These ones live long, look tall, are super\n\npale, drink the teas, eat the cakes, write the novels, mock the other races, do their fancy magic, waste time in making stuff pretty (Even their weapons!), and take their baths, and clean their [klofz] and ears, and that's pretty much it. Basically, they\n\nsuck.\n\nChapter Five: The Wood Elves.\n\nNasty Tree-huggers, that's what these Wood Elves are. Believe it or not, Wood Elves don't even use swords, axes, or even thereputic lavatorial papers. They use bows. And for lavatorial papers, I\n\nthink they use leaves. Also, Tree Elves are much like twigs. That's why another name for Elves in Blah is 'Twiggeh'. These Wood Elves are a bunch of pansies. They hide in the shadows to hunt for their prey. But, us Orkos hunt for our prey in the open,\n\npartly. Although, Wood Elves are good at sneaking around. I have not studied the Wood Elves much, be cause they are usually hiding from me. So, they're is still much to learn about them. Now, we're going to take a look at the gray ones.\n\nChapter Six:\n\nDark Elves.\n\nThese Elves look like the nighttime skies themselves. They are pretty much gray and kind of blue. They are pretty modest, for Elves, that is. They seem like the most humble of the Elves. Just like they're mysterious appearance, not much\n\nhas been recorded of the Dark Elves. Maybe they're is, but they did not share it with me. So, I am just going to tell you about their physical appearance, rather than their real culture. They have dark skin, dark eyes, but they usually dress with pretty\n\nbold colors. Also, I mainly noticed piercings and gray hairs. So, I don't talk about the Elves, because they're PRETTY LAME. So, now, we're going to shift gears again and learn about the Humans.\n\nChapter Seven: The Northern Humans.\n\nThese are smelly, diplomatic, tiny, ugly, weak, dum, and really dumb. You know what we are talking about. The Humans. But, these are the Humans in the North. I gathered this information in the city of [Ubrezchi]. They are a complicated race, so I'm going\n\nto break this up into different categories. First, we are going to talk about how we see them, or how they look. Humans are shorter than Elves, and fatter than Dwarves. They call themselves 'HOO-MANZ' and don't call themselves 'Sharaz', because they are\n\nso dumb, they don't even know what it means. They spoke with me, and said that they have something called 'Pulektikz' (TN: Politics, most likely.) I think it's like the [Krughai] Confederation, but they said it was more ugly. Also, they are greedy, like\n\nDwarves, but they are not greedy for gold, but Minaz in its stead. And, they NEVER admit it. So, basically, the Northern Humans are more evil than I ever thought. As you already know, the [Uhbrezchi] Humans are our allies in most wars, because they are\n\nsome of the only ones who don't betray us. Now, we will look into Adunia.\n\nChapter Eight: Adunians\n\nThey're neat. I obtained this information from the King of Adunia himself, Jarl [Lazchlahn Moare Ilindul]. So as you can see, this book gave\n\nme the honor to meet countless officials in the world. Anyway, the Adunians are said to be the oldest. Their King told me that they were the first human around, making him much older than the Adunians. Adunians also have politics like the [Uhbrezchinz],\n\nbut their's is much different. You see, in their culture, the youngest born is the one whose destiny is to lead, unlike more popular cultures where the Elders lead. These Adunians have many different ranks, too, like a High Thane, and High Marshall. Also,\n\nthese creatures are the longest lived Humans around, because apparently they have Elven blood in their history. However, they don't seem like Elves. No, Adunians are still much bigger than them, as they are pretty tall and mighty.\n\n[!] *A few pages appear to have been misplaced, instead replaced by notes.*\n\nMayra,\n\nHow I miss you. Our son has just arrived in Khaz'Kazak, and seems ever the firebrand. He's got your nose. I only hope he does well with young Hogan.\n\n[!] *What seems to be a second sheet lays here as well.*\n\nAlarik: oxoooooooxoooxoooo Hathrid: xoxxxxxxxxx\nHogan: x\nDuren: ox\nMidgor: xx\nDrynn: xxx\nNora: xxox\nThereon: xxxxxxxxx\n\nleft right here in [Gronkkzton], in the [kiddun] district. But, they all moved to [Mahlinoare] now, probably because we Orkos were too strong for them. There are four types of Kha, and we're going to delve into all of them right now.\n\nChapter Ten: Th\n\n[Tygur] Kha\n\nThese are the biggest of the Cat Creatures, and some of them are as big as an Ork. They look like [Tygurz], and they fight like them too. They are usually the Warriors of the Kha, and they are pretty dumb. I don't know much about them.\n\nChapter Eleven: The [Zcheetuh] Kha\n\nThese ones are the smallest, but unlike a fat Dwarf, they are REALLY fast. The [Zcheetuh] Kha look like [zcheetuhs], they have spots all over their skin. And to me, they make the best carpets. (TN: Yes. They do.)\n\nChapter Twelve: The Dark Kha\n\nThey look like [punturz] an they are sneaky, like [Punturz], too. Always look out for Dark Kha, because they can steel heaps of things at any moment. To me, they are rather like Dark Elves, strictly speaking. Sadly, that\n\nall I know about the Dark Kha, seeing as Kha are mysterious and they did not let me learn about them.\n\nChapter Thirteen: The [Lepurd] Kha\n\nThese ones obviously look like [Lepurdz], but unlike the other Kha, these ones don't exceed in any\n\nspecific skillset, but they are the best suited for doing anything they set their heart on. They are bigger than [Zcheetahz] and [Punturz], and they are faster than [Tygurz] and [Panturz]. You will most likely see these ones the most. Wait! We never\n\ntalked about the different types of Dwarves, did we? Well, we will rectify that now.\n\nChapter Fourteen: The Snow Elves\n\nI never heard of them. Their skin is white like snow. Their hair is white like clouds. They are Snow Elves. They are an enda\n\nspecies. To be honest, these Snow Elves are albino, and they do not like it when we call them that. The Snow Elves aided the Dwarves win the past war, so I don't really like them. They live in a place called [Frohztfohl]. But, that's all I know, because\n\nthey don't really like me.\n\n\nThis is only the first volume in my series of books dwelling upon the topic of Creatures. The end.", ""),
        StaticBook("Drugs", "§bMarren", "Drugs \n\nCactus Green:\n\nCactus Green (dye)\nGrass\n\n            Drugs\n\nBlack Cactus:\n\nCactus Green (dye)\nFern\nInk Sac\nCoal\n\n            Drugs\n\nPure Quartz:\n\nQuartz\nGlowstone Dust\nCocoa Beans\nFern\n\n            Drugs\n\nBlueberry Cactus:\n\nBlue Orchid\nCactus Green (dye)\nLight Blue (dye)\nFern\n\n            Drugs\n\nEternal Leaf:\n\nGrass\nFern\nVine\n\n            Drugs\n\nIce Kiss:\n\nSnowball\nAllium\nVine\n\n            Drugs\n\nBaby's Brain:\n\nMushroom Stew\nCactus Green (dye)\nFern", ""),
        StaticBook("Druidic Conduct", "§bTaynuel", "~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~\n          The Druidic\n     Code of Conduct\n\n       As created by\n   Apollan in the wake\n    of the Age of Poor\n          Judgement\n\n Scribed by Hare Druid       Taynuel Viellsinch\n\n~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~\n\nSection A: Druid Conduct\n\nGeneral Conduct\n\nA) Never insult a fellow brother or sister in any way. ((ic or ooc))\n\nB) Never kill a brother or sister ((for fun, etc.))\n\nC) Never steal from the Order or from a brother or sister.\n\nD) Don't destroy property of the Order.\n\nE) If you are below the rank of guide, it is not your place to make decisions that affect the Order as a whole.\n\nF) All Druids must conduct themselves in a mature and upright manner. ((ic or ooc))\n\nG) Never criticize a brother or sister in an overly negative way. ((ic or ooc))\n\nH) Calm the heck down!\n\nI) Always immediately contact a higher ranking Druid if you have a problem.\n\nJ) Stay active. ((on the forum))\n\nK) Never criticize someone. ((ooc for actions done ic or vice versa.))\n\nL) A Druid may  use violent force if they or other Druids are in danger, it  is self defense, or if it is in defense of nature or the balance.\n\nConduct Around the Shunned.\n\nA) When a shunned person enters the Grove, immediately instruct them to leave. If they do not leave instruct them once that you will forcibly remove them if they do not leave. If they still stay a Druid is free to use\n\nany means possible to remove a shunned from the Grove and the surrounding area.\n\nB) When a shunned person with KOS designation enters the Grove, immediately instruct them that they will be killed in the next ten seconds if they do not leave. When ten\n\nseconds has expired a Druid is free to slay any non-obeying shunned.\n\nC) When you see a shunned person, even with a KOS designation, somewhere other than a Grove, continue on your way and do not try to make contact.\n\nD) If a shunned tries to make contact with you somewhere outside a Grove, simply reply with the phrase \"Much wind pours from your mouth\" and move on. If they try to speak with you again, repeat the phrase. If they try a third time inform them that if they\n\ntry to speak to you again you will reply with violent force.\n\nE) If a shunned with a KOS designation tries to contact you somewhere other than a Grove, inform them that they have ten seconds to move away from the present location before violent force\n\nused.\n\nF) If at any time a shunned becomes aggressive or violent, refer to Section A, Subsection 1, Line L of the Druidic Code.\n\nSection B: Initiation and Ranking Up\n\n1) Outsider to Dedicant\na) Complete an extensive interview with a Guide.\nb) Recite the Dedicant's Vow.\n\n2) Dedicant to Druid\na) Stay a member for at least two Elven weeks. If seen as unready\n\nby the guide or higher, shall stay until the guide deems you ready.\nb) Complete a varying amount of Druidic Trials assigned successfully.\nc) Complete a Grand Task.\nd) Choose carefully what totem you will go under. This can only be changed once\n\nbecoming official.\ne) Become attuned in the ceremony performed by an Archdruid or Guide.\n\n3) Druid to Guide\na) Stay a member for four Elven weeks (dedicancy not included). If seen as unready by an arch or higher, be patient and gain wisdom.\n\nb) Once deemed as ready, take training underneath the Head Teacher as an apprentice, and watch how they are to Dedicants, how they assign tasks, how they handle the Grove, etc.\nc) Study up on the subject, as well as how to perform an attunement ceremon\n\nwhat the tasks entail, what the interviews for Dedicants are meant to have, as well as other things your Head Teacher suggests you learn.\nd) Practice giving a few Dedicants tasks under the supervision of your Head Teacher.\ne) Once you and your Head\n\nTeacher both feel that you are ready, the teacher will discuss with an Archdruid and you shall begin your Guideship.\n\nSection C: Punishments\n\n1) The punishment for breaking a rule depends on the person, severity, rule broken, and previous track record.\n\n2) The punishment for breaking a rule includes but is not limited to a warning, probation, a shun, a KOS des\n\nand banishment from Athera. ((server ban))\n\nSection D: Clauses\n\n1) All rules are subject to change.\n\n2) Section B, Subsection 1, Line C is optional if the Dedicant does not plan on becoming a Druid.", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature Healing", "§bKirrekith", "Nature Healing\nBy The Sequoia Druid\nPages 17\n\nThe Nature of Druidic Healing\nBy Lilith Winterleaf, the Sequoia Druid.\n\nDruid Healing is a topic many are curious about. Few, even amongst the druids know the true cost\n\non the person performing the action. Most of the druids use live catalysts to assist in the process of healing. This enables them to draw on this catalysts living energy, this in turn means less energy is draw from the provider to the recipient. However a\n\nskilled user of this magic type, can find themselves performing the action without a live catalyst without any danger to themselves. \n\nDruidic healing for the most part is basic. It is the recieving and giving of living essence from a provider to a\n\nrecipient. The druid focuses on the living tissue of the recipients wounds, and asks the body to mend itself, drawing on the catalyst and the druid. Giving their life essence in exchange for the healing aid.\n\nThis is where the costs and risk of a\n\nDruids healing is, and any would be druid healer should heed this warning well. A druid is capable of amazing feats of healing at times, however it all comes from ones self. In turn, causing possible lasting effects on the provider, and why living\n\ncatalysts are used to lessen the strain on ones self.\n\nI have personally healed a variety of wounds, from cuts and scrapes to sword clashed to broken bones to some far worse than that. They all draw from you to an extent, even when using a catalyst.\n\nSimple wounds may show no strain on you, it depends on the severity of the healing needed, and ones personal ability at healing. Practice being key in safeguarding ones self to the harmful effects. \n\nI am in the belief an example of an mistake\n\nis the most successful way to pass on this lesson to another, I myself, nearly lost my life while healing the King of Skravia back in Asulon. The story had them lured out to a base of mages, that had harassed their kingdom for a while. When they'd\n\ncornered the mage a trap was sprung, explosives unleased upon the King and Guards. Three died in the blast, others dragged the King back to Skravia (severely wounded) and to my Inn, as they knew I was a healer. His body a mess beyond words to describe, as\n\nanyone could imagine from an explosion of the magnitude. However one needed to try, coating his wounds in bonemeal and living slime I began my work drawing on the catalyst as well as myself to save the King. It took close to two hours of healing, and left\n\nhim in a coma for several days. Though I had overstretched myself, my skin a sickly colour, my eyes drained of colour, my lungs not drawing me enough air. I collapsed, and when I woke five weeks had passed, my limbs all sore and my hair a shade lighter,\n\nthough my skin bore a closer to normal tone. A druid, had come to restore the living essence unto me, otherwise I myself would have perished within the first days of unconsciousness, I was that weakened from the healing.\n\nUnderstand this tale to be a\n\nof physical limits of ones abilities to perform healing on a mortally wounded individual without assistance or true grasp of your strength in healing. If you step to far you may risk your life as I did. Find yourself a guide in which to walk between your\n\npersonal sacrificing and also of the catalysts you use to strengthen your abilities, find yourself always something to ease the act of healing, be that grass around you, flowers or trees. Any living catalyst to help carry the burden.\n\nAs wrote by Lilith Winterleaf, the final page missing as missed during the quick jotting down of information in the raid, however it still bares wise words for anyone believing themselves a druid of power. Always know ones limits, and be sure to use all\n\nat your disposal.\n\nBy Lilith Winterleaf, re-wrote by Kirrekith Reffahc of the 16th of the Grand Harvest, 1532, during raid on the Orks Capital. \n\nBy Kirrekith Reffahc.", ""),
        StaticBook("Dark Magic & You", "§bNienna Calm", "Dark Magic\n      And You!\n-------------------  A Guard's Guide to\n  Identifying Dark \n          Mages\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBy: Nienna Calm\n\n      ForeWord\n-------------------This book was written with the intent to educate the guardmen and women of the Caliphate of Khalestine. However, should you find this book in your possession, I would suggest reading it anyways. It is a good\n\nguide for identifying Dark mages.\n\n     What is a\n    Dark Mage?\n-------------------\nA dark mage is a user of magic that inherently harms others in order to function. Dark Mages can receive their powers in a variety of manners; including Pacts with dark gods, Rituals set for\n\nIblees, and other similarly horrifying methods. It is for this reason that all Dark Mages must be identified and killed on site, for they would not hesitate to do the same to you.\n\n    Necromancy\n-------------------\nNecromancy is the quintessential dark magic, and probably the first to ever be founded. It's age has not made it any less dangerous, however, and is directly responsible for all manners of Wraiths and Liches pla\n\nthis world today. Necromancers drain the very life force of plants, animals, and indeed people around them to cast their spells. In fact, they need a constant supply of life force to even survive, and must seek it out regularly.\n\nHow to Identify:\nThe bodies of necromancers are often in a very frail, almost decayed state. Since Necromancers need life force to cast spells, be on the lookout for dead or tainted patches of plant life, or reports of people feeling drained after\n\ntouching a specific person. These are both serious signs of a Necromancer infestation.\n\n   Frost Witches\n-------------------Frost Witches are monsters of Ice and Snow who disguise themselves as women and prey upon the libido of Men. The Frost Witch is an easy threat to counter in the hot weather of the Caliphate, but requires constant\n\nvigilance to identify. A Frost Witch's true form is bright blue, but using her curse's magic, she is able to take any form she pleases, so long as that form is female. Frost Witches use these disguises to trick men into a false sense of security,\n\nwhereupon they will take their true form, and devour the unsuspecting male.\n\nHow to Identify:\nThe easiest way to identify a Frost Witch is by touching their skin. A frost witch's body temperature is colder than anything one could imagine.\n\nThere is no way for them to disguise this. Also, because of this impairment, any disguised frost Witch will show signs of great fatigue under the Caliphate sun. Any women demonstrating a severe aversion to high temperatures and regular food should have\n\nthe temperature of their skin checked immediately.\n\n       Shades\n-------------------\nShades are the result of Iblees magic corrupting the mind and soul of an existing mage. These dark mages are ticking time bombs, for within them lies an eldritch, second personality that desires for only chaos an\n\ndestruction. Shades are capable of corrupting every form of Arcane magic, empowering their spells with the dark powers of Iblees himself.\n\nHow to Identify:\nShade mages are hard to identify, for their true nature is only\n\nrevealed when they cast spells. The hallmark of a Shade is the ability to create black tentacles out of thin air. Their ability to corrupt their own magic may cause them to cast spells that are not usually possible to create. If you suspect a mage of\n\nbeing a Shade, contact a cleric immediately. Shades suffer from severe aversion to holy spells and relics, and these can be used to verify if a mage is, in fact, a Shade.\n\n Soul Puppeteers\n-------------------\nSoul puppeteers are masters of curses. Using a small fetish (a doll of some sort), and a physical part of another person (such as their hair, blood, etc.), They are able to bring sickness, pain, and even death\n\nperson from an extreme distance. Random, shooting pains and illnesses with supernatural symptoms are strong signs of the interference of a Soul Puppeteer.\n\nHow to Identify:\nIt is difficult to identify a Soul Puppeteer\n\nstraight off. Be on the lookout for those who try to take blood or locks of hair from their enemies. If you suspect someone of Soul Puppetry, inspect them immediately for a fetish. It is not a guarantee that they will always have one on them, but it's the\n\nonly way to confirm for sure.\n\n Soul Contractors\n-------------------Soul Contractors are capable of fashioning magical legal documents that bind the signer to the very letter of their word. This would be potentially useful, if not for the material they must fashion these\n\ndocuments on. Soul Contracts can only be written upon the flayed skin of men that was taken whilst they were still alive. It is a brutal and horrifying process, that speaks to the inhumanity of these casters.\n\nHow to Identify:\nLike Soul Puppete\n\nit is hard to identify a Soul Contractor. The only way to tell for sure is to either catch them in the act, or discover their flaying equipment, which may not even be on their person.\n\n   Black-Dragon\n     Parasites\n-------------------A more recent addition to dark magic, black-dragon parasites are a growing problem in the Caliphate. A black-dragon parasite is an insidious form of insect that infects its hosts and causes\n\ncauses them to literally absorb blood and the flesh of the mortal races in return for benefits. It is unknown how is parasite passes from person to person, so it is absolutely vital that you keep any suspected carrier of this foul creature far away from\n\none's person at all times. It is unknown at this time if Black Dragon Parasite victims can be cured, so it is recommended that they be either expelled from the city on sight, or, in extreme cases, purged.\n\nHow to Identify:\nBlack-Dragon Parasite\n\nare easy to identify. All carriers of the parasite bear a tattoo of a black dragon on their person, usually winding around the stomach area. If you suspect someone of having this terrible disease, conduct a full-body search immediately.\n\n General Warning\n    Signs Of A\n    Dark Mage\n-------------------\nDark Mages can and will follow stereotypes at times. If you keep a look out, there are many signs that indicate if a person is just a dark mage in general. These are by\n\ndeciding factors in a suspect's ability to cast black magic, but they are definitely red flags to investigate.\n\nBe Suspicious Of Any Persons you see that:\n\n-Wear Primarily Black Robes.\n\n-Are Hooded at all times\n\n-Possess bright red eyes.\n\n-Project a magical Aura that Black, Gray, or Red in color.\n\nAs a guard of the Caliphate, it is up to YOU to root out these psychopathic evildoers wherever they might rise their ugly heads. For the sake of our grand city, do your duty well.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Coronation", "§bLilyana", "In the dark of night, an elder man hobbles with a walking stick towards the quiet village. The man’s curiosity is pricked by the silence in the town, a town that once was bustling with life regardless of the time. But, the pain in his back and ache in his\n\nfeet quickly nudged any questions from his mind as he hobbled along the path. He had been traveling for days, braving the wilderness and streets to reach this town, and finally he was close to his destination. However, he had no idea what he was heading\n\nto or why he started this journey. He awoke one morning, some days ago, and felt a tingling sensation in his fingertips, one he had not felt for years. As the days passed, the sensation slowly began to spread throughout his body and the tingling became\n\nstronger; he began to feel an urgency to understand what was causing it. Only days ago had he remembered what this feeling, this sensation, was; it was the excitement, the rush of energy, the craving for blood he felt before he killed a target of the\n\nCrimson Fist. Though his body old and tired now with age, this sensation revitalized him enough to send him into the unknown, to this very town. With each step, the pull on him became stronger, more intense, until now, as he stood outside a large barn,\n\nthe only lit building in the entire town, did he feel like he would burst from excitement. He licked his chapped lips, wrapped his wrinkled hands around the bar, and pulled with all his might on the door, opening it.\n\n    Cora danced around the flame\n\n the center of the barn, its light dancing along the walls. The deep crimson blood markings and gold jewelry that she wore contrasted against her pale skin. She paid the old man no attention, although she knew full well of his presence, and continued to\n\ndance around the fire, chanting verses of an old Crimson ritual. As the old man stepped into the light of the fire, Evaine quieted her chanting and slowed her dance. She stopped on the side of the wall where a robe of deep crimson hung on a peg. She\n\nfluidly placed the robe about her shoulders then made her way to a corner of the barn that the fire's light did not reach. She looked back over her shoulder, smirking at the old man, before disappearing into the shadows.\n\n The old man scanned\n\nthe barn with his old eyes, his poor ears only hearing the crackling of the fire. He saw nothing, heard nothing, but his very being knew someone, or something was there. From across the fire, from under the shadow of the barn's hay loft, two piercing red\n\neyes broke the darkness and stared at the man, as if they were staring into his soul. The sensation from earlier now rocked the elder; he clung to his walking stick as a force of power pulled him down onto his knees. The owner of the glowing eyes stepped\n\ninto the light of the flame, revealing himself to be an abnormally tall, toned, young man, Haemon. His hair of white gold flowed to his shoulder, his skin as pale as snow, but his eyes, they glowed like lumps of red, hot coal, burning in his eye sockets.\n\nHaemon walked around the fire, slowly approaching the elder. With each step, the elder gripped his walking stick tighter as the power that the boy was emanating tried harder and harder to pull him to the floor. When the boy stood over the old man, he was\n\nshaking with strain and effort to remain kneeling and barely spat out the words before collapsing:\n\n“M-M-My…..L-L-Lord-d-d….M-My P-Prince”\n\nWhen the old man awoke, he found himself sitting against the wall of the barn.\n\nHe felt a sharp pain at his arm and upon inspection found it bandaged tightly, a small blood stain slowly spreading on it. He went to tug on the bandage but a delicate hand quickly grabbed his hand and pulled it from the wound. The elder looked up,\n\nmeeting Cora’s gaze as she slowly shook her head. She pulled out a skin full of water and offered it to the man who, only realizing his thirst at the sight of the skin, quickly took it from her and drank it down. She left him a small plate, some bread and\n\ncheese, and walked around the flame to the other side of the fire. Following her movement, the old man realized that the boy had been sitting across from him this entire time, watching him. Cora slid down along the wall until she was next to the boy, then\n\ncurled herself against him, passing him a vial of a crimson liquid that the old man knew all too well, a vial of blood, his blood.\n\nHaemon lifted the vial to face level, staring at it as the fire's light illuminated the contents. Haemon smiled and as\n\nfor his name.\n\n\"Eren\", the old man responded.\n\nHaemon shook his head and smiled again, but this time his eyes glowed and a voice unlike the previous one asked for his “true name”. The old man dropped hid bit\n\nof cheese and drew his limbs into him. His gaze met the fiery gaze of Haemon and memories of a life he tried hard to forget washed over him.\n\n\"Doulos, my lord.\" The old man said, maintaining eye contact with Haemon as he spoke, \"Servant to you and yo\n\ncause.\"\n\nHaemon nodded and tossed the vial of blood into the fire. The flame erupted into a shower of sparks and turned black as the night around them, all except for the center which glowed with light. The light soon warped and changed and the old m\n\nwatched his own memories, his life from his eyes play before him for all to see. He watched the battle where the temple fell where he fought as a mere Doulos, a soldier and servant to the Crimson Prince’s cause. His escape into the woods as the temple\n\ncollapsed, his struggle for survival, his search for others, his attempts to rebuild what was lost. But alas, he watched himself give into what seemed to be reality that no others had survived, and watched as he isolated himself from the world, locking\n\nthe few relics of his past life away in a chest.\n\nThe flames died down, the color returned to its orange and red glow, and the barn returned to the quiet it had once been. Haemon stood before the flame and spoke of how few of his followers survived t\n\nbattle. He made a point to mention how few had attempted to continue his cause and gracefully pointed to the hay-loft above. The elder’s vision followed Haemon’s direction until it, only now, noticed countless bodies in the loft above, wrinkled and dry,\n\nseemingly drained of their blood. The man reeled back in fear from Haemon, but Haemon only smiled and waved his hand in front of him. He reassured the man that their fate was the fate of cowards and betrayers, those that tried to hide their connection to\n\nhim. \n\n“But the few,” Haemon said as he waved his hand to Cora and back to the old man, “the few that stayed true to me now reap great rewards.”\n\nHaemon explained how the rituals Core and he performed summoned the small\n\nbit of power that the Crimson Prince left in this world. That ritual created a connection between Haemon and the Crimson Prince; that the Prince now acted through Cora’s child, Haemon.\n\n\"I must feed on the blood of the living, fueling my power to all\n\nthe Prince to return to this world as his true self. We have been traveling to settlements, slowly feasting on its populace as my power grows in strength with each feasting.” Haemon pointed above to the bodies. \"It seems as my power grows, those of the\n\nold Crimson Fist that had survived are drawn to us. We’ve tested those that come, and those to have hidden from their past have been feasted on. While those who were true to our prince, have been given power.”\n\nThe old man perked up. \"Power, my lord?\n\nWhat do you mean?\"\n\nHaemon’s eyes glowed bright, his power exerting itself in the room once more. The fire reached for the ceiling as Haemon lifted his right hand from his side. As his hand lifted, so did the elder man, levitating him in mid-air. Hae\n\nwithdrew a silver dagger and sliced a thin line down his right forearm. Haemon began to recite a dark chant from deep within his mind and the blood that was slowly seeping from the wound began to rise into the air. The blood hovered over to the elder man\n\nand began to circulate around him, slowly at first but picking up speed quickly. With quick movement, Haemon shut his fist closed, forcing the blood that spun around the old man to suddenly stop and bury its way into his body. The old man howled with pain\n\n for only a moment before his eyes glazed over and his screaming silenced. The man’s body erupted into black flames, devouring him entirely, burning the flesh from his very bones. Cora stood and approached Haemon, wrapping her arms around his torso and\n\nshoulder as they watched on together. The Black Flame finally dissipated, leaving a figure in a crimson cloak where the old man once was.\n\n“Arise”, Haemon ordered with a voice not his own, “ Arise, my new Blood Mage.”\n\nThe figure stood from\n\nthe ash of the black fire and removed his hood, revealing Eren, but as his once youthful self. Eren stared at his hands, no longer wrinkled and weak; he felt his face for saggy skin and scars of old age but only found the tight, tough skin. He felt\n\nrevitalized, no empowered. The ritual had given him a small piece of his lord’s power, and that small piece had not only turned back time to when the old man was once called Doulos, it gave him strength, power, and life.\n\n\"Blood, the essence\n\nf life, is quite a powerful catalyst, as you now see with your own eyes, Doulos. It’s power is only limited by the Mage’s imagination.” Haemon said as Cora wrapped a small bandage around his wrist. \"Ours, theirs, it matters not where it comes from, but\n\nhe purity, power, and amount, that determines the strength of magic that comes from it.” Haemon showed the wound to Doulos, “Until our true lord is able to come into this world our magic is limited, but still powerful.”\n\nDoulos nodded, \"So what is th\n\nnext step? How do we bring forth our Lord, once and for all?\"\n\nCora stepped forward, revealing documents that had thought to have been lost with the years. “Cruor Veneficus, the Blood Tome. A magical tome that the Crimson Fist once had. If we were\n\nto reclaim this tome, we would be able to channel the magical energy from it, making our magic infinite and allow Haemon to focus his feeding on bringing forth our Lord.”\n\nDoulos nodded in agreement and asked \"But where do we start looking for the\n\ntome?\"\n\nCora smiled, “According to the documentation we found on the betrayers and cowards mentions the Cruor Veneficus last being seen in the Crimson Fist’s stronghold in the mountains of the North, the mountains\n\nwhere the settlement “Raven’s Nest” now stands. Word on the street of this town was that a coronation of some sort is to take place there as well, bringing in many guests.” Cora smiled a toothy grin, “If we do not find the Cruor Veneficus, at least there\n\nwill be many for my Prince to feast on.\"\n\n “Raven’s Nest is heavily defended and manned, how are we to take the city?” Doulos questioned.\n\nYou are not the only Blood Mage, Duolos.” Haemon answered as he motioned for Cora to open the barn's\n\ndoor. “There have been others that I found worthy to bestow power upon, like yourself, that now hide themselves amongst the cities of this land. They pretend to normal folk of their race, gathering information, and working their way into cities and\n\ngovernments.\" Haemon motioned outside the barn’s open door where the sunrise was just peeking over the horizon. The low light illuminated many hooded figures walking towards them. \"And many more followers are being drawn here, as you were, and they will\n\night alongside us, wielding powerful Blood Magicks.”\n\nHis questions answered and his mind set straight, Doulus nodded and resigned. \"Yes, my Lord. Raven's Nest cannot, will not, stand against our power and cause. He smiled widely,\n\n\"Our Lord, our Crismon Prince, will rise once more”.\n\n      The Coronation\n            of the\n        True Prince\n\n  A Sequel to the\n Rebirth of Evil\n\n\n\nWritten by: Lilyana", ""),
        StaticBook("Rebirth of Evil", "§bLilyana", "The Rebirth of Evil\n\n          By\n\n        Lilyana\n\nShe awoke in dire pain and confusion as the livelihood of the Crimson Fist’s purpose and existence, literally, weighed heavily upon her. The Champion’s mind sporadically showed images of a battle that just collapsed the temple containing most, if not all,\n\nof the Sanguine Hand, sending them to an early demise. Shouts meant to be full of victory turned into blood curdling screams of pain and death as they were overrun by their attackers. As she clawed at the gravel and dirt that trapped her, she tried\n\nlistening to her surroundings for other survivors calling out to her. But all her ears could hear were the memories of the stone walls around her cracking, sliding, grinding against one another, as they began to collapse inward on her and the others, and\n\ntheir screams. Oh how the memory of the screams tortured her mind, forcing her bloody and gashed hands to sift quicker. Her hands digging and sifting faster, trying to escape the memory,  but the images and sounds only seemed to come faster as she tried\n\nto flee, keeping in tempo with her movement. \n\nWith a heavy grunt and thrust, she thrusts her arm through a small lit-opening, finally feeling the cool air touch her skin.  This bolstered her will, giving her the strength to push herself through the\n\nsmall opening and back into the outside world. The elven woman lay there in the cool morning fog for what seemed like an eternity,  time completely lost to her, breathing in deeply the fresh air.  Slowly, from the dense fog a shadowy figure approaches her\n\nexhausted self and shoves a bow and arrow into her face. Panting, the woman looks up at the figure as he pulls back his hood, revealing the rugged features of a hunter. The man pulled back the weapon slightly from her face and asked her for her name.\n\n“M-my name…”, the elven woman spoke between her labored breaths, “ My name is Cora.”\n\nSeeing Cora’s condition and state, the hunter took pity on her that fateful morning and led her to his dwelling in the forest.  There, he fed and dressed her wounds\n\nnursing her back to health. Cora slowly came around to the man, knowing all too well how easily it is to lower ones guard when they begin to trust someone, but never spoke about the Crimson Fist. When he did question her about her past, she simply said\n\nthat she was a servant to the people that owned the, now, pile of rubble that he found her at. The man nodded, knowing it rude to question a person’s past, and went about his business. However, no matter how hard he tried to focus on cleaning his\n\nmorning’s kill or divert his gaze, he was mesmerized by Cora’s beauty and soon felt for her. Cora, knowing that the Crimson Hand’s was either utterly destroyed or scattered to the winds and that her previous life was too dangerous to try to start again\n\non her own, accepted the hunter’s affection with open arms and soon gave birth to a child of a rare complexion. The child’s skin was pale like snow, his hair like fine straws of white gold, and his eyes….crimson like blood. Because of his defining eyes,\n\nCora named him, Haemon, the Blood Child. \n\nHaemon’s features were the most exciting thing of his life as he lived a dull life in the forest. His father taught him how to string a bow, to catch a fish, to chop wood, and skin a kill. He promised to tak\n\nhim hunting when he was rightfully a man and left Haemon in charge of keeping order at home when he left on his numerous hunting trips. Cora took to motherhood surprisingly well. Her swift and accurate hands that once sliced a man’s throat before they\n\ncould blink now directed a needle and thread through cloth; her strength and determination that kept herself alive during fights with armed guards now carried in loads of firewood or cleaned the wash. She watched over Haemon throughout the years grow into\n\na fine young man, but also watched herself age. She knew that with her death, the Crimson Fist died as well, as did its unfinished work. So, as her husband left on one of his hunting trips, Cora brought Haemon into the cellar of their small home.\n\nThere, she had him swear to keep this information from his father, and showed him the history of her people, of his people. Haemon was fascinated by the information, rituals, chants, and stories his mother knew and begged to know all of it.\n\nSo, every time her husband would venture out into the woods, Cora would teach Haemon the ways of the Crimson Fist.\n\nThis continued for many years until the night before Haemon’s eighteenth birthday, when everything changed.\n\nThe hunter returned home earlier than expected, bringing with him a new bow to give to Haemon as a gift to use on their first hunt together. However, when he approached the home, all he could see was darkness through the windows. He quickly entered\n\nthe home, noticing that the fire and candles had been empty and snuffed for quite some time. The darkness however, did allow the light from the under the cellar door to draw his attention. He opened the door quietly, filling the room with light,\n\nand was met by the voices of his family, however could not make out what they were saying. Descending the staircase he saw his wife and son, dancing while around an open fire. An animal’s carcass and bloodied knife lay next to the flame and\n\nits blood was painted on their naked skin in weird designs and letters that he could not understand. Haemon was the first to notice his father’s presence, quickly dropping his hands arms, breaking the dance, and backing away from the cellar door.\n\nCora noticed the Haemon’s fear and quickly turned, meeting the horrified gaze of her husband. He, softly, began to question Cora and Haemon, but neither responded, both afraid to unveil the lies they have been keeping from him. The hunter became angry,\n\nhis voice raising as he stepped down the stairs, approaching his wife. Cora remained speechless, not knowing what to say or do, she simply looked at the floor. In a fit of tears and rage the man grabbed Cora’s arms and forced her against the wall,\n\nshouting at her, begging to her, for answers. Haemon watched in horror and fear as his father slammed his mother against the wall across the room, shouting at her, asking her questions, spewing lines of secrets, lies, and betrayal. But it was only Haemon\n\nthat noticed the fire in the center of the room grow larger and only Haemon that heard the flame telling him to stop his father. Haemon tried to cover his ears to escape the raspy voice but the flame’s voiceresonated inside of him, demanding Haemon to\n\ntake action.\n\nHaemon closed his eyes and screamed out, trying to smother the flame’s voice with his own, but when he stopped screaming, all was quiet. Haemon opened his eyes to see his hand firmly around the bloodied knife that had sat in the\n\ncenter of the room, and the knife he held was deeply embedded into his father’s back. His father twisted around to look at Haemon’s eyes as he desperately tried to reach for the knife in his back. Blood began to leak from his mouth as he tried mouthing\n\nwords, words that Haemon would never hear. His father fell towards the center of the room, his head cracking on the hard stone floor, blood leaking out of his body towards the fire. Haemon backed away from his mother who now shriveled into herself against\n\nthe wall, staring at her dead husband, at the pool of blood forming around his body.\n\nThe blood of the innocent man pooled and grew, eventually reaching the fiery claws of the flame. With an immense heat and power, the flame jumped high and turned\n\ncrimson, sparks flew from it in all directions. Haemon now stared at the fire, mesmerized by the dancing of its flames and heard the voice that spoke to him earlier, this time much clearer. He could not disobey it if he tried, the pull it had on his very\n\nbeing was too strong, so he walked into the flame. Cora tried to reach out to her son, but her body was frozen, whether in fear or by the flames own power she did not know, but when she called to him nothing escaped her throat. Haemon did not feel\n\nthe flame burn his skin, but felt it burn his insides, as if it had reached into his heart and soul and burnt them to ash. He could not scream out nor did his body react to the pain that his mind was feeling. His vision slowly became dark and blurred, his\n\nsense of feeling seemed to numb away, and his mind began to close in on itself.\n\nWhen Cora woke the next morning in her bed she quickly wretched onto the floor. The nightmare was so real to her, the heat of the fire, the smell\n\nof her husband’s blood, the image of her son walking into the fire, all shook her deeply.  She struggled out of bed and walked down to the kitchen, finding it cold and dark as it was every morning. She started a fire and hung a pot of water to boil while\n\nshe called out to Haemon to wake. However, he did not return her call. When she checked his bed, he found it vacant and undisturbed from the previous morning’s making. Fear struck at her as she turned to look at the closed cellar door. Slowly approaching\n\nit and wrapping her hand around the latch, she closed her eyes as she opened it and was greeted by nothing but the smell of burnt flesh. At the bottom of the staircase she could see her husband’s burnt skeleton in the same spot it was\n\nleft in her nightmare. She cried out and slammed the cellar shut and turned to run from the house, only to see a figure standing in the doorway, shadowed from the sun coming in around the figure. Its glowing, deep crimson eyes\n\nwere its only distinguishable characteristic.\n \nThe figure spoke to her with two voices, both familiar but from different lives.\n \n“Hello, Mother”", ""),
        StaticBook("Lily's Tale [1]", "§bLuxeanna Du Couteau", "Teh Tail Of\n\n        Teh Wulf\n\n            Nd\n\n        Teh Gazl\n\n\n          Bi: Lileh\n\n        Pt. 1 of 2\n\n       The Tale Of\n\n         The Wolf\n\n              &\n        The Gazelle\n\n          By: Lily\n    Recorded by:\nLuxeanna Du Couteau\n\n        Pt. 1 of 2\n\nIn the deepest part of the mainland, a forest exists that has gone untouched by man for all eternity. Here, animals and plants exist in a natural order, they way the great creators had intended.\n\n Living a life of meaning and happiness. And here, is where our tale begins.\n\nThe animals of this forest lived in a serene community. Each one, from the birds and the rabbits, to the deer and the bears, had a place in this community and lived amongst each other in harmony.\n\nHowever, there was one beast that lived outside this circle- The Wolf. The wolf would live in its den, hidden by the shadows of the thick forest. Few animals ventured to the wolf’s den, and even fewer returned.\n\nAll the animals understood the order of nature, they understood that the wolf was cursed with its hunger for meat and flesh, and they did not hold it against it. However the wolf’s personality, his menacing look,\n\nand his lack of patience, often made the other animals flee in fear of it.\n\nOf the few animals that tried to reach out to the wolf, children were often among them. They would hear the stories of the wolf and wish to play with it.\n\nHowever, the wolf found the games the children wished to play tiresome and annoying. In the wolf’s rage, he lashed out at one, taunting it with the bones of animals it had eaten and scaring it away.\n\nIn another instance the wolf lost his temper when dealing with an arrogant youth, letting its sharp teeth wrap around the child’s neck. The stories of the wolf go on and on, and the entire forest knew of them.\n\nBut what they didn’t know about was how lonely the wolf felt. Cursed by his rage, his temper, his looks, and his hunger the wolf was destined to live alone until the end of its days, watching the other inhabitants of the forest laugh and play.\n\nThat was, until the Gazelle arrived. Easily standing out with its foreign appearance and behavior, the gazelle wandered into the forest from a far away land, one with different customs and things.\n\nThe animals of the forest welcomed the gazelle, trying to make the gazelle feel welcomed amongst their busy life. The gazelle smiled brightly and followed their directions, confused and unsure of their behaviors, but maintained a happy\n\npersonality to look like one of them. For the gazelle had come from a land of destruction and chaos. Fighting amongst the animals would happen daily and then Man found its way into their home, obliterating it.\n\nThe gazelle was alone, lost and confused and simply wished to find a new family, but at the same time, desperately missed her own.\n\nAs the animals celebrated the gazelle’s welcome, the wolf slinked onto the edge of the festivities, carefully watching\n\nThe animals of the forest were celebrating amongst each other, eating berries and drinking honey, but the gazelle sat by herself. Although the festivities were for her, she felt like an outcast and was left\n\nby herself, the other animals unaware of her feelings because of the happy smile she wore at all times. But the wolf could smell the gazelle’s true feelings. Fear. Desperation. Terror.\n\nAll smells that the wolf had come accustomed to when smelling its prey now greeted him in the middle of a happy festival, and all were coming off so pungently from this new comer.\n\nThe gazelle graciously smiled to the animals as they celebrated, but as soon as they had turned to take another bite of blackberry, she ran. She dashed through the trees and lept over the logs, trying to get away. The sounds of the festival slipped away\n\nbehind her as she ran, but the thudding of paws greeted her. In a clearing the wolf leapt from the shadows of the trees and circled the gazelle slowly. The wolf questioned the gazelle as to why she left the party so soon\n\nand was surprised when she answered thatshe preferred to be alone sometimes. The wolf inquired about the gazelle, and as she told him the story of her homes fall and her travels, the wolf realized that the smellsof fear and desperationwere drifting away.\n\nThe gazelle followed the wolf back to his den, far from the other animals of the forest, in the quiet depths of the forest. She asked the wolf questions about the animals here, and the wolf answered her questions honestly.\n\nHe told her how the animals would squabble over who had the tastiest walnuts, how they had ridiculous dances, and were always full of energy, never resting.The gazelle laughed at many of the wolf’s stories, finding a comfort in the honesty and help.\n\nThe wolf’s den was also a comfort to the gazelle. Unlike the fancy burrows of the animals, the den remained natural in the depths of the forest, reminding her of her home.\n\nAs the days passed, the gazelle would often stop by the wolf’s den to take a break from the busy life of the forest animals.\n\nOther days the wolf would meet the gazelle at the crystal pond for some water. The other animals at first would scatter, hiding behind the trees and roots, but soon saw how the wolf would sit by the gazelle and drink peacefully.\n\n With a few recurrences, the animals no longer feared the wolf when he came to drink and welcomed him when he went to festivities with the gazelle. The animals noticed how much tamer the wolf was around the gazelle and were happy at this.\n\nBut they wondered how much the gazelle really knew about the wolf, and the dance with death she could possibly be playing.\n\nIt was decided amongst the animals that the gazelle must become aware of the wolf’s curse and his past. The animals debated about ways to separate the gazelle from the wolf to speak to her about the issues, but it wasn’t decided until\n\nan old turtle crept into the center of the discussion. The turtle spoke slowly, stating that while the concern for the newcomer warmed her old heart, she implores the community to not forget that the wolf has feelings too.\n\nThe wolf should be allowed to explain himself to the gazelle, and let his case be made known by himself, and not spoken of behind his back. The animals listened to the wise turtles words and agreed,so the following day their plan was put into action.\n\nThe field mice went to the gazelle, asking for her aide in knocking some berries from a small tree. With the gazelle away, the bear approached the wolf, for surely he was the safest of the animals should the wolf become angered.\n\nThe bear asked the wolf for its feelings about the gazelle, and for the first time that the wolf could remember saying, he said she made him happy. The bear smiled at this and informed the wolf that all of the animals wanted the wolf to be happy,\n\nbut they wanted the gazelle to be safe. The wolf stood up and declared that he too wanted her to be safe and would defend her from anything. The bear simply stared at the wolf and asked him how he would defend her from himself.\n\nThe wolf could not respond, realizing the bears words. The bear urged him to speak to the gazelle about his past before she finds out on her own and to let her make a decision on what to do.\n\nThe wolf thought long and hard about what to do, whether to hide his past and his curse, hoping for the best, or to be honest with the gazelle.\n\nThe gazelle approached the wolf’s den, excited to tell him about the trip she had taken with the field mice and the berries she had found, but the wolf was sullen and silent.\n\nWhen the gazelle nudged the wolf he became serious in a manner that she had never experienced before.The wolf told her of his curse. He told her of his rage, how he taunted and murdered the young animals of the forest.\n\nHe told her more stories existed, some even worse than the ones he had revealed, but could not say what he had done.But he looked to the gazelle and with honest eyes told her that her presence quenched the thirst he had,\n\n it quieted the rage, and dampened his temper. He told the gazelle about how happy she had made him, and felt like he had a meaning in life once more, to live and protect her.\n\nThe wolf became silent, looking at the gazelle for a sign of her acceptance, anything at all.\n\n But a tear fell from her eye and she ran away from the wolf.\n\nHe chased after her, calling after her to come back, to be with him, but the gazelle sprinted away, leaving the wolf in its tracks, fleeing from the forest.\n\nAs the days went on, the animals of the forest talked amongst themselves in whispers. They had seen the gazelle flee the forest, but they had not seen the wolf at the pond to drink.\n\nNone of the animals reported seeing the wolf stalk its food either, and they became concerned. The bear walked to the wolf’s den and found him sitting in the deep reaches of it, hidden by the shadows.\n\nThe wolf refused to speak to the bear, but the bear could tell that the wolf was dying. Not only from a lack of water and food, but it felt like the wolf’s spirit, one that would be intimidating to even the bear, was gone, drained from its body.\n\nThe animals feared they had done this to the wolf, that they would be the cause of the wolf’s death for scaring away the gazelle. They began blaming each other, the field mice pointing fingers at the briar rabbits, the birds ridiculing the deer.\n\n. But the old turtle sat on her stone quietly, looking into the forest, looking at something. Eventually the animals noticed the old turtle smiling and looked to see what was making the old girl happy at a time like this.\n\n Light filtered through the trees and a shadow moved slowly, overly cautious and unsure.\n\nThe gazelle walked slowly through the forest, past the animals that stared at it silently, towards the wolf’s den. The wolf crawled out of its den slowly, limping and shuffling through the hole before laying on the ground at the dens opening.\n\nThe gazelle kneeled down and looked at the wolf, withered and dying. The wolf smiled lightly between coughs, amazed that she had came back.\n\n“Yo-You came back….” the wolf sputtered between short breaths.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lily's Tale [1]", "§bLuxeanna Du Couteau", "Teh Tail Of\n\n        Teh Wulf\n\n            Nd\n\n        Teh Gazl\n\n\n          Bi: Lileh\n\n        Pt. 1 of 2\n\n       The Tale Of\n\n         The Wolf\n\n              &\n        The Gazelle\n\n          By: Lily\n    Recorded by:\nLuxeanna Du Couteau\n\n        Pt. 1 of 2\n\nIn the deepest part of the mainland, a forest exists that has gone untouched by man for all eternity. Here, animals and plants exist in a natural order, they way the great creators had intended.\n\n Living a life of meaning and happiness. And here, is where our tale begins.\n\nThe animals of this forest lived in a serene community. Each one, from the birds and the rabbits, to the deer and the bears, had a place in this community and lived amongst each other in harmony.\n\nHowever, there was one beast that lived outside this circle- The Wolf. The wolf would live in its den, hidden by the shadows of the thick forest. Few animals ventured to the wolf’s den, and even fewer returned.\n\nAll the animals understood the order of nature, they understood that the wolf was cursed with its hunger for meat and flesh, and they did not hold it against it. However the wolf’s personality, his menacing look,\n\nand his lack of patience, often made the other animals flee in fear of it.\n\nOf the few animals that tried to reach out to the wolf, children were often among them. They would hear the stories of the wolf and wish to play with it.\n\nHowever, the wolf found the games the children wished to play tiresome and annoying. In the wolf’s rage, he lashed out at one, taunting it with the bones of animals it had eaten and scaring it away.\n\nIn another instance the wolf lost his temper when dealing with an arrogant youth, letting its sharp teeth wrap around the child’s neck. The stories of the wolf go on and on, and the entire forest knew of them.\n\nBut what they didn’t know about was how lonely the wolf felt. Cursed by his rage, his temper, his looks, and his hunger the wolf was destined to live alone until the end of its days, watching the other inhabitants of the forest laugh and play.\n\nThat was, until the Gazelle arrived. Easily standing out with its foreign appearance and behavior, the gazelle wandered into the forest from a far away land, one with different customs and things.\n\nThe animals of the forest welcomed the gazelle, trying to make the gazelle feel welcomed amongst their busy life. The gazelle smiled brightly and followed their directions, confused and unsure of their behaviors, but maintained a happy\n\npersonality to look like one of them. For the gazelle had come from a land of destruction and chaos. Fighting amongst the animals would happen daily and then Man found its way into their home, obliterating it.\n\nThe gazelle was alone, lost and confused and simply wished to find a new family, but at the same time, desperately missed her own.\n\nAs the animals celebrated the gazelle’s welcome, the wolf slinked onto the edge of the festivities, carefully watching her.\n\nThe animals of the forest were celebrating amongst each other, eating berries and drinking honey, but the gazelle sat by herself. Although the festivities were for her, she felt like an outcast and was left\n\nby herself, the other animals unaware of her feelings because of the happy smile she wore at all times. But the wolf could smell the gazelle’s true feelings. Fear. Desperation. Terror.\n\nAll smells that the wolf had come accustomed to when smelling its prey now greeted him in the middle of a happy festival, and all were coming off so pungently from this new comer.\n\nThe gazelle graciously smiled to the animals as they celebrated, but as soon as they had turned to take another bite of blackberry, she ran. She dashed through the trees and lept over the logs, trying to get away. The sounds of the festival slipped away\n\nbehind her as she ran, but the thudding of paws greeted her. In a clearing the wolf leapt from the shadows of the trees and circled the gazelle slowly. The wolf questioned the gazelle as to why she left the party so soon\n\nand was surprised when she answered thatshe preferred to be alone sometimes. The wolf inquired about the gazelle, and as she told him the story of her homes fall and her travels, the wolf realized that the smellsof fear and desperationwere drifting away.\n\nThe gazelle followed the wolf back to his den, far from the other animals of the forest, in the quiet depths of the forest. She asked the wolf questions about the animals here, and the wolf answered her questions honestly.\n\nHe told her how the animals would squabble over who had the tastiest walnuts, how they had ridiculous dances, and were always full of energy, never resting.The gazelle laughed at many of the wolf’s stories, finding a comfort in the honesty and help.\n\nThe wolf’s den was also a comfort to the gazelle. Unlike the fancy burrows of the animals, the den remained natural in the depths of the forest, reminding her of her home.\n\nAs the days passed, the gazelle would often stop by the wolf’s den to take a break from the busy life of the forest animals.\n\nOther days the wolf would meet the gazelle at the crystal pond for some water. The other animals at first would scatter, hiding behind the trees and roots, but soon saw how the wolf would sit by the gazelle and drink peacefully.\n\n With a few recurrences, the animals no longer feared the wolf when he came to drink and welcomed him when he went to festivities with the gazelle. The animals noticed how much tamer the wolf was around the gazelle and were happy at this.\n\nBut they wondered how much the gazelle really knew about the wolf, and the dance with death she could possibly be playing.\n\nIt was decided amongst the animals that the gazelle must become aware of the wolf’s curse and his past. The animals debated about ways to separate the gazelle from the wolf to speak to her about the issues, but it wasn’t decided until\n\nan old turtle crept into the center of the discussion. The turtle spoke slowly, stating that while the concern for the newcomer warmed her old heart, she implores the community to not forget that the wolf has feelings too.\n\nThe wolf should be allowed to explain himself to the gazelle, and let his case be made known by himself, and not spoken of behind his back. The animals listened to the wise turtles words and agreed,so the following day their plan was put into action.\n\nThe field mice went to the gazelle, asking for her aide in knocking some berries from a small tree. With the gazelle away, the bear approached the wolf, for surely he was the safest of the animals should the wolf become angered.\n\nThe bear asked the wolf for its feelings about the gazelle, and for the first time that the wolf could remember saying, he said she made him happy. The bear smiled at this and informed the wolf that all of the animals wanted the wolf to be happy,\n\nbut they wanted the gazelle to be safe. The wolf stood up and declared that he too wanted her to be safe and would defend her from anything. The bear simply stared at the wolf and asked him how he would defend her from himself.\n\nThe wolf could not respond, realizing the bears words. The bear urged him to speak to the gazelle about his past before she finds out on her own and to let her make a decision on what to do.\n\nThe wolf thought long and hard about what to do, whether to hide his past and his curse, hoping for the best, or to be honest with the gazelle.\n\nThe gazelle approached the wolf’s den, excited to tell him about the trip she had taken with the field mice and the berries she had found, but the wolf was sullen and silent.\n\nWhen the gazelle nudged the wolf he became serious in a manner that she had never experienced before.The wolf told her of his curse. He told her of his rage, how he taunted and murdered the young animals of the forest.\n\nHe told her more stories existed, some even worse than the ones he had revealed, but could not say what he had done.But he looked to the gazelle and with honest eyes told her that her presence quenched the thirst he had,\n\n it quieted the rage, and dampened his temper. He told the gazelle about how happy she had made him, and felt like he had a meaning in life once more, to live and protect her.\n\nThe wolf became silent, looking at the gazelle for a sign of her acceptance, anything at all.\n\n But a tear fell from her eye and she ran away from the wolf.\n\nHe chased after her, calling after her to come back, to be with him, but the gazelle sprinted away, leaving the wolf in its tracks, fleeing from the forest.\n\nAs the days went on, the animals of the forest talked amongst themselves in whispers. They had seen the gazelle flee the forest, but they had not seen the wolf at the pond to drink.\n\nNone of the animals reported seeing the wolf stalk its food either, and they became concerned. The bear walked to the wolf’s den and found him sitting in the deep reaches of it, hidden by the shadows.\n\nThe wolf refused to speak to the bear, but the bear could tell that the wolf was dying. Not only from a lack of water and food, but it felt like the wolf’s spirit, one that would be intimidating to even the bear, was gone, drained from its body.\n\nThe animals feared they had done this to the wolf, that they would be the cause of the wolf’s death for scaring away the gazelle. They began blaming each other, the field mice pointing fingers at the briar rabbits, the birds ridiculing the deer.\n\n. But the old turtle sat on her stone quietly, looking into the forest, looking at something. Eventually the animals noticed the old turtle smiling and looked to see what was making the old girl happy at a time like this.\n\n Light filtered through the trees and a shadow moved slowly, overly cautious and unsure.\n\nThe gazelle walked slowly through the forest, past the animals that stared at it silently, towards the wolf’s den. The wolf crawled out of its den slowly, limping and shuffling through the hole before laying on the ground at the dens opening.\n\nThe gazelle kneeled down and looked at the wolf, withered and dying. The wolf smiled lightly between coughs, amazed that she had came back.\n\n“Yo-You came back….” the wolf sputtered between short breaths.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lily's Tale [2]", "§bLuxeanna Du Couteau", "The gazelle nuzzled against the wolf, “Of course, Of course I came back.” She could feel the wolf’s bones through its thin and sickly skin. “Oh if only I had gotten here sooner….”\n\nThe wolf closed his eyes, “Maybe it’s better-it’s better this way.”\n\n“Don’t talk like that. You’ll be alright,” the gazelle lied, trying to hold back her tears, “We’re together now, everything will be fine, you’ll see.\"\n\nThe wolf opened his eyes slowly and smiled, nuzzling the gazelle. “At-At least I got to see you….one last time.”\n\nWith this the last breath of the wolf escaped its mouth and his eyes closed.\n\nThe gazelle nudged the wolf as her tears flowed, “N-no….No...Please, you can’t…..please don’t leave me….”\n\nThe animals of the forest looked on in sadness, their hearts sunken low. It felt as if the wolf’s death, a part of the forest had died as well, and all of the animals could feel the loss the gazelle was feeling.\n\n\nThe gazelle nuzzled against the wolf as her tears fell on him, muttering softly, “I love you.”\n\nWith these words, her tears glistened brightly and touched the wolf’s body. Behind the forest animals, a green light shined so bright that it at first blinded them as they turned around. As their eyes adjusted, they gasped in amazement.\n\nThe old turtle that sat on the stone was the source of the light, and as they looked on, they watched the turtle transform. Soon, where the turtle once sat a shimmering Wood Elf sat.\n\nThe animals looked on in both fear and amazement, but the Elf spoke to them.\n\n“Fear not my friends. I am not here to harm you,” the elf announced, “For all eternity I have been assigned to ensure that the veil that protects this forest remained intact, protecting the life within, and its meaning.\"\n\nThe druid stood and walked over to the animals slowly, “The family you created here amongst each other, this is what our creators tried to have throughout the world, but it has failed in many, many places.\n\nBut you succeeded. Your love for one another, it stands the test of time, it stands against the evils of this world, and it will keep all of you together.”\n\nThe druid would make its way over to the gazelle, who had been staring at the wolf this entire time, and carefully lifted her face.\n\n “And you, one who knows loss more than anyone here, you were able to find companionship…..you were able to find love, where others saw fear.”\n\nThe druid would smile and caress the gazelle’s face, “And your love will stand the test of time, young one.”\n\nWith this the forest glowed brightly, the trees swayed, and the grass blew swiftly. The wolf’s body was lifted with the wind and spun as the light entered its body. With a blinding light, the wolf was set down, not on its side, but on its paws.\n\nThe wolf stood on its own, opening its eyes, and looking amazed. The gazelle rushed to the wolf, nuzzling him and he her.\n\n“You two, your love will defend not just each other, but all of those you love,” the druid said as it slowly began to fade from view, its being disappearing into the wind.\n\nWith the druid’s disappearance, the animals and the forest remained hidden in peace,  guarded against the evils of the world by the wolf and gazelle for the rest of time.", ""),
        StaticBook("Rebirth of Evil", "§bLilyana", "The Rebirth of Evil\n\n          By\n\n        Lilyana\n\nShe awoke in dire pain and confusion as the livelihood of the Crimson Fist’s purpose and existence, literally, weighed heavily upon her. The Champion’s mind sporadically showed images of a battle that just collapsed the temple containing most, if not all,\n\nof the Sanguine Hand, sending them to an early demise. Shouts meant to be full of victory turned into blood curdling screams of pain and death as they were overrun by their attackers. As she clawed at the gravel and dirt that trapped her, she tried\n\nlistening to her surroundings for other survivors calling out to her. But all her ears could hear were the memories of the stone walls around her cracking, sliding, grinding against one another, as they began to collapse inward on her and the others, and\n\ntheir screams. Oh how the memory of the screams tortured her mind, forcing her bloody and gashed hands to sift quicker. Her hands digging and sifting faster, trying to escape the memory,  but the images and sounds only seemed to come faster as she tried\n\nto flee, keeping in tempo with her movement. \n\nWith a heavy grunt and thrust, she thrusts her arm through a small lit-opening, finally feeling the cool air touch her skin.  This bolstered her will, giving her the strength to push herself through the\n\nsmall opening and back into the outside world. The elven woman lay there in the cool morning fog for what seemed like an eternity,  time completely lost to her, breathing in deeply the fresh air.  Slowly, from the dense fog a shadowy figure approaches her\n\nexhausted self and shoves a bow and arrow into her face. Panting, the woman looks up at the figure as he pulls back his hood, revealing the rugged features of a hunter. The man pulled back the weapon slightly from her face and asked her for her name.\n\n“M-my name…”, the elven woman spoke between her labored breaths, “ My name is Cora.”\n\nSeeing Cora’s condition and state, the hunter took pity on her that fateful morning and led her to his dwelling in the forest.  There, he fed and dressed her wounds,\n\nnursing her back to health. Cora slowly came around to the man, knowing all too well how easily it is to lower ones guard when they begin to trust someone, but never spoke about the Crimson Fist. When he did question her about her past, she simply said\n\nthat she was a servant to the people that owned the, now, pile of rubble that he found her at. The man nodded, knowing it rude to question a person’s past, and went about his business. However, no matter how hard he tried to focus on cleaning his\n\nmorning’s kill or divert his gaze, he was mesmerized by Cora’s beauty and soon felt for her. Cora, knowing that the Crimson Hand’s was either utterly destroyed or scattered to the winds and that her previous life was too dangerous to try to start again\n\non her own, accepted the hunter’s affection with open arms and soon gave birth to a child of a rare complexion. The child’s skin was pale like snow, his hair like fine straws of white gold, and his eyes….crimson like blood. Because of his defining eyes,\n\nCora named him, Haemon, the Blood Child. \n\nHaemon’s features were the most exciting thing of his life as he lived a dull life in the forest. His father taught him how to string a bow, to catch a fish, to chop wood, and skin a kill. He promised to take\n\nhim hunting when he was rightfully a man and left Haemon in charge of keeping order at home when he left on his numerous hunting trips. Cora took to motherhood surprisingly well. Her swift and accurate hands that once sliced a man’s throat before they\n\ncould blink now directed a needle and thread through cloth; her strength and determination that kept herself alive during fights with armed guards now carried in loads of firewood or cleaned the wash. She watched over Haemon throughout the years grow into\n\na fine young man, but also watched herself age. She knew that with her death, the Crimson Fist died as well, as did its unfinished work. So, as her husband left on one of his hunting trips, Cora brought Haemon into the cellar of their small home.\n\nThere, she had him swear to keep this information from his father, and showed him the history of her people, of his people. Haemon was fascinated by the information, rituals, chants, and stories his mother knew and begged to know all of it.\n\nSo, every time her husband would venture out into the woods, Cora would teach Haemon the ways of the Crimson Fist.\n\nThis continued for many years until the night before Haemon’s eighteenth birthday, when everything changed.\n\nThe hunter returned home earlier than expected, bringing with him a new bow to give to Haemon as a gift to use on their first hunt together. However, when he approached the home, all he could see was darkness through the windows. He quickly entered\n\nthe home, noticing that the fire and candles had been empty and snuffed for quite some time. The darkness however, did allow the light from the under the cellar door to draw his attention. He opened the door quietly, filling the room with light,\n\nand was met by the voices of his family, however could not make out what they were saying. Descending the staircase he saw his wife and son, dancing while around an open fire. An animal’s carcass and bloodied knife lay next to the flame and\n\nits blood was painted on their naked skin in weird designs and letters that he could not understand. Haemon was the first to notice his father’s presence, quickly dropping his hands arms, breaking the dance, and backing away from the cellar door.\n\nCora noticed the Haemon’s fear and quickly turned, meeting the horrified gaze of her husband. He, softly, began to question Cora and Haemon, but neither responded, both afraid to unveil the lies they have been keeping from him. The hunter became angry,\n\nhis voice raising as he stepped down the stairs, approaching his wife. Cora remained speechless, not knowing what to say or do, she simply looked at the floor. In a fit of tears and rage the man grabbed Cora’s arms and forced her against the wall,\n\nshouting at her, begging to her, for answers. Haemon watched in horror and fear as his father slammed his mother against the wall across the room, shouting at her, asking her questions, spewing lines of secrets, lies, and betrayal. But it was only Haemon\n\nthat noticed the fire in the center of the room grow larger and only Haemon that heard the flame telling him to stop his father. Haemon tried to cover his ears to escape the raspy voice but the flame’s voiceresonated inside of him, demanding Haemon to\n\ntake action.\n\nHaemon closed his eyes and screamed out, trying to smother the flame’s voice with his own, but when he stopped screaming, all was quiet. Haemon opened his eyes to see his hand firmly around the bloodied knife that had sat in the\n\ncenter of the room, and the knife he held was deeply embedded into his father’s back. His father twisted around to look at Haemon’s eyes as he desperately tried to reach for the knife in his back. Blood began to leak from his mouth as he tried mouthing\n\nwords, words that Haemon would never hear. His father fell towards the center of the room, his head cracking on the hard stone floor, blood leaking out of his body towards the fire. Haemon backed away from his mother who now shriveled into herself against\n\nthe wall, staring at her dead husband, at the pool of blood forming around his body.\n\nThe blood of the innocent man pooled and grew, eventually reaching the fiery claws of the flame. With an immense heat and power, the flame jumped high and turned\n\ncrimson, sparks flew from it in all directions. Haemon now stared at the fire, mesmerized by the dancing of its flames and heard the voice that spoke to him earlier, this time much clearer. He could not disobey it if he tried, the pull it had on his very\n\nbeing was too strong, so he walked into the flame. Cora tried to reach out to her son, but her body was frozen, whether in fear or by the flames own power she did not know, but when she called to him nothing escaped her throat. Haemon did not feel\n\nthe flame burn his skin, but felt it burn his insides, as if it had reached into his heart and soul and burnt them to ash. He could not scream out nor did his body react to the pain that his mind was feeling. His vision slowly became dark and blurred, his\n\nsense of feeling seemed to numb away, and his mind began to close in on itself.\n\nWhen Cora woke the next morning in her bed she quickly wretched onto the floor. The nightmare was so real to her, the heat of the fire, the smell\n\nof her husband’s blood, the image of her son walking into the fire, all shook her deeply.  She struggled out of bed and walked down to the kitchen, finding it cold and dark as it was every morning. She started a fire and hung a pot of water to boil while\n\nshe called out to Haemon to wake. However, he did not return her call. When she checked his bed, he found it vacant and undisturbed from the previous morning’s making. Fear struck at her as she turned to look at the closed cellar door. Slowly approaching\n\nit and wrapping her hand around the latch, she closed her eyes as she opened it and was greeted by nothing but the smell of burnt flesh. At the bottom of the staircase she could see her husband’s burnt skeleton in the same spot it was\n\nleft in her nightmare. She cried out and slammed the cellar shut and turned to run from the house, only to see a figure standing in the doorway, shadowed from the sun coming in around the figure. Its glowing, deep crimson eyes\n\nwere its only distinguishable characteristic.\n \nThe figure spoke to her with two voices, both familiar but from different lives.\n \n“Hello, Mother”", ""),
        StaticBook("The Coronation", "§bLilyana", "In the dark of night, an elder man hobbles with a walking stick towards the quiet village. The man’s curiosity is pricked by the silence in the town, a town that once was bustling with life regardless of the time. But, the pain in his back and ache in his\n\nfeet quickly nudged any questions from his mind as he hobbled along the path. He had been traveling for days, braving the wilderness and streets to reach this town, and finally he was close to his destination. However, he had no idea what he was heading\n\nto or why he started this journey. He awoke one morning, some days ago, and felt a tingling sensation in his fingertips, one he had not felt for years. As the days passed, the sensation slowly began to spread throughout his body and the tingling became\n\nstronger; he began to feel an urgency to understand what was causing it. Only days ago had he remembered what this feeling, this sensation, was; it was the excitement, the rush of energy, the craving for blood he felt before he killed a target of the\n\nCrimson Fist. Though his body old and tired now with age, this sensation revitalized him enough to send him into the unknown, to this very town. With each step, the pull on him became stronger, more intense, until now, as he stood outside a large barn,\n\nthe only lit building in the entire town, did he feel like he would burst from excitement. He licked his chapped lips, wrapped his wrinkled hands around the bar, and pulled with all his might on the door, opening it.\n\n    Cora danced around the flame in\n\n the center of the barn, its light dancing along the walls. The deep crimson blood markings and gold jewelry that she wore contrasted against her pale skin. She paid the old man no attention, although she knew full well of his presence, and continued to\n\ndance around the fire, chanting verses of an old Crimson ritual. As the old man stepped into the light of the fire, Evaine quieted her chanting and slowed her dance. She stopped on the side of the wall where a robe of deep crimson hung on a peg. She\n\nfluidly placed the robe about her shoulders then made her way to a corner of the barn that the fire's light did not reach. She looked back over her shoulder, smirking at the old man, before disappearing into the shadows.\n\n The old man scanned\n\nthe barn with his old eyes, his poor ears only hearing the crackling of the fire. He saw nothing, heard nothing, but his very being knew someone, or something was there. From across the fire, from under the shadow of the barn's hay loft, two piercing red\n\neyes broke the darkness and stared at the man, as if they were staring into his soul. The sensation from earlier now rocked the elder; he clung to his walking stick as a force of power pulled him down onto his knees. The owner of the glowing eyes stepped\n\ninto the light of the flame, revealing himself to be an abnormally tall, toned, young man, Haemon. His hair of white gold flowed to his shoulder, his skin as pale as snow, but his eyes, they glowed like lumps of red, hot coal, burning in his eye sockets.\n\nHaemon walked around the fire, slowly approaching the elder. With each step, the elder gripped his walking stick tighter as the power that the boy was emanating tried harder and harder to pull him to the floor. When the boy stood over the old man, he was\n\nshaking with strain and effort to remain kneeling and barely spat out the words before collapsing:\n\n“M-M-My…..L-L-Lord-d-d….M-My P-Prince”\n\nWhen the old man awoke, he found himself sitting against the wall of the barn.\n\nHe felt a sharp pain at his arm and upon inspection found it bandaged tightly, a small blood stain slowly spreading on it. He went to tug on the bandage but a delicate hand quickly grabbed his hand and pulled it from the wound. The elder looked up,\n\nmeeting Cora’s gaze as she slowly shook her head. She pulled out a skin full of water and offered it to the man who, only realizing his thirst at the sight of the skin, quickly took it from her and drank it down. She left him a small plate, some bread and\n\ncheese, and walked around the flame to the other side of the fire. Following her movement, the old man realized that the boy had been sitting across from him this entire time, watching him. Cora slid down along the wall until she was next to the boy, then\n\ncurled herself against him, passing him a vial of a crimson liquid that the old man knew all too well, a vial of blood, his blood.\n\nHaemon lifted the vial to face level, staring at it as the fire's light illuminated the contents. Haemon smiled and asked\n\nfor his name.\n\n\"Eren\", the old man responded.\n\nHaemon shook his head and smiled again, but this time his eyes glowed and a voice unlike the previous one asked for his “true name”. The old man dropped hid bit\n\nof cheese and drew his limbs into him. His gaze met the fiery gaze of Haemon and memories of a life he tried hard to forget washed over him.\n\n\"Doulos, my lord.\" The old man said, maintaining eye contact with Haemon as he spoke, \"Servant to you and your\n\ncause.\"\n\nHaemon nodded and tossed the vial of blood into the fire. The flame erupted into a shower of sparks and turned black as the night around them, all except for the center which glowed with light. The light soon warped and changed and the old man\n\nwatched his own memories, his life from his eyes play before him for all to see. He watched the battle where the temple fell where he fought as a mere Doulos, a soldier and servant to the Crimson Prince’s cause. His escape into the woods as the temple\n\ncollapsed, his struggle for survival, his search for others, his attempts to rebuild what was lost. But alas, he watched himself give into what seemed to be reality that no others had survived, and watched as he isolated himself from the world, locking\n\nthe few relics of his past life away in a chest.\n\nThe flames died down, the color returned to its orange and red glow, and the barn returned to the quiet it had once been. Haemon stood before the flame and spoke of how few of his followers survived that\n\nbattle. He made a point to mention how few had attempted to continue his cause and gracefully pointed to the hay-loft above. The elder’s vision followed Haemon’s direction until it, only now, noticed countless bodies in the loft above, wrinkled and dry,\n\nseemingly drained of their blood. The man reeled back in fear from Haemon, but Haemon only smiled and waved his hand in front of him. He reassured the man that their fate was the fate of cowards and betrayers, those that tried to hide their connection to\n\nhim. \n\n“But the few,” Haemon said as he waved his hand to Cora and back to the old man, “the few that stayed true to me now reap great rewards.”\n\nHaemon explained how the rituals Core and he performed summoned the small\n\nbit of power that the Crimson Prince left in this world. That ritual created a connection between Haemon and the Crimson Prince; that the Prince now acted through Cora’s child, Haemon.\n\n\"I must feed on the blood of the living, fueling my power to allow\n\nthe Prince to return to this world as his true self. We have been traveling to settlements, slowly feasting on its populace as my power grows in strength with each feasting.” Haemon pointed above to the bodies. \"It seems as my power grows, those of the\n\nold Crimson Fist that had survived are drawn to us. We’ve tested those that come, and those to have hidden from their past have been feasted on. While those who were true to our prince, have been given power.”\n\nThe old man perked up. \"Power, my lord?\n\nWhat do you mean?\"\n\nHaemon’s eyes glowed bright, his power exerting itself in the room once more. The fire reached for the ceiling as Haemon lifted his right hand from his side. As his hand lifted, so did the elder man, levitating him in mid-air. Haemon\n\nwithdrew a silver dagger and sliced a thin line down his right forearm. Haemon began to recite a dark chant from deep within his mind and the blood that was slowly seeping from the wound began to rise into the air. The blood hovered over to the elder man\n\nand began to circulate around him, slowly at first but picking up speed quickly. With quick movement, Haemon shut his fist closed, forcing the blood that spun around the old man to suddenly stop and bury its way into his body. The old man howled with pain\n\n for only a moment before his eyes glazed over and his screaming silenced. The man’s body erupted into black flames, devouring him entirely, burning the flesh from his very bones. Cora stood and approached Haemon, wrapping her arms around his torso and\n\nshoulder as they watched on together. The Black Flame finally dissipated, leaving a figure in a crimson cloak where the old man once was.\n\n“Arise”, Haemon ordered with a voice not his own, “ Arise, my new Blood Mage.”\n\nThe figure stood from\n\nthe ash of the black fire and removed his hood, revealing Eren, but as his once youthful self. Eren stared at his hands, no longer wrinkled and weak; he felt his face for saggy skin and scars of old age but only found the tight, tough skin. He felt\n\nrevitalized, no empowered. The ritual had given him a small piece of his lord’s power, and that small piece had not only turned back time to when the old man was once called Doulos, it gave him strength, power, and life.\n\n\"Blood, the essence\n\nf life, is quite a powerful catalyst, as you now see with your own eyes, Doulos. It’s power is only limited by the Mage’s imagination.” Haemon said as Cora wrapped a small bandage around his wrist. \"Ours, theirs, it matters not where it comes from, but\n\nhe purity, power, and amount, that determines the strength of magic that comes from it.” Haemon showed the wound to Doulos, “Until our true lord is able to come into this world our magic is limited, but still powerful.”\n\nDoulos nodded, \"So what is the\n\nnext step? How do we bring forth our Lord, once and for all?\"\n\nCora stepped forward, revealing documents that had thought to have been lost with the years. “Cruor Veneficus, the Blood Tome. A magical tome that the Crimson Fist once had. If we were\n\nto reclaim this tome, we would be able to channel the magical energy from it, making our magic infinite and allow Haemon to focus his feeding on bringing forth our Lord.”\n\nDoulos nodded in agreement and asked \"But where do we start looking for the\n\ntome?\"\n\nCora smiled, “According to the documentation we found on the betrayers and cowards mentions the Cruor Veneficus last being seen in the Crimson Fist’s stronghold in the mountains of the North, the mountains\n\nwhere the settlement “Raven’s Nest” now stands. Word on the street of this town was that a coronation of some sort is to take place there as well, bringing in many guests.” Cora smiled a toothy grin, “If we do not find the Cruor Veneficus, at least there\n\nwill be many for my Prince to feast on.\"\n\n “Raven’s Nest is heavily defended and manned, how are we to take the city?” Doulos questioned.\n\nYou are not the only Blood Mage, Duolos.” Haemon answered as he motioned for Cora to open the barn's\n\ndoor. “There have been others that I found worthy to bestow power upon, like yourself, that now hide themselves amongst the cities of this land. They pretend to normal folk of their race, gathering information, and working their way into cities and\n\ngovernments.\" Haemon motioned outside the barn’s open door where the sunrise was just peeking over the horizon. The low light illuminated many hooded figures walking towards them. \"And many more followers are being drawn here, as you were, and they will\n\night alongside us, wielding powerful Blood Magicks.”\n\nHis questions answered and his mind set straight, Doulus nodded and resigned. \"Yes, my Lord. Raven's Nest cannot, will not, stand against our power and cause. He smiled widely,\n\n\"Our Lord, our Crismon Prince, will rise once more”.\n\n      The Coronation\n            of the\n        True Prince\n\n  A Sequel to the\n Rebirth of Evil\n\n\n\nWritten by: Lilyana", ""),
        StaticBook("Love's Ending", "§bFranswaar Kellian", "The day was starting to fade as the sun slowly crept across the sky towards the ever nearing horizon.  I left my home in a hurry wearing the most formal clothes I could manage to find laying around in my home. Nervous and slightly, I finally arrived at my\n\ndestination. She was more beautiful then ever. Her long, flowing auburn hair, striking hazel eyes, and smile were enough to make a man collaspe. The indigo dress swayed in the wind as we walked towards the ballroom.\nOur hands intertwined, I, walking a\n\nslightly faster pace. \nInside was full of elegant music and exquisite decor. The chandelier lit up room from above. As we began to dance together, our eyes were inseperable. Though the ballroom was filled to the point where moving was nearly impossible,\n\nI could only see her. Every second see smiled my stomach fluttered with astounding joy. As the night came to a close, I strolled with her until we could see the sun begin to tower over the mountains. We sat in the meadow watching the sun rise, her head on\n\nmy shoulder. At that moment, I knew I was in love. Months passed and seasons changed. Every spare second I had I spent it with her. She was the only I seemed to care about. Time spent apart seemed as if it were a life time. Though the day came where I was\n\nneeded on a cargo ship to sail across the oceans to sell goods. \n3 years were spent on the water exporting, though it felt more like 30 years. When I arrived home, all I wanted to do was see her. When I exited the boat I ran to her home. Excitement\n\ncoursing through, and adrenaline pumping. I arrived to be greated by a baby crawling on the lawn. Tears began to rush down my face as I learned I became a father. I knelt down to the baby, only to be screamed at by the mother, not realizing who I was.\n\nI stood up a looked her in the eyes, only to she her change from angry to shock. Together we began to sob as we ran into eachother's arms. We spent the rest of the day conversing about my travels and where I've been. I then asked why she didn't tell me\n\nshe was pregant when I left. Her reply was as if a dagger was stabbed into my heart, then was proceeded to be ripped and forced into it again. The baby was not mine. I learned that in my absence the woman I spent every moment with, the woman that I\n\ndevoted my life for, married another man and bore him a child. I lied as best I could fighting the tears congulating her on the new born. She began to explain that I was gone for 3 years and that she began to fall in love with another man.\n\nI left her home with full intent of drowning myself in the harbor. I stood on the edge of the pier, looking out into the now black water, for night has fallen, and prayed that my soul be given mercy. I shut my eyes and began to lean forward.\n\nHalf way into my fall or the pier, a hand ripped me away. I struggled to free myself, but I was restrained against the cold, damp floor. My 'Savior' began to scream at me, though he was tuned out as I began to rethink what I should do.", ""),
        StaticBook("Trade prince", "§bBalgruff Frostbeard", "The Trade Prince of Sutica\n\nNowwwww this is a story all about how, my life got flipped, turned upside down. And I'd like to take a minute, just sit right there and Ill tell how I become the trade prince of a state called Sutica.\n\nInnnnnnn west Vandoria born and raised in the caliphate is where I spent most of my days. Chillin out relaxin lookin all cool, just fishin some salmon out behind the town. When a couple of guys who were up to some good started visiting my neighborhood.\n\nWe refused one little fight and the King got scared, he said you need to pay taxes or you can just leave....\n\nI begged and pleaded with them day after day but the civil war broke out and they tried to push us away. They called us some rebels demandin we\n\nhelp them win the war, We packed up all our stuff and said we might as well ship it! 3rd class yo! this is bad,\ngetting seasick into my vodka glass. Is this what livin in Sutica  is gonna be like??? Hmmmm.... heck it will be all right. We spotted land and\n\nwhen we came near the cliffs were tall and a cove was there, If anything I could say this Island was rare. But I thought nahh forget it, lets go set up! We.... came up the stairs about 7 or 8 and I yelled to the citizens, We can live here! Looked at the\n\nIsles I was finally there, Gonna get super rich as the trade prince of Sutica.\n\n\n\nSigned Balgruff Frostbeard", ""),
        StaticBook("A Realization", "§bSilv'ar Vanguard", "\"Dear Madam lily.\"\n*Hand Drawn Tree Would Sit here it would cover most of the page. It's Brances would weave and dive. Laced into the tree would be little leaves each containing a name. It's a family tree.\n\n\"I have come on to learn something. Something I knew deeply but feared all the same it. One of my most core belives has failed me. Infact it is the core belive of a whole race and people. The Laws of Gold and Silver have failed me.\n\nThis most likely means nothing to you but I hope to make it clear in this journal of my life and finally in my closing notes I will explain my new found understanding.\n\nThe Fires of Aegis. I was only told stories of this time as I was but a small child but these times forever haunted my father Eldorn for the rest of his life. As in the final mouments as he and my Mother Char'ame were rushing to leave my grandfather\n\nto the end with the remaining members of the White Ravens to prevent the dark forces from reaching the portal to the verge. \nNone the less I shall progress further. During the time spent in the Asulon rather intresting things\n\ntranspired. Of that the most important to my tale would be the seperation of the Elves. This is were the once united Mali slipt forming they're crude and weak ideals. The idea of purity rose from this. It's spiny ideals created me\n\nbut my father wasn't so easily tamed by it's will. Him and his wife Char'ame both found the laws just horrible. They felt all people all races were one and the same. Because of this they fled the city.\nI spent the next 160 years with them and the rest of\n\nFamily. helping to care for all of the adopted family members that my parrents brought into the Vanguard house hold. I loved each one of them as a brother and sister thinking nothing of they're 'Purity'. That is till we landed on the shores of Vailor.\n\nThis is where it all changed for me. It had only be two weeks since we had landed and my mother was deadly ill. It was belived to be one of the last cases of the plague from the fringes. We tried day and night to find a cure. Though\n\nthere was one place we had not tried. The Silver City of the Pure Elves. I knew that they held great alchemical knowlege. But Eldorn my father told me a I must not venture into thoughous walls because I would face untold horrors. I was navie and only\n\nThought of my mother's life. I ventured into the city. I passed every test of purity they had by the skin of my teeth. But I was in. I soon forgot even why I was there I was so involved with the ways of the Pure Elves.\n\nI belived every last scrap of information I was given. I studied the Silver and Golden laws to death. Some might even say I was a scholar in them. It wasn't till the faithful day when the last king of the Pure Elves killed 20 so called impure children by\n\nremoving every one of they're eyes while they were alive and pouring molten gold into they're eye sockets and finally hanging them by they're arms from the gates of the city did cracks begin to form in my head about what these laws really\n\nimplyed. They were sick laws that governed all. This is when I to fled the city shortly before it fell to the hand of Oren. I soon faced my dying father. Who told me of the final days of my mother who simple wanted to see her\nlong gone son\n\nwho left her on her death bed... *A small carefully drawn portrait of a well rounded elf's face would sit here. Her eyes firm but happy her lips gentle but demanding. at the bottom would be the name 'Char'ame Vanguard'\n\n\"Shortly after my father passed away I left in search of a new way forward. I found this with the Tahu'norian Elves. It was a small band of Elves who lived at the time at the foot of a small village called Salvus.\n\nI will keep this part short as it's a rather hard time in my life. I came to live with these Elves as my Cousin Ante'ame was one of them she was kind and led me out of my sadness of my time spent with the 'Pure Elves'. He spent his days\n\nworking the fields tending to the animals and generally just leading a calm life in the woods. till the events of 1501. When Salvus killed almost 70% of the Tahu'nor elves but I will keep that tale short. I was lost with the aparent death of my cousin\n\nthe last living family member I had. I found comfort in the laws of Silver and Gold beliving that they could under a fairer hand. I knew that the city of Haelu'nor was not the place to try this. I saw the place that killed my last love ones and I saw a\n\na spark. In the year 1505 I joined the Salvian Guard. and spent the next 6 years defending that city and all it belived. I met many of my current friends such as Lemik, Roopak, and Archrim all great lads. But most importantly I had a purpose I grasped for\n\nit to develop and create the world in which people could be the best they can be. Through the laws of Silver and Gold. Because of this I gained the title of Consul of Salvus. The Second in command of the nation in Vailor.\nBut these were\n\nturrbulent times. As you well know. With the birth of the Salvian Rebles under the rain of David Campos I was instated as the 2nd and last Chancellor of\nSalvus and for 3 months I fought to save the nation I belived in but failed.\nThrough your\n\nfear and kindness you gave many of us a way out. A safe way to Sutica. Where I once again as the old leader was voted in as the First Trade Prince of Sutica. but this is where my real tale starts. I had lost what I had strove for. I had lost my trust in\n\nthe laws of Silver and Gold. They had failed me once more. I didn't have my heart in the job and as such I felt unfit to rule. I stepped down after 2 years. Taking for the first time in 30 years a break from the world of politcs. I begain to look else\n\nfor some redeeming part of the laws of silver and gold and as a last act of trust I married a women I did not love. Lori'liem. A wonderful women I wasted. We had three children. Two were twins Alem'sion and Ale'sul. Both boys and we had\n\none girl. Lori'sul. When they were around the age of 17 and 23 Lori'leim killed her self. It was a dark time when I simplely fell apart. My children disowned me as I simple didn't love or care for them. I found my last peace in my work as a builder.\n\nI poured my heart and soul into every design hoping to find something they're for my dead heart and my dying mind. Fast forward a bit till you become the Trade Princess. I saw you as yet another leader whom I would serve to the best of my ability\n\nIt wasn't untill I was mid through the redesign of the city when I started to relized who you really were... *A small sketch of a park with a house in the corner would sit here covering the rest of the page.* such a kind and wonderous women\n\none who I would relish to oppertunity to learn and spent more time with. Though I should make my intensions clear. I am not seeking... such a crude term but Sex or Children. Simply someone to share my never endding days with. I have already had my fair\n\nshare of children and simplely do not which for more. *Finally a carefully sketched out and colored drawning of Lilyana sitting on a bench next to a large willow tree with a small set of roses. A small turtle would be sitting in the edge of the pond its\n\n*head looking up towards the Madam who was flipping through a small booklet her silken light blue dress would flow slightly in the wind her eyes would be looking down into the pond. They're deep green pupils gazing down into the water of the lightly\n\ncolored water* The reason I ask you is because I have learned my beloved laws of Silver and Gold were never the truth. *The Book would end with another smaller tree*\n\n((OOC NOTES))\nI just want to make this clear as I know most of your other relations you have on this server tend to be mostly OOC I'm not looking for an OOC relation this is Silv'ar\nnot me. Just want to make that clear so I don't mislead you\n\nat all. Not that I'm saying we can't plan things OOCly but I'd like to keep the relation mostly IC.\n((OOC NOTE END))", ""),
        StaticBook("The White Ravens", "§bSilv'ar Vanguard", "A guide to the History\nOf the White Ravens\n\n-The Grey Wardens\n\n-The Reform\n\n-The Golden Days\n\n-The Founding\n\n-The Clipping\n\n-The Grey Wardens-\nIn the earily days of\naegis a old elf who\nlived in a church between the city\nof Malinor and Ravenhold created a church. This Church was where he safed many people but once they left his church\n\nThey were beyond his help and to combat this he created the Grey Wardens a small guild that lived close to the church. They started out with few members. They spend there days watching the roads between the two citys.\n\nThough the Wardens of Mailnor heard there name and became angered over the name. So the leader of the grey wardens turned the page and Reformed the grey wardens.\n\n  -The Reform-\nThe whole grey wardens was reworked into the White Ravens.\nLed by Alavaska. The White Raven grew to a massive size and led many to safety. It wasn't till the creation of the Council till the numbers swell to massive size.\n\n-The Golden Days-\nIt was a breif time when the white ravens fought the undead and won several attacks and led two seiges on there fortress and won them! but these days were short. The leader of the White Ravens Vanished and the end of Aegis had come.\n\n-The Founding-\nThe people of Aegis found a new land where the White Ravens founded and created a wonderful city and created a prosperous nation.\n\n-The Clipping-\nThis was the end.\nThe Specters\n\n*The page would end there clearly torn out. A small mark would be left of a tree on a cliff and a number.\n'1501'.", ""),
        StaticBook("Of the Hand", "§bShynys Itesti", "Disciples of the Hand\n\nFaction Skills:\n\n-Alchemist\n-Blacksmith\n-Chef\n-Enchanter\n-Leatherworker\n-Stonemason\n-Tinker\n-Woodworker\n\nDisciples of the Hand\n\nFaction Discription:\n\n- Individuals that work raw materials into fine armor, ancient enchants, and funny gadgets.\n\nDisciples of the Hand\n\n- These individuals work long hours, perfecting their crafts, training their skills, and trying to make a name for themselves by paying great attention to detail on singular items.\n\nDisciples of the Hand\n\n-These workers use raw materials to create and manufacture expensive products that can be sold throughout the world.\n\nDisciple of the Hand\n§\\\n\nFaction Speaker:\n\n-These individuals represent their faction at meetings with the leader.\n\n-They inform the leader of any pressing matters or concerns within their faction.\n\nDisciple of the Hand\n\nFaction Speaker:\n\n- These individuals negotiate things with the town leader.\n\n- These individuals are elected by their faction. Elections take place once a month.\n\nDisciple of the Hand\n\nFaction Speaker:\n\n- Should this position become vacant, or the individual become disposed for over a week, emergency election will be held.\n\nDisciple of the Hand\n\nSpeakers to Date:\n\n- Shynys Itesti 8/24", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Druid Conduct§0\n§0Nature Healing§0\n§0Aspect Studies§0\n§0Thanhium study§0\n§0Dark Magic and You§0\n§0\n§0Any other druid books!", ""),
        StaticBook("Xionist Tome I", "§bGallidron Elindrow", "Xionism§0\n§0The Faith of Primeval Man§0\n§0\n§0\"We are born by the Dark;§0\n§0Made Men by the Dark;§0\n§0Undone by the Dark;§0\n§0We fear the Old Dark.\"§0\n§0- The Adage of the Old Lords\n\nThe Dawn of Creation, i§0\n§0 §0\n§0In the beginning, there was only darkness; an endless emptiness where time did not exist, and the abyss was infinite and unshined.\n\nThis place was known as the Void, where chaos controlled all things: where everything and nothing took place at the same time.\n\nAs said in the accounts of the wizard Availer, there was a great flash of light in the Void, and from a shining breach in its abyss came the nameless Creator. It was from him that the cosm of Creation was formed;\n\nthat a grand veil was shaped around his essence, keeping the chaos at bay while laws and structures were applied to the Creator’s empty realm.\n\nThe breach in existence continued to shine upon Creation, strengthening the Veil and keeping the horrors of the Void from entering.\n\nFrom his endless banks of power, the Creator shaped two prime elements: light, which reflected raw Creation, and dark, which stood as it's shadow and reflection.\n\nFrom light, the Creator shaped the immortals, whom directly descended and bore fractions of their father’s power. To them he gifted the realms of light, known now as the Seven Skies.\n\nFrom dark, the Creator shaped the soul, as well as Aos and Eos. From the clay and water of these material realms, creatures of sentience were born to inhabit the lands, granted the lesser souls of beasts.\n\n From the cleanest essence of the seas and from the sturdiest stones of the mountains, four men and four women were shaped, and then blessed with the souls of Men;\n\nthe greatest embodiments of the dark, of which the four men were deemed the Four Brothers. Upon the centerpoint of the realm Aos, Aegis was deemed their birthplace.\n\nThe Exegesis of the Banks§0\n§0“In a dark time, the eye begins to see, §0\n§0I meet my shadow in the deepening shade; §0\n§0I hear my echo in the echoing wood-- §0\n§0A lord of nature weeping to a tree.\n\nI live between the heron and the wren, §0\n§0Beasts of the hill and serpents of the den. §0\n§0What's madness but nobility of soul?§0\n§0I am quintessence; darkness, what light can never cull.”§0\n§0\n\nAs equilibrium was settled upon the realms of light and dark, with Men in their mortal realms and immortals in their skies of light, the Creator recognized that the realms of dark did not reflect that of light in the means that gifted\n\nthe Four Brothers and the one race of Man bore no power of Creation. Thus the Creator recognized this weakness, and fearing that the mortal realms would succumb to chaos and undoing, he granted to these realms the banks of life:\n\nmassive collections of a pure quintessence of dark, known as lifeforce, of which brought order and balance to each mortal world by the laws of the Cycle of the Banks.\n\nThe Cycle was deemed the true moderator of the mortal realms, for upon every living being was a tie to the banks and the lifeforce that lingered them. Lifeforce itself was what caused life itself to function;\n\nwithout lifeforce, the heart could not beat, and thus Men would be condemned to flesh of cold stone and blood of brackish water without it.\n\nWith the banks to breath life into the realms of Man, it communed with their mortal souls and made them compassionate, it gave them reason, and it made them alive\n\n -- bringing to light the formation of all organic life destined to sprawl across Aos and Eos, forms plentiful and never few.\n\nThe Aspects, immortals claimed to be the rulers of nature, are condemned as false lords: for they sought to usurp the control of the banks for themselves, and thus rule all of life;\n\nbut could not pass the jurisdiction of their Creator, therefore dwarfing heavily their control on the true Cycle of things.\n\nThe Exegesis of the Old Ones§0\n§0“That is not dead which can eternal lie;§0\n§0And with strange aeons, even death may die.”\n\nWhen the Creator shaped the quintessence of dark, he was uncertain of it’s power; fearing what it would do to his mortal children, the Four Brothers, should he cast it upon both them and all the desolate, lifeless world.\n\nSo the nameless Creator looked beyond his cosm of light and dark, his Creation, and reached past the veil; snatching from the precipices of the Void two great terrors,\n\nwith one of graceful, drifting make of merciful spirit, and the other a maddening coil of limbs wrapped around a knowing mind. The Creator offered to these otherlings souls to imbue them to a world greater than that of the Void,\n\n and an essence to bring them the contentment of the dark; and thus they accepted, for they did not know of the Creator’s true bindings.\n\nFor the amalgam of wisdom and limbs, he granted unto it the name Widukind; and shaped it into a seed, planting it deep into the ground. The Creator condemned Widukind to an existence of stillness,\n\n with limbs to grow again as stiff roots imprisoned deep within the mortal earth, and all around it. Thus Widukind became the first tree -- and thus became the herald of all trees and green life to come.\n\nFor the graceful and drifting one, he granted unto it the name of Dresdrasil; and shaped it into a great angel of the flesh, bound for the sea, and thus plummeted it into the endless oceans.\n\nThe Creator condemned Dresdrasil to the existence of a water-wayfarer, imprisoning her within the depthless deep. Thus Dresdrasil became the mother of all beasts of the waters of all seas:\n\nthe herald of depthless horrors, and forebear of the simplest of shoreline dwellers.\n\nBut the Creator was not satisfied, for more work was to be done upon the mortal realms. Thus, he reached down and tore a great root from the earth,\n\ncursing Widukind to bleed eternally and writhe in pain for a year; causing the worlds to lurch, and shape mountains, hills, §0deserts.Thereafter§0, the Creator granted a soul to the stolen root; naming it Feldamfir.\n\n Feldamfir awoke enraged, for the lifeforce of Widukind dwelled him, and the first tree’s spite of the Creator therein carried on into his soul. So Feldamfir was recognized for his fury and was set upon the world\n\nwith the task to carve them grand caverns and canyons. Thus, Feldamfir became the World-Eater, and was condemned to a millennium of slumber after his duty was complete in soon time.\n\nThus the trifecta of the beyond became the heralds of all greater shape in the mortal world, but retained promise in their gifted souls to take revenge upon their oppressor in the end and tear the cosm asunder.\n\nThe Exegesis of Calor Mors§0\n§0“There shall be three years of terrible winters, §0\n§0and summers of black sunlight;§0\n§0Divided man will lose all hope, and succumb to sin, accursed interbreeding, and war;\n\nFêldamfir, the World Eater, shall come lunging from the earth - buckling the mountains and folding the realms;§0\n§0Dresdrasil, the Mother, shall go into a frenzy, and drown all life with the ocean’s tides;\n\nIblees, the Betrayer, will come flaming across the cosm to destroy the Seven Skies;§0\n§0The Immortals shall enter the realms of mortality one last time to do battle with the rebelling Old Ones;\n\nGazardiael will slay Fêldamfir, but die from it's bite;§0\n§0Widukind’s scorched roots shall twist in panic, and crush the remnants of the mortal world;\n\nAnd finally, the fiends of the Void will seek the Creator’s shining breach in existence, and devour the sun.”\n\nThe exegesis of Calor Mors bears a prophecy that foretells of the cosmos’ utter destruction, and death for all whom inhabit it. It is the point in existence when the immortals whom sacrifice themselves to become stars run out\n\nof incandescent power to light up all of Creation in the wake of the sun’s transition to night, and when the sun itself, being the tear which the Creator came from, begins to flicker and darken as the Veil,\n\n the wall that separates the Void from Creation, is breached by the limitless Voidal horrors beyond it\n\nUpon the darkening of the stars, Fêldamfir the World Eater, whom had devoured a majority of Athera, will go mad and seek to desolate the mortal planes of Aos and Eos.\n\n As all of mortality die in this undoing, they die cursed as their wretchedness remains as their sole legacy. By some means, Iblees shall gain power from this cataclysm and break free from his prisons to bring mayhem to the realms of the immortals.\n\nHe will occupy all that Creation does not fill with fire and shadow, and after the oldest known of the immortals, Gazardiael, comes down to battle crazed Fêldamfir, he shall slay it but die from the wounds it afflicted upon him\n\nWidukind, the Child of the Wood, shall be burned by Iblees’ fires, and crush the mortal planes around his hold in alarm. Dresdrasil, the mother of the seas, will will the waves to drown the remnants of life. The crescendo of Calor Mors transpires\n\nwhen the Immortals fail to overpower all of the Old Ones, wherein their distractedness causes the sun to be swallowed by invading Voidal horrors; thus utterly erasing all light and Creation and restoring the gap it formed in the endless abyss.", ""),
        StaticBook("Xionist Tome II", "§bGallidron Elindrow", "“Calor Mors” means “light death” in the supposed tongue of the immortals, referencing the undoing of the sun. It is a prophecy said to happen many eras - thousands and thousands of years - after the dawn of Creation\n\nand is inescapable, for the immortals who sacrificed themselves to become stars to keep the Veil protected from the Void did not realize their endless power was able to be nulled,§0\n§0thus snuffing out their light.\n\nThe cause of this sudden lack of power is said to come when the essence of chaos overtakes that of all structure and order in Creation, causing cosmic laws to either become distorted or defunct entirely.\n\nThe Black Nexus, the artifact that empowers the existence of the Khôrguul (the Dark Wraiths) was feared in the past by Xionist seers for it's possible destruction, for the raw essence of chaos dwelled it's containment,\n\nand upon the Nexus’ destruction would be released into the mortal world; bringing about events similar to Calor Mors, but on a much smaller scale confined only to the mortal realms.\n\nThe First Ages of Man, ii§0\n§0“Their faces were not made for wrinkles, their§0\n§0  pure blood to stagnate, their great hearts to fail;§0\n§0The blank grey was not made to blast their hair,\n\nBut like the climes that know nor snow nor hail,    §0\n§0They were all summer; lightning might assail    §0\n§0  And shiver them to ashes,§0\n§0but to trail§0\n§0a long and snake-like life of dull decay\n\nwas not for them—they had too little clay.”§0\n§0\n§0In the beginning, when the Creator shaped the Four Brothers and their wives, he named unto them the race of Man.\n\nAs the Four Brothers were shaped in one similar, humanoid image, the Four Brothers and their primeval descendents all bore a similar, perfected visage; as though fragments, all pieces of one great image of mortal flawlessness.§0\n\nHoren was of a sturdy heart and of the greatest compassion, but was neither tall nor short, nor strong nor weak. Malin was lithe, taller than his other brothers, and with a mind brimming with intellect and kindness.\n\nUrguan was stocky and honed, chiseled like a statue, but with kind eyes and an honorable soul. Finally, Krug was like a tower, brutish and huge, but of all Four Brothers he bore the greatest wisdom.\n\nIt is said, in the first year before history, that the Four Brothers bore flesh of cold stone and blood of brackish water, for their God forged them of the darkest of four souls, the greatest of four Lordsakes, but without the warm aura of life.\n\nSo the Creator brought forth the quintessence of lifeforce to the mortal worlds and blessed the Four Brothers before their stoney existence was passed on to their unborn sons.\n\nor an age - the age of Man - the Four Brothers and their descendents lived in peace, for the warm darkness of their souls did not yet know of sin nor strife\n\nThe Exegesis of the Lordsake§0\n§0“And that was our business -- war.§0\n§0We bought our daily bread with coin paid to us for conquering;§0\n§0perhaps us getting killed was just balancing the scales.”\n\nWhen the essence of souls and the essence of life were formed by the Creator, he applied unto the souls of all Men an inherit power that reflects the actions of mortality to very present day;\n\nthe power of Lordsakes, shadowed shards of cosmic authority that stood as the very core as mortal charisma -- the very push and pull of a lord’s ways, of a king’s ways, and the reason that their ultimate word held great sway over kingdoms\n\nand empires of future fragmented Men. The Lordsakes were no artifacts, nor any enchanted items, nor any arcane power, but merely the way of mortal will; how the passion of a king can drive forth his kingdom,\n\n and how the rites of succession shall pass such power down to both sons and conquering foes.§0\n§0\n§0There lies an old legend detailing the nature of the Lordsakes, and their exact origin, reading as followed from ancient texts;\n\nAs the Creator forged Man, gave him life, and imbued him with a soul, he set upon each of them - the Four - four Lordsakes to match the number of the brothers, and as these Lordsakes were set upon their soul essences,\n\nan existential scale coiled around the berth of the mortal planes. The Lordsakes were destined to deem the Four Brothers as sole rulers of the race of Man, yet, there were four Lordsakes -- not one.\n\nThe old Seers of Aegis said, “the one who controls the sole Lordsake, controls the world”, for the nature of the Lordsake was to be singular, wielded by one king and one king alone; the king of kings, the king of Men,\n\nand the lord of the planes of dark. But the Creator feared this prospect -- he feared it for it destined Man to make rise of a king powerful enough to match his own cosmic authority, for the Creator was the king of all light,\n\nand bore no right to stand as the king of Men when he himself was no mortal at all.\n\nSo the Creator shattered the one Lordsake into four pieces, and gifted them in secret to the Four Brothers recently forged. This was the intention of the Lordsakes; to remain with the Four Brothers so they may retain reign forever more.\n\nHowever, blackness came upon them, usurping them from their thrones whenever history marked them to fall. So the four Lordsakes descended to rest as the burdens as the next four kings to take the brothers’ places.\n\nBut with the passing of the Fallen One’s First Sin, Man’s hearts had been tainted by their disunity and the unseen aura their first enemy exuded; causing havoc, rebellion, and inner-conflict to sprout within the four kingdoms of remnant Man.\n\nSo the four Lordsakes shattered, by nature, into smaller pieces; all clinging to the souls of the rebel lords throughout ancient history. Each generation brought different rebel lords,\n\nor dukes who attained kingship through abandonment of the state their loyalty lied in; and each generation, the Lordsakes broke down until they were tiny pieces, held by all remnant Men who stood as leaders of other many Men under their banners.\n\nBut the Seers of the Dissentuous Way foretold this prophecy: one day, a true descendant of the race of Man shall rise up, and take claim to the world -- conquering all known lands and thus attaining the sole Lordsake,\n\nthe right to rule all of mortality. The right to mirror the light of the Creator, and to stand in his shadow as his equal in the dark.\n\nThe Intrusion of the Immortals, iii§0\n§0“‘Whom the gods love die young’ was said of yore,    §0\n§0  And many deaths do they escape by this: The death of friends, and that which slays even more—    §0\n§0  The death of friendship, love, youth, all that is,\n\nExcept mere breath; and since the silent shore    §0\n§0  awaits at last even those who longest miss    §0\n§0the old archer’s shafts, perhaps the early grave§0\n§0which men weep over may be meant to save.”\n\nThe old Daemon Iblees grew curious of mortal contentment, and thus stepped down from the Seven Skies to embrace the peace of the dark; not knowing his mortal essence of light was not meant to become one with its’ counterpart.\n\nSo the dark that clung to his being shrouded the light of his existence, and corrupted the mortal quintessence he embraced.\n\nIblees became fallen; in limbo between the light and the dark, embodying the very precipices of chaos that lingered outside the cosmic veil of Creation. This is written as the First Sin; for from his foolishness, Iblees became a bastion of ill will & sin,\n\nwhich tainted the centerland of Aegis with each step he took upon it. His curiosities turned to spite, wherein he tried to trick the Four Brothers with promise of false glory and power. Krug, the wisest, saw through the ruse,\n\nand was quick to wage battle against the fallen Immortal; but was burned whole by his counterstrike.\n\nFrom this, the race of Man was destined to be tainted by evil, for when the Fallen One committed the First Sin, it’s malignant fallout breached all souls of mortality and sprouted like a cancer. Thus, they discovered war, and waged it against Iblees\n\nfor thirty years. In time, the Fallen One fell to their mortal powers, but in turn cast a spell upon each of the Four - declaring them cursed as such;\n\nHoren’s sons shall be short-lived, with the burden of time’s scythe lying heavy upon their shoulders.§0\n§0\n§0Malin’s sons shall suffer sparse sons, and seek companionship of the oaks in search of lives less lonely.\n\nUrguan’s sons shall be born shorter than he, and shall not inherit his kindness, but black greed.§0\n§0\n§0Krug’s sons shall be born burned, with his blood-bound wisdom shrouded by blood-born fury.\n\nBut it was not the curses that Iblees hoped to break the Four with, but the undoing of their unity. No longer were they mortal man, but were mere mortal humans, elves, orcs and dwarves;\n\nsplintered and factionalized by the Fallen One’s affliction. The dawn of this disunity falls not only upon Iblees, however, but also upon Aerial - for she came down to bless their curses away with false promises and hollow magic.\n\nThe fragmentation of the race of Man under these Immortal methods was deemed the Second Sin.\n\nAerial’s blessings were falsehoods, for she gifted to the descendents what they already bore:§0\n§0She blessed Horen with the ascension to the Seven Skies upon death,\n\ncondemning the souls of his mortal sons both alive and unborn to dwell a plane of existence in which they do not belong. This promise tainted the human race,  and cursed future generations to faith in Immortals\n\nan a Creator ever-absent; leaving their prayers to fall upon deaf ears, and to leave them both ignorant and unanswered.\n\nShe blessed Malin with long life, shrouding his mind with impatience, for life under her magic became a breeding stigma and the conception of an elderly, tired culture obsessive for the sparse chance of children.\n\nShe blessed Urguan with strength, when they were already mighty - leaving the dwarves to question their ire and collect within great clans of the earth to put a shroud over their false weakness.", ""),
        StaticBook("Xionist Tome III", "§bGallidron Elindrow", "She blessed Krug with honor; that which his children already had, therein clouding their wisdom further with backward barbarism and tribalistic ways, for they aligned their honor with the uncertain spirits in desperation for another font of wisdom to\n\nguide them.§0\n§0The intrusion of Iblees and Aerial caught the attention of the other powerful Immortals who, though it was against their father’s wishes, saw merit in mortal meddling; some seeing the broken race of Man as malleable,\n\nand others looking upon them with pity. This brought forth the Third Sin; when the Immortals meddled with the mortals like puppets, condemning all humans, elves, orcs and dwarves to the scourge of deific agendas, and generations of shadowed proxy wars.§0\n\nThe Desolation; Xion, iv§0\n§0 “Remember Aegis; may the Abyss whisper up to you from its’ ageless darkness, ‘Go tell the dissentuous, passerby, that here by Immortal law, Xion shall lie.’”\n\nAt the peak of the war of the Ascended and the Undead, Aegis was torn asunder and sickened by the miasma of the Fallen One. Little could the Ascended do but offer false hope;\n\nleaning on crutches and depending on the races of remnant Man in order to charge into battle, leaving their white visages untouched by the grime of war’s spilt blood and battlefield soot.\n\nThe four great seats of the descendents had fallen threefold, with Malinor the only bastion remaining. As remnant Man plunged themselves into the Nether in a frenzied search to destroy the source of Iblees’ power,\n\nhis trap had already been readied; they destroyed the nexus of the realm of fire, and released it upon the centerland of Aegis.\n\nAll of it was scorched black by a fire that would not die; leaving mortals still living to flee to the ports of another mortal world, entered by a portal heralded by the white wizard Availer. But even as the remnants of Man escaped\n\nhellfire and Immortal fury, the desolation continued-- for the Aengul Aerial descended onto the charred land of Aegis in order to make battle with Iblees, the Fallen One,  whom rose from the hellfires unleashed by his destroyed nexus.\n\nThose that heralded the old Dissentuous Way beheld the battle themselves-- the Lord of Embers and the Lord of the Dark, whereas the Nameless Lord and the Lord of the Oaks were far beyond the wasteland of Aegis;\n\nunable to behold the blinding visage of the battle that the Lords of Embers and Dark beheld.\n\nIt is said by the Lord of Embers, the herald of the Way of Embers, that before the fallout of the Immortal duel unraveled his soul, he escaped into the darkest of caverns; catching only a glimpse of the terrible cataclysm\n\nthat the Immortals set upon the sacred mortal centerland. And thus the Abyss was born -- a giant scar which encompasses all that Aegis once had before it. Horrified by the destruction of Aegis, the Lord of Embers cursed\n\nthe name of the Immortals, and proclaimed the Abyss as the damned realm of Xion. §0\n§0And thus the name Xion was born, and Xionism arisen, for beneath the earth and upon the edge of Xion itself,\n\nthe Lord of Embers coveted it’s doctrines in wait for one to claim them.\n\nThe Fates of the Lords§0\n§0 §0\n§0After the first ages of Man passed and as the 30 year war made it’s dawn, four lords under Horen sought to combat the Fallen One with a power as great as his own;\n\nand thus they set out and found the gift of a Draakar, rejected by all but them, for they recognized it’s potential. From the artifact came great knowledge and the power of darkening\n\n- to steal away immortal power, and then shift it from a magic of light, to dark. They used the darkening to steal away Iblees’ corrupted essence and transform it into an art of worldly alignment and higher truth\n\nlending the four lords the powers to manipulate life itself. With this, they achieved mortal transcendence, and affixed upon their souls an everlasting nature.\n\nBut as the next millennia came to pass, the lords grew less fond of the descendents, for they were fractured and fragmented by the old Fallen One’s curses. So within this millenia they forged the Dissentuous Way - to dissent against the immortals.\n\nIt was by this way many primeval mortals lived, for in the old times the words of the transcendent lords could be heard unbiased; untainted by the present day hold in which the immortals have upon the remnants of Man.\n\nThus many came to follow the lords and their ideology.§0\n§0But their followings faded overtime, with the Dissentuous Way less of an occult presence, and more of tribal faith. The seers of the old times guided descendents who would heed their grim wisdoms,\n\nand it was through these seers that the ways of the lords were kept alive, even as the lords themselves secluded themselves to darkness. Thus they watched the world through the millennia,\n\nshrouded by night and the shadows of day. Soon, time waned upon them, and in due time, the four lords became three, for the one known as the Nameless Lord set off north to forge great glories, and was never seen again.\n\nSo the three lords lingered until the desolation of Aegis by the coming of the Undead. As great catacalysmic fires fell upon the centerland upon the first condemning day of the great exodus, a great fear fell upon the three lords;\n\nand while one, the Lord of Oaks, walked in the shadow of remnant Man and followed them into the Verge, the other two remained near their homeland, wishing to see it fall as they saw it rise.\n\nThe one known as the Lord of Embers fled to the lands of Urguan to the east and beheld a battle of immortals take place upon broken Aegis, but was forced to flee beneath the earth as the hellfires\n\nof divine war reached far and blinded the lord. But the Lord of the Dark remained within charred Aegis itself, and as Aegis came to shatter and become the Abyss, the Lord of the Dark sank with it;\n\nbecoming one with Xion while his brother would tell of the great sin from afar.\n\nThe Foundation of Xionism, v§0\n§0\n§0“Xionism hath been deemed Xionism in tribute to the land of Xion; all that encompasses the Abyss and it's scars. But the name it is a scar itself; like flesh distorted by fire,\n\nit is different. It hides the true name of what once lingered, and what now stands as the Wound of the World. The ancient Lord of Embers spoke of brilliant lights and deafening booms when he dwelled the old lands prior to the Abyss' forging.\n\nIt was only after the gaping maw had been shaped that he realized what had taken place. It was then he realized that two divines invaded a realm they do not belong to, to wage a battle upon land they hold reign over, to settle a score\n\nhey wrought in their own skies. They brought their destruction to Old Aegis and undid it. It was not enough that the fires of the Betrayer had scorched it black -- they thought tearing it asunder like the sandpit\n\nof a child was appropriate action. As if the world would not notice.§0\n§0If one wishes to regard any sin as the greatest it would be this one. The centerpoint of this estranged, unexplained cosmos had been wiped clean from the slate.\n\nThe first thing that the absent Creator created had been eviscerated by His children. And now we are cursed with a cycle; to occupy land, destroy it, and move. To act as the malignant nomad, unaware of our ways.\n\nThis is the core of Xionism; vengeance. We few seek vengeance for what hath been stolen. The world was not meant to endure such sorrow, as to witness the end of the First Creation.“§0\n§0 -The Origin of Xion\n\n“Mortality -- a term used to define man. Any who was born of life is defined as mortal, regardless of state among the spectrum between life and death, all defined as one under Mortality. Deities, however, are born of immortality\n\nand without the potential of death, only imprisonment or weakening. They are not of Mortality, rather Immortality.”§0\n§0-The Origin of Xion\n\nThe Disiples of Xion, referred to as Xionists, are those who adhere to the ideology of Xion. Adherents cite the Aenguls and Daemons as the source of destruction, as they are otherworldly and invade our realm in order to\n\nweave destruction. Some outright kill, some weave plague and woe, others oppose mortal powers. All oppress mortality and have taken the throne as ruler of ourselves. Each commit sacrilege against the likes of mortality and\n\ndesecrate our world. Xion seeks to usurp them, to regain control of ourselves and eject the deceivers from their stolen seats.\n\nThe manner in which said removal is to be performed is through unification. The gods act as parasites: they require worship in order to uphold additional scales of power. By spreading the ideology of antitheism and Xion, it instills a\n\nstigma and disdain for godly worship. In this awareness, said worship will dwindle in popularity and draws mortality closer to usurping the gods by weakening them. However the gods are few, and Mortality is many, yet the power is\n\ndivided still. Only through unification of all mortals to fight the gods may, as together we are mighty but alone we are lesser. Unification can only come through conversion, thus Xion seeks to add to its numbers hugely by spreading its truths.", ""),
        StaticBook("Oren History: I", "§bJacob Chapel", "History of Oren\n       Part I\n\n\nThis history has been collected by me, Jacob Chapel. Sources are various scrolls and texts and the museum of Petrus, beside common knowledge and tales. Enjoy.\n\n((Written by Pepernoot))\n\nIf you're interested in the history before Al'Khazar, I suggest you pick up the first part of the Holy Scrolls, the Nativities. If you're interested in the history past Siegmund Carrion's election, then you need to find part two of The History of Oren.\n\nThe days of Aegis\nThe history of Oren begins in Aegis, the land of the Descendants. To the west of the Cloud Temple laid its lands. The capital of Al’Khazar was surrounded by pastures, farmlands and forests, and to the north laid the snowy lands,\n\ncontaining cities and towns such as Alstion and Snowy Fields. In the south were the islands known as the Whisper Isles, surrounded by the calm waters of the Whisper Lake, and filled with fishermen and craftsmen such as glassblowers.\n\nThe first king was Horen himself. Many Kings ruled the Kingdom wisely after him, Kings such as Saint Daniel. The Kingdom’s grand city was built by this King Daniel in 1300. Eventually King Pampo Perea, Saint Daniel’s seneschal, took power, with Edmund\n\nSheffield as his seneschal.\nAs time passed the Undead became stronger and bolder in their attacks on the Kingdom. Snowy Fields fell and was razed, but soldiers from Alstion retook it and the town was rebuilt. This turned out to be futile eventually as\n\nthe northern towns fell one by one. Even King Perea himself was killed by the Undead, within the tall walls of Al’Khazar. Edmund Sheffield took his place, but was corrupted by the dark forces and turned to the Nether. His “abduction” was accompanied by a\n\ngreat siege of Al’Khazar, in which a portal to the Nether opened in the city square. After a great battle the City of Man was sacked. Human refugees poured across the Kingdom and the city of Galahar, built on the ruins of the old town of Kramoroe, was\n\nnamed capital.\n\nEdmund’s son Enor Sheffield took his place, but under his rule the situation worsened drastically, the corruption of his father and his own weakness led to great dissent in the Kingdom.\n\nMeanwhile Gaius Marius, Hochmeister of the Teutons had carved out a realm for himself to the east of the Kingdom, and had even participated in wars against Oren, allying with its enemies. It is important to note that even though the Undead threatened\n\nOren, and although Oren was the strong defence between the Undead and the rest of the races, this didn’t deter them to wage wars against the Kingdom. Infighting between the nations of Malinor, Krug, Urguan and Oren was common.\nRebellion brew in the\n\nKingdom, and in Galahar the Phoenix Rebellion formed. As the weak King Enor saw his position wasn’t tenable anymore he named Gaius Marius as King of Oren. This treason lead to the rise of the rebellion and a war between the part of Oren controlled by\n\nthe Teutons and the Phoenix Rebellion, led by Eze’kiel Tarus as the Phoenix King. Eventually the two sides reach an agreement, splitting the realm into two parts. The Kingdom of Renatus, ruled by King Tarus, and the Realm of Hanseti, ruled by Gaius.\n\nAfter giving up his crown the mad King Enor and his followers settled on an island, and together with Queen Dawn Perea, wife of the old King Perea he created the nation of Salvus.\nHumanity could not rest though, as the Undead continued their march\n\nrelentlessly. In 1349 Galahar was attacked by the forces of evil, but even though the attack was repelled, the city was evacuated for fears of Miasma. Eventually Aegis fell in 1351, and the humans fled through the portal to the verge,\n\nalong with the other Descendants. \n\nA new dawn in Asulon\nAfter a brief stay in The Verge, the Descendants settled in the new lands of Asulon. Oren was still divided into the three Kingdoms, and Renatus settled around their new capital of Arethor.\n\nIn these turbulent times a new human nation sprung up, the Kingdom of Seventis.\n\nWhen King Eze’kiel resigned from the throne, Renatus was ruled by the Regent King Reynard Lycian until Godfrey Horen appeared. Godfrey claimed direct decent from King Horen\n\nand quickly received support from the Dukes and other nobles of the realm. He consolidated power and strengthened the Kingdom, which was plagued by instability and attacks from the other nations.\n\nSoon after Godfrey started his reign a coalition against\n\nSalvus formed by The Kingdom of Seventis, Alras, the Orcs and most importantly what was called the Twilight Army. This coalition did not have official support from Renatus, but most of its nobles supported it. Opposing the coalition were Salvus itself,\n\nthe Dwarves of Urguan and the Realm of Hanseti. After the humiliating defeat, and the betrayal of Queen Dawn’s guards, which murdered her, Salvus was absorbed into Renatus.\n\nAs the great plague hit Asulon, the Kingdom of Seventis was hit most severely,\n\npresumably due to its location close to the Cloud Temple. Most of its inhabitants died, allowing for an absorption into the Kingdom of Renatus to protect the weakened towns.\n\nThe Teutons could not stand by idly as Renatus grew, and went to war against\n\nthe Kingdom. Swiftly the war turned against their favour, and it became clear that they were losing the war. To avoid destruction of his nation, Hochmeister Mirtok surrendered it to King Godfrey, saying the words “I will die for this nation, but I will\n\nnot let it die for me”.\n\nThe annexation of Hanseti meant the unification of humanity, and the Holy Oren Empire was formed. In the Empire the Crownlands, Salvus and Hanseti were ruled as separate kingdoms, with the Emperor presiding over the individual\n\nkings, while also being king of the crownlands.\n\nAfter a rebellion against the crown, House Tarus, House Elendil and House Norsem were shunned and fell out of favour in the Empire. Soon though, the lands of Asulon were submerged by floods,\n\nand a second flight was needed.\n\nAnthos, a land plagued by war\nJust as the flight from Aegis, the new land was not reached in one trip. There were stops at the isles of Elysium and Kalos. One notable thing in Kalos was the formation of the\n\nPhoenix Kingdom, by Eze’kiel Tarus. The kingdom was quickly destroyed, even before leaving for Anthos.\n\nIn Asulon the Teutonic Order was nigh destroyed by the Flays, and the Hochmeister killed. A few remnants of the Order made it to Anthos, albeit\n\nwithout a Hochmeister to guide them. The Teutons settled on the southern side of the ice wall of the North. Meanwhile the Empire of Oren settled a bit further south, with Abresi as their capital.\n\nSoon after arriving it turned out this land was not as\n\nsafe as the Descendants had hoped. North of the wall the Teutons discovered a savage race of boarmen and a land filled with dragons.\n\nThis was not the most pressing issue to Oren though, as the Empress of Oren was kidnapped by the Orcs, and a joint\n\nHuman and Dwarf force invaded their capital and massacred nearly every orc present.\n\nEver present Elven aggression led to a crusade against the Princedom of Malinor. The Order of the White Rose quickly destroyed their forces and burned the Malenorian\n\nfarms. The elves eventually surrendered to the siege, proving the enormous strength of a united Oren.\n\nUnited it would not be for long, as the Teutons rebelled. In the first battle, at Kralta, the Teutons were crushed. At the second battle the\n\nBlackmonts stood alone against the Teutons on the Iron Fields, outnumbered two to one. The Teutonic Order was victorious.\n\nThe civil war was decided at the Siege of the Dreadfort, where the Chivays, and the Carrions (then bannermen of the Blackmonts)\n\ndistinguished themselves. The defeat at the Battle of Iron fields had led to close cooperation between the White Roses and the Blackmonts, which turned out to be more than the Teutons could handle. Although the Dreadfort was severely damaged the siege\n\nwas broken and the Teutonic Order defeated. Soon afterwards the Teutonic Order was disbanded by Emperor Godfrey.\n\nThe Scourge, the name of the goatmen and the dragons from the north made their first move south beyond the wall, destroying the old\n\nTeutonic castle of Greywyn with flames, and taking it for their own.\n\nThen, in a huge shock to the empire, Emperor Godfrey died. He was succeeded by his son, Horen.\n\nAs the princedom of Malinor was absorbed into the Empire as\n\nprotectorate, the expansion of Oren led to great concern with the other races. They formed a coalition and in the War of Anthos they attacked Oren, putting a halt to the expansion. Most prominent in the coalition were the Dwarves, angered by the\n\nexecution of one of their Dwarven lords by nobles of Oren. In the Battle of the Crossroads the coalition was defeated though, by a brilliant Orenian flanking manoeuvre.\n\nIn 1415 the Empire was left in disarray as Emperor Horen left, along with his\n\nmost loyal houses. The dwarves used this weakness to form their Grand Kingdom of Urguan in the War of Asulon. They dissolved the Empire of Oren and many parts, including Salvus and Adunia swear loyalty to the dwarves as vassal states. What remains of the\n\nEmpire was renamed to the Holy Kingdom of Oren and ruled by William Horen. After more chaos and disarray many more little human states sprung up or seceded from the Kingdom, leading to the current prominent Houses, states and cultures. \n \nEventually the\n\nGrand King of Urguan, Thorin Grandaxe dies. This caused the human vassal states to be released from their oaths to the dwarves. This made way for a renewed unification. As King William was pushing his powers and support from his nobles to the limit,\n\nHouse Winter made a secret pact with Ruska. This forced King William to step down, giving control of Oren to his cousin Silus. This was only temporary, as the states of Salvus, Oren, Ruska and the new state of Herendul were confederated into an empire,\n\nwith an elective monarchy. At the election Siegmund Carrion was voted in as Emperor of Oren.\n\nThis concludes part one of Oren's history. The events after Siegmund Carrion's election, up to the first years in Athera will be discussed in part two.\n\n((So you made it to the end. I hope you liked it. You can leave a message to me on the forums, username: Pepernoot))", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "w", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "A book I have made to document all of my known enchanting glyphs.§0\n§0X=Material§0\n§0_=Space\n\nUnbreaking§0\n§0To make an item stronger so it won't break as quickly.§0\n§0X_X§0\n§0_X_§0\n§0X_X\n\nEfficinecy§0\n§0To make an item (not a weapon) work better. Cut better, break faster, etc.§0\n§0X__§0\n§0_XX§0\n§0X__\n\nSmite§0\n§0Unsure of use.§0\n§0_XX_§0\n§0XXXX§0\n§0XXXX§0\n§0_XX_\n\nShapness§0\n§0Makes weapons sharper.§0\n§0_X_______§0\n§0XXXXXXXXX§0\n§0XXXXXXXXX§0\n§0_X_______\n\nLooting§0\n§0Not sure what this does§0\n§0_x_____x_§0\n§0xxxx__xxx§0\n§0_x_____x_\n\nThorns§0\n§0Perhaps emit a damaging aura?§0\n§0X_X_X_X_X§0\n§0X_X_X_X_X§0\n§0X_X_X_X_X§0\n§0X_X_X_X_X", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Day 1,§0\n§0Today is my first day on my own. I'm excited to explore and learn about the world I live in. The first thing I'd like to do is find a library and read about everything. I've heard that net everyone is as nice as my parents and I want to know who I\n\ncan trust. The stories my mother and father told me say that not everyone is the same, but some groups of people, like orcs, can be quite hostile. Some humans even hate Kha like myself. I wonder if I'll ever meet my real parents. I suppose only time will\n\ntell...\n\nDay 2§0\n§0I've learned a lot. I've learned of my people. Of humans, of orcs, of elves, etc... I've been exploring, gathering, researching, learning... I've decided I'm going to find my way to learn magic. Though, mages seem to have a strange lack of physical-\n\nability as a cost for their connection to the void. I will have to prepare for this cost. If they are, on average, weaker than the commoner, I could just be above average in my physical ability before I start... right? I've always wanted to learn how to-\n\nuse a dagger anyway. How I will go about this, I do not yet know, but I may have to join some militia... I've always hated conflict... But I have always loved a challenge.\n\nDay 3§0\n§0Muna - Mother§0\n§0Muuna - Goddess? Metztli?§0\n§0Sa'vi - Hello and Goodbye.§0\n§0[!] A slightly different handwriting would begin here§0\n§0Mul'ta - Thank you§0\n§0Tra'kul - You're welcome§0\n§0Per'ta - Brother§0\n§0Mau'na - Sister\n\nPatta - Father§0\n§0The most important of these words are Sa'vi and Muuna. They will be used most frequently by many of the Kha you may meet. §0\n§0-Signed Do'Tekai\n\nDay 5§0\n§0I feel I've advanced so much in just 5 elven days! I feel that the work I've studied in my experiments will make me most useful. I am now studying music. Maybe that will make up for my horrible singing voice. This 'redstone' stuff works wonders! Why\n\ndo so few people use it? I've even toughened the security in my home with it! Now if only i had a good source of minae... I can't seem to find a good way to make any. Perhaps I should fish more often. Everyone needs food!", "")
    )

    private fun _AXIOS_CERU_3(): List<StaticBook> = listOf(
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "The Book of Songs§0\n§0I will write my songs for The Electric Piano in this book. The... notes... from lowest to highest will be labeled 1 through 8. This in theory, would only work for this version of the electric piano.\n\nMy First:§0\n§01-5-5-1-5--1-5-5-1-5§0\n§02-6-6-2-6--2-6-6-2-6§0\n§03-7-7-3-7-8-7-6§0\n§02-6-6-2-6-7-6-5§0\n§01-5-5-1-5-6-5-1§0\n§08-7-6-5-4-3-1", ""),
        StaticBook("Aeromancy", "§bGreat Ser Arhadir Owl", "Aeromancy\n\nBy Master Of Air Ser Arhadir Owl\nDate 23rd of The Amber Cold, 1474\n\nChapter 1: How to use Air Evocation\nChapter 2: Extra Information\nChapter 3: Spells\n\nChapter 1:\n\n1. The first step you should take to learning Aeromancy is by observing the wind itself in action, but to do this in a windy area where you can see what the wind does to nature.\n\n2. The next step is to learn how to clear your mind and focus just on the void.\n\n3. After these first two steps you must learn to pull from the void, you do this by clearing your mind and focusing on the void and by thing about the wind itself which you have been studying.\n\n4. Slowly you will be able to bring small amounts of Air from the void into the world. During this stage you will find that your body is weaken and you may faint a few times trying to hold a connect to the void or by trying hard spells.\n\n5. Once you learnt how to pull Air from the void you can start using it practically. one of the first spells you can learn is Force push which is a basic burst of Air, this can be used to blow out torches and maybe even push people back a bit.\n\n6. From here it is up to you to develop Aeromancy better to learn new and intresting spells.\n\nChapter 2:\n\nQuestion that people have about Aeromancery's and what they can do with Aeromancy.\n\n-Aeromancer’s cannot control Air in the natural world only control and summon Air which they have summoned from the void.\n\n-Aeromancer’s cannot fly because the cannot hold a stable connection to the void while moving that fast.\n\n-Aeromancer’s do not have the ability to change the weather.\n\nChapter 3:\n\nBasic:\n-Force Push: The most basic of all the spells this essentially allows of an Aeromancer to push a target away or knock them over.\n\n-Air Ball: Another basic spell which allows for the Aeromancer to summon a ball of Air which can be fired at targets at different speeds.\n\nAdept:\n\n-Tornado: This is a much more defence spell which allows the Aeromancer to summon a tornado around a person or the Aeromancer him or her self,-\n\n- this tornado can protect from distance attacks (Best against other elemental magic attacks) or weak melee attacks.\n\n-Air Jump: An Aeromancer can fire a blast of air underneath him or her self which can then propel the Aeromancer up a few meters.\n\n-Air Burst: This spell fires a blast of air out from all around the Aeromancer which can be powerful enough to knock over many targets all out the Aeromancer.\n\n-Air Stream: This spell is when an Aeromancer fires a constant stream of Air which can push objects or people back, even knocking them over or stopping them from moving.\n\nAdvanced:\n\n-Air Leap: The same as an Air Jump but allows you to jump much higher with much more precision.\n\n-Speed Run: While the idea of this speed seems easy it requires a lot of concentration for a mage to be moving at a speed and be usings his or her magic, this spell allows for the Aeromancer to run slightly faster than normal.\n\n-Air Bolt: This is a sudden and extremely power blast of air which which can be fire long distances. When this bolt hits it can knock a person out cold or even break bones.\n\nThis concludes the Tome.", ""),
        StaticBook("My Return", "§bWargoth Malog'Yar", "These past several years mi have been on pilgrimage. It was nub a pilgrimage to any particular holy place or shrine, but was rather a sacred wandering of sorts. Mi explored wild and uninhabited places, and further hardened mi already thick hide.\n Mi\n\nhave meditated atop the highest peaks of frozen mountains. Mi have trod the scorched earth of volcanic regions where the air is clouded with smoke agh ash. Mi have stalked through the underbrush of mi people's jungles, braving the dangers of the many\n\npredators thereof.\n In mi travels, mi have observed the skies by morning, listened to the voice of the wind by day, gazed into fires by evening, agh dreamed both wonderous agh terrible dreams by night. All of existence, both waking agh sleepeing, blah\n\nmi. The spirits guide mi through the stars agh moon, agh drive mi on through the sun. They whisper in the grass of the fields agh murmur in the caves. The waves of the sea shout to they who listen.\n Mi return this day from mi pilgrimage to find mi peop\n\nscattered, agh their blargs empty. The streets are silent. The walls have been thrown down. Just as well though. I hated that goi. The orcs have done far hosher. San'Orca; San'Vitar; Kodar'goi; San'Thraka. These were gois worthy of Krug!\n Still though,\n\nbrothers agh sisters are left without a land. The sharas mouth boastful things in their perpetual arrogance, agh their wood albai whores have spat on the shadow of Enrohk  by stretching their hands out against his shrines. Darûkûrz bûbri, afar angathfark\n\nagh vadokanuk, ashdautus vrasubatlat! All who read this, heed those words. Let them be the words of all the Orcish people. Gruk them agh lock them in lats spirit.\n Meanwhile, the spirit realm screams, agh the bub'hosh powers of the universe shift. Mi\n\nhear the moaning agh creaking of all reality in mi sleep. The h'ash arms have fallen. Such is the price of betrayal, though mi blah it with a heavy soul. Those arms could have upheld us for all eternity, had carelessness nub taken hold of our Rex. Kharak\n\nwas a fine Rex, but his ambition outgrew his wisdom. Now, that which brought us such power has been layed low, agh we suffer for it. The powers with which he played are not toys, agh Orgon is nub a howler on a leesh. Had he gruk'd that sooner, things\n\nwould still be as they ought to be. Let all living shamans learn from his error, agh excercize due caution when wielding such power.\n Now, Kinul stands where mi old master agh the giver of mi sight once stood. These days weigh heavily on mi bones. Now,\n\nhowever, is nub a time to mourn. Now is the time to steel ourselves. We are nub as the sharas, who live on through their gois agh land. Wi are nub as the albai, who grow fat at the trough of comfort agh luxury. We are nub as the gazat, who hole up in\n\ncaves, agh find their security in riches. We are the orcs, the cubs of Krug. Our strength is to be found in hardship agh trial. \n Since the days of the Ancients, our kind has fought for every morsel of food; every cup of water; every inch of land;\n\nevery breath of air; agh every second of existence. These days are nub to be lamented, but rather let us stand tall as ever. Let us remember who we are. These days are not a grave to lie down in. They are a crucible to purify us. They are a fire to harden\n\nour skins.\n We are the uruk, the goblins, agh the ologs. We are the cubs of Krug. We are the orcs. Look upon lats ancestors, brothers agh sisters, agh shout proudly. Raise lats lusks, lats zults, lats o'ligs, agh lats fists. Bare lats tusks, stand atop\n\nthe bloody agh broken heaps of lats enemies, agh roar. For the spirits. For the Ancestors. For Krug!\n -Malog, Wargoth of the Yar Clan.\n 7th of The Grand Harvest, 1583.", ""),
        StaticBook("Job application", "§bIsabelle Wood", "Job applications§0\n§0\n§0Hello, I am writing to ask if there are any availible positions open at the library? I am a hard worker and I will be availible for the most part. I would love to work at a library I find them very interesting and fascinanting. Please\n\nsend a response as soon as possible.§0\n§0\n§0- Isabel Wood§0\n§0  (ElephantChef)", ""),
        StaticBook("Grass", "§bTa'Kayla", "The Grass Family is undoubtedly the most important plant family in the world. Agriculturally, Economically, and Ecologically. They are also the major providor for cereal crops and razing for both wild and domesticaded herbivors.\n\nEcological Importance: Occuring everywhere in the world besides in large thickets or rainforests. They appear in brackish water, salt marshes, lakes, and rivers.§0\n§0\n§0Adaptations: The Grass Family experience many different types of breeding which\n\nincludes cross-pollination, self-pollination, cloning, and apomixis. An example of this is when an weedy grass is an apomixis they can expand rapidly when in a good environment. The seeds are normally spready by water, man, or animal. Grass elonate after\n\ncutting, and shoots flattened by wind, rain, or animals grow up again. §0\n§0\n§0Economic and Agricultural importance: Most of our worlds food comes from The Grass Family. Some of these include wheat and barley. However grasses also provide food for as\n\nmentioned before wild and domesticated animals. Along with those assumptions for early settlements are known to be based near rivers where agriculture was first started letting civilizations pop up. §0\n§0\n§0Conclusion: In conclusion the reason why Grass or the\n\nGrass Family is so important is because it provided and still provides the structure of our lives and civilization.", ""),
        StaticBook("Observations", "§bTa'Kayla", "During my travels into The Darkbloom Forest i encountered many a creature and plant life. Some of these creatures were Wolves, Owls, Bullet Ants, Snakes, and a  few Leaf Apes. I also sadly came upon the dead corpse of a Ker Wolf. How it met its fate i\n\ncan't say. However i also say a variety of plants around, however i only wrote down about three of them as i didn't wan't to take up more space than needed.§0\n§0\n§0Blistering Toad: One of the first fungi i encountered, the Blistering Toad\n\nlooks Gnarled, Orange, adn Red, they appear to grow on flalen or rotten logs, and i have deemed them to be not same to eat and toxic. Possibly good for assassination attempts. §0\n§0Danger Level: MEDIUM HIGH§0\n§0\n§0Blissfoil: The second that i encountered\n\nwas the Blissfoil. Blissfoil gather together in patches and are blue in color. After deeming the color as not having it be good for you i decided not to taste it but to smell it, upon touching it my fingers became slightly numb, possibly good for docters\n\nfor pain relief.§0\n§0\n§0the final plant that i have written down, but i still remembered about a few more even if i didn't write it down. The final plant is known as Hafling's Grass.§0\n§0\n§0Halfling's Grass: Very short grass around the size of said\n\nHafling or possibly dwarf. Upon coming into contact with the grass i had a calming feeling came over me. Possibly good for docters, same reason as the Blissfoil.§0\n§0\n§0Danger Level: LOW§0\n§0\n§0\n§0Edit: Blissfoil Danger Level: LOW", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature's Poem", "§bTa'Kayla", "~The Sagehand Grove~§0\n§0\n§0 Oh mighty Cernunnos!§0\n§0 Oh fair Cerridwen!§0\n§0\n§0 Blessed be us whom dwell within your sacred roots!§0\n§0\n§0 Wether it be trodden path or midnights gaze the song of life flows within our blessed veins!\n\n Sun shines and flowers bloom a distant call is heard from you, good health to my breast and freedom through the air, I know that you bring me to you!§0\n§0\n§0 Hidden away to sacred grounds mighty cloven hoofs shall fill thou halls.\n\n Piercing calls and broken soul i can only carry on. Green flesh yet brown blood i clutch your swift song. Young Malin they say learnt from your gaze, shall i not also sway?§0\n§0\n§0 Pine or Oak i swiftly mistook and have fallen around, scared yet safe, with\n\ndanger abroad i wonder?§0\n§0\n§0 Oh dreaded Cernunnos! Oh forgiving Cerridwen!§0\n§0\n§0 Please do not scold me so!§0\n§0\n§0 Ash and smoke drown out your cries as steel upon steel fades. No more shall i\n\nwalk under your watchful gaze for i cannot bare it! The birds shall not sing and the deer never show. Where is the day i live?§0\n§0\n§0 Oh please, oh please i understand my mistake! Worry not for i will live another day, no life yet a breathing soul i keep my\n\nsacred vow. §0\n§0\n§0 Falling tears and bountiful death i understand now. Your calls for caution were misunderstood for that of joy, and here i lay now. Broken as a shade of green my blessed veil falls. §0\n§0\n§0 Oh Cernunnos! Oh Cerridwen! I herby\n\nfall. Understand why i have fallen and take me to your next soul.", ""),
        StaticBook("Jarrod's Script", "§bKass", "Jarrod and Kass§0\n§0\n§0\n§0Act I§0\n§0\n§0K: Why hello Hannah. I told you not to doubt what I'd do.§0\n§0\n§0J: Hello, Mother.§0\n§0(Stands with his head tilted forwards, eyes staring, unblinking at hannah)\n\n(Hannah's reaction, blah, blah, blah)§0\n§0\n§0K: Soulless revenge is something than embodies so many things. This perhaps was one of them.§0\n§0\n§0J: Why didn't you just listen mother?§0\n§0\n\nK: Isn't it wonderful Hannah?! This IS what you waited for, Isn't it?!§0\n§0\n§0J: (Remains silent, his gaze still fixated on Hannah)§0\n§0\n§0(Hannah reacts again, yada yada yada)\n\nNow here's the fun part! Free Play! Say or do anything corresponding with the show!", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "The Druidic Code of Conduct§0\n§0\n§0Originally written by Dragon Druid Apollan§0\n§0\n§0Revised and restored by Tortoise Druid Muildir and Hare Druid Taynuel§0\n§0\n§019th Snow's Maiden, 1638\n\nRule 1) Respect and love your brothers and sisters, no matter their transgressions, with the exception of druids who bring harm and danger to their kin.§0\n§0\n§0Such druids forsake their brothers and sisters and deserve no such care.\n\nRule 2) Defend your brothers and sisters in times of danger as you would defend yourself, no matter their color or creed.§0\n§0\n§0We are all children of the Aspects, and as such we should be ready to take up arms for our family.\n\nRule 3) No matter the consequences, a druid must always speak the truth.§0\n§0\n§0When we are caught in a lie, trust is lost, and with it respect. Without each, we cannot hope to grow strong as a family.\n\nRule 4) A druid must never show favoritism with any one group.§0\n§0\n§0The balance is blind to the squabbles of the descendants, and to serve it best we must be accepting of all that need our help.\n\nRule 5) A druid may act as an advisor, but a druid at the head of a nation is destined for split loyalties and favoritism.§0\n§0\n§0Such can never be permitted without repercussion.\n\nRule 6) In the event that a major conflict arises between druids that cannot be solved by the involved parties, the event should be brought before a neutral archdruid or hierophant to aid in solving the dilemma.\n\nRule 7a) Only a guide or an archdruid of an affected circle may make decisions that affect that circle.§0\n§0\n§0Any druid below that rank or from a separate circle has no authority over that circle.\n\nRule 7b) In regards to decisions that affect the Order as a whole, a meeting must be called between the archdruids and guides of all circles to make a decision.§0\n§0\n§0One neutral party, a Grand Druid, must be present to aid in mitigation.\n\nRule 7c) In the event of an emergency where no archdruids or guides of any circle are available, a hierophant may be called upon for a split-second decision.§0\n§0\n§0This may ONLY occur if no archdruid or guide is available.\n\nRule 7d) In the event that a hierophant's decision is found to be damaging to a circle or the Order as a whole, it may be revoked by an archdruid.\n\nRule 8) A druid must never use overly critical or insulting language when referring to or speaking to their brothers and sisters.§0\n§0\n§0I.E., a druid can say their ACTIONS are stupid, but it is not okay to call the druid stupid.\n\nRule 9) When criticizing a fellow druid for their actions, avoid being overly harsh.§0\n§0\n§0We are all only descendants, and we are all very capable of making mistakes. Until we stop breathing, however, they are not irredeemable.\n\nRule 10) A druid should never take from their brothers and sisters without permission.§0\n§0\n§0Stealing damages the bonds of trust required for strong, healthy relationships between druids to form.\n\nRule 11) A druid should never intentionally cause damage to any property of another druid or the Order.\n\nRule 12) A druid must carry themself in a mature, respectable manner at all times.§0\n§0\n§0Remember that you represent the entire Order in all interactions. Your actions reflect on druids as a whole.\n\nRule 13) A druid must never boast of their achievements or their strength, nor should they ever seek power for power's sake.§0\n§0\n§0Absolute power corrupts absolutely.\n\nRule 14) A druid must never use their gifts in such a way as to show off around their brothers and sisters. The gifts must be used to defend and restore balance as is their original intention.\n\nRule 15) When an archdruid makes a decision, all druids of a circle should do all in their power to heed the archdruid's word.§0\n§0\n§0Have faith in the judgement of the archdruids you have chosen.\n\nRule 16) If a guide or archdruid feels that a decision made by an archdruid was not in the best interest of their circle, they may challenge it if at least one third of the active population of the circle is in agreement. In such a case, a neutral elder\n\nshould be called forth to help resolve the matter.§0\n§0\n§0If a neutral elder is unavailable, a neutral archdruid may be substituted.", ""),
        StaticBook("Kass' Script", "§bKass", "Jarrod and Kass§0\n§0\n§0Act I§0\n§0\n§0K: why hello hannah. I told you not to doubt what I'd do.§0\n§0\n§0J: Why hello mother.§0\n§0\n§0K: Soulless Revenge embodies so many things. This perhaps was one of them.\n\nJ: why didn't you just listen mother?§0\n§0\n§0K: Isn't it wonderful Hannah?! This IS what you were waiting for, wasn't it? (Common, two-toned laugh)§0\n§0\n§0(Hannah reacts again, yada yada yada)§0\n\nNow the fun part! Free Play! say or do anything to correspond with the show!", ""),
        StaticBook("Ceru I", "§bMarch Ash", "It is visceral. I smell the dry earth, the rind of the roots, the bitter air of tangled moss. The shade is cool. But then I exhale, and I am pulled back. I think I would be just as happy were I born a tidal wave or a breeze.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "[!]§0\n§0A note! The words are a scribbly mess; they are scored deeply as though the writing implement had been pressed hard against the paper in concentration.§0\n§0\n§0\"Allo Velvet!§0\n§0Thank you for the bear! I'll look after him!\n\nHoping we can have that cake and biscuits soon!§0\n§0\n§0Hyacinth\"§0\n§0\n§0\n§0((Sorry I haven't been on much, got dem real life troubles. Add me on Discord: #Leonie4343 if you wanna!))", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Athin§0\n§0Frost vine§0\n§0Crouching feather§0\n§0Tippens root§0\n§0Alabaster leaf\n\nMandragora§0\n§0Serpents stalk§0\n§0Dwarfs pumpkin§0\n§0Drakes tail§0\n§0Flametongue root§0\n§0Crimson vase§0\n§0Crouching feather\n\nHalflings grass§0\n§0Larihei's fingers§0\n§0Elfs hair vine§0\n§0Crouching feather\n\nDwarfs pumpkin§0\n§0Goblins ivy§0\n§0Clutchers straw\n\nBlood lotus§0", ""),
        StaticBook("The Trib. of War", "§bAndria Ith'ael", "The Tribulation of War\n\n      Authored by\n     Andria Ith'ael\n\n      Dedicated to\n      Luna Ith'ael\n  Who did not live to\n  see the end of this\n       tri\n\n      Prologue\n This account tells the tale of the two major events which occurred within the time of author’s service as the Maheral of the Mali’aheral. Such events were known as the following: the securing of the Aldersberg Refuge and the Oren\n\nCivil War which granted independence to Renatus (and Aesterwald.) It is not just for the sake of recording that the author writes such, as it is hoped that this account will prevent further incidents from occurring again.\n\n     Chapter I\n  The betrayal of the Mali’aheral by the Mali’ame, more specifically: Phaedrus and Elrona Lle’hileia, was the final event of the steadily growing discontent of the lesser Elves in Annil’sul. All but one of the nations in the world\n\nsupported Phaedrus Lle'hielia - the rebelion leader. \n\n  It was on the mountains aside of the town of Aldersberg where a meeting that conceived the plans for shelter in such a dreadful time took place. It consisted of approximately five\n\nMali'aheral - alongside a Valah by the name of Ser Gawain Briarwood. One Mali’aheral by the name of Thurdan Orathon, who had served previously as Okarir’tir, and his persistence in such was both amusing and counter-productive. \n\n  In the next Elven\n\nday, I found myself alongside the same Mali'aheral in the court of Emperor Tuvya Carrion. Knowing of Thurdan’s plans, I tried to convince him via a polite and quiet conversation hat we should ask for shelter in these dark days. My offer was refused quite\n\nobstinately by the former Okarir'tir, who foolishly went ahead and asked the Emperor regarding his own plan. \n Alas, Thurdan’s efforts were in vain; the Emperor rejected his offer, citing prior crimes against House Carrion by the Mali’aheral,\n\nwhich essentially was the Mali’aheral support for the Chivay take over in the Fringe. Disappointed, Thurdan stormed from the palace without another word, which was a rather a rude gesture.\n Due to this, I stepped forward, and proceeded to ask for what\n\nbelieved was ayla for the Mali'aheral: a refuge. The response I received was much better than those earlier, and the Emperor Tuvya offered us shelter in Petrus and the option to ask Duke Olivier de Savoie of the town of Aldersberg. However, I do not\n\nrecall if such an offer was made by the town of Karovia. \n It was a half or day or so walk from Petrus to Aldersberg, and there I met with Delonna Aeléyèlsa. He directed me to the Duke Olivier de Savoie and the three of us proceeded to have a meeting i\n\nthe Aldersberg Library. There I offered assistance in war (I had previously said that I would die for Oren in war if it meant my people were well) and many crafts and labours. In exchange I asked for a citadel. In response, I received a chuckle as he saw\n\nmy desperation and proclaimed that those terms were acceptable.\n Foolishly I told him we would not live within Aldersberg and said we would remain on his land, to which he questioned my knowledge of the feudal system, however this comment had no lastin\n\neffects and work soon began once more. The Citadel was soon constructed at a pace not seen before.  For my efforts, Delonna Aeléyèlsa bestowed the title of Maheral upon me. Despite our kind’s new found peace, one event would linger in my mind for many\n\nyears to come. My spouse, Janos Ith’ael soon arrived in Aldersberg and came to speak with me. He displayed his intentions to join the Marked Men, to which I refused on the grounds of him already having a child and a wife. Reluctantly he agreed and I soon\n\ntold him to ask the leaders of the Marked Men about it. However I heard no word from Janos again until long after the events of this book, I had almost thought he died up until very recently.\n\n     Chapter II\n In the year of 1480, my daughter, Luna Ith’ael went to live a few miles walk from Aldersberg in the fortress of Kaer Angren with a family friend of mine, Kalameet Izalith. The fortress was used by the Order of the Red Dragon,\n\nand before them, the Order of the Flaming Rose. I had overheard her talking with one by the name of Martial Solatius about the book of Creation and when I arrived in the fortress, I spoke to her regarding such. I warned her of the dangers of believing in\n\na deity and how it prevents enlightenment. She protested though soon listened to my guidance. I did not speak with Martial regarding such (though I did with Kalameet) as he gave me a tour of the fortress.\n\nI also gave him advice on leadership, and he told me about how Great Ser Maric Varodyr (the commander of the Order) would later receive what he deserved. I took little note of such a hint. \n Alas, it all soon became clear within the next month as the\n\nOrder of the Red Dragon soon declared itself independent of the Carrion dominated Holy Oren Empire. It became my objective to try and remove my daughter from the fortress as Aldersberg took no side in the conflict. My efforts proved useless as I came\n\nacross the broken bridge which lead to the gate of Kaer Angren.\n\n    Chapter III\n As the nations were choosing their sides; it soon became known that I would have to choose one as well. The State of Laureh'lin changed sides thrice\n\nthroughout the war, though as I went to a meeting with the now King Maric Varodyr, they were with Renatus and Kaer Angren. Delonna Aeléyèlsa accompanied myself, for he was more literate in ways of Valah politics.\n The meeting was a sucess and the two\n\nof us pledged to support him; an exception remained with the bringing of soldiers, we did not do such due to the neutrality of Aldersberg.\n King Maric also promised to sign another concordat, however with a different name, for\n\nthe Crimson-Silver Concordat has long passed.\n Raids and skirmishes soon were fought outside Kaer Angren, and I was caught in at least one. I remember seeing one of my own kind, named Braxus Ni’leya fight with the Carrions; I was rather disappointed in\n\nand proceeded to speak with the Tilruir’tir, Asul’athri Parir’taliyu regarding such. From then on the war seemed to be closer to home.\n  House Carrion and its supporters then moved forth from Petrus to try and take Kaer Angren by force. As the day of t\n\nbattle approached, I was steadfast that none of my kin would fight in such a battle. This was to the dismay of two of my kind: Kalameet Izalith, who worked closely with Maric and Martial during the war, and Braxus Ne’leya, who asked for soldiers to\n\nreinforce the House Carrion supporters. I also told my daughter to not remain in the fortress, and return to the Citadel.\n  Braxus then told me that House Carrion wished to speak with me and I agreed to come to such. I left a note in my inn room which\n\nstated that if I did not return from such an endeavour, I had been executed by the Carrions and the Sillumir were to fight with Renatus and their allies. Hence forth I made the trek to Petrus, and met with Braxus and Viktor Carrion. The lattter guided me\n\ninto the palace in Petrus, where the two of us held a meeting.\n  Viktor tried to convince me that the Carrions had done ayla by my people, and to such I agreed. Though I adamantly said that I would remain neutral - which deceived them of my true plans.\n\nThe one clad in Carbarum and Iron armour was also steadfast that Olivier de Savoie had supported the Carrion regime, which was rather untrue.\n\n    Chapter IV\n The battle ended with a victory for King Maric and it soon resulted in the collapse of the the Holy Oren Empire. No Sillumrian fought in the battle from my memory,  though in hindsight, such would have been better to have supported\n\nRenatus. However an opportunity to make a formal alliance with the new born nation was soon permitted after a lengthy discussion between Olivier de Savoie, Kalameet Izalith, Kalenz Uradir and myself. \n   Coincidently, Gawain Briarwood (whose alias was\n\nMartial Solatius) soon arrived at the gate of the Citadel asking if we wished to ally with them. Without hesitation I agreed, though no Concordat was signed. I consider such to be my greatest decision in my service as Maheral.\n Months later, after peac\n\nhad once again befallen the lands of a now divided Oren, I made another trek to the fortress of Kaer Angren with one by the name of Avern’dionne Adriane. The beginnings of the conquest of Laureh’lin soon approached. The tribulation of the Mali'aheral was\n\ndestined to end soon.\n\n     Chapter V\n It is known that a story has no merit without a moral; however, there is not one of such in this story, for there is multiple concepts presented in it.  It can simply be described as the following: the one\n\nwho takes initiative is the one who succeeds; progress and health can also involve sacrificing one’s plans for the betterment of our kind; and two societies cannot exist with one ruling the other, they must be equal or integrated - both of which are\n\nimpossible. \n If one remembers such, then they will be able to prosper in an age of constant war unlike any other. The world is far more turbulent, and alliances change fast. No treaty stands forever and our kind must be weary of such for only two peop\n\nhave kept their treaties like tradition: the Heartlander Valah and the Kharajyr. At any moment, another tribulation may begin and our kind must be weary.\n\naymaehr'sae hiylun'ehya\n~Andria Ith'ael", ""),
        StaticBook("Ceru III", "§bMarch Ash", "On the mountaintop, the stone beneath my feet, I see kilometers of frozen wastes all around me. The wind bites at my skin. The cold helps me to concentrate on my mission. To change the descendants, or destroy them utterly. It is harsh.\n\nBut, they have the choice to die, and that is mercy.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "In this tome, I shall be detailing my accounts as I delve into the ruins beneath Kal'Omith.§0\n§0=================§0\n§0\n§0 KAL'NIKAER:§0\n§0The ruins of olde Kal'Omith. §0\n§0  §0\n§0      By Agamm Ireheart\n\nDetailing back to the olden days, I gave my accounts on the other ruins within the deep roads, but it seems that of recent events, a new ruin was discovered. The city? Kal'Nikaer, which was discovered by several dwarves, and they reported in waves of  §0\n§0§\n\nundead coming from the ruins. They have taken care of the hordes, but believe there are more to be seen. The Doomforged have been looking at the ruins more than anyone. Thond Doomforged, is one of the original dwarves to have discovered Kal'Nikaer.\n\nThond sees the ruins as a place fitted for testing Dwed strength, even possibly re-using the deep roads.", ""),
        StaticBook("Dwed Language I", "§bAgamm \"Ruins-Delver\" Ireheart", "THE LANGUAGE §0\n§0           OF THE§0\n§0             DWED§0\n§0 §0\n§0\n§0    By Agamm Ireheart§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0      Research from§0\n§0 Tyrras var Emrys\n\nI myself speak the tongue of the dwed, but, I use Tyrras's way order as a way to format the Lexicon.§0\n§0Enjoy the book, and I hope you can use it well.\n\n           RACES§0\n§0\n§0Dwarves- Dwed/Dwedmar, Khazad/Khazadmar§0\n§0Elves-§0\n§0Elger/Elgus§0\n§0Orcs-§0\n§0Ork/Orkos§0\n§0Humans-§0\n§0Umri/Umros§0\n§0Goblins-§0\n§0Grobbi/Grobbins\n\nForeign/Foreigners-§0\n§0Yrrok/Yrrommar\n\n             A§0\n§0A(singular)- E§0\n§0Above-Morred§0\n§0Adjective Suffix(used only for clans and on people, a possessive  suffix or prefix can be used as well.)-Mos/Os/Rum§0\n§0Advisor-Thelur§0\n§0Again/Another-Narhe§0\n§0Age-Kez§0\n§0Air-Kaas\n\nAlchemy-Gauld§0\n§0Alcohol-Graz§0\n§0Alcohol = Naztrak§0\n§0Ale = Beoir§0\n§0All (This tem is tricky. Explained slightly more in the special term division) = Khrum§0\n§0Ally / Allies = Karrim / Karrimar§0\n§0Also = Nar’os§0\n§0Ancestor = Keznol§0\n§0Ancient = Karik / Hraaken / Den\n\nAnd = Nar / Na§0\n§0Anger = Krav§0\n§0Anguish = Azmoroth§0\n§0Anvil = Kadrin / Kazdorin§0\n§0Any = Vos§0\n§0Anyway = Vosirk§0\n§0Arch (Leadership Position) = Ard- / Nak-§0\n§0Are = Ath§0\n§0Arena = Kahros§0\n§0Armor = Klad§0\n§0Arrival = Karnes\n\nAttack = Kahr§0\n§0Axe (High uses the same word, but used in different instances, each word differentiates from itself.) = Az / Kathadun\n\n           B§0\n§0Back (Refers to the anatomical “Back”, which comprises all muscles located in the fore body, which the person cannot see.) = Fraed§0\n§0Bad (Synonym to evil) = Vad / Vaador§0\n§0Bag (Sack, a mobile “Chest” ) = Alaek§0\n§0Balance = Unn\n\nBane = Alar / Noril§0\n§0Bar (Tavern) = Beorim§0\n§0Battle = Krazdran§0\n§0Be (If used with adjective forms “Been”) = Mer§0\n§0Bear = Hefjhor§0\n§0Beard = Trumm§0\n§0Beast (Suffix, must have a substantive before it) = -Dharok§0\n\nBeast (Suffix, must have a substantive before it) = -Dharok§0\n§0Before (Used to refer to a long past) / (Verb (needs suffix) ) = Den Kezzan / Dekan§0\n§0Below = Yarred§0\n§0Betray = Ogdaros§0\n§0Better (Good + Potential) = Thorokian§0\n§0Birth = Threin§0\n§0Black = Khorok / Vuur\n\nBlackaxe = Kharadunvuur§0\n§0Blaze = Nook§0\n§0Blood = Ardol§0\n§0Boat (Syn. to Ship) = Wathol§0\n§0Boiler (Synonym to furnace) = Nokri§0\n§0Book (Usually used to define lore of olden Dwarves. synonym to Scrolls and Tomes) = Kron§0\n§0Bourbon = Galraz\n\nBow (Action to kneel before a king or superior) = Nelaraz§0\n§0Bow (Item / Weapon) = Thrimmek§0\n§0Bravery (Syn. to courage) = Parathak§0\n§0Brawl (Usually used to depict a fight out of trivialities, Friendly fight.) = Nazaig§0\n§0Breach = Kodeh§0\n§0Break = Nazka§0\n§0Bring = Nighi\n\nBroad (Synonym to wide, can work as an adjective, if preceded by a possesive.) = Nazad§0\n§0Brothel = Belkar§0\n§0Brother (Can be used to refer a clan member) = Kronul§0\n§0Brow (Eyebrow) = Trullki§0\n§0Bull = Demm§0\n§0Burn = Karaave§0\n\nBurrow / Hole / Privy = Undor / Undo§0\n§0But = Ur§0\n\n            C§0\n§0Can = Ovar§0\n§0Cannon = Ithring§0\n§0Carcass (Corpse) = Naktrul§0\n§0Carve = Thruz§0\n§0Cave = Umgor / Ulnafer / Skulder§0\n§0Cave's Folk = Umgorumm§0\n§0Chair = Nakhimir§0\n§0Champion = Baladan / Az'adar\n\nChancellor = Darmag§0\n§0Charge = Velannak (Vel'Annak)§0\n§0Chicken (Animal) = Pul§0\n§0City = Kal’ (Used as a prefix.)§0\n§0Civilization (Usually used as a prefix for an empire) = Onor§0\n§0Clan = Kazamar§0\n§0Clan suffix (Used when speaking of a person who belongs to a clan) = -rumm\n\nClose = Nerak§0\n§0Cloud = Norkai§0\n§0Cluster (Used to determine a group of plants, animals, or Orkos.) = Nozagar§0\n§0Collar (Necklace) = Nahibelk§0\n§0Comfort = Salez§0\n§0Command = Velerak§0\n§0Common = Omur\n\nContinent prefix (Can be used for defining the term “World”, also stands for a massive landscape, or something that has massive ammounts of.) = Khor’§0\n§0Core = Derkon§0\n§0Council = Nozkron§0\n§0Cow = Keed§0\n§0Create (Syn. to Make) = Yemka\n\nCrown = Rulkahd§0\n§0Crystal = Krest\n\n             D§0\n§0Daughter = Bedwan§0\n§0Day = Lakh§0\n§0Death = Kavir / Kraviil§0\n§0Deep = Nerroth§0\n§0Defend = Verdig / Kerli§0\n§0Diminutive Suffix = -ki /-eki§0\n§0Discomfort (Synonym to anything that may seem hard or uneasy for a Dwarf.) = Krovehni\n\nDo = Kav§0\n§0Doom (Synonym to Chaos and Corruption) = Dor§0\n§0Doomforge = Dorkadrel§0\n§0Doomseeker = Dorgrond§0\n§0Door/Doorway/Gate/Entrance = Edhekal§0\n§0Dragon = Drakna / Drakmar§0\n§0Drink = Drinkir\n\nDwarfling(Child Dwarf)= Dwedki§0\n\n            E§0\n§0East = Kahae§0\n§0Empire = Oramar§0\n§0Enemy = Kaznok§0\n§0Energy = Manok§0\n§0Engineer = Klazrel§0\n§0Exile = Tagar\n\n           F§0\n§0\"Fuck\"=Gilthok/Gailuk§0\n§0Fantasy = Akaneh§0\n§0Farewell = Yankar§0\n§0Fast (Swift, Quick) = Vlokon§0\n§0Father = Ka'az §0\n§0Feast = Anbekiamar§0\n§0Feline = Mew§0\n§0Field = Aindar§0\n§0Find / Found = Nadra / Nadraaz§0\n\nFire = Karaad§0\n§0Fish = Wurok§0\n§0Fishing = Kavir wurok§0\n§0Fist (Can be used as a syn. to strength) = Rym§0\n§0Flee = Vervak (Stems from the word Defend)§0\n§0Flesh = Ka- (Used as a prefix) / Kadeek§0\n§0Food = Anbek§0\n\nFor (Reason, if used with a possesive it could be used like “For someone”) = Yoth§0\n§0Forest = Hefruth§0\n§0Forest's Folk = Hefrumm§0\n§0Forge = Kadrel§0\n§0Forgeling (One who works the forge, used ONLY as a sustantive) = Kadros§0\n§0Fort = Kaz'ad§0\n§0Friend = Nak\n\nFriendship = Nakaran§0\n§0Frost = Azwyr§0\n§0Frostbeard = Azwyrtrumm§0\n\n            G§0\n§0Glory = Narvak§0\n§0Go (synonym to “Come”) = Harath§0\n§0Goat = Lann§0\n§0Gold = Aurok§0\n§0Golem = Khoren§0\n§0Gone (Can be used to define dead, too.) = Haritz§0\n§0Good = Thorok§0\n§0Grand = Bak / Kathaik§0\n§0Grandaxe = Kathaikaz\n\nGrape = Anmar§0\n§0Greed = Derkalimin / Dermin§0\n§0Greetings = Kazahar§0\n§0Gross (Displeasing)=Kossog§0\n§0Group = Barel§0\n§0Guard = Akvel§0\n§0Guide = Akhoral§0\n§0Guild = Barimmar§0\n§0Gut (Sometimes used as a slang term for Courage) = Rul\n\n             H§0\n§0Hall = Khaz§0\n§0Hammer = Ord§0\n§0Hand = Anar§0\n§0Have = Zal / Aghar§0\n§0He (Male derogative) = Ka§0\n§0Heart = Amoruk§0\n§0Hello = Oz§0\n§0Help (Aid) = Ghoran§0\n§0Her = Laad§0\n§0Hidden (Used with verb forms Hide) = Rhadav\n\nHigh = Az§0\n§0Him = Kaad§0\n§0Hold = Tal'§0\n§0Holy = Kirkja / Yemarin§0\n§0Honour (Can be used as Pride) = Anart§0\n§0Horn = Ordul§0\n§0How = Ekor§0\n§0However = Ekorien§0\n§0Hunt = Grunga / Grung\n\n            I§0\n§0I = Tha / K§0\n§0If = Kolun  §0\n§0In (Inside, also synonym for “On”) = Nir§0\n§0Industry = Nolvar§0\n§0Inert (Death or limit of objects, such as someting running out of fuel.) = Narrek§0\n§0-Ing = -Uroz / -Geron§0\n§0Into = Nirlak\n\nIreheart = Khronammoruk§0\n§0Iron = Khro / Khron§0\n§0Ironborn = Khrothrein§0\n§0Irongut = Khrorul\n\n            J§0\n§0Journal = Kronotharem§0\n§0Journey = Otharem\n\n             K§0\n§0Kill = Kavok / Kerr§0\n§0King (Literally “High Lord”) = Thrummaz§0\n§0King = Kunnek\n\n            L§0\n§0Lake = Wunder§0\n§0Large / Huge / Big = Ahran§0\n§0Large (Used in Objects, and length.) = Melkan§0\n§0Lava (Syn. Magma) = Boor§0\n§0Leader = Rikkin§0\n§0Leather = Kudelek§0\n§0Left (Direction) = Neeft§0\n§0Legend = Kronos\n\nLegion = Arkammar§0\n§0Less (Suffix) = -Avos §0\n§0Letter (Synonym for Design, and used with potential forms “Word” and then also used with a verb forms “Speech” or “Speak”) = Galat§0\n§0Life = Anym / Konym§0\n§0Light = Varekan§0\n§0Limb = Trullak§0\n§0Lizard = Knut\n\nLord = Thrumm / Rik§0\n§0Lose (Synonim to Lost) = Vroth§0\n§0Love = Nogazen§0\n\n            M§0\n§0Magic = Lordak§0\n§0Maiden (Fe-Dwarf who is un-married) = Lathain / Vilket§0\n§0March = Veloz / Velak§0\n§0Mason Station (Mason Table) = Kaladok vo-Nakhum§0\n§0Master = Kronok§0\n\nMate (To define a person who belongs to the same guild or group of the speaker) = Barithon§0\n§0Mate (To engage in intercourse) = Nogaak§0\n§0Meat (Can be used as a prefix to denote meat of (something))= Ust\n\nMerchantry / Merchant (If used with a person, add an adjective.) = Bergukli§0\n§0Metal = Ithor / Akash§0\n§0Might = Daar§0\n§0Mind = Varak§0\n§0Mine (As in cave.) = Mugdor§0\n§0Mine (Synonym to My) = Thas§0\n§0Money = Minek§0\n§0Mother = Beka§0\n§0Mountain = Agnar\n\nMountain's Folk = Agnarumm§0\n§0Mouth = Waerod§0\n§0Myth = Akkor\n\n            N§0\n§0Name = Daram§0\n§0Nether = Kharvul§0\n§0Night = Uloon§0\n§0No (Negative. Can  used as a suffix.) = Lare / -are / -vare / -fhare§0\n§0North = Nikaer§0\n§0Nothing = Olkodran (Can be used to define Void.)\n\nNumber = Volein (If used with plural forms \"All the Numbers\" or \"Count\" (Not the title.) )\n\n            O§0\n§0Obsidian = Orvul§0\n§0Of (belonging to) = Eron§0\n§0Of (Part of, used as a interim suffix to merge two words (King' of 'dwarves) = -'ur'-§0\n§0Of (sword OF sth) = -ram / -ethos§0\n§0Open = Aarak§0\n§0Or (As in \"Sth OR sth\") = Ov\n\nOrder (Almost never used in common day-to-day, refers to a group of people, like a guild.) = Dverga\n\n             P§0\n§0Pact = Khroven (Blood Pact is Ardol yol-Khroven)§0\n§0Pale = Arakrumm\n\nPart (Of. It means that something that is fragment of something, can be used to denote, for example, the stone needed for stonebrick, or, even so, to resume sentences like “Armor Piece”) = -Trihim / -Ohin.§0\n\nPattern (Refers to all terms of the words, including patterns of behaviour. It can be a synonym to trace.) = Vlazeth§0\n§0Pelt (Synonym for Fur) = Kudelor§0\n§0Pickaxe = Azarak§0\n§0Pig = Kerrem§0\n§0Plural suffix= -mar§0\n§0\n§0Volume two will follow up on the rest.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Deep Roads", "§bAgamm \"Ruins-Delver\" Ireheart", "THE RUINS OF§0\n§0       KAL'OMITH§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0   By Agamm Ireheart§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0==================\n\n  Deep beneath the bustling markets of Kal'Omith lies strange ruins. The Dwarven city lies within mountains, and seem peaceful enough. But alas, there's more than what meets the eye. There are ruins beneath Kal'Omith, and little is known about them. The\n\nonly things known about them are that they're called \"The Deep Roads\" and that they once had goblins living there. Yet, dwarven architecture is visible from what I've seen. I've given names to the small pockets of settlements that I've found. Of course\n\nI've used the term \"city\" but I mean settlement. Here's what I have named:§0\n§0Kal'Kraviil or \"City of the dead\". This ruin has plenty of dwarven remains, all in disturbing piles, as if they were suddenly killed at once, aware of their fates, yet fearing it.\n\nThe second one:§0\n§0Kal'KavirGrobbi, or \"City of the dead goblins\". I've named it that because of the goblin-like structures, and fortifications built. This one had undead and spiders lingering, so I left as soon as a saw enough. It reeked of death as well.\n\nThe third;§0\n§0Kal'Hefruth or \"City of the forest\". I named it so because of the strange vegetation in the area. It was beautiful, peaceful, yet unnerving. This settlement is the most important, as there are two paths from the area that would take one to the\n\nmysterious places.\n\n       KAL'KRAVIIL§0\n§0  Deep, past the halls that linger of decay, lies the abandoned, dead settlement of what I call, Kal'Kraviil. It is here, where the mystery begins. The buildings look familiar to the ones above, but the shadows have taken the area.\n\nThe remains of its past residents scattered everywhere. Many seem to be on the courtyard, meaning death was sudden, and perhaps, evil. It is given that many knew of their deaths, and others, oblivious to it. They were once dwed.§0\n§0It is quite a disturbing\n\nsight. But it is here that my friend found a tome called \"Directions\". The contents of the book begins in Dwarvish, then a section is written in common tongue. It reads the same tale of the king of Urguan, as the runestone by the entrances to the ruins.\n\nUnfortunately, I still have no idea what it means.\n\n   KAL'KAVIRGROBBI§0\n§0  This settlement was hazardous. The stench of rot lingered, like the undead that wandered there. The area seems to be an old goblin-like fort, with tents and wooden walls and fortifications. It seemed too primitive to be of Dwarven\n\nhandiwork. I managed to get past the hordes of undead, and reached the inner part of the fort, but the cave there was sealed off. Not much was located there, and so, I left.\n\n       KAL'HEFRUTH§0\n§0  Here, I found giant glowing mushrooms and tall trees and bushes. Streams flowed as well, and it was quite a sight. Some tents littered the area, but not much happens there. Then there are the two paths from there. At the end of the\n\narea, there is a cave, and inside is the dormant spiders that are mammoth. Then there is a gate, where going through would take you to the \"Lava pits\". I'd like to call it \"Derkon Boor\" or Lava Core, as it seems to be the core of a volcano or something.\n\nThe contents of Lava pits are unnerving, with altars and statues that revers death in the most corrupted ways. I would advise anyone who would venture to the area to be prepared in every way when going to the pit.", ""),
        StaticBook("Directions", "§bAgamm \"Ruins-Delver\" Ireheart", "DIRECTIONS§0\n§0\n§0\n§0         By§0\n§0            Dwedmariin§0\n§0    §0\n§0\n§0    Recreated By:§0\n§0    Agamm Ireheartaw\n\n       THOUGHTS§0\n§0  [Due to the tragic loss of the important artifact, I, Agamm Ireheart, blood of Igor, promise to recreate the tome found in the ruins of what I call Kal'Kraviil. The book was called \"Directions\" and its author, as I remember, was\n\nDwedmariin. This should be taken with a grain of salt, as my memory is faint, as so much has happened from when my colleague and I stumbled into the tome to the moment I write this very book. I hope to recreate as much as possible.]\n\n      DIRECTIONS§0\n§0       Dwedmariin§0\n§0\n§0[The first few lines were written in dwarvish, and is forgotten in time.]§0\n\n[Here, are what I recall from the tome that was understandable]§0\n§0The world was young, the mountains green, No stain on the moon was seen, No words were laid on stream or stone,§0\n§0When Urguan and walked alone. He named the nameless hills dells; He\n\ndrank from yet wells;  He stooped and looked to thr drear, And saw a crown of stars appear, As gems upon silver thread, Above the shadows of his head. The world grey, the mountains old, The forge's fire is ashen-cold; No harp is wrung, No hammer falls;\n\nThe darkness dwells in Halls. The Shadow lies upon his tomb, In Aegis, in Khaz'Urguan.§0\n§0But still the sunken stars appear, In ark and dreadless, hair of year; There lies his crown in children deep, till Urguan wakes again from sleep.\n\n [The \"directions\" given were crptic, but may convey some important meaning, perhaps, the revival of Urguan himself. If so, when? Where? Why?]§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0==================", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=§0\n§0\n§0SONGS OF URGUAN:§0\n§0Dwarven songs and hymns that can be sung lyric book§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0 By Agamm Ireheart\n\n     SONG ONE:§0\n§0      Loch'Omith§0\n§0\n§0\n§0 By yon bonnie banks, and by yon bonnie brae...§0\n§0Deh loights shoine oveh Kal'Omith.§0\n§0Where me and my kinsdwed, shall neveh meet again, by deh sorrowful 'alls of Kal'Omith...\n\n  CHORUS: Fer ye'll take deh 'ighroad and I'll take deh deeproads,§0\n§0I'll meet Urguan befer ye! §0\n§0Twas den when we parted, in yon dark glen, §0\n§0On steep, steep Urguan's Agnar!§0\n§0Where in orange hue we view deh 'ighlands!\n\nAnd deh sun roise from deh earth.§0\n§0Deh tears of 'is sons, shall foreveh be in our 'earts, as dehy part from,§0\n§0Kal'Omith....\n\n         SONG II§0\n§0     ANVIL'S HYMN§0\n§0\n§0 Deep, Deep, beneath deh keeps, we stroike deh anvil loike a drum, hum, hum loike a drum, deh anvil's 'ymn shall be sung...§0\n§0  Fer eons we stood, foightin 'gainst moighteh threats,§0\n§0fer eons we bled, §0\n\nbleedin' out our mortal wounds,§0\n§0Fer eons we dug, dug beneath our 'alls.§0\n§0But we dwed onleh throive fer 'un thin', und dat be gloreh.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Ivan is a bam", ""),
        StaticBook("Flower Language", "§bElorna Avern Lle'hileia", "The Language\n       of Flowers\n\n\nA guide to the secret meaning given to each flower by the wood elves of old.\n\n    \n      In memory of\n      Mayilu Avern\n\n            ~*~\n\nWhen I was a girl I remember my mother walking with me through fields of flowers, pointing to each variety and describing what they symbolised. I do not know how commonly used this secret language was, but I will always remember the solemnity of my\n\nmother's voice as she told me the meaning behind each poppy and rose. Of course, the meanings given in this book do not describe the actual traits of each flower, simply the cultural meanings they are used to represent. One does not have to use this guide\n\nbut I hope it may inspire you while selecting a bouquet for a lover, picking a gift for a friend, or simply decorating your home and garden with life and color.\n\n\nThe Dove Druid,\nElorna Avern Lle'hileia.\n\n     The Dandelion\nThis simple flower is sometimes considered a weed, yet even that which is small and unassuming can hold hidden significance. \n\nMeanings: Fortitude, Constance, Faith, Wishes Coming True.\n\n        The Poppy\nThis deep red flower is often used medicinally to relieve pain or aid in sleep. It is often associated with both dreamers and the dead.\n\nMeanings: Dreams, Imagination, Mourning, Eternal Sleep, Relief from Suffering.\n\n       The Orchid\nThe orchid is as elegant as it is exotic, and its blue coloring is rare in the natural world.\n\nMeanings: Beauty, Refinement, Elegance, Fragility, Femininity, Rare Riches.\n\n       The Allium\nThese are a very unique looking flower, each one made up of thousands of tiny purple blossoms that form into a large, perfect sphere.\n\nMeanings: Perfection, Royalty, Many Children, a Prosperous Family.\n\n    The Azure Bluet\nSometimes called Eyebrights, they are a pale blue that often appears white. They are sometimes considered a symbol for the Druidic goddess Cerridwen.\n\nMeanings: Fertility, Motherhood, Peace, Cleanliness.\n\n      Oxeye Daisy\nWhen you see these daisies - actually chrysanthemums - in bloom it is always a sure sign of summer.\n\nMeanings: Innocence, Simplicity, Modesty, Shyness, Youth, Summer.\n\n        The Lilac\nLilacs are clusters of tiny purple blossoms that grow on leafy green bushes, and are quite popular for their aromatic scents.\n\nMeanings: New Love, Sweetness, Affection, Flirtatiousness, Spring.\n\n     The Sunflower\nThe sunflower is said to always point toward the sun. We could all learn something from the sunflower about optimism and looking on the bright side!\n\nMeanings: Hapiness, Sunshine, Good Luck, Good News, Friendship, Cheer.\n\n        The Peony\nThese flowers come in soft, pastel hues, their blossoms made up of many layers of petals. They are often associated with the wealthy and high-born.\n\nMeanings: Riches, Honor, Pride, a Carefree Life, Greed, Extravagance.\n\n       The Tulip\nTulips come in many varieties and can mean different things depending on color.\n\nGeneral Meaning: Fame.\nRed: Declaration of Love.\nOrange: Hope, Bright Smiles.\nPink: Purity of Love\nWhite: Honesty, Sorrow.\n\n        The Rose\nThe rose is one of the most iconic flowers of romance and love. Depending on how the rose is presented, it can have many meanings.\n\nFull Rose: True Love, Sincerity, Respect.\nRosebud: Innocent Love, a Pure Heart.\n\n        The Fern\nThe fern is a simple, green plant that bears no bright colors nor scented blossoms. Its reserved simplicity is used as a contrast to dazzling flowers.\n\nMeanings: Old Age, Wisdom, Mystical Knowledge, Sages.\n\n     The Ivy Vine\nIvy is another green plant that, while not a flower, holds significance in the language of flowers. \n\nMeanings: Wedded Love, Fidelity, Faithfulness, Loyalty, Eternal Ties.\n\n     The Mushroom\nMushrooms are not often used in bouquets or arrangements, yet they hold strong symbolism, especially in Druidic circles.\n\nMeanings: Magic, Prophecy, Visions, Virility, Masculinity, Long Life, Spiritual Journeys.\n\n        The Leaf\nThe symbolism and meaning behind each tree would take another book itself, but here are a few meanings associated with tree leaves:\n\nOak: Strength, Nobility, Virtue.\nBirch: Healing, Rebirth, Spirits of the Dead.\nPine: Ancient Wisdom.\n\nAnd so concludes this book, may it broaden your understanding of the natural world and its many hidden intricacies. May your life be long and happy, and may the Aspects guide your path.\n\n\n           ~*~", ""),
        StaticBook("Arc. Magci Wiki", "§bKypris Solaria", "Arcane Magic\n-------------------Arcane magic is the most widely spread magic throughout the realms.  It is a type of magic that does not require more than study and repetition,and so well known that many books are sold and written about it,\n\nthough of course, some types are kept secret from the masses. A mage that uses arcane magic draws his powers from the void, a realm of thoughts and emptiness, which he must first learn how to connect to.\n\nThe Void, Mana and Auras\n------------------- The Void: Arcane Magic is the archtype of magic which is fueled by The Void, or more specifically mana.  The Void is an empty plane of existence where things may be created\n\nwith the use of ones imagination and willpower.Many teachers of the Arcane often teach students with the following proverb: \"The Void is nothing, yet everything. Like our imagination, it may be dull and empty; or it can be full and colorful\".\n\nThe Void as a whole is simply a plane where nothing exists, unless the mage makes it exist. Since one must connect to the Void, this means a basic line of communication must be established, generally gained through meditation and practice.\n\nSince Void users must be connect to draw on the Void, distractions are capable of severing the connection for Novices,while Masters are capable of holding onto their connects until they are drained of mana,\n\nor wounded. It should be noted that only mages of proper age can connect to the Void.Those under the age of 16 can receive mental damage if they connect to the Void, and so those of the age of 16 and above can begin to practice magic.\n\n- Mana: When a mage is connected to the Void, he/she may imagine things in this Void; however, a mage must use mana to either draw this spell into the world, or apply it to someone/something.\n\nMana is sightless and is as invisible as the air in our world (See Auras and Arcane Evocation for more details on this). It flows through our world like streams, and is in every living thing, whether it is plants or animals.\n\nAlthough mana comes at a cost, since it flows through people, Void users have gained the ability to use this mana to draw from the Void. Void users often get fatigued when over using mana\n\nand have in rare cases died due to the use of large amounts of mana. Another draw-back with the use of mana is the effect (or lack thereof) on our mortal planewhen you draw on the Void for magics such as Evocations,\n\nthis element which has been evoked must eventually return to the Void.However this can become complicated with such magics as Alteration, as it has the ability to prolong the existence of Void based elements in our world.\n\n- Mana Pools: Mana pools are how much mana we have in our person, and this determines how grand our spells can be, along with the duration a spell can be held in our world. Mana pools are governed by two things;\n\nSkill level and Magic type. Skill level is simply how skilled you are at manipulating mana for your specific Sub-type. For example; the longer you train in the use of Evocations,the better and more efficient you are at manipulating mana for that sub-type.\n\nMagic type is a little more complicated, different magic Sub-types are naturally more mana consuming then others, a few examples of very draining Sub-types are as followed: Alteration, Arcane Evocation and Conjuration.\n\nAlthough far more difficult to learn if one of these are your first Sub-type, you will naturally get a bigger mana pool then someone who's first Sub-type was Illusion, or perhaps an Evocation.\n\n- Auras: Auras are a side-effect from the Void, and is deeply personal to mage's. An Aura directly reflects a mages personality through it's color, which will be listed below this section\n\nAura is generated from the mana pool regularly, and if a mage uses large amounts of mana at once for spells, they in turn produce more aura. Imagine a fire; the more wood you put on it\n\nthe more smoke is formed. Much like this fire, the mana pool can generate more aura depending on how much mana you're using at a given time. Auras are generally harmless and are simply a way of self expression.\n\nSome magics such as Arcane Evocation, Alteration, and Shade are directly tied to Auras, and are heavily effected by them. Auras are able to be openly role-played without being Lore breaking, so feel free to get interesting with it.\n\nBlue: Calculatedness and logicality (Sometimes emotional coldness) \n\nPurple: Mysteriousness\n\nWhite: Benevolence, order and innocence.\n\nBrown: Nurturing and caring\n\nYellow: Greed (Sometimes optimism)\n\nRed: Dominance, power and control (Sometimes tyrannical natures)\n\nGreen: Harmony and balance (Sometimes vanity).\n\nBlack: Chaos and malevolence.\n\nSub-types of Arcane Magic\n------------------- Arcane is the Arch-type of all the following Sub-types, these are also known as \"roots\".\n\n- Alteration:\n\nAlteration is manipulation of properties in the physical world.\n\nTransfiguration [Includes enchanting, transmutation and warding/abjuration]\nTelekinesis [The manipulation and lifting of objects]\nVoid Translocation [Dematerialising matter in the Void for later use]\n\n- Evocation:\nEvocation is the construction of artificial elements or organic tissues.\nElemental Evocation\nArcanism\nConjuration\n\n- Illusion:\nIllusion is the manipulation of emotions, and the five senses, such as sight, smell, sound, touch and taste.\n\nSensory Illusion [A form of illusion in which the mage attempts to influence the mind to fabricate senses and emotions.]\nCognitism [A specific form of Illusion that uses visual aids and mental manipulation to increase intelligence and imagination.]\n\nMental Magic [Involves accessing the minds of individuals to read thoughts and make illusions.]", ""),
        StaticBook("Intro to Alchemy", "VonEbs", "An Introduction to the Alchemist\nForward:\nGreetings, my dear reader. Within the pages of this tome I shall be explaining to you what it takes to be a true Alchemist. I shall run you through our mindset, qualities and traditions.\n\nChapter I: The Recipe for a Good Alchemist.\nIt takes a special breed of person to be an Alchemist; a rare combination of patience, creativity, intellect, enthusiasm, and of course, an unusual resistance to explosions. For those who wish to follow\n\nthe noble path of an Alchemist, you must first consider if you possess these key traits, for if you do not, much like a potion made with a flawed recipe, you will be ineffective.\nDo not mistake what I say for supremacism, but rather realize\n\nthat it is simply an effort to save you. If you do not possess these qualities, dear reader it is quite possible that you, in the attempt to follow our path, may indeed end up harming yourself and others. Now, allow me to go more in-depth about what\n\nthese traits mean, in order of their importance to an Alchemist: \nPatience. Alchemy, I’m afraid, is not a fast nor particularly interesting process. An Alchemist will spend most of their time for their elembic to boil so that the extract of\n\nyour ingredients may be collected for use, or simply waiting for a potion to brew. Very little of the time is takes to create a potion is actually spent creating the mixture, although this time is indeed the most interesting part of Alchemy in my\n\nopinion. Because of all this, dear reader, Patience is vital to an Alchemist, as it would be, in my opinion, apt to give Alchemy the formula consisting of 25 “parts”:\n7 parts waiting(brewing, waiting for the elembic’s water to boil.)\n5 parts setting\n\nup, cleaning up, and inspecting your equipment.\n5 parts testing(making sure your potion works.)\n3 parts failure (realizing that your recipe is bollocks and starting over.)\n3 parts extracting (boiling down the ingredients and\n\ncollecting the condensation using an alembic.)\n1 parts explosion (surprisingly common.)\n1 parts mixing the bloody potion.\nIntellect. You may be wondering why this is not the first on my list... Well, someone whom is completely\n\ndull, yet is willing to give up hours upon hours to make up for it, is much more likely to succeed than someone brilliant who will become frustrated and rushed the second they see that their brew will be taking a very long time to finish.\n\nSo, while this trait is near-vital, it can be made up for by a massive amount of patience and dedication.\nCreativity. Please note, that I am writing here the explain what it takes to be an Alchemist, not a good Alchemist. Creativity is something\n\ncompletely vital to anyone who wishes to be a master alchemist... but it is not to those whom only wish to dabble in the art. Creativity is essential for those who wish to truly excel at the art, because one cannot just follow recipes forever. There\n\ncomes a time, when an apprentice Alchemist must take these matters into their own hands. No more books, no more explanations as to why these particular ingredients must be used... there comes a time, when every Alchemist must learn to rely on their\n\nown extensive knowledge of herbs and ingredients, of what they represent... To craft a potion of their very own. This is much more difficult than it may seem... but I shan’t go into that, as this is no true Alchemical textbook, but\n\nrather, an introduction to the science. \nLastly, Enthusiasm. This trait may seem unusual to list, as most would automatically think of someone whom is impulsive and stupid... ‘overtly’ enthusiastic. No, Alchemy requires a special type of\n\nenthusiasm. One tempered by logic and patience. Enthusiasm is needed because without the drive to learn more, to improve your skill and to create potions of wonder... you are nothing, and shall go nowhere. The reason this is listed at the bottom of\n\nthe list instead of the top, is simply because by reading this far, you have shown an ounce of this quality, and as such, you may now take pride in knowing that you have the potential to learn this art.\n\nChapter 2: The Life of an Alchemist\nThe Lifestyle.\nThe life of an Alchemist, despite its labours, is a rewarding and interesting one. Many nations, many companies, and many guild need\n\nAlchemist, which means that an Alchemist is very rarely without options. Indeed, one of the most fulfilling parts of being an Alchemist is the satisfaction of knowing you are needed and wanted. An Alchemist rarely stays in one\n\nlocation whilst unemployed, often traveling from kingdom to kingdom, seeking a guild or company that has use for them. In my experience, this does not take a long time... Although it is important to measure one’s options before taking a job. This\n\nis not to say that an Alchemest /must/ be employed, gods know many Alchemists choos a life of reclusion and introversion; preferring the company of their ingredients and their equipment to the company of people or currency.\n\nAs an Alchemist, there are many different paths one may choose from; be it creating poisons or creating potions to heal the ill and wounded. These paths will play a large role in deciding how easily one can get jobs, and from whom. For example, one\n\nwho makes poisons may find it harder to get a job than one who create healing serums, as poisons are hard to mass-produce, which means are of no real use to an army, and they are certainly of no use for a peaceful company of guild.\n\nThis is much the opposite for healing potions, as oddly enough they are some of the simplest to make, and can be made in quite large quantities without losing much of their effectiveness, so all the aforementioned employers could\n\nhave a use for an Alchemist of healing. Do not think that these are the only two paths that an Alchemist may follow, however- and certainly no Alchemist sticks only to one branch of the science- think of it more as a specialization. I,\n\nfor example, specialize in incendiary and explosive concoctions.. Alchemist Fire, if you will. This means that I am often asked to work for militaries of small offensive guilds... even research companies.\n\nIn conclusion, the lifestyle in terms of business, for an Alchemist, is much dependent upon what they specialize in, and regardless of their business opportunities, the life of an Alchemist is rarely full, and in my opinion, very fulfilling.\n\nThe Morality of an Alchemist.\nMany jobs seem to have a set “morality.” Soldiers are expected to be lawful and trsutworthy, Mages to be intellectual and neutral... the Morality of an Alchemist is much the same as that of a Mage, and\n\nis, much like one’s business opportunities, dictated by the Alchemist’s specialization. To use the same examples as before, it would be more likely for an Alchemist specializing in poisons to fall into a darker, more “evil” morality, as they\n\nwould likely find their work with small groups of assassins of the like. Whereas an Alchemist specializing in healing, would be much more likely to fall into a “good” morality, as by nature, their job of choice is to help people.\n\nThere are not set rules, of course, a healing Alchemist may very well choose to assist an unlawful organization, and an Alchemist of poison may choose to supply a small vigilante group with his poisons. In essence, the Morality of an Alchemist is\n\nneutral, and is dictated completely by the individual.\nThe Living Conditions of an Alchemist\nThis section will be somewhat smaller, as there is very little that can be written about this. An Alchemist’s abode will generally have only one constant; a\n\nlab, usually located in a basement. This lab would contain an Alchemist’s supplies, equipment, and is generally kept locked, as much inside would be fragile or dangerous. As for why it is usually found in a basement... Well.. The earth seems to be a\n\nbit more resistance to explosions that wooden or cobblestone walls.\nFarewell:\nI thank your, read reader, for taking the time to read my small introduction to the Alchemist. I bid you good ever, good day, and farewell.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Who ever is reading this i see you have found my book. If you care enough to countie i am warning you there is no going back.\n\nI am a frost witch,§0\n§0many people think we are bad and we are.§0\n§0We brew. we dont murder people,but we do hurt them. We need of us. If you are a lady go into the forest near j-berg. Say angtshon bergshan testie. if one of us hear you we will come out and\n\nTeach you the basics of brewing. You can use it for bad or good,but if you dont want to learn more on your own chose bad§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0    From,§0\n§0       Helen Thomas", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "It is to my understanding that you wield extensive power in the black market in Sutica. I ask you for a favor. §0\n§0\n§0At the moment, Oren is in chaos. Dreadlanders raid us everyday and night. The guard is ineffective and\n\ndull. To prevent our city from falling into chaos, we must have \"groups\" to blame. We in our history, starting from the White Rose all the way to Felsen, has detested the idea of elves in our town. Although our government has changed on that position,\n\nrather, the king's views, there are people that are willing to turn a blind eye. As I remember in Felsen, there were executions every elven day. It was glorious to behold. §0\n§0Now, as to your role, it is quite simple. Scare off or ship dark elves to\n\nJOhannesburg. Although dark elves are rare within our walls, I have heard of the great number of them in Sutica. If you could send them to us, or scare them away from Sutica to go to Johannesburg, it gives us the nessecary course of action for\n\npeace in Oren. §0\n§0\n§0The Creator has blessed us with a new land. I ask that you assist us for your aid may prove to be repaid twice fold in the days to come. Dark elves feed paranoia. They feed to the heart of our city, that of justice, of peace\n\nand nessecary good. §0\n§0\n§0I know you may have no qualms with your role, but rest assured, these dark elves would be better off with us. I hope that you do think so too.§0\n§0\n§0Payment is negotiated as 32 iron bars for the first dark elf\n\nand 24 for every dark elf after. It is to my knowledge that these dark elves must be women. The lords are tired sometimes and must relax at the hands of a warm, tight embrace.§0\n§0\n§0Oren is facing a time like never before. I hope ye' can help us\n\nprosper, together.§0\n§0\n§0Ave Oren.§0\n§0-The name is marked by black ink. It seemed to be smudged beyond recognition.-", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "voidal theory§0\n§0\n§0alchemy guide§0\n§0\n§0volume 1 to 3§0\n§0\n§0the pumkin lord§0\n§0\n§0golems", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "§0Journal============§0\n§0\n\nDay One:§0\n§0\n§0Today we landed on this strange isle people are calling it Axios and we have settled in a lake town.  §0\n§0We are close to the sea and a large forest. I have lost all my tools and am now blind in the arts of woodwork.\n\nDay Two:§0\n§0\n§0I bought an iron axe with what little money survived and have started to gather resources. §0\n§0\n§0I also found a volcano with Roopak and I decided to make a Guild for adventurers.\n\nDay Three:§0\n§0\n§0I went adventuring with a girl called Artemis, we travelled across peaks for a whole day untill we came to a giant portal. We travelled across mountains and into spike fields. I also saw a ruin I would like to explore further at a later date.\n\n\n§0I also built some of the guild hall under the Library, Shynys and I are working on it together. §0\n§0\n§0I have heard that Thain has not yet awaken in this Realm. I long to see. I have\n\nDay Four:§0\n§0\n§0I still wait for Thain, I miss her very dearly, yet not much has happened. I have started to build my guild hall.\n\nDay Five:§0\n§0\n§0I have finished my guild hall and am writing up the poster now. Thain has still not awoken and I am doing well as a steward. §0\n§0\n§0I met a nice young woman named Artemis I think she will be good for the guild. She thinks I am in love!\n\nDay Six: §0\n§0\n§0I have helped design a new district for Sutica. Iris Wharf.\n\nDay Seven: §0\n§0\n§0I created the poster and have noted down the replies. The trial is tommorrow and only Silv'ar can not attend. §0\n§0\n§0I have done plenty of preparation.  Thain woke today and I put a bouquet of flowers in her house with a note.\n\nDay Ten:§0\n§0\n§0I have learnt of a water evocation teacher who goes by the name of Steven §0\n§0(Spoduminister)", ""),
        StaticBook("Intro. Alchemist", "§bRosso", "Forward:\n\nGreetings, my dear reader. Within the pages of this tome I shall be explaining to you what it takes to be a true Alchemist. I shall run you through our mindset, qualities, and traditions.\n\nChapter I: \n\nThe Recipe for a Good Alchemist.\n\nIt takes a special breed of person to be an Alchemist: a rare combination of patience, creativity, intellect, enthusiasm, and of course, an unusual resistance to explosions. For those who wish to follow the noble path of an Alchemist, you must first\n\nconsider if you possess these key traits, for if you do not, much like a potion made with a flawed recipe, you will be ineffective.\n\nDo not mistake what I say for supremacism, but rather realize that it is simply an effort to save you.\n\nIf you do not possess these qualities, dear reader it is quite possible that you, in the attempt to follow our path, may indeed end up harming yourself and others. Now, allow me to go more in-depth about what these traits mean, in order of their\n\nimportance to an Alchemist: Patience. Alchemy, I'm afraid, is not a fast nor particularly interesting process. An Alchemist will spend most of their time for their elembic to boil so that the extract of your ingredients may be collected for use, or simply\n\nwaiting for a potion to brew. Very little of the time it takes to create a potion is actually spent creating the mixture, although this time is indeed the most interesting part of Alchemy in my opinion. Because of all this, dear reader, Patience is vital\n\nto an Alchemist, as it would be, in my opinion, apt to give Alchemy the formula consisting of 25 \"parts\":\n\n>7 parts waiting (brewing, waiting for the elembic's water to boil.)\n\n>5 parts setting up, cleaning up, and inspecting your equipment.\n\n>5 parts testing (making sure your potion works.)\n\n>3 parts failure (realizing that your recipe is bollocks and starting over.)\n\n>3 parts extracting (boiling down the ingredients and collecting the condensation using an alembic.)\n\n>1 parts explosion (surprisingly common.)\n\n>1 parts mixing the bloody potion.\n\nIntellect. You may be wondering why this is not the first on my list... Well, someone whom is completely dull, yet is willing to give up hours upon hours to make up for it, is much more likely to succeed than someone brilliant who will become frustrated\n\nand rushed the second they see that their brew will be taking a very long time to finish. So, while this trait is near-vital, it can be made up for by a massive amount of patience and dedication.\n\nCreativity. Please note that I am writing here to explain what it takes to be an Alchemist, not a good Alchemist. Creativity is something completely vital to anyone who wishes to be a master alchemist... but it is not to those whom only wish to dabble in\n\nthe art. Creativity is essential for those who wish to truly excel at the art, because one cannot just follow recipes forever. There comes a time, when an apprentice Alchemist must take these matters into their own hands. No more books, no more\n\nexplanations as to why these particular ingredients must be used... there comes a time, when every Alchemist must learn to rely on their own extensive knowledge of herbs and ingredients, of what they represent... To craft a potion of their very own. This\n\nis much more difficult that it may seem... but I shan't go into that, as this is no true Alchemical textbook, but rather, an introduction to the science.\n\nLastly, Enthusiasm. This trait may seem unusual to list, as most would automatically think of someone who is impulsive and stupid... 'overly' enthusiastic. No, Alchemy requires a special type of enthusiasm. One tempered by logic and patience.\n\nEnthusiasm is needed because without the drive to learn more, to improve your skill and to create potions of wonder... you are nothing, and shall go nowhere. The reason this is listed at the bottom of the list instead of the top, is simply because by\n\nreading this far, you have shown an ounce of this quality, and as such, you may now take pride in knowing that you have the potential to learn this art.\n\nChapter 2:\n\nThe Life of an Alchemist\n\nThe Lifestyle.\nThe life of an Alchemist, despite its labours, is a rewarding and interesting one. Many nations, many companies, and many guilds need Alchemists, which means that an Alchemist is very rarely without options. Indeed, one of the\n\nmost fulfilling parts of being an Alchemist is the satisfaction of knowing you are needed and wanted. An Alchemist rarely stays in one location whilst unemployed, often traveling from kingdom to kingdom, seeking a guild or company that has use for them.\n\nIn my experience, this does not take a long time... Although it is important to measure one's options before taking a job. This is not to say that an Alchemist /must/ be employed, gods know many Alchemists choose a life of reclusion and\n\nintroversion--preferring the company of their ingredients and their equipment to the company of people or currency. As an Alchemist, there are many different paths one may choose from; be it creating poisons or creating potions to heal the ill and\n\nwounded. These paths will play  large role in deciding how easily one can get jobs, and from whom. For example, one who makes poisons may find it harder to get a job than one who creates healing serums as poisons are hard to mass-produce, which means are\n\nof no real use to an army, and they are certainly of no use for a peaceful company of a guild. This is much the opposite for healing potions, as oddly enough they are some of the simplest to make, and can be made in quite large quantities without losing\n\nmuch of their effectiveness, so all the aforementioned employers could have a use for an Alchemist of healing. Do not think that these are the only two paths that an Alchemist may follow, however--and certainly no Alchemist sticks only to one branch of\n\nthe science--think of it more as a specialization. I, for example, specialize in incendiary and explosive concoctions. Alchemist Fire, if you will. This means that I am often asked to work for militaries of small offensive guilds… even research companies.\n\nIn conclusion, the lifestyle in terms of business, for an Alchemist, is much dependent upon what they specialize in, and regardless of their business opportunities the life of an Alchemist is rarely full, and in my opinion, very fulfilling.\n\nThe Morality of an Alchemist.\nMany jobs seem to have a set \"morality.\" Soldiers are expected to be lawful and trustworthy, Mages to be intellectual and neutral... the Morality of an Alchemist is much the same as that of a Mage, and is, much like\n\none's business opportunities dictated by the Alchemist's specialization. To use the same examples as before, it would be more likely for an Alchemist specializing in poisons to fall into a darker, more \"evil\" morality, as they would likely find their work\n\nwith small groups of assassins of the like. Whereas an Alchemist specializing in healing, would be much more likely to fall into a \"good\" morality, as by nature, their job of choice is to help people. There are not set rules, of course, a healing\n\nAlchemist may very well choose to assist an unlawful organization, and an Alchemist of poison may choose to supply a small vigilante group with his poisons. In essence, the Morality of an Alchemist is neutral, and is dictated completely by the individual.\n\nThe Living Conditions of an Alchemist\nThis section will be somewhat small, as there is very little that can be written about this. An Alchemist's abode will generally have only one constant: a lab, usually located in a basement. This lab would contain\n\nan Alchemist's supplies, equipment, and is generally kept locked, as much inside would be fragile or dangerous. As for why it is usually found in a basement… Well… The earth seems to be a bit more resistant to explosions than wooden or cobblestone walls.\n\nFarewell:\nI thank you, reader, for taking the time to read my small introduction to the Alchemist. I bid you good ever, good day, and farewell.\n\nAn Introduction to the Alchemist\n\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven", ""),
