        StaticBook("Elves amirite", "§bNathan Solidor", "FlugalMC agrees to help Squish Co with crafting and or item gathering in the future in return for 1 thanium ice chest and possible other future requests.§0\n§0FlugalMC also agrees to remember the name Squish CO if he gets a nobel status.§0\n§0\n§0Signed- Squishybear7", ""),
        StaticBook("Comp. Alchemy:V3", "§bAislin Maar", "§0§lComprehensive§0\n§0§l      Alchemy§0\n§0\n§0       Volume II§0I§0\n§0\n§0__________________§0\n§0\n§0       Written By§0\n§0       §0\n§0       §0§nAislin Maar§0\n§0   §0\n§0          1577\n\n§nTable of Contents§0\n§0\n§0§lVolume III§0\n§0\n§0§l-Brewing Basics§0\n§0§l-Recipes§0\n§0--Practical§0\n§0--Healing§0\n§0--Offensive & Defensive\n\n§lPart One: Brewing Basics§0\n§0\n§0Now we have our herbs, we have our lab, all our tools are laid out and ready to be used.. But, now what? Now, we have to put those herbs to use. Herbs can all be prepared in some way,\n\nand you will use your tools and your lab for this. Every container, every vial, every cutting board and spoon and knife and bowl must all be perfectly clean, and free of any kind of foreign contamination. If they are not, your potion is likely to fail,\n\neven if you put in all the right ingredients. Alchemy is a science, and it must be done correctly. First, water symbols, then fire, then air, then finally earth. Always in this order, and never any other. Before this, though, we need our bases. Some\n\nsimpler potions only require distilled water. We can get distilled water by filtering regular water through charcoal in our alembic. A simple, easy process to do. Aqua Vitae is our more powerful base. This is created by distilling a peculiar red fungus\n\ncalled Nether Wart into water in your alembic. Making Aqua Vitae is very good practice for budding alchemists. Nether Wart can usually be procured from skilled farmers, and if that fails, try buying it in the auctions.\n\nNote that when I recipe calls for two air symbols, one of grace and one of calm, the two symbols will not be the same herb. You will need to experiment and rationalize to find out which herbs you will be using. Herbs typically do not need to be brewed\n\nin a specific order within their own symbol. You should also think about which herbs you’re choosing. A student of mine once came to me quite perplexed. He asked me why he wasn’t able to make the simplest of potions, a minor potion of mending.\n\nI asked him which herbs he had chosen for his two representations of life. He informed me he had chosen Serpent’s Stalk for the water symbol and Goblin’s Ivy for the earth symbol, but that is still wouldn’t work. He said they both had representations\n\nof life, and that surely it must work. I instructed him to look back at his notes again, and to read off the various representations of Goblin’s Ivy, and he did. Resilience, poison, and life. Now, an herb that also represents poison wouldn’t do very well\n\nin a healing potion, now would it? When looking for the right herb for a recipe, you should always take into account the other representations of the herb, and consider if they are consistent with the potion you are trying to brew.\n\n§lPart Two: Recipes§0\n§0\n§0§o§nChapter One:§0 §0\n§0\n§0§oPractical Potions§0\n§0\n§0§o§nChapter Two:§0 §0\n§0\n§0§oHealing Potions§0\n§0\n§0§o§nChapter Three:§0 §0\n§0\n§0§oOffensive and Defensive Potions\n\n§l§nChapter One: Practical Potions§0\n§0\n§0§nSoft Relaxant:§0\n§0A calming potion for those with high levels of anxiety or those who simply need to calm down after a hectic day. This potion will also relieve muscle pain, though it is not a healing potion. §0\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae. §0\n§02 Air Symbols (Steadiness, Calm)§0\n§0\n§0§nBeauty Sleep:§0\n§0A potion with a sweet taste that causes the drinking to fall asleep quickly. It is a natural, rejuvenating sleep, and the drinker can be woken by normal means.\n\nIt will not render the drinker unconscious, but simply aides those who have trouble falling asleep.§0\n§0Base: Aqua Vitae§0\n§03 Air Symbols (Steadiness, Speed, Calm) §0\n\n§nPotion of Beauty:§0\n§0This potion will give the drinker softer, more even toned skin, as well as thicker, fuller, and stronger hair. It will brighten the eyes, getting rid of bags from tiredness, and even helps with acne.§0\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae§0\n§03 Air Symbols (Focus, Beauty, Joy)§0\n§0\n§0\n§0§nCleansing Incense:§0 §0\n§0This liquid, when exposed to air, will begin to release a gas with a very pleasant, calming §0scent.It§0 works well as a non burning incense,\n\nand will make any room smell better, or any date more romantic. §0\n§0\n§0Base: Aqua Vitae§0\n§02 Fire Symbols (Heat, Courage)§0\n§01 Air Symbol (Focus)§0\n\n§l§nChapter Two: Healing Potions§0\n§0\n§0§nPotion of Mending:§0\n§0A very basic, and very useful potion. Capable of healing minor cuts and wounds, as well as stopping bleeding. Every healer should have this,and it can be found in many household cabinets as it\n\nis a common potion used for first aid. §0\n§0Base: Distilled Water§0\n§01 Water Symbol (Life)§0\n§01 Earth Symbol (Life)§0\n\n§nSoothing Ointment:§0\n§0A watery ointment that can be applied to the skin to relieve itching and burning. It leaves a pleasant sensation behind. Apply liberally and allow to sit on the affected area for up to five minutes. Remove excess with a damp cloth.\n\nAvoid contact with eyes and do not ingest. §0\n§0Base: Aqua Vitae§0\n§02 Water Symbols (Rejuvenation, Life)§0\n§01 Fire Symbol (Compassion)§0\n\n§nQuick Healing:§0\n§0A simple potion meant to heal up a cut or a would in when you find yourself in a bind. This brew is popular among soldiers on the battlefield, as it’s quick and efficient effects can save lives when on the move.\n\nBase: Distilled water§0\n§01 Air Symbol (Speed)§0\n§01 Earth Symbol (Life)§0\n§0\n§0§nFrozen Blood:§0\n§0This thick potion will act as a liquid bandage when applied to a wound. While not doing much for healing or blood clotting, sometimes it can be the best you can do\n\nin a certain situation. It is a slimy substance that steams profusely after being applied to the skin.§0\n§0\n§0Base: Aqua Vitae§0\n§02 Water Symbols (Ice, Freezing)§0\n§01 Air Symbol (Speed)§0\n§01 Earth Symbol (Life)\n\n§nGreater Potion of Healing:§0\n§0\n§0The strongest healing potion that is commonly available, this brew will seal wounds, stop bleeding, repair fractures, and can be consumed to stop internal bleeding. It’s said to reduce tumors and even to\n\nhelp cleanse internal organs or poisons and other toxins.§0\n§0\n§0Base: Aqua Vitae§0\n§02 Water Symbols (Ice, Freezing)§0\n§01 Air Symbol (Speed)§0\n§02 Earth Symbol (Life, Perserverence)§0\n§0\n\n§l§nChapter Three: Offensive and Defensive Potions§0\n§0\n§0\n§0§nPotion of Celerity:§0\n§0This particular brew is one that can be quite useful on the battlefield. It provides the drinker with a burst of energy that allows them to move faster\n\neven with a set of iron armor on. §0\n§0\n§0Base: Aqua Vitae§0\n§01 Fire Symbol (Power)§0\n§03 Air Symbols (Swiftness, Speed, Travel)§0\n§0\n\n§nStoneskin:§0\n§0A legendary brew, Stoneskin. Tales of old have told that entire wars have been won with this concoction. A handful of men have taken down hundreds with this flowing through their blood, charging through lines and slaughtering\n\ntheir enemies with ease. A miracle to some, perhaps, and the greatest bane of others. However, it comes with a price. Stoneskin has a terrible side effect known as Akmen’s Syndrome. Continued usage of Stoneskin will cause nerves to break down,\n\na loss of feeling in the skin, degradation of the body, and eventually death. Still, this potion can be life saving, if you’re up for the risk.§0\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae§0\n§02 Water Symbols (Life, Rebirth) §0\n§03 Fire Symbols (Power, Chaos, Anger) §0\n§01 Air Symbols (Speed) §0\n§04 Earth Symbols (Fear, Perseverance, Brutality, Resiliance) §0\n\n§nSmoke Whispers:§0\n§0This potion is thrown down, breaking the glass and causing the liquid inside to make contact with open air. When this happens, a violent reaction occurs, and a large cloud of black vapor will expand from the point of impact in the\n\nblink of an eye. This can be extremely helpful to those who wish to escape someone without being seen, or even to prank your friends!§0\n§0\n§0Base: Aqua Vitae§0\n§02 Fire Symbols (Chaos, Courage)§0\n§02 Air Symbols (Swiftness, Speed)§0\n\n§nAlchemist’s Fire:§0\n§0A famed and fabled potion. Alchemist’s Fire is a favorite in any army’s arsenal. It can be an effective combatant, or it can be your worst nightmare. This potion is thrown down onto the ground, where the glass explodes and releases\n\nthe liquid inside. The potion itself is EXTREMELY flammable, and highly reactive to any air that touches it. It must be brewed in a cold lab, under very careful circumstances. This potion is just as liable to kill the user as it is to kill their intended\n\nvictim. The liquid, when released, will immediately ignite and spread over an area of about 4 meters by 4 meters. The reaction itself releases air, adding more fuel to the fire that has already started. It’s also known well to arsonists who wish to light\n\na building aflame in a matter of seconds. This potion is not for the faint of heart.§0\n§0\n§0Base: Aqua Vitae§0\n§02 Water Symbols (Fluidity)§0\n§04 Fire Symbols (Heat, Destruction, Rage, Chaos)§0\n§01 Air Symbol (Speed)\n\n§nFrost Oil:§0\n§0An oil used on arrows to cause the victim so be slowed greatly. A useful item to have in combat.. If you’re a sure shot, that is.§0\n\nBase: Lard ((Aqua Vitae))§0\n§03 Water Symbols (Fluidity, Freezing, Ice)§0\n§02 Air Symbols (Speed, Flight)§0\n§01 Earth Symbol (Endurance)", ""),
        StaticBook("Comp. Alch.:V2B4", "§bAislin Maar", "§l  Comprehensive§0\n§0§l     Alchemy§0\n§0\n§0       Volume II§0\n§0        Book IV§0\n§0-------------------§0\n§0\n§0      Written By§0\n§0\n§0      §0§nAislin Marr§0\n§0\n§0         1577\n\n§l     Chapter 4§0\n§0§l       Earth§0\n§0\n§6§oTippen's Root§0\n§0\n§0A very foul smelling weed which is the bane of many gardeners and farms. It grows rampant and it near impossible to get rid of, as any remnant of the root when it is evicted from it's place in\n\nsoil will simply continue to grow and sprout up once more. It has an odd appearance of purple flowers above the surface, and an reddish-brown root with black spots below the surface. It is used in nearly all healing potions, and has blood clotting\n\nproperties within the root itself, and can be used directly on a wound. That is, if you can stand the horrid stench of it.§0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0 Life, Regrowth, Resilience, Strength, Endurance§0\n\n§6§oAlabaster Leaf§0\n§0\n§0A small leafy plant found growing on fallen trees and up the trunks of those still standing. It can blend in with the leaves of its host tree, and it’s leaves hold a residue that can cause intense nausea, though it isn't particularly\n\nharmful until brewed into the right mix.§0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0 Immobility, Perseverance.\n\n§6§oSaffvil§0\n§0\n§0A green grass that spreads over the bodies of dead animals and rotting plants. It is extremely hard to get rid of, and smells like death itself. It’s commonly known to be used in potent poisons.§0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0 Resilience,\n\nEndurance, Death.\n\n§6§oClutcher's Straw§0\n§0\n§0A plant that grows about as tall as a common grass, but has a brown color, appearing almost like it’s dead. The blades are not as sharp as normal grass, but the roots beneath the soil are pitch black and razor sharp, so caution is\n\nadvised. It grows in hot, dry regions.§0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0 Impairment, Loss, Brutality.\n\n§6§oMetzli's Tangle§0\n§0\n§0A thick, green vine found in jungles. It has few leaves protruding from it’s main stalk, and relies mainly on it’s host tree for water and nutrition. It cannot survive on it’s own, and therefore can only be found against a large\n\ntree trunk in the humid, dark jungles.§0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0 Dependency, Fear\n\n§6§oGoblin's Ivy§0\n§0\n§0A murky brown colored ivy that spreads like a weed in deserts. It is extremely hard to get rid of and loves growing up the sides of any building, ruin, or mountain it can get onto. However, it doesn't get very tall, but remains the\n\nbane of those who must deal with it. It’s a weak reagent usually used in poisons. §0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0 Resilience, Poison, Life.", ""),
        StaticBook("Comp. Alch.:V2B3", "§bAislin Maar", "§0§l §0§lComprehensive§0\n§0§l       Alchemy§0\n§0\n§0       §0Volume II§0\n§0        Book III§0\n§0__________________§0\n§0\n§0       Written By§0\n§0\n§0      §0 §0§nAislin Maar§0\n§0\n§0           1577\n\n§nTable of Contents§0\n§0\n§0§lVolume II§0\n§0§lHerbology§0\n§0\n§1§l-Water§0\n§4§l-Fire§0\n§7§l-Air§0\n§6§l-Earth\n\n     §0§lChapter Two§0\n§0§l         Air§0\n§0\n§7§oLarihei's Fingers:§0\n§0\n§0A plant with smooth, burrowing roots and small, short leaves in the warmer summer months. It grows exlusively in snowy regions, and can sometimes be hard\n\nto find among the snowpack. It has no immediate healing properties and is not particularly edible, but when brewed into the right mix, it can make you much more acute and aware.§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0\n§0Grace, Beauty, Steadiness, Focus, Swiftness§0\n\n§7§oElf's Hair Vine:§0\n§0\n§0A sturdy white vine that is covered in dozens of small, white flowers. It grows on high mountain cliffs, out of the direct wind, as the flowers are very fragile and will blow away very easily. Harvest with great care, as the\n\ngentlest touch can send the flowers floating away on the lightest of breezes.§0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0\n§0Grace, Speed, Flight, Travel\n\n§7§oCrouching Feather:§0\n§0\n§0A flowering plant that is a very rare sight. It has three, white curved petals that flow downward from the top of the stem, looking like a feather. It only blooms at night, and usually grows beneath the shade of trees in\n\nforests. The petals are very delicate and liable to break and float away with a strong gust. §0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0\n§0Speed, Travel, Grace, Beauty\n\n§7§oHalfling's Grass:§0\n§0\n§0A tall plant that grows in damp, well lit forests. The roots are soft and round, and are the bit that an alchemist needs. The leaves can be smoked and have an effect similar to cactus §0green.It§0 can also be processed into an oil,\n\nwhich has an even stronger effect. It’s named for being able to grow to the same height as Halflings, and also for its preference by that race as pipeweed.§0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0\n§0Calm, Joy, Lifting", ""),
        StaticBook("Comp. Alch.:V2B2", "§bAislin Maar", "§l   Comprehensive§0\n§0§l      Alchemy§0\n§0\n§0        Volume II§0\n§0         Book II§0\n§0-------------------§0\n§0\n§0       Written By§0\n§0 §0\n§0       §0§nAislin Marr§0\n§0\n§0          1577\n\n     §0§lChapter Two§0\n§0§l        Fire§0\n§0\n§4§oCrimson Vase§0\n§0\n§0A flower with deep red petals that curve gently upwards in the likeness of a vase. It’s petals are very delicate and harvesting it for use as a reagent should be done with case or\n\nit will become ineffective. Touching the petals gives the skin a warm sensation, though not unpleasant. It is found in dry forests among grasses and can be easily spotted by it’s remarkable red color. §0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0 Heat, Warmth, Love,\n\nPassion, Compassion.\n\n§4§oDrake's Tail§0\n§0\n§0A thorny plant growing in hot, humid places. It’s fairly rare to find, often growing in shade near a source of water, such as an oasis. It has red hued, thorny stems, and short roots beneath the sand. When harvesting, you must bring\n\nthick leather gloves, as being struck by one of the thorns will cause a high fever. §0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0 Heat, Destruction, Chaos.\n\n§4§oDrawf's Pumpkin§0\n§0\n§0An orange-leafed shrub which is found in swamplands, Dwarf’s Pumpkin is short and stumpy, with thick leaf stems and broad leaf blades. The leaves themselves are what you want to harvest, but be prepared for a strong odour on your\n\ntrip back to the lab. This plant is often used to brew a strong ale that dwarves prefer.§0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0 Rage, Envy, Bitterness, Power, Strength.\n\n§4§oBlood Lotus§0 §0\n§0\n§0A vining plant that crawls up the walls of sandstone caverns, it’s well adapted to it’s hot residence. It has incredibly nutritious properties, and there are those who saw you can live entirely off of a diet of these alone.\n\n§nRepresentations:§0 Hope, Courage.\n\n§4§oFlameroot Tongue§0\n§0\n§0This plant appears almost like a rose in a forest field, opting to live in sunny, water abundant regions. It’s thick stalks are covered in brambles and has bright red roots. The brambles, if allowed to prick the skin, will cause\n\nintense, blistering pain, the likes of which will cause most to pass out. The use of thick leather gloves is highly recommended when harvesting. §0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0 Heat, Anger, Destruction.", ""),
        StaticBook("Comp. Alch.:V2B1", "§bAislin Maar", "§0§lComprehensive§0\n§0§l      Alchemy§0\n§0\n§0§l     §0 Volume II§0\n§0         Book I§0\n§0------------------§0\n§0\n§0       Written By§0\n§0       §0\n§0       §0§nAislin Marr§0\n§0\n§0          1577\n\n§nTable of Contents§0\n§0\n§0§lVolume II§0\n§0§lHerbology§0\n§0\n§1§l-Water§0\n§4§l-Fire§0\n§7§l-Air§0\n§6§l-Earth\n\n     §0§lChapter One§0\n§0§l        Water§0\n§0\n§1§oMandragora:§0\n§0\n§0While not an herb by definition, this plant is used very often in alchemy as a strong healing reagent. The tuber can be found in pools of water, spotted by their leaves on the surface.\n\nThe leaves are commonly used as garnish and even for making tea, while the meat of the plant is below the surface, suspended by the leaves. The meat is the part of the plant you seek, but be very cautious, as the plant can at certain seasonal times,\n\nrelease spores that cause paralysis. Caution is advised when harvesting this herb. §0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0 §0\n§0Rejuvenation, Life, Rebirth, Regrowth, Fluidity\n\n§1§oSerpent's Stalk:§0\n§0\n§0A fairly common plant, Serpent's Stalk is often found growing next to water in dry biomes. It is easily mistaken for sugar canes or reeds, as it has a very similar color to it and it grows in the same manner, as a stalk going\n\nstraight up into the air. It has wonderful properties for healing, being mostly used for the treatment of burns. The inside of the stalk is almost gel like, and when collected from the stalk and mixed with water, it has a cooling effect. §0\n\n§nRepresentations:§0 §0\n§0Life, Rebirth, Calmness, Fluidity\n\n§1§oFrost Vine:§0\n§0\n§0A blue hued vine growing among snowy cliffs in mountainous regions, Frost Vine has an aptitude for healing as many other Water symbols do. It can be rubbed on the skin to cool a burn or loosen stiff muscles.\n\nProlonged use is not advised, but will not cause permanent numbness. §0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0 §0\n§0Life, Calmness, Ice\n\n§1§oBlissfoil:§0\n§0\n§0A plant characterized by thin stems topped with small blue flowers, blissfoil is a plant that can help relieve pain and numb an area. Be cautious in it’s use, as too much can cause permanent numbness.\n\nA brew of pure blissfoil should be used sparingly and never consumed directly. It’s natural home is in dry regions among grasses. §0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0 §0\n§0Calmness, Peace, Apathy§0\n\n§1§oAthin:§0\n§0\n§0A blue colored algae that grows in icy waters. Athin can only be found in cold regions, and those seeking it should come prepared to ward off the elements. Fishing nets can sometimes get this algae caught in themselves.\n\nThe algae is extremely heat resistant, and is one of the best reagents to use for that sort of potion or oil. §0\n§0\n§0§nRepresentations:§0 §0\n§0Freezing, Life, Ice, Balance.§0", ""),
        StaticBook("Comp. Alchemy:V1", "§bAislin Maar", "§l   Comprehensive§0\n§0§l      Alchemy§0\n§0\n§0        Volume I§0\n§0\n§0-------------------§0\n§0\n§0       Written By§0\n§0\n§0       §0§nAislin Marr§0\n§0\n§0          1577\n\n§lTable of Contents§0\n§0§lVolume I§0\n§0\n§0-Alchemy Introduction§0\n§0-Basic Materials§0\n§0-Symbols§0\n§0-Gathering\n\n§lPreface:§0 These volumes were originally written when I realized that the most widely spread and commonly used texts at the time were outdated, inaccurate, or simply misleading. I had many requests from people asking for apprenticeship,\n\nand would often point them to these books. It stuck me one day that these books had become more of a hinderance to my students than a help, and so I set out to write my own. That being said, these are written to be used by alchemy students as a textbook\n\nand a learning tool so that they can further themselves in the skill, with or without the aid of a teacher.§0\n\n§lChapter One: The Basics of Alchemy§0\n§0 §0\n§0The first thing you should know about alchemy is that is requires patience. It is tedious, long winded, and tiring. You will find yourself isolated in your lab for many hours,\n\nalone in fields gathering herbs for days on end, and struggling with a recipe or brew for months. You cannot act in the way a blacksmith acts, taking one chunk of metal and seeing it transform in your hands,by your own hands, before your eyes.\n\nIt is, in fact, a very complex and time consuming skill to learn and to practice. However, it is one of the most rewarding. Alchemy at it’s core is about discovery, exploration, experimentation, and in the end the finished product\n\nwill bring you such a sense of joy and achievement that you will never question the hours, days, and months you spent working towards it. Patience is the companion of wisdom.§0\n§0To define alchemy in its most basic sense is to say that\n\nherbs are combined to create a potion. While true on the surface, alchemy requires much more thought than simply placing herbs into a flask and brewing them for some grand drink. Every herb we use on a frequent basis has an\n\nassociation of meanings to it, often pertaining to its physical features, herbal uses, and overall symbol. This is where we delve into the subject of Equivalent Exchange. I speak not of turning dirt to gold but of exchanging something of equal value to\n\nreceive a new product. In this sense is where we find the correlation between alchemy and equivalent exchange. When I first began to learn alchemy, the concept made little sense to me. I was teaching myself alchemy, stubborn as I was, refused to seek a\n\nteacher. Determination and independence can be a drawback, though, and it took me far longer than it should have to understand the concept and how it pertained to alchemy. As many do, I assumed you could simply mash some herbs, throw them in a pot,\n\nand get something out of it. On a very basic level, this is possible, but you will never find anything of true substance this way. To truly begin to unlock the potential of alchemy, we have to use the concept of equivalent exchange.\n\nIn the sense that I will teach you, equivalent exchange does not deal with the physical sense of the herb per say. Instead, we look to the symbolic meanings of the herb, and then to the aspects of the potion we want to create. A very basic example, the\n\nmatters of which will be detailed in later volumes of this series, will be presented as a learning tool. A potion of mending is a very common, yet often life saving brew. It is in nearly every healers supply, and even found in households as a sort of\n\nfirst aid. It is one of the most simple and most widely sold brews, as it is extremely beneficial in our world of violent natured men. We seek a potion that can heal wounds both external and internal, often applied directly to a wound, soaked into\n\nbandages, or in some occurrences, consumed directly. A minor potion of healing cannot repair a shattered bone, cure an illness, or mend a severed appendage, but it can still save a life in the right time. With this, we find our symbolic meaning: life.\n\nFrom here, we can take this idea, a word, feeling, or sense of being, and look to our arsenal and find herbs which correlate to it. Here, I will touch briefly on the symbology of herbs. Water, Fire, Air, and Earth. All herbs are categorized as such,\n\nand all wield a different sense, feeling, and state. For simplicities sake, I will not go further on that here. For now, I will tell you that we can, within our index of herbs, find two that represent life. Tippen’s Root, a common tuber used\n\nin nearly every healing potion, and Serpent’s Stalk, a thin plant with healing properties. Both these herbs are symbolic of Life, and therefore can be used in exchange for our life giving potion. Life for life, as it were, but in a sense of meaning\n\nthrough symbols. In this way, you must think of the recipes for a potion as a mirror to what the potion is used for. This is how we address the concept of equivalent exchange.§0\n\n§l Chapter Two: Basic Materials§0\n§0\n§0Some of the most basic tools an alchemist should have within their lab are, of course, an alchemy set, also known as an alembic, a pestle and mortar, several cutting boards, spoons, knives of\n\nvarious sizes, leather gloves, safety glasses, lab coats, cleaning supplies and a sink with fresh water (preferably spring water), vials, bottles, and jars of all shapes and sizes. Filters are also necessary, both paper and metal, for keeping stray\n\nbits of your reagents out of the finished product. You’ll need plenty of storage space and a large, clean worktop on which to practice. You should aim for a lab with a relatively neutral room temperature, if not cold for those who can stand it.\n\nA hot lab is a dangerous lab. I also suggest a writing desk for note taking, research, and study, as well as bookshelves for keeping notes and textbooks organized. When gathering herbs, you’ll need to equip yourself with several pairs of alchemist's\n\nshears. These are specialized shears that are made with aurum, which is a soft and easily broken metal. Due to this, you’ll find yourself wanting to have many of these in your bag when you go out gathering, as it may be a waste of your day if you come\n\nback with too few herbs. It is wise for all alchemists to befriend a skilled blacksmith, as they can not only craft you the shears (hopefully, for a discounted price) but they can also repair your nearly ruined shears with ferrum. You can also have your\n\nshears enchanted, so using Unbreaking and Efficiency is recommended. Ensure that you wear your leather gloves when gathering more volatile herbs, and in some cases, safety glasses or even a respiratory mask is recommended.\n\n§lChapter Three: Symbols§0 §0\n§0\n§0Once a student has a basic grasp on the idea of equivalent exchange, we can begin to progress into the topic of symbols. As stated in chapter one, alchemy is ruled by the four basic elements of our creation:\n\nwater, fire, air, and earth. These symbols make up the four greater groups of herbs, like four great pillars holding up an all encompassing roof. Each symbol bears it’s own personality, so to speak. Water is calm, fluid, and life giving.\n\nIt refreshes, but it can also be dangerous. Freezing ice and powerful currents can cause death just as easily as any other element. Fire is hot, rageful, destructive. It is also powerful, passionate, and emotional. Air is light, soothing, and graceful.\n\nIt’s also lawless, and quick, whisking away a leaf in the blink of an eye.  Earth is steady, strong, and resilient. It can also hold dangers, entombing us in it’s unyielding grasp. With these four symbols we can generalize nearly everything we see at it's\n\nvery basic core. Through both the idea, concept, and physical attributes of anything in our world, we can associate it to one or more of these symbols in some way. We also must recognize that these symbols must be placed in a specific order,\n\nor you will find yourself failing every time. Water, Fire, Air, Earth. This is our order for brewing, and we must use it every time, even when not all symbols may be used in a specific potion. Now that we have laid in your mind the basic foundations\n\nof symbols, let’s take a look at the most how to gather these herbs, and what precautions we should take when venturing out into the world.§0\n\n§lChapter Four: Gathering§0 §0\n§0\n§0After having a general understanding of the basics, we’ll talk about how to physically gather your own herbs. As previously stated, you’ll need to equip yourself with alchemist’s shears and leather gloves when gathering.\n\nI also recommend bringing several jars or containers to properly store the gathered plants. “Herbs” is a very general term for the kind of reagents you’ll use, as some are not technically herbs at all. This being said, you’ll be searching in all manner\n\nof places for herbs, from scorching deserts, to muddy swamps, to snow peaked mountains. Make sure you’re properly dressed for the occasion and that you have adequate amounts of food and water for your journey. As well, bringing a minor healing potion for\n\nfirst aid would be smart, in case something does go wrong. Alchemist’s shears are made with two aurum ingots and two basic sticks, and can be crafted by adequate blacksmiths. They can be repaired with a single ferrum ingot by more highly skilled\n\nblacksmiths, though, so make sure you don’t ruin them entirely. You may also want to find an enchanter to imbue your shears with runes for Unbreaking and Efficiency so that they well last longer. You’ll have to search for many hours for some herbs,\n\nand you will more often than not find yourself unsuccessful when gathering. Worry not, though, as all alchemist’s must progress and learn to better spot the herbs they need. §0\n§0", ""),
        StaticBook("Gathering Herbs", "§bKatherine Fisher", "===================§0\n§0\n§0       The Art of§0\n§0    Gathering Herbs§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0       Written by§0\n§0      Liriel Fisher§0\n§0   §0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0==================\n\nForeword:§0\n§0This book was written by myself, and the research done by my close friend and alchemic colleague Nemir. As we consider ourselves budding and self-taught alchemists, we have spent many, many hours in libraries and pouring over every tome\n\nrelating to the profession that we could get our hands on. Through our experimentation and unwavering dedication to the art of alchemy, we have found ourselves in need of a comprehensive collection of knowledge on just where to find the herbs we depend on\n\nThus, The Art of Gathering Herbs was born. I hope you find it’s contents helpful.\n\nChapter 1-1: Introduction to Gathering§0\n§0-------------------Herbs are the most vital and important part of an alchemist’s professional life. They provide the basic necessary ingredients involved in all proper potions, and thus, must either be purchased\n\nor procured on your own. Purchasing dozens of herbs can be not only expensive, but extremely time consuming as you seek out sellers or wait for that one vital herb to show up in auction. Herbs can be found through various professions other than alchemy\n\nitself, such as lumberjacking, farming, and fishing. Herbs can also be obtained through treasure caskets retrieved from gathering. However, the best way for an alchemist to retrieve their own herbs while continuing to advance in the skill is to gather\n\nthem on their own with a pair of handy alchemists shears.  These special shears can be obtained from an adequate blacksmith at minimum.  They are made from two gold ingots, and two sticks. In my experience, I would recommend obtaining a large amount\n\nof these tools if you are unskilled as they will break quite easily in inexperienced hands. Personally, I carried ten pairs of these on my person when out searching for herbs as a fairly skilled alchemist. My more experienced companion, Nemir,\n\nwas able to obtain many more herbs than I was, but finding an herb can be very enlightening when you discover it with a pair of alchemists shears.  I would not recommend using this method of obtaining herbs until you are at least fair in skill, as even at\n\nthat level of knowledge you will not be very successful.\n\nChapter 1-2: Regions§0\n§0-------------------Now, finding the various herbs has been a rigorous and lengthy process. Nemir and I have narrowed the various types of regions into four types:§0\n§0Hot, cold, wet, and dry.\n\nYou can group various types of biomes into one category, such as taiga, ice Plains, and extreme Hills, which are all cold, etcetera.§0\n§0Dry would be plains and forests, hot is desert and mesa, and wet is jungle and swamps.\n\nChapter 2: Herbs and Where to Find Them§0\n§0-------------------Water Symbols\n\nMandragora: One of the most basic herbs in alchemy is mandragora. These rooty plants can be found in wet biomes, typically swamps, within ponds. They are usually found clinging to other aquatic plant life, such as lily pads and algaes.\n\nSerpernt's Stalk: A staple in any alchemist or healer's stash, Serpent's Stalk is often found alongside ponds and rivers in dry climates. In my search for the herb, I commonly found this snakeish plant growing up the stalks of sugar cane near water.\n\nFrost Vine: This interesting blue vine can be found snaking up the trunks of trees and rock walls of cold regions. The vines are often clearly visible and easy to find.§0\n\nBlissfoil: This healer's friend can be easily spotted in fields and forests in dry regions by their brilliant blue hued flowers. Though the stalk of the plant is the useful part, the flower petals are what will help you find them easily.\n\nAthin: An intriguing, blue colored algae, Athin is commonly found in cold regions, growing remarkably among icy waters. Though it can be a bit hard to spot in the bleak landscape, finding an iced over pond will surely yield this herb.\n\nFire Symbols:§0\n§0Crimson Vase: Found most commonly in dry regions, this interesting flower can be found in grassy fields and spotted by its - you guessed it - crimson colored petals.\n\nDrake's Tail: This herb is truly remarkable in my opinion. It grows where few other plants can, in the hot desert heat, perched among the pale yellow sandstone rocks. Wearing leather gloves when harvesting this is highly advised.\n\nDwarf's Pumpkin: One might think it's bright orange petals which give it it's name would be an excellent indicator of this herb's presence, but surprisingly, I had the best luck finding this peculiar plant by rummaging through the short grasses of swamps.\n\nBlood Lotus: Another remarkably persistent plant, this deep red herb can be found in hot regions snaking it's way up sandstone cliffs, and winding it's way through the sands.§0\n\nFlametongue Root: Like Drake's Tail, caution is advised when harvesting this volatile herb. As the name suggests, this root grows in the ground amongst the grasses of dry regions.\n\nAir Symbols:§0\n§0Larihei's Fingers: This particular plant grows in cold regions among the long grasses and ferns of the forest. Not much is known about this herb, but it is always advisable to have a few of each herb on hand.\n\nElf's Hair Vine: Finding this herb will lead you on a trek up a mountain to find the cold, snow topped peaks where this beautiful flowered vine grows. A hardy plant, this flower can be seen peaking out through snowy cliffs.\n\nCrouching Feather: This beautiful plant is named for its feather like white petals, and can most commonly be found among the tall grasses of dry regions. Though not as rare as some may believe, this herb requires patience to gather.\n\nHalfling's Grass: This plant is named for its common use by the Halfling race as a smoking herb. Typically it is found in dry climates throughout plains and forests alike. Search for this plant in tall grasses.\n\nEarth Symbols:§0\n§0Saffvil: This grassy plant is found in wet biomes predominantly, and can be found by searching through the short grasses that grow close to the soil.\n\nTippen's Root: An extremely common place, but also very useful plant, this root is widely used by both alchemist and healer. This plant is found in the grassy plains and forests of dry biomes.\n\nAlabaster Leaf: This small green leafy herb can be found snaking it's way through the branches and hiding amongst the leaves of a host tree. They attach themselves to all types of trees in dry regions.\n\nClutcher's Straw: A dry, brittle plant that can be found growing in hot regions. Search for this herb in low-lying shrubberies.\n\nMetzli's Tangle: A controversially named herb, this plant can be found snaking it's way up jungle trees in wet biomes.\n\nGoblin's Ivy: As it's name suggests, this herb can be found growing in hot, desert climates that Uruk's prefer. It is most commonly found on stone surfaces, but it can also be found growing up cactus plants.§0", ""),
        StaticBook("Element Symbols", "§bLuna Nevermore", "Symbols for higher level alchemy. Thes are used to find the types and attributes of different potion ingredients.\n\nWeak Earth:                   Name: Goblin’s Ivy§0\n§0Representation: Resilience, Poison, Life\n\nModerate Earth                                                      Name: Saffvil§0\n§0Representation: Resilience, Endurance, Death\n\nModerate Earth                                                       Name: Alabaster Leaf§0\n§0Representations: Immobility, Perseverance\n\nModerate Earth                                                    Name: Tippen’s Root§0\n§0Representation: Life, Regrowth, Resilience, Strength, Endurance\n\nModerate Earth                                                    Name: Desert Berry Bush§0\n§0Representation: Protection, Resilience\n\nStrong Earth                                                          Name: Starved Coyote’s Tick§0\n§0Representations: Protection, Resilience, Connection\n\nWeak Fire                                                              Name: Desert Bush Berry§0\n§0Representations: Empowerment, Overwhelming\n\nModerate Fire                                                      Name: Flametongue’s roots§0\n§0Representation: Heat, Anger, Destruction\n\nModerate Fire                                                      Name: Dwarf’s Pumpkin§0\n§0Representation: Rage, Envy, Bitterness, Power, Strength\n\nModerate Fire                                                      Name: Blood Lotus§0\n§0Representation: Hope, Courage§0\n\nModerate Fire                                                      Name: Elrow Berries§0\n§0Representations: Rage, anger, burning\n\nStrong Fire                                                          Name: Crimson Vase§0\n§0Representation: Heat, Warmth, Love, Passion, Compassion\n\nStrong Fire                                                          Name: Drake’s Tail§0\n§0Representations: Heat, Destruction, Chaos\n\nWeak Water                                                           Name: Blissfoil§0\n§0Representation: Calmness\n\nModerate Water                                                    Name: Serpent's Stalk§0\n§0Representations: Life, Rebirth, Calmness, Fluity\n\nModerate Water                                                    Name: Mandragora§0\n§0Representation: Rejuvenation, Life, Rebirth, Regrowth\n\nModerate Water                                                    Name: Zawabate§0\n§0Representations: Life, Calmness, Rebirth\n\nModerate Water                                                    Name: Frost Vine§0\n§0Representation: Life, Calmness, Ice\n\nStrong Water                                                        Name: Athin§0\n§0Symbol: Freezing, Ice, Life, Balance\n\nWeak Air                                                               Name: Jailer’s moss§0\n§0Representations: Travel, Movement\n\nModerate Air                                                          Name: Swamp’s blossom§0\n§0Representations: Quickness, speed, Travel\n\nStrong Air                                                            Name: Crouching Feather§0\n§0Representations: Speed, Travel, Gracefulness\n\nStrong Air                                                             Name: Elf’s hair vine§0\n§0Representations: Travel, Gracefulness, Speed, Flight", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "----Document Of Trade----§0\n§0\n§0This document is a offical contract of the trade route of the Wood Elf Kingdom and SCT. The trade route will be strictly profitable for both parties involved. It is to benifit the Kingdom and the SCT company. Each party\n\ncan ask for any item. The requested party can deny the trade if they either cannot fill their side of it or simply refuse to fill it. §0\n§0\n§0No mina will be passed between partys unless they are in agreeance to the over all price.§0\n§0\n§0If either party i\n\npleased with the other party, they are in full right to terminate this contract.§0\n§0\n§0All in agreeance with this contract sign here.§0\n§0\n§0SCT: Luna Nevermore§0\n§0(DjDerpCat)§0\n§0Wood Elfs:", ""),
